('source.greek.hesiod_hymns_evelyn_white.gutenberg','Hesiod, the Homeric Hymns, and Homerica, tr. Hugh G. Evelyn-White (1914)','Homeric Hymns (Ὕμνοι Ὁμηρικοί)','HISTORICAL_TRANSLATION',3,'Project Gutenberg (eBook #348)','Anonymous archaic poets; translated by Hugh G. Evelyn-White','lang.en','1914','2026-10-24','https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/348',NULL,'English text of the Homeric Hymns, Hesiod and the Homerica','Public-domain translation distributed by Project Gutenberg','English prose translation of the archaic Greek Homeric Hymns (Loeb Classical Library, 1914), with the line numbers of the Greek text',NULL,'Public web page',0,'Cite hymn and line numbers; publish independent retellings rather than the translation.',0,'English prose translation of the Greek','URL_SYNTAX_VALID','Project Gutenberg eBook #348 (Evelyn-White''s Hesiod, the Homeric Hymns, and Homerica), matched through public search-index metadata on 2026-10-24.'),
('source.greek.iliad_butler.gutenberg','Homer, The Iliad, rendered into English prose (tr. Samuel Butler, 1898)','Ἰλιάς','HISTORICAL_TRANSLATION',3,'Project Gutenberg (eBook #2199)','Homer (Homeric tradition); translated by Samuel Butler','lang.en','1898','2026-10-24','https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/2199',NULL,'Twenty-four books','Public-domain translation distributed by Project Gutenberg','English prose translation of the archaic Greek epic; book and line references follow the Greek text',NULL,'Public web page',0,'Cite book and line numbers of the Greek text; publish independent retellings rather than the translation.',0,'English prose translation of the Greek','URL_SYNTAX_VALID','Project Gutenberg eBook #2199 (Butler''s prose Iliad), matched through public search-index metadata on 2026-10-24.'),
('source.egypt.bentresh_stela.tla','Stele Louvre C 284 ("Bentresch-Stele"), Thesaurus Linguae Aegyptiae text record','Bentresh Stela (Louvre C 284)','ACADEMIC_EPIGRAPHIC_DATABASE',2,'Thesaurus Linguae Aegyptiae (Berlin-Brandenburg and Saxon Academies); object in the Musée du Louvre','Anonymous Late Period composition attributed to the reign of Ramesses II','lang.egy','Late Period or Ptolemaic (date disputed)','2026-10-24','https://thesaurus-linguae-aegyptiae.de/text/4VLZLA44UVGJZN22WIWP774LOQ',NULL,'Louvre C 284','Ancient inscription in the public domain; database transcriptions and modern translations keep their own terms','Hieroglyphic stela found in 1829 in a small Ptolemaic shrine next to the Khonsu temple at Karnak; a pseudo-historical tale set in the reign of Ramesses II',NULL,'Public web page',0,'Cite the stela by its sequence of episodes; publish independent retellings rather than any modern translation.',0,'Egyptian text with modern transcription','URL_SYNTAX_VALID','Thesaurus Linguae Aegyptiae text record for Louvre C 284, matched through public search-index metadata on 2026-10-24.'),
('source.egypt.dorbiney.bm','Papyrus D''Orbiney (the Tale of Two Brothers), British Museum EA 10183','Papyrus D''Orbiney','MUSEUM_OBJECT_AND_PRIMARY_TEXT',2,'The British Museum','Copied by the scribe Ennana, Nineteenth Dynasty','lang.egy','c. 1215–1185 BCE (dates given vary)','2026-10-24','https://www.britishmuseum.org/collection/object/Y_EA10183-2',NULL,'EA 10183, nineteen sheets','Ancient papyrus in the public domain; museum images and modern translations keep their own terms','Late Egyptian hieratic papyrus of the Nineteenth Dynasty containing the complete Tale of Two Brothers, Anubis and Bata',NULL,'Public web page',0,'Cite the papyrus by episode; publish independent retellings rather than any modern translation.',0,'Late Egyptian hieratic text','URL_SYNTAX_VALID','British Museum collection records for EA 10183 (sheets), matched through public search-index metadata on 2026-10-24; the museum and other sources give dates between c. 1215 and 1185 BCE.'),
('source.egypt.hatshepsut_birth_cycle.tla','Divine birth cycle of Hatshepsut, Deir el-Bahari: Thesaurus Linguae Aegyptiae scene texts (after Brunner''s numbering)','Geburtslegende der Hatschepsut, Deir el-Bahari','ACADEMIC_EPIGRAPHIC_DATABASE',2,'Thesaurus Linguae Aegyptiae (Berlin-Brandenburg and Saxon Academies)','Inscriptions of the reign of Hatshepsut','lang.egy','Reign of Hatshepsut (c. 1479–1458 BCE)','2026-10-24','https://thesaurus-linguae-aegyptiae.de/text/HT6VX6MTDFA23BCFLNEKOTHO2I',NULL,'Scene texts numbered after H. Brunner, Die Geburt des Gottkönigs; e.g. scene 14 (Thoth presents the child and its ka to Amun) and scene 15 (the gods fix the child''s lifetime)','Ancient inscriptions in the public domain; database transcriptions and modern translations keep their own terms','Relief cycle with captions in the northern half of the middle colonnade of Hatshepsut''s temple at Deir el-Bahari, presenting the queen as daughter of Amun-Re',NULL,'Public web page',0,'Cite scenes by content and, where known, Brunner''s scene numbers; publish independent retellings rather than any modern translation.',0,'Egyptian text with modern transcription','URL_SYNTAX_VALID','TLA scene-text records for the birth cycle (scene 14, text HT6VX6MTDFA23BCFLNEKOTHO2I; scene 15, text 5DMSOIGK6NB4NHNNKVT2XOJU5U), citing Naville, Deir el Bahari II, Sethe, Urkunden IV and Brunner; matched through public search-index metadata on 2026-10-24.'),
('source.egypt.shabaka_stone.bm','The Shabaka Stone (Memphite Theology), British Museum EA 498','Shabaka Stone','MUSEUM_OBJECT_AND_PRIMARY_TEXT',2,'The British Museum','Inscribed under King Shabaka (25th Dynasty) as a copy of an older text','lang.egy','c. 710 BCE (museum date; composition date disputed)','2026-10-24','https://www.britishmuseum.org/collection/object/Y_EA498',NULL,'EA 498','Ancient inscription in the public domain; museum images and modern translations keep their own terms','Stone slab with two horizontal lines naming Shabaka above about sixty vertical columns of the Memphite Theology, partly worn away by later use as a millstone; first translated by Breasted (1901)',NULL,'Public web page',0,'Cite the inscription by section; publish independent retellings rather than any modern translation.',0,'Egyptian hieroglyphic text','URL_SYNTAX_VALID','British Museum collection record for EA 498, matched through public search-index metadata on 2026-10-24; scholars disagree whether the text was composed in the 25th Dynasty or copied from an older original.'),
('source.sumerian.inana_ebih.etcsl','Inana and Ebiḫ','in-nin me-ḫuš-a (ETCSL 1.3.2)','PRIMARY_TEXT_DIGITAL_EDITION',2,'Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature, University of Oxford','Anonymous Old Babylonian Sumerian composition','lang.sux','Old Babylonian copies (c. 1900–1600 BCE)','2026-10-24','https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/section1/tr132.htm',NULL,'ETCSL 1.3.2','Ancient composition in the public domain; ETCSL transliteration and translation keep their own terms','Sumerian narrative hymn in which Inana, slighted by the mountain range Ebiḫ, seeks An''s approval, is warned against the attack, and destroys the mountain',NULL,'Public web page',0,'Cite ETCSL 1.3.2 by section; publish independent retellings rather than the ETCSL translation.',0,'Sumerian composite text with English translation','URL_SYNTAX_VALID','ETCSL translation page tr132 for composition 1.3.2, matched through public search-index metadata on 2026-10-24; Old Babylonian witnesses include a tablet from Ur in the British Museum.');

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO entities(
    id,canonical_name,name_zh,original_name,transliteration,primary_type,primary_civilization_id,historical_period,description,research_status,evidence_status,record_version,metadata_json,created_at,updated_at
) VALUES
('deity.greek.eileithyia','Eileithyia','厄勒梯亚','Εἰλείθυια','Eileithyia','DEITY','civ.greek','Archaic Greek hymn (7th–6th century BCE)','Goddess of childbirth, daughter of Zeus and Hera; in the Hymn to Apollo Hera keeps her on Olympus so that Leto labours nine days, until Iris brings her to Delos (Homeric Hymn 3).','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('deity.greek.iris','Iris','伊里斯','Ἶρις','Iris','DEITY','civ.greek','Archaic Greek hymn (7th–6th century BCE)','Swift messenger goddess; in the Hymn to Apollo the goddesses on Delos send her to fetch Eileithyia with the promise of a great golden necklace (Homeric Hymn 3).','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('creature.greek.delphic_drakaina','Delphic she-dragon','德尔斐的母龙','δράκαινα','Delphic she-dragon','MONSTER','civ.greek','Archaic Greek hymn (7th–6th century BCE)','Great she-dragon by the spring below Parnassus that nursed Typhaon for Hera; Apollo shoots her, and from her rotting (pythein) the place is called Pytho (Homeric Hymn 3, lines 300–374). Later writers call the serpent Python.','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('hero.greek.anchises','Anchises','安喀塞斯','Ἀγχίσης','Anchises','HERO','civ.greek','Archaic Greek hymn (7th–6th century BCE)','Trojan prince who herds cattle on Mount Ida; Aphrodite, made by Zeus to desire him, comes to him disguised as a Phrygian princess and bears him Aeneas (Homeric Hymn 5).','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('hero.greek.aeneas','Aeneas','埃涅阿斯','Αἰνείας','Aeneas','HERO','civ.greek','Archaic Greek hymn (7th–6th century BCE)','Son of Aphrodite and Anchises, foretold in the Hymn to Aphrodite to be reared by the mountain nymphs of Ida and to rule among the Trojans (Homeric Hymn 5, lines 196–199, 256–280).','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('hero.greek.tithonus','Tithonus','提托诺斯','Τιθωνός','Tithonus','HERO','civ.greek','Archaic Greek hymn (7th–6th century BCE)','Trojan beloved of Eos; she asked Zeus to make him immortal but forgot to ask for ageless youth, so he grew old for ever and she shut him in a chamber where his voice flows on endlessly (Homeric Hymn 5, lines 218–238; Theogony 984).','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('hero.greek.ganymede','Ganymede','伽倪墨得斯','Γανυμήδης','Ganymede','HERO','civ.greek','Archaic Greek hymn (7th–6th century BCE)','Trojan youth whom Zeus carried off for his beauty to pour wine for the gods; Zeus consoled his father Tros with immortal horses (Homeric Hymn 5, lines 202–217).','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('deity.greek.pontus','Pontus','蓬托斯','Πόντος','Pontus','PRIMORDIAL_DEITY','civ.greek','Archaic Greek hymn (7th–6th century BCE)','The barren Sea, born of Earth without love; with Earth he fathers Nereus, Thaumas, Phorcys, Ceto and Eurybia (Theogony 131–132, 233–239).','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('deity.greek.styx','Styx','斯梯克斯','Στύξ','Styx','DEITY','civ.greek','Archaic Greek hymn (7th–6th century BCE)','Eldest daughter of Ocean; bore Zelos, Nike, Kratos and Bia to Pallas, came first to Zeus''s side against the Titans, and was made the great oath of the gods (Theogony 361, 383–403, 775–806).','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('deity.greek.phoebe','Phoebe','福柏','Φοίβη','Phoebe','DEITY','civ.greek','Archaic Greek hymn (7th–6th century BCE)','Titaness, daughter of Uranus and Gaia; with Coeus she bore Leto and Asteria (Theogony 136, 404–410).','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('hero.greek.actaeon','Actaeon','阿克泰翁','Ἀκταίων','Actaeon','HERO','civ.greek','Archaic Greek hymn (7th–6th century BCE)','Hunter, son of Aristaeus and Autonoe, raised by Chiron; changed into a deer by Artemis when he saw her bathing and devoured by his own fifty hounds on Cithaeron (Apollodorus, Library 3.4.4).','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('hero.greek.niobe','Niobe','尼俄柏','Νιόβη','Niobe','HERO','civ.greek','Archaic Greek hymn (7th–6th century BCE)','Daughter of Tantalus and wife of Amphion of Thebes, who boasted she was more blessed in children than Leto; Artemis and Apollo shot her children, and she was turned to weeping stone on Sipylus (Apollodorus, Library 3.5.6).','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('hero.greek.callisto','Callisto','卡利斯托','Καλλιστώ','Callisto','HERO','civ.greek','Archaic Greek hymn (7th–6th century BCE)','Companion of Artemis in the chase, sworn to maidenhood; loved by Zeus, turned into a bear and shot down by Artemis, then set among the stars as the Bear (Apollodorus, Library 3.8.2).','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('hero.greek.iphigenia','Iphigenia','伊菲革涅亚','Ἰφιγένεια','Iphigenia','HERO','civ.greek','Archaic Greek hymn (7th–6th century BCE)','Daughter of Agamemnon, brought to Aulis to be sacrificed to Artemis so the fleet could sail; the goddess snatched her away to the Taurians, put a deer in her place and made her priestess (Apollodorus, Epitome 3.21–22).','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('hero.greek.orion','Orion','俄里翁','Ὠρίων','Orion','HERO','civ.greek','Archaic Greek hymn (7th–6th century BCE)','Giant hunter, born of Earth or son of Poseidon, who could stride across the sea; carried off by Eos to Delos and slain by Artemis (Apollodorus, Library 1.4.3–1.4.5).','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('hero.greek.pentheus','Pentheus','彭透斯','Πενθεύς','Pentheus','HERO','civ.greek','Archaic Greek hymn (7th–6th century BCE)','King of Thebes, son of Agave and Echion, who tried to stop the Bacchic rites; spying on the women on Cithaeron, he was torn apart by his own mother in her frenzy (Apollodorus, Library 3.5.2).','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('person.greek.lycurgus_edonian','Lycurgus (king of the Edonians)','吕枯耳戈斯（厄多涅斯人的国王）','Λυκοῦργος','Lycurgus (king of the Edonians)','PERSON','civ.greek','Archaic Greek hymn (7th–6th century BCE)','Son of Dryas, king of the Edonians in Thrace, who insulted and expelled Dionysus; driven mad, he killed his own son, and his people had him torn apart by horses on Mount Pangaeum (Apollodorus, Library 3.5.1).','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('person.greek.icarius_attica','Icarius (of Attica)','伊卡里俄斯（阿提卡人）','Ἰκάριος','Icarius (of Attica)','PERSON','civ.greek','Archaic Greek hymn (7th–6th century BCE)','Athenian who received Dionysus and learned from him to make wine; shepherds who drank it unmixed thought themselves poisoned and killed him, and his daughter Erigone hanged herself in grief (Apollodorus, Library 3.14.7).','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('hero.greek.diomedes','Diomedes','狄俄墨得斯','Διομήδης','Diomedes','HERO','civ.greek','Archaic Greek hymn (7th–6th century BCE)','Son of Tydeus, lord of Argos and one of the foremost Achaean warriors at Troy; with Athena beside him he wounds Aphrodite and then Ares himself (Iliad 5).','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('creature.norse.audhumla','Auðumbla','欧德姆布拉','Auðumbla','Auðumbla','DIVINE_BEAST','civ.norse','Snorri''s Edda (c. 1220)','Primeval cow formed from the melting rime, whose four rivers of milk fed Ymir and whose licking of the salty ice-blocks brought forth Búri (Gylfaginning 6).','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('artifact.norse.skidbladnir','Skíðblaðnir','斯基德布拉德尼尔','Skíðblaðnir','Skíðblaðnir','SHIP','civ.norse','Snorri''s Edda (c. 1220)','Ship made by the sons of Ívaldi and given by Loki to Freyr; it has a fair wind as soon as its sail is hoisted and can be folded like a cloth and kept in a pocket (Skáldskaparmál 35; Gylfaginning 43).','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('giant.norse.hrungnir','Hrungnir','赫朗格尼尔','Hrungnir','Hrungnir','GIANT','civ.norse','Snorri''s Edda (c. 1220)','Strongest of the giants, with a three-cornered heart of stone, a stone head, a stone shield and a whetstone for a weapon; after a horse race with Óðinn he boasts drunkenly in Ásgarðr and is slain by Thor at Grjótúnagarðar (Skáldskaparmál 17).','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('giant.norse.mokkurkalfi','Mökkurkálfi','默库尔卡尔菲','Mökkurkálfi','Mökkurkálfi','GIANT','civ.norse','Snorri''s Edda (c. 1220)','Clay giant nine leagues tall made by the giants at Grjótúnagarðar to stand beside Hrungnir, given a mare''s heart that quailed when Thor came; felled by Thjálfi (Skáldskaparmál 17).','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('text.egypt.bentresh_stela','Bentresh Stela','本特蕾什石碑','Bentresh-Stele','Bentresh-Stele','INSCRIPTION','civ.egyptian','Late Period Egyptian text','Hieroglyphic stela (Louvre C 284) telling how the god Khonsu-the-Provider was sent from Thebes to the land of Bakhtan to heal Bentresh, sister of the queen Neferure; set in the reign of Ramesses II but composed much later, perhaps in the Persian or Ptolemaic period.','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('person.egyptian.bentresh','Bentresh','本特蕾什','Bntrš','Bntrš','PERSON','civ.egyptian','Late Period Egyptian text','Younger sister of the queen Neferure and daughter of the prince of Bakhtan, possessed by a spirit and healed by the god Khonsu-the-Provider in the Bentresh Stela.','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('historical.egyptian.ramesses_ii','Ramesses II','拉美西斯二世','Rꜥ-ms-sw','Rꜥ-ms-sw','HISTORICAL_FIGURE','civ.egyptian','New Kingdom, 19th Dynasty','Nineteenth-Dynasty king of Egypt (reigned c. 1279–1213 BCE), named Usermaatre-Setepenre; the Bentresh Stela, composed centuries later, sets its tale of Khonsu''s healing journey in his reign.','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('text.egypt.two_brothers','Tale of Two Brothers','两兄弟的故事','Papyrus D''Orbiney','Papyrus D''Orbiney','TEXT','civ.egyptian','New Kingdom, 19th Dynasty','Late Egyptian tale preserved complete in Papyrus D''Orbiney (BM EA 10183): Bata, falsely accused by his elder brother Anubis''s wife, flees to the Valley of the Pine, where the gods fashion him a wife; he dies and is restored, and becomes king through a cycle of transformations.','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('hero.egyptian.bata','Bata','巴塔','Bꜣtꜣ','Bꜣtꜣ','HERO','civ.egyptian','New Kingdom, 19th Dynasty','Younger brother in the Tale of Two Brothers, a herdsman with the strength of a god, who flees false accusation, places his heart on the flower of a pine, dies and is restored, and becomes king of Egypt (Papyrus D''Orbiney).','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('hero.egyptian.anpu','Anubis (elder brother in the Tale of Two Brothers)','阿努比斯（两兄弟故事中的哥哥）','Inpw','Inpw','HERO','civ.egyptian','New Kingdom, 19th Dynasty','Elder brother of Bata in the Tale of Two Brothers, a farmer who is deceived by his wife, later restores Bata to life and succeeds him as king; his name is the god''s name Anubis, but in the tale he is a man (Papyrus D''Orbiney).','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('deity.egyptian.khnum','Khnum','克努姆','H̱nmw','H̱nmw','DEITY','civ.egyptian','Egyptian religion, all periods','Ram-headed creator god who fashions beings on the potter''s wheel; in the Tale of Two Brothers he makes a wife for Bata at Pre-Harakhti''s command, and in Hatshepsut''s birth cycle he shapes the child and her ka.','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('text.egypt.hatshepsut_birth_cycle','Divine birth cycle of Hatshepsut','哈特谢普苏特的神圣诞生浮雕','Geburtslegende','Geburtslegende','INSCRIPTION','civ.egyptian','New Kingdom, 18th Dynasty','Sequence of reliefs with captions in Hatshepsut''s temple at Deir el-Bahari showing Amun-Re begetting the queen with Ahmose in the form of Thutmose I, Khnum shaping her on the potter''s wheel, her birth and her presentation to Amun.','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('historical.egyptian.hatshepsut','Hatshepsut','哈特谢普苏特','Ḥꜣt-šps.wt','Ḥꜣt-šps.wt','HISTORICAL_FIGURE','civ.egyptian','New Kingdom, 18th Dynasty','Eighteenth-Dynasty queen who ruled Egypt as king (c. 1479–1458 BCE), named Maatkare; the birth cycle in her temple at Deir el-Bahari presents her as the daughter of Amun-Re, conceived when the god visited Queen Ahmose.','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('historical.egyptian.ahmose_queen','Ahmose (queen of Thutmose I)','雅赫摩斯王后','Iꜥḥ-ms','Iꜥḥ-ms','HISTORICAL_FIGURE','civ.egyptian','New Kingdom, 18th Dynasty','Great Royal Wife of Thutmose I and mother of Hatshepsut; in the Deir el-Bahari birth cycle Amun-Re comes to her in the form of her husband and she bears the divine child.','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('historical.egyptian.thutmose_i','Thutmose I','图特摩斯一世','Ḏḥwtj-ms','Ḏḥwtj-ms','HISTORICAL_FIGURE','civ.egyptian','New Kingdom, 18th Dynasty','Eighteenth-Dynasty king, husband of Ahmose and father of Hatshepsut, whose form Amun-Re takes in the Deir el-Bahari birth cycle.','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('deity.egyptian.heqet','Heqet','赫凯特','Ḥqt','Ḥqt','DEITY','civ.egyptian','Egyptian religion, all periods','Frog-headed goddess of birth who holds the sign of life to the child Khnum shapes on the potter''s wheel and leads Queen Ahmose to the birth chamber in the Deir el-Bahari birth cycle.','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('historical.egyptian.shabaka','Shabaka','沙巴卡','Šꜣbꜣkꜣ','Šꜣbꜣkꜣ','HISTORICAL_FIGURE','civ.egyptian','Third Intermediate Period, 25th Dynasty','Kushite king of Egypt of the Twenty-fifth Dynasty (late 8th century BCE) who had the Memphite Theology copied onto a stone slab in the temple of Ptah at Memphis, saying the old original was worm-eaten.','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('text.ugaritic.baal_cycle.ktu1_3_1_4','Baal Cycle, tablets KTU 1.3–1.4 (the palace of Baal)','巴力史诗 KTU 1.3–1.4（巴力的宫殿）','KTU 1.3–1.4','KTU 1.3–1.4','TABLET','civ.ugaritic','Late Bronze Age (c. 1400–1185 BCE)','Two tablets of the Ugaritic Baal Cycle telling how Anat and Athirat win El''s consent for a palace for Baal, how Kothar-wa-Khasis builds it of silver and gold, and how Baal, after a debate over its window, thunders from it and challenges Mot.','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('place.sumerian.ebih','Ebiḫ','埃比赫山','ebiḫ','ebiḫ','MYTHICAL_PLACE','civ.sumerian','Old Babylonian copies of a Sumerian composition','Mountain range (usually identified with the Jebel Hamrin) that refused to bow to Inana and was destroyed by her in the Sumerian hymn Inana and Ebiḫ (ETCSL 1.3.2).','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('text.sumerian.inana_ebih','Inana and Ebiḫ','伊南娜与埃比赫','in-nin me-ḫuš-a','in-nin me-ḫuš-a','TEXT','civ.sumerian','Old Babylonian copies of a Sumerian composition','Sumerian narrative hymn (ETCSL 1.3.2), attributed by later tradition to Enḫeduana, in which Inana destroys the mountain range Ebiḫ for failing to show her respect, despite An''s warning.','PARTIAL','SOURCE_BACKED',1,'{}','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z');

