('storyver.norse.gylf_4_12_creation_long','story.norse.ymir_world','斯诺里《埃达·欺骗古鲁菲》第4–12章（长篇讲述）','Snorri''s Edda, Gylfaginning 4–12 (long retelling)','source.norse.prose_edda.vsnr','Gylfaginning 4–12 (sequential retelling)','lang.non','维京北方研究学会（VSNR）出版、福克斯校订的斯诺里《埃达》（序言与《欺骗古鲁菲》）','尼夫尔海姆、穆斯佩尔与苏尔特、金伦加鸿沟、尤弥尔、母牛与布里、博尔与贝斯特拉、杀死尤弥尔、大地与海、天空与群星、米德加尔德、最初的人与奥丁的家、夜与昼、太阳与月亮、追逐日月的狼','SOURCE_BACKED','PUBLIC_CONTEXT',0,'福克斯（Faulkes）学术版的编辑内容受版权保护；斯诺里的中世纪原文属公共领域，本站发布按章节顺序的独立讲述，不转载任何译文。','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z'),
('storyver.norse.skald_35_sif_long','story.norse.forging_treasures','斯诺里《埃达·诗语法》第35章：希芙的金发（长篇讲述，开头据《欺骗古鲁菲》第31章）','Snorri''s Edda, Skáldskaparmál 35: Sif''s hair (long retelling, opening from Gylfaginning 31)','source.norse.skaldskaparmal.vsnr1998','Gylfaginning 31; Skáldskaparmál 35 (sequential retelling)','lang.non','维京北方研究学会（VSNR）出版、福克斯校订的斯诺里《埃达》之《诗语法》与《欺骗古鲁菲》','希芙与乌勒尔、被剪掉的金发、索尔的愤怒、伊瓦尔迪的儿子们、洛基的赌注、金鬃野猪、指环德劳普尼尔、锤子与苍蝇、三位裁判、洛基的礼物、勃洛克的礼物与判决、缝上的嘴','SOURCE_BACKED','PUBLIC_CONTEXT',0,'福克斯（Faulkes）学术版的编辑内容受版权保护；斯诺里的中世纪原文属公共领域，本站发布按章节顺序的独立讲述，不转载任何译文。','2026-10-24T22:00:00Z');

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO story_sections(
    id,story_version_id,section_order,heading_zh,heading_en,body_zh,body_en,anchor_claim_id,evidence_note,uncertainty_note
) VALUES
('storysec.v0360.greek_hymn3_apollo_evelyn_white.1','storyver.greek.hymn3_apollo_evelyn_white',1,'众神起身','The gods rise','诗人一开头就说，他要歌颂远射之神阿波罗，因为当阿波罗走进宙斯的宫殿时，众神都在他面前发抖，纷纷从座位上站起来，只有母亲勒托还安坐在宙斯身边。勒托解开阿波罗的弓弦，合上他的箭袋，亲手从他肩上取下弓，挂在父亲宫殿的金钉上，然后领他入座。宙斯用金杯斟满琼浆，欢迎自己的儿子，其余的神也都坐了下来。勒托心中欢喜，因为她生下了一个手持弓箭的强大儿子。诗人于是向她致意：有福的勒托啊，你生下了光荣的孩子——阿波罗王和喜爱箭矢的阿耳忒弥斯，她生在俄耳堤癸亚，他生在多石的得洛斯，靠在钦托斯山的长坡上，挨着伊诺波斯河边的棕榈树。接着诗人问自己：既然人人都歌颂你，我该从哪里唱起呢？他决定从头唱起，唱勒托怎样寻找一个能让孩子出生的地方。','The poet begins by saying he will sing of Apollo who shoots from afar, before whom the gods tremble when he enters the house of Zeus: they all rise from their seats, and only Leto his mother stays seated beside Zeus. Leto unstrings his bow and closes his quiver, takes the bow from his shoulders with her own hands, hangs it on a golden peg in his father''s house and leads him to his seat. Zeus welcomes his son with nectar in a golden cup, and the other gods sit down. Leto rejoices because she has borne a mighty son who carries the bow. The poet greets her: blessed Leto, you bore glorious children, lord Apollo and Artemis who delights in arrows — her in Ortygia and him in rocky Delos, leaning against the long slope of Cynthus beside a palm tree by the streams of Inopus. Then he asks himself how he should sing of the god whom all men praise, and chooses to begin at the beginning, with Leto''s search for a place where her child could be born.','claim.v0360.hym_apollo.01','《荷马颂歌·致阿波罗》第1–18行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_hymn3_apollo_evelyn_white.2','storyver.greek.hymn3_apollo_evelyn_white',2,'无处安身','No place to rest','勒托怀着远射之神，走遍了希腊的土地和岛屿，想找一个肯让她生产的地方。诗中列出了一长串地名：克里特、雅典城、埃癸那岛、以长船闻名的优卑亚、埃盖、厄瑞西埃、海边的珀帕瑞托斯、色雷斯的阿托斯山、珀利翁山的高峰、色雷斯的萨摩色雷斯、伊达山的山峦、斯库罗斯、福开亚、因布罗斯、莱姆诺斯、富饶的莱斯沃斯、最肥沃的希俄斯、多岩的弥玛斯、科律科斯的山顶、克拉洛斯、埃塞吉昂的高山、萨摩斯的泉水、米卡勒的陡峭山峰、米利都、科斯岛、克尼多斯、卡帕托斯、纳克索斯、帕罗斯和岩石嶙峋的瑞纳亚。可是这些地方都吓得发抖，没有一处胆敢接纳福玻斯，不管它们多么肥沃。人们都听说，这个将要出生的神会有极大的威力，谁也不愿意让他生在自己的土地上，怕他将来嫌弃自己。勒托就这样一处一处地走，直到最后来到了得洛斯。','Carrying the far-shooting god, Leto went through the lands and islands of Greece looking for a place that would let her give birth. The poem names a long list: Crete, the city of Athens, the isle of Aegina, Euboea famed for ships, Aegae, Eiresiae, Peparethus by the sea, Thracian Athos, the high peaks of Pelion, Samothrace, the ridges of Ida, Scyros, Phocaea, Imbros, Lemnos, rich Lesbos, fertile Chios, rugged Mimas, the heights of Corycus, Claros, the steep hill of Aesagea, watered Samos, the peaks of Mycale, Miletus, Cos, Cnidos, Carpathus, Naxos, Paros and rocky Rhenaea. Yet all of them trembled and feared, and none dared receive Phoebus, however fertile it was. People had heard that the god about to be born would have great power, and no land wanted him to be born on its soil, afraid that he would one day scorn it. So Leto went from place to place, until at last she came to Delos.','claim.v0360.hym_apollo.02','《荷马颂歌·致阿波罗》第19–50行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_hymn3_apollo_evelyn_white.3','storyver.greek.hymn3_apollo_evelyn_white',3,'得洛斯的誓言','The oath on Delos','勒托来到得洛斯，对这座小岛说：得洛斯啊，你愿意做我儿子福玻斯·阿波罗的家，在你这里为他建一座丰盛的神庙吗？没有别的地方愿意碰你，你也不会有好牛好羊，不会长出葡萄和庄稼。可如果你有了远射之神阿波罗的神庙，所有的人都会带着百牛大祭来到这里，祭品的香气会永远从你身上升起。得洛斯听了很高兴，却又说出了自己的担心：人们说阿波罗将是一个暴烈的神，在神和人中间都有大权。我很怕他一出生看见这片光秃秃的岩石，就看不起我，一脚把我踢进海底，让章鱼和海豹在我身上安家。女神啊，除非你肯发下大誓，说他会先在这里建起他的神庙，否则我不敢答应。勒托便以大地、上面的天空和流下来的斯梯克斯河水起誓——这是众神最庄严的誓言——说福玻斯的祭坛和圣域将永远在这里，他会尊崇得洛斯胜过一切地方。','Leto came to Delos and said to the island: ''Delos, would you be the home of my son Phoebus Apollo and let him build a rich temple on you? No other land will touch you, and you will never be rich in cattle or sheep, nor bear vintage or crops. But if you have the temple of far-shooting Apollo, all men will bring you hecatombs, and the savour of sacrifice will rise from you for ever.'' Delos was glad, yet spoke her fear: ''They say Apollo will be a violent god, with great power among gods and men. I fear that when he first sees the light he will scorn this bare rock, overturn me with his feet and sink me in the sea, where octopuses and seals will make their homes on me. Unless, goddess, you swear a great oath that he will build his temple here first, I dare not.'' Leto swore by Earth, by the broad heaven above and by the falling water of Styx — the greatest and most awful oath of the gods — that Phoebus''s altar and precinct would always be there and that he would honour Delos above all places.','claim.v0360.hym_apollo.03','《荷马颂歌·致阿波罗》第51–88行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_hymn3_apollo_evelyn_white.4','storyver.greek.hymn3_apollo_evelyn_white',4,'被拦住的生育女神','The birth goddess kept away','勒托在得洛斯开始了阵痛，一连九天九夜，痛苦难当。最尊贵的女神们都来到她的身边：狄俄涅、瑞亚、伊克奈亚的忒弥斯、呻吟着的海后安菲特里忒，还有许多别的女神。只有白臂的赫拉没有来，她坐在宙斯的宫殿里；生育女神厄勒梯亚也没有来，因为她根本不知道这件事——赫拉嫉妒勒托将要生下一个强大而无可指摘的儿子，就故意把厄勒梯亚留在奥林波斯山顶的金云底下。众女神看着勒托受苦，便派伊里斯去请厄勒梯亚，答应送给她一条九肘长的大项链，用金线穿成。她们嘱咐伊里斯，要避开赫拉，悄悄地对厄勒梯亚说话，免得赫拉用话把她拦下。脚步如风的伊里斯立刻出发，飞快地穿过天与地之间，来到众神居住的奥林波斯山，把厄勒梯亚叫出大殿，一字不差地转达了众女神的话，说动了她的心。两位女神像两只受惊的鸽子一样，迈着轻快的脚步回到了得洛斯。','On Delos Leto was racked with labour for nine days and nine nights. The noblest goddesses were with her: Dione, Rhea, Ichnaean Themis, loud-moaning Amphitrite and many others. Only white-armed Hera stayed away, sitting in the house of Zeus; and Eileithyia, goddess of birth, did not come, for she had not heard. Hera, jealous that Leto was to bear a strong and blameless son, kept her on the peak of Olympus beneath golden clouds. The goddesses, seeing Leto suffer, sent Iris to bring Eileithyia, promising her a great necklace nine cubits long, strung with golden threads. They told Iris to call her aside, away from Hera, lest Hera''s words turn her back. Swift Iris sped through the space between heaven and earth to Olympus, called Eileithyia out of the hall, gave her the goddesses'' message word for word and won her over. The two goddesses went back to Delos, stepping lightly like timid wild doves.','claim.v0360.hym_apollo.04','《荷马颂歌·致阿波罗》第89–114行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_hymn3_apollo_evelyn_white.5','storyver.greek.hymn3_apollo_evelyn_white',5,'棕榈树下','Under the palm tree','厄勒梯亚一踏上得洛斯，阵痛就攫住了勒托，她就要生了。她双臂抱住一棵棕榈树，跪在柔软的草地上，身下的大地都露出了笑容。孩子跃了出来，来到了光明之中，在场的女神们一齐高声欢呼。她们用清水把这个婴儿洗得干干净净，用一块崭新的白色细布把他包起来，又在他身上系了一条金带子。勒托没有亲自给阿波罗喂奶，而是由忒弥斯用她不朽的双手把琼浆和美味的神食送进他的嘴里。勒托非常高兴，因为她生下了一个强大的、手持弓箭的儿子。颂歌开头说勒托在宙斯身边为儿子骄傲，到这里才讲清楚那份骄傲是怎样来的：一位四处被拒绝、阵痛了九天的母亲，终于在一块贫瘠的岩石上，抱着一棵棕榈树，生下了光明之神。后来的得洛斯人一直把岛上的一棵棕榈树看作阿波罗出生的见证。','As soon as Eileithyia set foot on Delos, the pains took hold of Leto and she was ready to give birth. She threw her arms around a palm tree and knelt on the soft meadow, and the earth beneath her smiled. The child leapt forth to the light, and all the goddesses cried aloud. They washed the infant purely and cleanly in clear water, wrapped him in a fine new white garment and fastened a golden band about him. Leto did not nurse Apollo herself; Themis with her immortal hands gave him nectar and delicious ambrosia. And Leto rejoiced because she had borne a strong son who carries the bow. The hymn opened with Leto''s pride beside Zeus; only here does it show where that pride came from — a mother turned away everywhere, in labour for nine days, who at last on a barren rock, clasping a palm tree, bore the god of light. The Delians long pointed to a palm on the island as a witness of his birth.','claim.v0360.hym_apollo.05','《荷马颂歌·致阿波罗》第115–126行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_hymn3_apollo_evelyn_white.6','storyver.greek.hymn3_apollo_evelyn_white',6,'弓与竖琴','The bow and the lyre','福玻斯一尝到神食，金色的襁褓就再也包不住他了，身上的带子也束不住他，所有的布条都松开了。他立刻对不朽的女神们说：让我拥有心爱的竖琴和弯弓吧，我还要向人们宣告宙斯不会改变的旨意。说着，长发的远射之神阿波罗就迈开大步，走在宽阔的大地上，女神们都惊奇不已。这时整个得洛斯都披上了黄金，就像一座山顶开满了林间的花朵，因为她看到了宙斯和勒托的儿子，欢喜他选中了自己作为家园，胜过所有的海岛和陆地，并且从心底里爱她。诗人接着赞美阿波罗：你有时登上钦托斯山的岩石，有时在海岛和人群中漫游；你有许多神庙和林木茂盛的圣林，所有的山巅和高峰、流入大海的河流都是你所喜爱的。可是福玻斯啊，你心里最喜欢的还是得洛斯。','As soon as Phoebus had tasted ambrosia, the golden swaddling bands could no longer hold him, nor could the cords restrain him; all the bands fell loose. At once he said to the deathless goddesses: ''The lyre and the curved bow shall be dear to me, and I will declare to men the unfailing will of Zeus.'' With that the long-haired far-shooter strode out over the wide earth, and all the goddesses marvelled. Then all Delos was laden with gold, like a hilltop heavy with woodland flowers, as she looked on the son of Zeus and Leto, glad that he had chosen her for his home above all islands and mainland, and loved her in his heart. The poet then praises the god: sometimes you climb rocky Cynthus, sometimes you wander among islands and men; you have many temples and wooded groves, and all the peaks and headlands and rivers flowing to the sea are dear to you. Yet, Phoebus, it is in Delos that your heart delights most.','claim.v0360.hym_apollo.06','《荷马颂歌·致阿波罗》第127–139行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_hymn3_apollo_evelyn_white.7','storyver.greek.hymn3_apollo_evelyn_white',7,'得洛斯的节日','The festival on Delos','诗人描写了得洛斯的盛会：穿着长袍的伊奥尼亚人带着妻儿聚集到岛上来纪念阿波罗，用拳击、舞蹈和歌唱来取悦他。如果有人在他们聚会的时候来到这里，会以为他们是长生不老的神，因为他们个个风度翩翩，男人和束着漂亮腰带的女人们，还有他们的快船和丰富的财物，都叫人看了心里欢喜。还有一件奇事，它的荣耀永远不会消失：那就是得洛斯的少女们，远射之神的侍女。她们先歌颂阿波罗，再歌颂勒托和阿耳忒弥斯，然后唱起古代男女的故事，让听众着迷。她们会模仿各种人的口音和咿呀之声，每个人听了都以为是自己在说话。最后，诗人请求少女们记住他：如果有远方的旅人问起，哪一位歌手唱得最甜美，就请回答：是一位住在多岩的希俄斯的盲人，他的歌永远最好。他答应要把她们的名声带到他所到的每一座城市。','The poet describes the gathering on Delos: the long-robed Ionians come together with their children and wives to honour Apollo, delighting him with boxing, dancing and song. Anyone arriving while they are gathered would think them deathless and ageless, so graceful are they all — the men and the fair-girdled women, their swift ships and their great wealth. There is a further wonder whose fame will never perish: the maidens of Delos, handmaids of the far-shooter. They praise Apollo first, then Leto and Artemis, and then sing of men and women of old, enchanting all who listen. They can imitate the voices and chattering of all people, so that each would say he himself was speaking. At the end the poet asks the maidens to remember him: if a stranger ever asks who is the sweetest singer, let them answer that it is a blind man who lives in rocky Chios, whose songs are the best for ever. He in turn promises to carry their fame to every city he visits.','claim.v0360.hym_apollo.07','《荷马颂歌·致阿波罗》第140–178行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_hymn3_apollo_evelyn_white.8','storyver.greek.hymn3_apollo_evelyn_white',8,'奥林波斯的舞蹈','The dance on Olympus','颂歌的第二部分换了一个场景。勒托光荣的儿子弹着空心的竖琴，穿着神圣的芬芳长袍，前往多岩的皮托；他的金拨子一碰琴弦，就发出迷人的声音。他像思想一样迅速，从大地升上奥林波斯，来到宙斯的宫殿，加入众神的聚会。不朽的神们立刻想起了竖琴和歌唱：缪斯们用美妙的声音一齐歌唱众神不朽的礼物，也歌唱人类的苦难——他们在不死的神手下，愚昧而无助地活着，找不到医治死亡的药，也挡不住衰老。秀发的美惠女神、欢乐的时序女神、哈耳摩尼亚、赫柏和宙斯的女儿阿佛洛狄忒手拉着手跳舞；在她们中间唱歌的，是一位既不丑陋也不矮小、身材高大美丽的女神——喜爱箭矢的阿耳忒弥斯，阿波罗的孪生姐妹。阿瑞斯和眼光锐利的赫尔墨斯也在她们中间嬉戏。福玻斯·阿波罗高高地迈步，弹着竖琴，周身闪着光芒。金发的勒托和智慧的宙斯看着他们的儿子在众神中间嬉戏，心里充满了喜悦。','The second part of the hymn changes scene. Leto''s glorious son goes to rocky Pytho playing his hollow lyre, clad in divine, perfumed garments, and at the touch of his golden plectrum the lyre sings sweetly. Swift as thought he rises from earth to Olympus, to the house of Zeus, to join the gathering of the gods. At once the deathless ones think of the lyre and song: the Muses together sing of the gods'' undying gifts and of the hardships of men, who live witless and helpless under the deathless gods, unable to find a cure for death or a defence against old age. The rich-haired Graces, the cheerful Hours, Harmonia, Hebe and Aphrodite daughter of Zeus dance holding hands; and among them sings one neither mean nor small but tall and lovely to look on — Artemis who delights in arrows, Apollo''s twin. Ares and keen-eyed Hermes play among them too. Phoebus Apollo strides high, playing his lyre, with radiance shining round him, and golden-haired Leto and wise Zeus rejoice to see their son at play among the deathless gods.','claim.v0360.hym_apollo.08','《荷马颂歌·致阿波罗》第179–206行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_hymn3_apollo_evelyn_white.9','storyver.greek.hymn3_apollo_evelyn_white',9,'泰尔福萨的诡计','Telphusa''s trick','诗人又问：我该怎样歌颂你呢？他选择歌唱阿波罗怎样寻找一个地方，为人们建立神谕所。远射之神从奥林波斯下来，先到了庇厄里亚，走过沙地的莱克托斯、埃尼厄涅斯和珀尔海比亚，又到了伊俄尔科斯，踏上了以船闻名的优卑亚的凯奈昂。他站在勒兰托斯平原上，却不喜欢在那里建庙。他渡过海峡，走过青翠的米卡勒索斯和忒乌墨索斯，来到忒拜城的所在——那时那里还没有人居住，到处是森林。他又经过翁凯斯托斯波塞冬的圣林，渡过刻菲索斯河，来到泰尔福萨泉边一片安静的地方。他喜欢这里，便对泉水女神说：泰尔福萨，我想在这里建一座美丽的神庙，作为人们的神谕所。可泰尔福萨看见他就生了气，不愿让他夺走自己的荣耀，便劝他说：这里总有快马的蹄声和骡子到我的泉边饮水，人们会更喜欢看车马而不是你的神庙。不如去帕尔纳索斯山下的克里萨吧，那里没有车马的喧闹。阿波罗听信了她的话。','Again the poet asks how he should sing of the god, and chooses to tell how Apollo sought a place to found an oracle for men. Coming down from Olympus, the far-shooter went first to Pieria, passed sandy Lectus, the Enienae and the Perrhaebi, came to Iolcus and set foot on Cenaeum in Euboea, famed for ships. He stood in the Lelantine plain but did not wish to build there. Crossing the strait, he went over green Mycalessus and Teumessus and came to the site of Thebes — then still uninhabited and covered with forest. He passed Poseidon''s grove at Onchestus, crossed the Cephisus and came to a quiet place by the spring of Telphusa. He liked it and said to the spring nymph: ''Telphusa, here I mean to build a glorious temple, an oracle for men.'' But Telphusa was angry at heart, unwilling to lose her own glory, and said: ''The trampling of swift horses and of mules watering at my springs will always trouble you here; men will prefer to gaze at chariots rather than at your temple. Go rather to Crisa below Parnassus, where there is no din of chariots.'' And Apollo believed her.','claim.v0360.hym_apollo.09','《荷马颂歌·致阿波罗》第207–276行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_hymn3_apollo_evelyn_white.10','storyver.greek.hymn3_apollo_evelyn_white',10,'神庙的地基与泉边的母龙','The foundations and the dragon at the spring','阿波罗来到佛勒古埃斯人的城市，又走上帕尔纳索斯山下积雪的山坡，来到克里萨。那是一处朝西的山肩，头顶悬着巨大的岩石，下面是一道荒凉崎岖的山谷。福玻斯·阿波罗决定在这里建造他美丽的神庙，说：我要在这里为人们建一座神谕所，住在富饶的伯罗奔尼撒、欧洲和四面环海的岛屿上的人都会来这里，带来完美的百牛大祭，向我求问，我会在这座丰盛的神庙里给他们不会错的指引。他亲手奠下了又宽又长的地基，厄尔吉诺斯的两个儿子特洛福尼俄斯和阿伽墨得斯在上面铺设了石头门槛，无数的人们用凿好的石块围着它建起了神庙，让它永远为人们所歌唱。可是附近有一眼清澈的泉水，泉边住着一条巨大而凶猛的母龙，这个怪物给当地的人们带来了许多灾难，害死了无数的人和他们细腿的羊群，因为她是一个嗜血的祸害。诗人说，正是这条母龙，曾经从金座的赫拉手里接过可怕的提丰，把他抚养长大。','Apollo came to the city of the Phlegyae and up the snowy slopes below Parnassus to Crisa, a shoulder of the hill turned westward, with a great cliff hanging over it and a hollow, rugged glen below. There Phoebus Apollo resolved to build his lovely temple and said: ''Here I mean to build a glorious temple, an oracle for men, and all who dwell in the rich Peloponnese, in Europe and in the sea-girt islands will bring me perfect hecatombs and consult me, and I will give them unerring counsel in my rich temple.'' He laid out the foundations, broad and very long, and on them Trophonius and Agamedes, sons of Erginus, laid a threshold of stone, while countless tribes of men built the temple around it with wrought stones, to be sung of for ever. But near by was a sweet-flowing spring, and there lived a great, fierce she-dragon, a fell plague that did much harm to the people of the land and their thin-shanked sheep, for she was a bloodthirsty bane. It was she, the poet says, who once received fearful Typhaon from gold-throned Hera and reared him.','claim.v0360.hym_apollo.10','《荷马颂歌·致阿波罗》第277–304行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_hymn3_apollo_evelyn_white.11','storyver.greek.hymn3_apollo_evelyn_white',11,'赫拉独自生下的孩子','The child Hera bore alone','诗人在这里插进了一段旧事。从前，宙斯从自己的头里生出了眼睛明亮的雅典娜，赫拉大为愤怒，在众神的集会上说：你们听着，宙斯先娶了我做他忠实的妻子，现在却撇开我，独自生下了眼睛明亮的雅典娜，她在众神中出类拔萃；而我生的儿子赫菲斯托斯却瘸着腿，我自己一生下他就把他扔进了大海。现在我也要想办法生一个孩子，不玷污我和你的婚床，却能在众神中出类拔萃。说完，她就远离众神，用手掌拍打大地，向大地、天空和住在塔耳塔罗斯底下的提坦们祈祷，请他们赐给她一个不靠宙斯、力量却不比宙斯差的孩子。大地被她触动，赫拉心中欢喜，以为祈祷会应验。从那时起整整一年，她都不再走近宙斯的床榻，也不再坐到他的宝座边出主意，只待在自己的神庙里，享用人们的祭品。时候到了，她生下了一个既不像神也不像人的孩子——可怕而残暴的提丰，人类的祸害。赫拉把他交给那条母龙抚养，让恶与恶在一起。','Here the poet tells an older story. Once, when Zeus bore bright-eyed Athena from his own head, Hera was furious and said in the assembly of the gods: ''Hear me: Zeus made me his true wife, and now without me he has borne bright-eyed Athena, who excels among the gods; while my own son Hephaestus was born lame, and I myself cast him into the sea. Now I too will contrive to bear a child, without shaming his bed or mine, who will excel among the gods.'' She went apart from the gods, struck the ground with her flat hand and prayed to Earth, to Heaven and to the Titans who dwell beneath the earth around Tartarus to give her a child without Zeus, no weaker than he. Earth was stirred, and Hera rejoiced, thinking her prayer would be fulfilled. For a full year she did not come to Zeus''s bed nor sit by his throne to advise him, but stayed in her temples, delighting in offerings. When the time came she bore a child like neither gods nor men — fearful, cruel Typhaon, a bane to mortals — and gave him to the she-dragon to rear, evil to evil.','claim.v0360.hym_apollo.11','《荷马颂歌·致阿波罗》第305–355行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_hymn3_apollo_evelyn_white.12','storyver.greek.hymn3_apollo_evelyn_white',12,'皮托这个名字','How Pytho got its name','凡是遇上这条母龙的人，末日都会到来，直到远射之神阿波罗向她射出一支强劲的箭。她被箭射中，痛苦地躺在地上，大口喘气，在地上翻滚，发出可怕的嘶叫，在林中扭来扭去，最后吐出了带血的气息。福玻斯·阿波罗站在她旁边夸耀说：现在你就在这养育众生的大地上腐烂吧，你再也不能害死吃大地果实的人类了，他们会到这里来献上完美的百牛大祭。提丰和名声不好的喀迈拉都救不了你，黑色的大地和闪光的太阳神会让你在这里烂掉。神圣的太阳光果然让她烂掉了，所以这个地方叫做皮托，人们称这位神为“皮托的”阿波罗，因为那条怪物在那里被烈日烤得腐烂了。这时阿波罗才明白泉水女神骗了他，便怒气冲冲地回到泰尔福萨，说：你骗了我，把这块可爱的地方留给自己。你的荣耀将归于我。他推倒一大块岩石，堵住了她的泉水，在附近的林子里为自己建了一座祭坛，从此人们称他为泰尔福萨的阿波罗。','Whoever met the she-dragon, the day of doom swept him away, until far-shooting Apollo let fly a strong arrow at her. Struck, she lay rent with bitter pangs, gasping for breath and rolling on the ground; a dreadful noise rose as she writhed this way and that through the wood, and she breathed out her life in blood. Then Phoebus Apollo boasted over her: ''Now rot here on the earth that feeds men; you will no longer be a fell bane to men who eat the fruit of the earth and will bring perfect hecatombs here. Neither Typhoeus nor ill-famed Chimaera will save you; the black earth and shining Hyperion will make you rot here.'' And the holy might of the sun made her rot away, so the place is called Pytho, and men call the god Pythian, because the monster rotted there under the sun. Then Apollo knew that the spring had deceived him, and in anger went to Telphusa: ''You tricked me to keep this lovely place for yourself; your glory shall be mine.'' He pushed a crag over her streams, built himself an altar in a grove near by, and there men call him Telphusian.','claim.v0360.hym_apollo.12','《荷马颂歌·致阿波罗》第356–387行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_hymn3_apollo_evelyn_white.13','storyver.greek.hymn3_apollo_evelyn_white',13,'海豚','The dolphin','神庙建好了，福玻斯·阿波罗开始考虑，该找什么样的人来在多岩的皮托做他的祭司，为他主持祭祀。他正这样想着，看见暗紫色的海面上有一条快船，船上是许多高贵的人，从米诺斯的城市克诺索斯来的克里特人。他们驾船往沙地的皮洛斯去，为了做生意。福玻斯·阿波罗化作一条海豚，跳上他们的快船，像一只巨大而可怕的怪物躺在甲板上。船上的人谁也没有认出他来，想把他扔下海去，可他一抖动，船上的木板就全都晃动起来。他们吓得坐在船舱里一声不出，不敢解开缆绳，也不敢放下黑船的帆，只能让船按原来的方向航行，因为强劲的南风从后面推着他们的船。船就这样自己航行着，经过了马勒亚角，经过拉科尼亚的海岸、太阳神的城市、皮洛斯，绕过伯罗奔尼撒，一路驶进克里萨宽阔的海湾。他们想在皮洛斯靠岸，船却不听舵，像被神引着一样，一直把他们带到了葡萄园遍布的克里萨的港口。','When the temple was built, Phoebus Apollo pondered what men he should bring to be his ministers in rocky Pytho. As he considered, he saw a swift ship on the wine-dark sea, and in it many good men, Cretans from Minos''s city of Cnossos, sailing for sandy Pylos to trade. Phoebus Apollo leapt aboard their swift ship in the shape of a dolphin and lay there, a great and awesome monster. None of them recognised him; they wanted to throw the dolphin overboard, but he kept shaking the ship''s timbers. Terrified, they sat silent in the ship, not loosing the sheets nor lowering the sail of their dark-prowed ship, but sailing on as they had set out, with a strong south wind driving them from behind. So the ship ran on by itself past Malea, along the Laconian coast, past the town of the sun-god and Pylos, around the Peloponnese and into the broad gulf of Crisa. They wished to land at Pylos, but the ship would not obey the steering-oar; as though guided by the god, it carried them on to the harbour of Crisa, rich in vines.','claim.v0360.hym_apollo.13','《荷马颂歌·致阿波罗》第388–439行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_hymn3_apollo_evelyn_white.14','storyver.greek.hymn3_apollo_evelyn_white',14,'德尔斐的祭司','The priests of Delphi','船靠了岸，远射之神阿波罗像一颗正午的星星一样从船上跃出，火花四溅，光芒直冲天际。他走进自己的神庙，在宝贵的三足鼎之间点燃了火焰，火光照亮了整个克里萨，克里萨的妇女们都在神的光芒中大声惊呼。然后他又像思想一样迅速地回到船边，化作一个强壮的青年，长发披在宽阔的肩上，问克里特人是谁，从哪里来。克里特人的首领告诉他，他们本想去皮洛斯，却被一位神违背他们的意愿带到了这里。阿波罗说：你们再也回不到可爱的故乡了，你们将在这里守护我的富饶神庙，受到所有人的尊敬。我就是宙斯的儿子阿波罗。因为我曾化作海豚跳上你们的船，你们就向我祈祷，称我为“海豚的阿波罗”，祭坛也叫这个名字。他领着他们唱着“伊厄·派安”走上皮托。克里特人问他，在这片既没有葡萄园也没有牧场的地方，他们靠什么生活。阿波罗笑着说：你们每人右手拿一把刀，不停地宰杀羊群，人们带来的祭品就足够你们享用。只是要守规矩，如果你们说出狂妄的话、做出傲慢的事，别人就会来做你们的主人，你们将永远受人支配。','When the ship came to shore, far-shooting Apollo leapt from it like a star at midday, sparks flying from him and the brightness reaching to heaven. He entered his shrine and kindled a flame among his precious tripods, and its blaze lit all Crisa, so that the wives of Crisa cried aloud at the rushing light of Phoebus. Then, swift as thought, he sprang back to the ship in the form of a strong young man, his long hair over his broad shoulders, and asked the Cretans who they were and whence they came. Their leader said they had meant to sail to Pylos but a god had brought them here against their will. Apollo answered: ''You will never return to your homes; you shall keep my rich temple here and be honoured by all men. I am Apollo, son of Zeus. Because I first leapt on your ship as a dolphin, pray to me as Delphinius, and the altar too shall bear that name.'' He led them up to Pytho singing Ie Paean. When the Cretans asked how they should live in a place with neither vineyards nor pastures, Apollo smiled: ''Each of you, knife in hand, will slaughter sheep continually; the offerings men bring will be abundant. Only keep my rule: if any of you speaks a vain word or does an insolent deed, other men will be your masters, and you will be subject for ever.''','claim.v0360.hym_apollo.14','《荷马颂歌·致阿波罗》第440–546行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_hymn4_hermes_evelyn_white.1','storyver.greek.hymn4_hermes_evelyn_white',1,'洞中的仙女','The nymph in the cave','诗人请缪斯歌唱赫尔墨斯，宙斯和迈亚的儿子，库勒涅山和牛羊成群的阿卡狄亚的主人，众神迅捷的使者。迈亚是一位秀发的仙女，性情羞怯，不喜欢和众神来往，独自住在一个幽深的山洞里。宙斯常常在深夜里去找她，那时白臂的赫拉正沉沉地睡着，不死的神和会死的人都不知道这件事。等到宙斯的心愿实现，第十个月挂上天空，迈亚就生下了一个孩子：一个机灵狡猾的孩子，强盗、偷牛贼、带来梦境的人、夜里的守望者、守在门口的贼，他很快就要在不死的神中间显出了不起的本事。诗人特意说出他出生的时刻：他清晨出生，中午就弹起了竖琴，傍晚就偷走了远射之神阿波罗的牛——那是每月的第四天，女王迈亚生下他的日子。整首颂歌讲的，就是这个孩子出生后头两天里做的事。','The poet asks the Muse to sing of Hermes, son of Zeus and Maia, lord of Cyllene and of Arcadia rich in flocks, swift messenger of the gods. Maia was a rich-haired nymph, shy, who shunned the company of the blessed gods and lived in a deep, shadowy cave. There Zeus used to come to her in the dead of night, while white-armed Hera lay sweetly asleep, and neither deathless gods nor mortal men knew of it. When the will of Zeus was fulfilled and the tenth moon was fixed in heaven, Maia bore a son: wily and cunning, a robber, a cattle-driver, a bringer of dreams, a watcher by night, a thief at the gates, who was soon to show wonderful deeds among the deathless gods. The poet marks the hour exactly: born at dawn, at midday he played the lyre, and in the evening he stole the cattle of far-shooting Apollo — on the fourth day of the month, the day on which queenly Maia bore him. The whole hymn tells what this child did in his first two days.','claim.v0360.hym_hermes.01','《荷马颂歌·致赫尔墨斯》第1–19行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_hymn4_hermes_evelyn_white.2','storyver.greek.hymn4_hermes_evelyn_white',2,'乌龟变成竖琴','The tortoise becomes a lyre','赫尔墨斯从母亲不朽的怀里一跳下来，就不肯安安静静地躺在神圣的摇篮里，跨过高高的门槛，去找阿波罗的牛群。他在洞口碰到了一只乌龟，正在洞前的草地上慢吞吞地吃草。宙斯这位能干的儿子一看见它就笑了，说：好兆头！我不会小看你。可爱的东西，你来得正好，你是宴会的伴侣，跳舞的朋友。你住在山里，身上却披着这样漂亮的壳，我要把你带回家去。你活着的时候能抵挡害人的巫术，死了以后却能唱出最美的歌。他把乌龟抱进洞里，用一把灰色的铁凿挖出了它的生命，然后按照尺寸截下芦苇，穿过龟壳的背面固定好，在外面蒙上一张牛皮，装上两根琴臂和一根横梁，再拉上七根用羊肠做的和谐的琴弦。做好以后，他拿起这件可爱的玩具，用拨子一根根地试弦，琴声在他手下发出奇妙的声音。神一边弹，一边即兴唱起歌来，就像年轻人在宴会上互相打趣一样：他唱宙斯和穿着漂亮鞋子的迈亚怎样相爱，唱自己高贵的出身，也唱母亲山洞里的侍女、华美的住所和锅碗瓢盆。','As soon as Hermes leapt from his mother''s immortal womb he would not lie still in his holy cradle, but sprang over the threshold of the high-roofed cave to look for Apollo''s cattle. There he found a tortoise, slowly feeding on the grass before the cave. The cunning son of Zeus laughed when he saw it and said: ''A lucky sign for me! I will not scorn you. Welcome, lovely thing, companion of the feast and friend of the dance. You live on the mountains, yet wear such a sparkling shell; I will take you home. Living, you are a charm against harmful witchcraft; dead, you will make the sweetest song.'' He carried it inside, scooped out its life with a chisel of grey iron, cut stalks of reed to measure and fixed them through the back of the shell, stretched oxhide over it, fitted two horns and a cross-piece, and strung seven harmonious strings of sheep-gut. Then he took up the lovely toy and tried each string with a plectrum, and it sounded wonderfully under his hand. The god sang to it, improvising as young men banter at feasts: of Zeus and neat-shod Maia and their love, of his own glorious birth, of his mother''s handmaids, her splendid home and her tripods and cauldrons.','claim.v0360.hym_hermes.02','《荷马颂歌·致赫尔墨斯》第20–61行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_hymn4_hermes_evelyn_white.3','storyver.greek.hymn4_hermes_evelyn_white',3,'五十头牛','Fifty cattle','可是赫尔墨斯心里想的是别的事。他把空心的竖琴放回神圣的摇篮里，满心想吃肉，就从芬芳的山洞里跳出来，像强盗在漆黑的夜里那样四处窥探。太阳带着马车和马沉入大洋的时候，赫尔墨斯来到了庇厄里亚的阴暗山岭，那里有众神的牛在可爱的草地上吃草。迈亚的儿子、眼光锐利的阿尔戈斯杀手从牛群里赶走了五十头叫声洪亮的牛。为了不让人看出牛的去向，他想出一个妙计：让牛倒着走，蹄子向前、尾巴向后，在沙地上留下相反的足迹；他自己则走另一条路。他在海边的沙滩上，用柽柳和桃金娘的嫩枝编成一双奇妙的鞋子，把带着叶子的枝条绑在脚下，这样他的脚印就谁也认不出来了。这是一双连神也想不到的鞋子，他像一个准备远行的人那样，匆匆赶着路，离开了庇厄里亚。诗人说，这个出生才一天的孩子，已经比任何老练的盗贼都更狡猾了。','But Hermes had other things in mind. He laid the hollow lyre in his holy cradle and, craving meat, sprang out of the fragrant cave, looking about like a robber in the dark of night. As the sun was sinking into Ocean with his horses and chariot, Hermes came to the shadowy mountains of Pieria, where the cattle of the blessed gods grazed the lovely meadows. The keen-eyed slayer of Argus, son of Maia, cut out fifty loud-lowing cows from the herd. To hide their path he hit on a trick: he drove them backwards, hooves in front and tails behind, so that their tracks in the sandy ground ran the wrong way; and he himself went by another road. On the sand by the sea he wove wonderful sandals of tamarisk and myrtle twigs, binding the leafy shoots under his feet so that no one could make out his footprints — sandals no god could have imagined. Like one making a long journey in haste, he left Pieria behind. A child one day old, the poet says, was already more cunning than any seasoned thief.','claim.v0360.hym_hermes.03','《荷马颂歌·致赫尔墨斯》第62–86行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_hymn4_hermes_evelyn_white.4','storyver.greek.hymn4_hermes_evelyn_white',4,'昂刻斯托斯的老人','The old man at Onchestus','赫尔墨斯赶着牛穿过平原，经过鲜花盛开的昂刻斯托斯时，一个老人正在葡萄园里劳作，看见了这个赶牛的孩子。迈亚光荣的儿子先开口对老人说：老人家，你弯着腰在葡萄园里干活，等你这些葡萄藤结满果实的时候，你的葡萄酒一定很多。可是记住：你看见了也要当作没看见，听见了也要当作没听见；没有伤害到你的事，你就不要多嘴。说完他就赶着健壮的牛群继续往前走。光荣的赫尔墨斯赶着它们走过许多阴暗的山岭、回声隆隆的峡谷和开满鲜花的平原。神圣的黑夜，他幽暗的帮手，快要过去了，催人起身干活的黎明很快就要到来，明亮的月亮、帕拉斯的女儿、墨伽墨得斯之子，刚刚登上她的瞭望台。这时宙斯强大的儿子把福玻斯·阿波罗的宽额头的牛赶到了阿尔甫斯河边。它们来到一座高顶的牛棚和饮水处，在开满鲜花的草地上饱餐了一顿，然后被赶进了牛棚，嘴里还嚼着莲花和带露的莎草。这位老人后来成了阿波罗追查此事时唯一的目击者。','As Hermes drove the cattle across the plain past flowery Onchestus, an old man tending his vineyard saw the child driving them. The glorious son of Maia spoke to him first: ''Old man, digging your vines with bowed shoulders, you will have wine enough when all these bear fruit. But see that you have not seen and hear that you have not heard, and keep silent when nothing of yours is harmed.'' Then he drove the sturdy cattle on. Glorious Hermes drove them through many shadowy mountains, echoing gorges and flowering plains. Divine night, his dark helper, was mostly spent, and dawn that sets men to work was coming quickly, and the bright moon, daughter of Pallas son of Megamedes, had just climbed to her watch-place, when the strong son of Zeus drove the broad-browed cattle of Phoebus Apollo to the river Alpheus. They came to a high-roofed byre and drinking troughs before a splendid meadow, fed well on the flowers, and were driven into the byre still munching lotus and dewy galingale. The old man was later the only witness when Apollo came asking.','claim.v0360.hym_hermes.04','《荷马颂歌·致赫尔墨斯》第87–104行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_hymn4_hermes_evelyn_white.5','storyver.greek.hymn4_hermes_evelyn_white',5,'阿尔甫斯河边的火','Fire by the Alpheus','到了阿尔甫斯河边，赫尔墨斯捡来许多木柴，开始想办法生火。他拿起一根月桂树枝，在一根石榴木上用力钻动，热气就冒了出来。诗人特意说：是赫尔墨斯第一个发明了钻木取火的火棒和火。他在一个坑里堆满干柴，火焰熊熊燃烧，远远地冒着热气。接着，他把两头叫声洪亮的弯角母牛拖到火边，他力气很大，把两头牛都掀翻在地，扭断了它们的脖子。他剥下牛皮，切下带着肥油的肉，用木签穿起来烤，还把黑色的血灌进肠子里。他把牛皮摊在一块干燥的岩石上——诗人说那些牛皮直到他的时代还摊在那里。然后，赫尔墨斯把烤好的肉从火上取下来，分成十二份，每一份都完全相同，像是要献给众神。他闻着烤肉的香气，非常想吃，可是作为一位神，他终究没有让肉进入自己神圣的喉咙。他把肥肉和许多肉放进高高的牛棚里，作为偷牛的证据藏起来，把牛蹄和牛头放进火里烧得干干净净，又把那双树枝鞋扔进了水流湍急的阿尔甫斯河，把火熄灭，用沙子盖住黑色的灰烬。','At the Alpheus Hermes gathered a pile of wood and began to seek the art of fire. He took a fine laurel branch and twirled it hard in a piece of pomegranate wood, and the hot smoke rose — for, the poet says, it was Hermes who first invented fire-sticks and fire. He piled dry wood thickly in a sunken pit, and the flame blazed far. Then he dragged two lowing, crook-horned cows close to the fire, for great strength was with him, threw them both panting on their backs and pierced their spines. He cut up the rich fatty meat, roasted it on wooden spits and filled the guts with dark blood. The hides he stretched out on a dry rock, where, the poet says, they remain to this day. Then Hermes took the roasted meat from the fire and divided it into twelve portions, each made perfect, as if for the gods. He longed for the meat, for the sweet savour tormented him, god though he was; yet his proud heart did not let the flesh pass down his holy throat. He stored the fat and meat in the high byre as a sign of his theft, burned the heads and feet to ashes, threw his twig sandals into the deep-eddying Alpheus, quenched the embers and covered the black ashes with sand.','claim.v0360.hym_hermes.05','《荷马颂歌·致赫尔墨斯》第105–141行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_hymn4_hermes_evelyn_white.6','storyver.greek.hymn4_hermes_evelyn_white',6,'母亲的责备','His mother''s rebuke','天亮以前，赫尔墨斯回到了库勒涅山的高峰。漫长的路上，没有一位神、没有一个人遇见他，连狗也没有叫。宙斯幸运的儿子侧着身子，像秋天的风、像一阵雾一样，从门闩的锁孔里钻进了山洞。他悄悄地走进富丽的内室，脚步轻得连地面都没有响一声，然后连忙钻进摇篮，把襁褓裹在肩上，像一个无助的婴儿那样躺着，手指玩弄着腿上的被单，左手还紧紧地抱着那把心爱的竖琴。可是他瞒不过母亲。女神对他说：你这个狡猾的家伙，深更半夜你从哪里回来，身上还带着无耻的气味？我看阿波罗很快就会用捆不开的绳子把你绑走，要不然你就会在山谷里做一辈子的贼。走吧，你父亲把你生出来，是给会死的人和不死的神添乱的。赫尔墨斯狡猾地回答：母亲，你为什么拿吓唬小孩的话来吓我？我要选最好的本事，永远照顾你和我自己。我们为什么要待在这个山洞里，没有祭品，也没有人祈祷？我要和阿波罗一样享有尊荣。如果父亲不给我，我就去做盗贼的首领，去皮托撬开阿波罗的大神庙，偷走三足鼎、金子和铁器。','Before dawn Hermes came back to the bright peaks of Cyllene. On the long road no god or man met him, and no dog barked. Luck-bringing Hermes, son of Zeus, slipped edgeways through the keyhole of the hall like the autumn breeze, like mist. Straight through the cave he went to the rich inner room, stepping softly so that he made no noise as on a floor, then quickly got into his cradle, wrapped his swaddling clothes about his shoulders like a helpless babe and lay playing with the covering about his knees — and in his left hand he kept his sweet lyre. But he could not hide from his mother. The goddess said: ''You sly rogue, where have you been in the night, clothed in shamelessness? Now I think Apollo will soon carry you off with unbreakable bonds, or you will live a robber''s life in the glens. Go! Your father begot you to be a great worry to mortal men and deathless gods.'' Hermes answered craftily: ''Mother, why try to frighten me like a feeble child? I will try whatever plan is best, to care for you and me for ever. Why should we stay in this cave without gifts or prayers? I will have the same honour as Apollo. If my father will not give it, I will be prince of robbers and break into Apollo''s great house at Pytho for tripods, gold and iron.''','claim.v0360.hym_hermes.06','《荷马颂歌·致赫尔墨斯》第142–183行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_hymn4_hermes_evelyn_white.7','storyver.greek.hymn4_hermes_evelyn_white',7,'阿波罗寻牛','Apollo seeks his cattle','黎明从大洋深处升起，给人们带来光明。阿波罗来到神圣的昂刻斯托斯，看见那个老人正在路边的葡萄园里修整篱笆，就问他：老人家，我从庇厄里亚来找我的牛，五十头弯角的母牛，它们都离开了牛群，公牛和四条忠实的看牛狗却留在原地，这真是怪事。你看见有人赶着它们走过吗？老人回答说：我的朋友，人眼看见的东西太多，很难都说清楚。不过我好像看见一个孩子赶着牛，我说不准他是谁，他还是个婴儿，手里拿着一根棍子，左右来回地走着，让牛倒着走，头朝着他。阿波罗听了，立刻往前赶路。他看见一只展开长翅膀的鸟，马上明白偷牛的就是宙斯的儿子。他急忙赶往神圣的皮洛斯，用紫云遮住宽阔的肩膀，一路上看着那些足迹惊叹：这真是奇事！这些足迹确实是牛的，可都朝着长满水仙的草地往回走；而这些足迹既不像男人，也不像女人，也不像灰狼、熊或狮子，更不像鬃毛浓密的马人，是谁迈着这样奇怪的大步走路？','Dawn rose from deep-flowing Ocean bringing light to men, and Apollo came to holy Onchestus, where he found the old man tending the hedge of his vineyard by the road. ''Old man,'' he said, ''I have come from Pieria seeking my cattle, fifty crook-horned cows, all strayed from the herd, while the black bull grazes alone and the four fierce dogs stay behind like men of one mind — a strange thing. Have you seen anyone pass with them?'' The old man answered: ''My friend, it is hard to tell all one sees with one''s eyes. Yet I think I saw a child — I cannot say for certain — a mere infant, with a staff, driving the cattle; he walked from side to side and drove them backwards, their heads facing him.'' Apollo hurried on. He saw a long-winged bird and knew at once that the thief was the child of Zeus. He sped to holy Pylos, his broad shoulders veiled in purple cloud, and marvelled at the tracks: ''This is a wonder! These are the tracks of cattle, but turned back toward the asphodel meadow; and these are not the steps of man or woman, nor of grey wolves, bears or lions, nor of a shaggy-maned Centaur — who takes such monstrous strides?''','claim.v0360.hym_hermes.07','《荷马颂歌·致赫尔墨斯》第184–212行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_hymn4_hermes_evelyn_white.8','storyver.greek.hymn4_hermes_evelyn_white',8,'库勒涅的山洞','The cave on Cyllene','阿波罗来到库勒涅山林木茂盛的山上，来到岩石中幽深的山洞，那是不死的仙女迈亚生下宙斯之子的地方。可爱的香气弥漫在神圣的山坡上，许多细腿的羊在那里吃草。远射的阿波罗匆匆走过石头门槛，进入幽暗的山洞。赫尔墨斯看见阿波罗为牛群发怒，就缩进芬芳的襁褓里，像一堆被灰烬盖住的余火那样，把头、手和脚缩成一团，假装刚刚洗过澡、准备甜甜地睡觉，腋下却还夹着那把竖琴。阿波罗一眼就认出了美丽的仙女和她的爱子，小小的孩子裹在狡猾的伎俩里。他打量着这座大屋的每一个角落，拿起闪亮的钥匙，打开了三个储藏室，里面满是琼浆和可爱的神食，还有大量的金银和仙女的紫色、银色衣裳，就像众神神圣的家里应有的那样。他找遍了每个角落，然后对赫尔墨斯说：躺在摇篮里的孩子，快告诉我牛在哪里，不然我们就要不客气了。我会抓住你，把你扔进阴暗的塔耳塔罗斯，你母亲和父亲都救不了你。赫尔墨斯狡猾地回答：我才出生，脚还很嫩，地面还很硬，我只关心睡觉、母亲的奶和温暖的洗澡水。我连牛是什么都没听说过。','Apollo came to the wooded mountain of Cyllene, to the deep cave in the rock where the deathless nymph Maia had borne the child of Zeus. A sweet fragrance spread over the holy hill, and many thin-shanked sheep grazed there. Far-shooting Apollo hurried over the stone threshold into the dusky cave. When Hermes saw Apollo angry about his cattle, he snuggled down in his fragrant swaddling clothes, curled up like a heap of embers covered with ashes — head, hands and feet drawn together — like a babe fresh from his bath, longing for sweet sleep, though he still had the lyre under his arm. Apollo knew at once the lovely nymph and her dear son, a little child wrapped in sly wiles. He peered into every corner of the great dwelling, took a shining key and opened three closets full of nectar and lovely ambrosia, with much gold and silver and the nymph''s purple and silvery garments, such as are kept in the holy houses of the gods. When he had searched every nook he said: ''Child in the cradle, tell me where my cattle are, or we two will quarrel. I will take you and cast you into dusky Tartarus, and neither your mother nor your father will save you.'' Hermes answered craftily: ''I was born yesterday; my feet are soft and the ground is hard. I care for sleep, my mother''s milk and warm baths. I have never even heard of cattle.''','claim.v0360.hym_hermes.08','《荷马颂歌·致赫尔墨斯》第213–277行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_hymn4_hermes_evelyn_white.9','storyver.greek.hymn4_hermes_evelyn_white',9,'去见宙斯','To Zeus','赫尔墨斯一边说，一边眼睛不停地眨着，眉毛扬来扬去，四处张望，吹着长长的口哨，像在听一个无聊的故事。远射之神阿波罗轻轻地笑了，对他说：你这个狡猾的骗子，听你说话的样子，我看你以后一定会在许多夜里撬开人家的房子。你将来会被称为盗贼的首领，这就是你在不死的神中间的特权。来吧，从摇篮里出来，夜的伙伴。说着，福玻斯·阿波罗抓起孩子就要带走。可是强大的阿尔戈斯杀手在被举起来的时候，故意放出一个征兆，一个肚子里的鲁莽信使，紧接着又打了一个喷嚏。阿波罗听见了，一下子把光荣的赫尔墨斯扔在地上，虽然急着赶路，还是坐在他面前嘲笑他说：放心吧，裹着襁褓的孩子，宙斯和迈亚的儿子，靠着这些征兆，我总会找到我那些强壮的牛，而你会给我带路。赫尔墨斯跳起来，把襁褓拉到耳边，说：远射之神，你要把我带到哪里去？就为了牛的事这样折磨我？我真希望所有的牛都死光！我没有偷你的牛，也没看见别人偷。你只管说，我们去宙斯那里，让他来评理。','As he spoke, Hermes kept glancing about with twinkling eyes, his brows going up and down, and gave a long whistle, as if listening to an idle tale. Far-working Apollo laughed softly and said: ''You crafty cheat, by the way you talk I think you will often break into well-built houses by night. This shall be your honour among the deathless gods: you will be called prince of robbers for ever. But come, companion of dark night, get down from your cradle.'' With that Phoebus Apollo caught up the child to carry him off. But the strong slayer of Argus, as he was lifted, sent forth an omen, a rude messenger of the belly, and then sneezed. Apollo heard it and dropped glorious Hermes on the ground; though eager to go, he sat before him and mocked him: ''Take heart, little swaddling baby, son of Zeus and Maia. By these omens I shall find my strong cattle, and you will lead the way.'' Hermes sprang up, pulled his swaddling clothes about his ears and said: ''Far-shooter, where are you taking me? Why do you plague me so about cattle? I wish every cow were dead! I did not steal your cattle nor see another steal them. Come, let us go before Zeus and let him judge.''','claim.v0360.hym_hermes.09','《荷马颂歌·致赫尔墨斯》第278–322行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_hymn4_hermes_evelyn_white.10','storyver.greek.hymn4_hermes_evelyn_white',10,'宙斯的判断','The judgement of Zeus','两位神来到芬芳的奥林波斯山顶，来到父亲宙斯面前，正义的天平就摆在那里。阿波罗先开口，把事情一五一十地说了：这个孩子在库勒涅山出生不久，就偷走了我的牛，让牛倒着走，自己脚上绑着树枝走路，在阿尔甫斯河边过了夜；昂刻斯托斯的老人可以作证。我找到他时，他还装成婴儿，说什么也不承认。赫尔墨斯接着说话，他指着阿波罗，说：父亲宙斯，我会对你说真话，因为我老实，不会说谎。今天太阳刚升起，阿波罗就到我家来找他的牛，没有带一个神作证，还威胁要把我扔进塔耳塔罗斯。他正当青春，我昨天才出生，哪里像个偷牛的强盗？我以众神华美的门廊起誓，我没有把牛赶回家，也没有越过门槛。你知道我没有罪，我会在合适的时候帮助弱小的人，你也要帮帮年轻的孩子。赫尔墨斯说这话时，眼睛一直眨着，襁褓还挂在胳膊上。宙斯看着这个狡猾的孩子那么巧妙地抵赖，忍不住大笑起来。他命令两兄弟同心协力去找牛，让赫尔墨斯做向导，毫不隐瞒地指出藏牛的地方。宙斯点了点头，聪明的赫尔墨斯只好服从。','The two came to the summit of fragrant Olympus, to their father Zeus, where the scales of judgement were set. Apollo spoke first and told it all: this child, soon after his birth on Cyllene, stole my cattle, drove them backwards, walked with twigs bound to his feet and spent the night by the Alpheus; the old man of Onchestus is witness; and when I found him he lay like a baby and denied everything. Then Hermes spoke, pointing at Apollo: ''Father Zeus, I will tell you the truth, for I am honest and cannot lie. He came to our house today at sunrise looking for his cattle, with no god as witness, and threatened to cast me into Tartarus. He is in the bloom of youth; I was born yesterday — do I look like a cattle-driver? I swear by these finely made porches of the gods that I did not drive the cattle home nor cross the threshold. You know I am not guilty; I will help the weak in due time — so help the young.'' All the while he winked, and his swaddling cloth hung on his arm. Zeus laughed aloud to see the mischievous child deny the theft so skilfully. He bade the two be of one mind and search for the cattle, with Hermes as guide to show the place without deceit. Zeus nodded, and clever Hermes obeyed.','claim.v0360.hym_hermes.10','《荷马颂歌·致赫尔墨斯》第323–396行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_hymn4_hermes_evelyn_white.11','storyver.greek.hymn4_hermes_evelyn_white',11,'竖琴换牛群','The lyre for the cattle','两兄弟来到阿尔甫斯河边的牛棚，赫尔墨斯把牛从石洞里赶了出来。阿波罗看见那两张牛皮摊在岩石上，惊叹这个刚出生的孩子竟有力气宰杀两头牛。他想用结实的柳条把赫尔墨斯捆起来，可是柳条一落地就生了根，在牛群四周长成一片，把所有的牛都缠住了。这时赫尔墨斯拿出竖琴，用拨子一根根地拨动琴弦，琴声美妙动听。阿波罗听了，笑了起来，那可爱的声音直透进他的心里，一股甜蜜的渴望攫住了他。赫尔墨斯放心地站在阿波罗的左边，一边弹琴一边唱，歌唱不死的神和黑色大地怎样诞生，众神怎样分得各自的领地；他先歌颂缪斯之母谟涅摩叙涅，然后按照年纪和出身，一一歌颂众神。阿波罗被深深打动，说：你这个偷牛贼，你这件东西值五十头牛！这是哪里来的本事？我从没听过这样的歌。赫尔墨斯说：我不吝惜这门手艺，今天就把它送给你。你是宙斯身边的第一位，你也懂得用各种技艺。拿去吧，带着它去参加宴会和舞蹈。阿波罗把竖琴接过来，把闪亮的牛鞭交给赫尔墨斯，让他照看牛群。从此阿波罗成了竖琴的主人，赫尔墨斯成了牧人的神。','The brothers came to the byre by the Alpheus, and Hermes drove the cattle out of the rocky cave. Apollo saw the two hides on the rock and marvelled that a newborn child had the strength to slay two cows. He tried to bind Hermes with strong withies, but the withies, as they fell to the ground, took root and grew all around, entangling the cattle. Then Hermes took up the lyre and tried each string with the plectrum, and it sounded wonderfully. Apollo laughed for joy; the lovely sound went to his heart, and sweet longing seized him. Hermes, taking courage, stood at Apollo''s left playing and singing — of the deathless gods and the dark earth, how they came to be and how each received his portion; first he honoured Mnemosyne, mother of the Muses, then the other gods in order of age and birth. Apollo, deeply moved, said: ''Cattle-slayer, this is worth fifty cows! Where did this skill come from? I have never heard such song.'' Hermes said: ''I do not grudge you my art; I give it to you today. You sit first beside Zeus and know all skills. Take it to the feast and the dance.'' Apollo took the lyre and put the shining whip in Hermes'' hand to keep the herds. So Apollo became master of the lyre, and Hermes god of herdsmen.','claim.v0360.hym_hermes.11','《荷马颂歌·致赫尔墨斯》第397–502行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_hymn4_hermes_evelyn_white.12','storyver.greek.hymn4_hermes_evelyn_white',12,'兄弟的盟约','The brothers'' pact','两兄弟赶着牛群回到神圣的草地，然后回到积雪的奥林波斯山，宙斯看见他们和好，心里很高兴。从那以后，赫尔墨斯一直爱着勒托的儿子，阿波罗也爱着他。赫尔墨斯又用芦苇做了一支可以吹出远远声音的排箫，阿波罗担心他会把竖琴和弓也偷走，就要他以斯梯克斯的河水起誓。赫尔墨斯点头发誓，永远不偷远射之神的东西，也不走近他坚固的家。阿波罗也起誓，在众神和人中间，再没有谁比赫尔墨斯更亲近、更受他珍爱。他送给赫尔墨斯一根华美的金杖，那是带来财富和繁荣的三叉金杖，能保护他。至于预言，阿波罗说，那是宙斯只交给他一个人的，他不能给别的神；不过他告诉赫尔墨斯，在帕尔纳索斯山的山坳里住着三位长着翅膀的处女姐妹，她们会教人占卜，如果吃了金色的蜂蜜，就会说出真话。宙斯也让赫尔墨斯做所有鸟兽和牛羊的主人，并且只让他一个人做通往冥王哈得斯的使者。迈亚的儿子就这样与所有的神和人交往；诗人最后向宙斯和迈亚的儿子致意，答应在下一首歌里再记起他。','The brothers drove the cattle back to the holy meadow and returned to snowy Olympus, and Zeus was glad to see them reconciled. From that time Hermes loved the son of Leto, and Apollo loved him. Hermes made himself another instrument, pipes of reed whose sound carries far; and Apollo, fearing he might steal the lyre and the bow as well, asked him to swear by the water of Styx. Hermes nodded and swore never to steal anything of the far-shooter''s nor come near his strong house. Apollo swore that no one among gods or men would be dearer to him than Hermes. He gave him a splendid golden staff of wealth and riches, with three branches, to guard him. But prophecy, Apollo said, Zeus had given to him alone, and he could not share it; yet he told Hermes of three winged sister maidens who live under a fold of Parnassus and teach divination, and speak truth when they have eaten golden honey. Zeus also made Hermes lord over all birds and beasts and flocks, and the only appointed messenger to Hades. So the son of Maia keeps company with all gods and men; and the poet bids farewell to the son of Zeus and Maia, promising to remember him in another song.','claim.v0360.hym_hermes.12','《荷马颂歌·致赫尔墨斯》第503–580行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_hymn5_aphrodite_evelyn_white.1','storyver.greek.hymn5_aphrodite_evelyn_white',1,'阿佛洛狄忒的力量与雅典娜','Aphrodite''s power, and Athena','诗人请缪斯歌唱金色的阿佛洛狄忒，塞浦路斯的女神。她在众神心中唤起甜蜜的渴望，征服了会死的人类、天上飞的鸟和陆地上、海里所有的野兽：她的事业，所有的生灵都关心。可是有三位女神，她既不能说服，也不能欺骗。第一位是持埃癸斯盾的宙斯的女儿、眼睛明亮的雅典娜。金色的阿佛洛狄忒做的事情不能让她高兴，她喜欢的是战争和阿瑞斯的事业，喜欢战斗和厮杀，也喜欢光荣的手艺。是她第一个教大地上的工匠制造战车和镶铜的马车，也是她在屋子里教皮肤柔嫩的少女们做光荣的手工活，把这门技艺放进每个少女的心中。诗人在颂歌的一开头就先划出界限：爱的力量虽然统治着万物，却并不统治一切；而后面整首诗要讲的，正是这股力量怎样连女神自己也躲不过。','The poet asks the Muse to tell of golden Aphrodite, the Cyprian, who stirs sweet desire in the gods and subdues the tribes of mortal men, the birds that fly in the air and all the creatures of dry land and sea: all of them care for her works. Yet there are three hearts she can neither bend nor ensnare. First is the daughter of aegis-bearing Zeus, bright-eyed Athena. She takes no pleasure in the deeds of golden Aphrodite, but delights in wars and the work of Ares, in strife and battle, and in glorious crafts. She first taught earthly craftsmen to make chariots and cars wrought with bronze, and she teaches tender maidens in the house the glorious works of handicraft, putting the skill in each one''s mind. At the very start the poet draws a boundary: the power of love rules all things, but not quite everything — and the rest of the hymn will show how that power does not spare even the goddess herself.','claim.v0360.hym_aphrodite.01','《荷马颂歌·致阿佛洛狄忒》第1–15行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_hymn5_aphrodite_evelyn_white.2','storyver.greek.hymn5_aphrodite_evelyn_white',2,'阿耳忒弥斯','Artemis','第二位是戴着金冠、喜爱喧闹的狩猎女神阿耳忒弥斯，爱笑的阿佛洛狄忒从来不能用爱情制服她。阿耳忒弥斯喜欢的是弓箭，是在山中追杀野兽，是竖琴、合唱的舞蹈和响亮的呼喊，是幽暗的树林和正直的人们居住的城市。诗人在这里只用了几行，却勾画出一位与阿佛洛狄忒截然相反的女神：一个在山野间奔跑、与弓箭和猎犬为伴、在仙女们的歌舞中领头的少女。在前一首颂歌里，阿耳忒弥斯在奥林波斯的舞蹈中唱歌，是阿波罗的孪生姐妹；在这一首里，她被放在爱神力量的边界之外。颂歌并不讲她的故事，而是用她来说明：有些神的心，是爱欲到不了的地方。这一点对后面的故事很重要，因为接下来，阿佛洛狄忒自己就会被她平时施加于别人的力量所支配。后来的诗人常把阿耳忒弥斯描写成守护少女和野兽的女神，而这几行颂歌，是这一形象最早、最简洁的见证之一。','The second is Artemis of the golden arrows and the loud hunting cry, whom laughter-loving Aphrodite never tames in love. Artemis loves the bow and arrows and the slaying of wild beasts in the mountains, the lyre and the dance and thrilling cries, shady woods and the cities of upright men. In a few lines the poet sketches a goddess wholly unlike Aphrodite: a maiden running through the wilds with bow and hounds, leading the nymphs in song and dance. In the previous hymn Artemis sang in the dance on Olympus as Apollo''s twin; here she is placed outside the reach of love''s power. The hymn does not tell her story but uses her to show that some gods'' hearts are places desire cannot reach. That matters for what follows, because Aphrodite herself is about to be ruled by the power she usually wields over others. Later poets often describe Artemis as guardian of maidens and of wild beasts, and these lines are among the earliest and briefest witnesses to that picture.','claim.v0360.hym_aphrodite.02','《荷马颂歌·致阿佛洛狄忒》第16–20行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_hymn5_aphrodite_evelyn_white.3','storyver.greek.hymn5_aphrodite_evelyn_white',3,'赫斯提亚的誓言','Hestia''s vow','第三位是可敬的处女赫斯提亚，阿佛洛狄忒的事业同样不能让她高兴。她是狡猾的克洛诺斯第一个生下的孩子，又因为持埃癸斯盾的宙斯的意旨，成了最小的孩子。波塞冬和阿波罗都曾向这位尊贵的女神求婚，可她坚决地拒绝了。她摸着持埃癸斯盾的父亲宙斯的头，发下大誓，要一生一世做处女，这个誓言后来也实现了。于是父亲宙斯给了她一份美好的荣誉来代替婚姻：她坐在每一户人家的正中，得到最好的那一份祭品。在众神的所有神庙里，她都受到尊敬；在所有凡人中间，她是众神里最受尊崇的一位。颂歌说她“第一个也是最小的”，后世的注释者解释说，这是因为克洛诺斯第一个吞下了她，又最后一个把她吐出来，她因此像是重新出生了一次。在希腊人的家里，火塘就在屋子的中央，人们献祭时第一份和最后一份往往都献给她。','The third is the revered maiden Hestia, whom Aphrodite''s works do not please either. She was the first child of wily Cronos and the youngest too, by the will of aegis-bearing Zeus. Poseidon and Apollo both sought this queenly goddess, but she was wholly unwilling and stubbornly refused. Touching the head of father Zeus who holds the aegis, she swore a great oath that she would be a maiden all her days — an oath that has been fulfilled. So father Zeus gave her a fine honour instead of marriage: she sits in the middle of every house and has the richest portion. In all the temples of the gods she has a share of honour, and among all mortals she is chief of the goddesses. The hymn calls her both first and youngest; later commentators explain that Cronos swallowed her first and disgorged her last, so she was as if born a second time. In Greek houses the hearth stood at the centre of the home, and offerings often began and ended with her.','claim.v0360.hym_aphrodite.03','《荷马颂歌·致阿佛洛狄忒》第21–32行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_hymn5_aphrodite_evelyn_white.4','storyver.greek.hymn5_aphrodite_evelyn_white',4,'宙斯的回敬','Zeus''s answer','除了这三位，无论是有福的神还是会死的人，没有谁能躲开阿佛洛狄忒。她甚至迷住了喜欢雷电的宙斯的心——宙斯是最伟大的神，享有最高的荣耀，可只要她愿意，她就能轻易地欺骗他那聪明的心，让他和凡间的女子结合，使他忘掉自己的姐姐和妻子赫拉。赫拉是不死的女神中最美丽的一位，是狡猾的克洛诺斯和瑞亚最光荣的女儿，宙斯以他永不错误的智慧，娶了这位贤惠的女神做妻子。阿佛洛狄忒常常在众神中间笑着夸耀，说她让众神和凡间的女子结合，生下会死的儿子，又让女神们和凡间的男人结合。宙斯于是决定回敬她：他在阿佛洛狄忒心里放进对一个凡人的甜蜜渴望，让她自己也尝一尝和凡人同床的滋味，免得她以后在众神中间嘲笑别人。这个凡人就是安喀塞斯，他当时正在伊达山泉水众多的高山上放牛，模样像一位不死的神。','Apart from these three, there is nothing among the blessed gods or mortal men that has escaped Aphrodite. She leads astray even the mind of Zeus who delights in thunder, the greatest of all, who has the highest honour; whenever she wishes, she easily deceives his wise heart and makes him mate with mortal women, forgetting Hera, his sister and wife, the loveliest of the deathless goddesses, most glorious daughter of wily Cronos and Rhea, whom Zeus in his unfailing wisdom made his chaste and careful wife. Aphrodite used to boast, laughing sweetly among the gods, that she had joined gods with mortal women who bore them mortal sons, and goddesses with mortal men. So Zeus resolved to answer her: he put into her heart sweet desire for a mortal man, so that she too should not be kept from a mortal''s bed, nor mock the other gods for it. That man was Anchises, who was then herding cattle on the steep hills of many-fountained Ida, in body like the deathless gods.','claim.v0360.hym_aphrodite.04','《荷马颂歌·致阿佛洛狄忒》第33–55行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_hymn5_aphrodite_evelyn_white.5','storyver.greek.hymn5_aphrodite_evelyn_white',5,'帕福斯与伊达山','Paphos and Ida','爱笑的阿佛洛狄忒一看见安喀塞斯，就爱上了他，强烈的渴望攫住了她的心。她来到塞浦路斯，走进帕福斯她那芬芳的神庙——那里有她的圣域和芬芳的祭坛。她关上闪亮的门，美惠女神们在那里为她沐浴，用永不褪色的神油涂抹她的身体，那是神才用的油，带着为她准备的香气。她穿上华美的衣服，戴上金饰，然后离开芬芳的塞浦路斯，在高高的云中飞快地赶往特洛伊，来到伊达山——泉水众多、野兽之母的伊达山。她穿过山岭，直奔牛栏。跟在她身后的，是摇着尾巴的灰狼、目光凶猛的狮子、熊和永远吃不饱的敏捷的豹子。女神看见它们，心里高兴，就在它们的胸中放进了渴望，于是它们都成双成对地躲进了阴暗的山谷里。她自己则来到那几间建造精巧的小屋前。这一段是整首颂歌里最有名的场面之一：连山中的野兽都受到了女神经过时的力量的感染。','When laughter-loving Aphrodite saw Anchises she loved him, and desire seized her heart. She went to Cyprus, to Paphos, to her fragrant temple, where she has a precinct and a fragrant altar. She shut the shining doors, and there the Graces bathed her and anointed her with heavenly oil such as is on the bodies of the eternal gods, sweet and divine, which she had ready. Clothed in rich garments and decked with gold, she left fragrant Cyprus and sped high among the clouds toward Troy, to many-fountained Ida, mother of wild creatures. She went straight over the mountain to the homestead, and after her came fawning grey wolves, grim-eyed lions, bears and swift leopards, ravenous for deer. Seeing them, she was glad in heart and put desire in their breasts, so that they all lay down two by two in the shadowy glens. She herself came to the well-built huts. This is one of the most famous scenes of the hymn: even the beasts of the mountain feel the power of the goddess as she passes.','claim.v0360.hym_aphrodite.05','《荷马颂歌·致阿佛洛狄忒》第56–74行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_hymn5_aphrodite_evelyn_white.6','storyver.greek.hymn5_aphrodite_evelyn_white',6,'牧人小屋','At the herdsman''s hut','她发现安喀塞斯独自一人留在小屋里，他有着众神赐予的美貌。其他牧人都赶着牛到长满青草的牧场上去了，只有他留在家里，来回走着，弹着竖琴，声音清亮。宙斯的女儿阿佛洛狄忒站在他面前，模样和身材都像一个没有结婚的少女，免得他用眼睛认出她来而感到害怕。安喀塞斯看见她，惊讶地打量着她的身材、模样和闪亮的衣服：她穿着一件比火焰还明亮的长袍，戴着弯弯的胸针和闪光的耳环，柔软的脖子上挂着华美的项链，胸前的光芒像月亮一样。安喀塞斯心中充满了爱意，对她说：欢迎你，女王，不管你是哪一位来到这里的有福的女神——是阿耳忒弥斯、勒托、金色的阿佛洛狄忒、出身高贵的忒弥斯，还是眼睛明亮的雅典娜？或者你是一位美惠女神，或是住在这座美丽的山上、泉水和草地里的仙女？我要在山顶上为你建一座祭坛，四季都向你献上丰盛的祭品。请你善待我，让我在特洛伊人中间出人头地，让我的后代兴旺，让我自己长久地活在阳光下，在人们中间享福，直到老年。','She found Anchises left alone at the huts, with beauty given by the gods. The other herdsmen had gone with the cattle to the grassy pastures; he alone stayed, pacing to and fro and playing loudly on the lyre. Zeus''s daughter Aphrodite stood before him, like an unwed maiden in height and form, so that he would not be afraid when he set eyes on her. Anchises marvelled at her height and beauty and her shining garments: she wore a robe brighter than fire, twisted brooches and shining earrings, lovely necklaces about her soft throat, and her breast shone like the moon. Love seized Anchises, and he said: ''Hail, lady, whichever of the blessed ones you are who come to this house — Artemis, Leto, golden Aphrodite, high-born Themis or bright-eyed Athena? Or one of the Graces, or a nymph of this lovely mountain, its springs and grassy meadows? I will build you an altar on a peak and offer rich sacrifices in every season. Be kind to me: make me a man of note among the Trojans, give me flourishing offspring, and let me live long and happily in the sunlight among the people, and come to old age.''','claim.v0360.hym_aphrodite.06','《荷马颂歌·致阿佛洛狄忒》第75–106行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_hymn5_aphrodite_evelyn_white.7','storyver.greek.hymn5_aphrodite_evelyn_white',7,'弗里吉亚的公主','The Phrygian princess','宙斯的女儿阿佛洛狄忒回答说：安喀塞斯，你是地上的人中最光荣的，我不是女神，你为什么把我比作不死的神？我是一个凡间的女子，生我的是凡间的母亲。我的父亲是著名的俄特柔斯，你也许听说过他的名字，他统治着有坚固城墙的整个弗里吉亚。我也懂你们的语言，就像懂我自己的语言一样，因为在我家里抚养我的是一个特洛伊的保姆，从我小时候起就一直照顾我。可是刚才，持金杖的阿尔戈斯杀手赫尔墨斯把我从金箭的阿耳忒弥斯的舞蹈中抢走了——我们许多仙女和待嫁的少女正在一起跳舞，四周围着数不清的人群。他带着我飞过许多凡人耕种的田地，越过许多没有人分配、没有人耕种的荒野，那里有吃生肉的野兽在阴暗的山谷里出没，我以为我的脚再也碰不到养育生命的大地了。他说，我要被称为安喀塞斯的合法妻子，为你生下光荣的孩子。我恳求你，以宙斯和你高贵的父母的名义，把我这个没有经历过爱情的少女带去见你的父亲、母亲和兄弟们。','Aphrodite, daughter of Zeus, answered: ''Anchises, most glorious of earth-born men, I am no goddess; why do you liken me to the deathless ones? I am mortal, and a mortal woman was my mother. Otreus of famous name is my father, if you have heard of him, who reigns over all well-walled Phrygia. I know your tongue as well as my own, for a Trojan nurse reared me at home and cared for me from when I was a little child. But just now the slayer of Argus with the golden wand snatched me from the dance of Artemis of the golden arrows — many nymphs and marriageable maidens were dancing, and a countless throng stood round us. He carried me over many fields of mortal men and much unallotted, untilled land, where flesh-eating beasts roam the shadowy dells, until I thought my feet would never touch the life-giving earth again. He said I should be called the wedded wife of Anchises and bear you goodly children. I beg you by Zeus and by your noble parents: take me, untried in love, and show me to your father, your mother and your brothers.''','claim.v0360.hym_aphrodite.07','《荷马颂歌·致阿佛洛狄忒》第107–142行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_hymn5_aphrodite_evelyn_white.8','storyver.greek.hymn5_aphrodite_evelyn_white',8,'婚床','The marriage bed','女神说着，把甜蜜的渴望放进了安喀塞斯的心里。爱情攫住了他，他说：如果你是一个凡间的女子，生你的是凡间的母亲，你的父亲是著名的俄特柔斯，你是因为不死的向导赫尔墨斯的意旨来到这里，要被永远称为我的妻子，那么，在我和你结合之前，无论是神还是凡人，谁也不能阻止我，即使远射之神阿波罗亲自从银弓上向我射出痛苦的箭，也不能阻止我。说完，他拉住她的手。爱笑的阿佛洛狄忒转过脸，垂下美丽的眼睛，慢慢地走向那张铺得很好的床。床上铺着柔软的毯子，上面还盖着安喀塞斯亲手在高山上杀死的熊和吼叫的狮子的皮。颂歌只用几行诗说，按照众神的意旨和命运，一个凡人和一位不死的女神同床共枕，而他并不清楚自己做的是什么。到了牧人们把牛羊从开满鲜花的草地赶回羊栏的时候，女神让安喀塞斯沉沉地睡去，自己穿好了华美的衣服。','So speaking, the goddess put sweet desire in Anchises'' heart. Love seized him and he said: ''If you are mortal and a mortal woman bore you, if Otreus of famous name is your father, and you have come here by the will of Hermes the immortal guide, and are to be called my wife for ever, then no one of gods or mortal men shall restrain me until I have lain with you — not even if far-shooting Apollo himself should let fly grievous shafts from his silver bow.'' With that he caught her hand. Laughter-loving Aphrodite turned her face away, her lovely eyes cast down, and crept to the well-spread couch, which was laid with soft coverings and with the skins of bears and deep-roaring lions that Anchises himself had slain on the high mountains. The hymn says only, in a few lines, that by the will of the gods and of destiny a mortal man lay with a deathless goddess, not clearly knowing what he did. At the hour when herdsmen drive their oxen and sheep back to the fold from the flowery pastures, the goddess poured sweet sleep upon Anchises and clothed herself again in her rich garments.','claim.v0360.hym_aphrodite.08','《荷马颂歌·致阿佛洛狄忒》第143–167行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_hymn5_aphrodite_evelyn_white.9','storyver.greek.hymn5_aphrodite_evelyn_white',9,'醒来的恐惧','The fear on waking','女神穿好衣服，站在小屋里，头几乎碰到精工建造的屋顶，脸颊上闪耀着不朽的美，那是戴着美丽花冠的库忒瑞亚才有的美。她叫醒安喀塞斯说：起来吧，达耳达诺斯的子孙，你为什么睡得这样沉？看看我，我还像你刚才第一眼看见的那个样子吗？安喀塞斯一下子醒了过来，看见阿佛洛狄忒的脖子和美丽的眼睛，吓得把目光转到一边，用斗篷蒙住了英俊的脸，恳求她说：女神，我第一眼看见你，就知道你是一位神，可你没有对我说实话。我以持埃癸斯盾的宙斯的名义恳求你，不要让我从此在人间虚弱无力地活着，可怜可怜我吧，因为一个和不死的女神同床的凡人，是不会长久强壮的。安喀塞斯的害怕并非没有道理：在希腊神话里，凡人接近神的身体往往要付出代价。颂歌没有让女神立刻安慰他，而是让她先说出自己的感受：这件事对她来说，同样是一种羞辱。','Clothed again, the goddess stood in the hut, her head reaching to the well-hewn roof-beam, and from her cheeks shone unearthly beauty such as belongs to rich-crowned Cytherea. She roused Anchises: ''Up, son of Dardanus, why sleep so heavily? Look and tell me whether I seem like the one you first saw with your eyes.'' He started from sleep at once, and when he saw Aphrodite''s neck and lovely eyes he was afraid, turned his eyes aside, hid his handsome face in his cloak and implored her: ''Goddess, as soon as I first saw you I knew you were divine, but you did not tell me the truth. By Zeus who holds the aegis I beg you, do not let me live among men a feeble shadow, but have pity on me; for a man who lies with a deathless goddess is not left hale.'' His fear had reason: in Greek myth a mortal who comes close to a god''s body often pays for it. The hymn does not have the goddess comfort him at once; first she speaks of her own feeling — for her, too, this is a humiliation.','claim.v0360.hym_aphrodite.09','《荷马颂歌·致阿佛洛狄忒》第168–190行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_hymn5_aphrodite_evelyn_white.10','storyver.greek.hymn5_aphrodite_evelyn_white',10,'埃涅阿斯与伽倪墨得斯','Aeneas and Ganymede','宙斯的女儿阿佛洛狄忒回答说：安喀塞斯，凡人中最光荣的人，鼓起勇气，不要太害怕。你不用担心会从我或别的有福的神那里受到伤害，因为众神爱你。你会有一个亲生的儿子，他将在特洛伊人中间为王，他的子子孙孙也会一代代地繁衍下去。他的名字要叫埃涅阿斯，因为我曾为一个凡人的床而感到可怕的痛苦。诗人在这里用了一个文字游戏：这个名字被解释为与“可怕”一词相近。女神接着说，在凡人中间，你们家族的人一向最接近众神的模样和容貌。看看伽倪墨得斯吧：深谋远虑的宙斯因为他的美貌把这个金发的少年带走，让他住在众神中间，在宙斯的宫殿里为众神斟酒，这真是一件奇事，所有不死的神都尊敬他，他从金碗里倒出红色的琼浆。他的父亲特洛斯却悲痛欲绝，不知道一阵神风把他心爱的儿子带到了哪里，一天天地为他哭泣。宙斯可怜他，就送给他一群能载着不死的神奔跑的骏马作为补偿，又派向导赫尔墨斯告诉他：他的儿子将像众神一样不死不老。特洛斯听到这个消息，不再悲伤，高兴地驾着那些快如疾风的马车。','Aphrodite, daughter of Zeus, answered: ''Anchises, most glorious of mortal men, take courage and do not fear too much. You need fear no harm from me or the other blessed ones, for you are dear to the gods. You shall have a dear son who will reign among the Trojans, and children''s children will spring up after him continually. His name shall be Aeneas, because I felt awful grief in falling into a mortal''s bed.'' The poet plays on the name, linking it to the word for ''dreadful''. She goes on: of all mortals, men of your race are always nearest to the gods in beauty and stature. ''Consider Ganymede: wise Zeus carried off golden-haired Ganymede for his beauty to live among the deathless ones and pour drink for the gods in Zeus''s house — a wonder to see, honoured by all the immortals as he draws red nectar from the golden bowl. But grief filled the heart of Tros his father, who did not know where the heaven-sent whirlwind had taken his son, and mourned him day after day. Zeus pitied him and gave him as recompense high-stepping horses such as carry the immortals, and sent Hermes to tell him his son would be deathless and unageing like the gods. Then Tros mourned no longer but rejoiced and drove his storm-swift horses.''','claim.v0360.hym_aphrodite.10','《荷马颂歌·致阿佛洛狄忒》第191–217行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_hymn5_aphrodite_evelyn_white.11','storyver.greek.hymn5_aphrodite_evelyn_white',11,'厄俄斯与提托诺斯','Eos and Tithonus','阿佛洛狄忒又讲了另一个特洛伊人的故事。金座的黎明女神厄俄斯也从你们的族人中带走了一个人，那就是像众神一样的提托诺斯。她去请求黑云中的克洛诺斯之子宙斯，让提托诺斯不死，永远活着。宙斯点头答应，实现了她的愿望。可是厄俄斯太傻了，她没有想到还要请求宙斯让他永远年轻，免去可怕的衰老。所以，只要可爱的青春还在提托诺斯身上，他就和早起的、金座的厄俄斯一起住在大地的尽头、大洋的河边，享受着欢乐。可是当最初的白发从他漂亮的头上和高贵的下巴上长出来的时候，厄俄斯就不再和他同床，不过她仍然把他留在自己的宫殿里，用食物和神食养着他，给他华丽的衣服。等到可恨的衰老完全压倒了他，他连四肢都动不了、抬不起来的时候，厄俄斯想出了一个最好的办法：她把他安放在一间屋子里，关上了闪亮的门。他的声音在里面不停地流淌，可他再也没有从前柔软的四肢里的力气了。阿佛洛狄忒说，正因为如此，她不愿意让安喀塞斯也这样永远不死。','Aphrodite told of another Trojan. Golden-throned Eos too carried off one of your race, godlike Tithonus. She went to ask the dark-clouded son of Cronos that he be deathless and live for ever, and Zeus nodded and granted her wish. But Eos was foolish: she did not think to ask youth for him, to strip him of grievous old age. So while sweet youth held him, he lived rapturously with early-born, golden-throned Eos by the streams of Ocean at the ends of the earth. But when the first grey hairs began to ripple from his comely head and noble chin, Eos kept away from his bed, though she still cherished him in her house, nourishing him with food and ambrosia and giving him rich clothing. And when loathsome old age pressed full upon him, and he could not move or lift his limbs, this seemed to her the best counsel: she laid him in a room and shut the shining doors. There his voice flows on endlessly, but there is no strength now in his once-supple limbs. That, Aphrodite says, is why she would not have Anchises be deathless in that way.','claim.v0360.hym_aphrodite.11','《荷马颂歌·致阿佛洛狄忒》第218–238行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_hymn5_aphrodite_evelyn_white.12','storyver.greek.hymn5_aphrodite_evelyn_white',12,'山中的仙女','The nymphs of the mountain','阿佛洛狄忒说：如果你能保持现在的容貌和身材活下去，被称为我的丈夫，悲伤就不会笼罩我的心。可是不久，无情的衰老就会降临到你身上。而我，因为你，将在不死的神中间永远蒙受羞辱：从前众神都怕我的话语和计谋，因为我让他们和凡间的女子结合；现在我再也不能在众神中间开口夸耀了，因为我自己也和一个凡人同了床。至于孩子，等他一见到阳光，深胸的山中仙女们就会抚养他。她们住在这座又大又神圣的山上，既不算凡人，也不算不死的神，她们活得很长，吃神的食物；当她们出生的时候，松树或高大的橡树也一起在大地上长出来，等到她们的死期来临，那些树先枯萎，树皮剥落，枝条落下，仙女的灵魂也就和树一起离开阳光。孩子五岁时，我会把他带来给你。你要说他是一位山中仙女的孩子。如果你说出真相，夸耀自己和戴着美丽花冠的库忒瑞亚同过床，宙斯就会在愤怒中用冒烟的雷电击中你。说完，女神就飞向了多风的天空。诗人向塞浦路斯的女王致意，说他从她开始，现在要转向另一首歌。','Aphrodite said: ''If you could live on as you are now in looks and form, and be called my husband, sorrow would not enfold my heart. But soon pitiless old age will come upon you. And I shall be greatly shamed among the deathless gods for ever because of you: once they feared my words and wiles, by which I joined them with mortal women; now I shall no more open my mouth to boast of this among them, since I myself have lain with a mortal. As for the child, as soon as he sees the light the deep-breasted mountain nymphs who live on this great and holy mountain will rear him. They rank neither with mortals nor with immortals; they live long and eat heavenly food. When they are born, pines or high-topped oaks spring up with them on the earth, and when the fate of death is near, the trees wither, their bark shrivels, their twigs fall, and the nymphs'' souls leave the light together with them. When he is five years old I will bring him to you. Say he is the child of a mountain nymph. If you tell the truth and boast that you lay with rich-crowned Cytherea, Zeus in his anger will smite you with a smoking thunderbolt.'' With that she sped up to windy heaven. The poet bids farewell to the queen of Cyprus: having begun with her, he will turn to another song.','claim.v0360.hym_aphrodite.12','《荷马颂歌·致阿佛洛狄忒》第239–293行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_theogony_titans_children.1','storyver.greek.theogony_titans_children',1,'混沌之子','The children of Chaos','赫西俄德说，最先出现的是混沌卡俄斯，接着是宽胸的大地盖亚，她是住在积雪的奥林波斯山顶上的众神永远稳固的根基；然后是道路宽阔的大地深处幽暗的塔耳塔罗斯；还有爱神厄洛斯，不死的神中最美的一位，他能松弛四肢，征服所有神和人的心智与谋划。在这一切的开端，诗人接着写道：从混沌之中生出了厄瑞玻斯和黑色的夜。厄瑞玻斯是幽暗本身，是大地之下、冥界之上那片无光的黑暗；夜则是覆盖天地的黑色女神。他们不是由谁结合所生，而是直接从混沌里出现的。在《神谱》的世界里，这一对幽暗的兄妹比天空、群山和大海都更早，比所有提坦和奥林波斯众神都更古老。后来诗中说起冥界的入口和亡魂的道路时，常常提到厄瑞玻斯这个名字，把它当作通向冥府的黑暗地带。他是众神谱系中最早的名字之一，却几乎没有自己的故事；他存在的方式，就是作为光明出现之前的那一片黑暗。','Hesiod says that first of all Chaos came to be, and next wide-bosomed Earth, the ever-sure foundation of all the deathless ones who hold the peaks of snowy Olympus; and dim Tartarus in the depth of the wide-pathed Earth; and Eros, fairest among the deathless gods, who unnerves the limbs and overcomes the mind and wise counsels of all gods and men. Then the poet goes on: from Chaos came forth Erebus and black Night. Erebus is darkness itself, the lightless dark beneath the earth and above the underworld; Night is the black goddess who covers heaven and earth. They are not born of any union but come straight out of Chaos. In the world of the Theogony this dark pair is older than heaven, mountains and sea, older than all the Titans and the Olympians. Later Greek poetry often uses the name Erebus for the dark region the dead pass through to Hades. He is one of the first names in the genealogy of the gods, yet he has almost no story of his own; he exists as the darkness before light appeared.','claim.v0360.theog_titans.01','赫西俄德《神谱》第116–123行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_theogony_titans_children.2','storyver.greek.theogony_titans_children',2,'埃忒耳与赫墨拉','Aether and Hemera','接下来，《神谱》说，黑夜和厄瑞玻斯相爱结合，黑夜怀孕，生下了埃忒耳和赫墨拉。埃忒耳是天上明亮清澈的上层空气，是众神呼吸、星辰闪耀的光明高空；赫墨拉是白昼，是白天本身的女神。在这两行诗里，赫西俄德写出了一个奇妙的想法：光明是从黑暗里生出来的。幽暗的厄瑞玻斯和黑色的夜，生下的却是最明亮的空气和白天。古代的读者常常注意到这一点，有人把它看作古希腊人对昼夜交替的诗意解释：每一个白天都是从夜晚中诞生的。埃忒耳和赫墨拉在希腊神话里几乎没有冒险故事，他们更像是世界的组成部分，而不是有个性的人物。可是在《神谱》的秩序中，他们的位置很重要：在天空乌拉诺斯出现之前，在大地生出群山和大海之前，光明和白昼就已经存在了。后面的诗里，赫墨拉还会再次出现：她和母亲黑夜在大地边缘的铜门槛前交替进出，一个出门，另一个就回家。','Next, the Theogony says, Night conceived in love with Erebus and bore Aether and Hemera. Aether is the bright, clear upper air of heaven, the luminous height where the gods breathe and the stars shine; Hemera is Day, the goddess of daylight itself. In these two lines Hesiod sets down a striking thought: light is born of darkness. Dim Erebus and black Night bring forth the brightest air and the day. Ancient readers often noticed this, and some saw in it a poetic account of the alternation of night and day: every day is born of a night. Aether and Hemera have almost no adventures in Greek myth; they are more parts of the world than characters. Yet in the order of the Theogony their place matters: before Heaven appeared, before Earth brought forth mountains and sea, light and day already existed. Hemera will appear again later in the poem, passing in and out at a bronze threshold at the edge of the world with her mother Night — when one goes out, the other comes home.','claim.v0360.theog_titans.02','赫西俄德《神谱》第124–125行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_theogony_titans_children.3','storyver.greek.theogony_titans_children',3,'十二位提坦','The twelve Titans','大地首先生下了和她一样大的繁星点点的天空乌拉诺斯，让他把自己完全覆盖，作为众神永远稳固的居所；她又生下了高高的群山，那是住在山谷里的仙女们喜爱的地方；她还不经过爱情，生下了波涛汹涌的荒凉大海蓬托斯。然后，她与天空同床，生下了深深旋流的俄刻阿诺斯、科俄斯、克利俄斯、许珀里翁、伊阿佩托斯，还有忒亚、瑞亚、忒弥斯、谟涅摩叙涅、戴着金冠的福柏和可爱的忒提斯。在他们之后，最小的孩子出生了，那就是狡猾的克洛诺斯，他最可怕，憎恨他强壮的父亲。这十二个孩子后来被称为提坦。诗人在这里只是一一列出他们的名字，没有讲他们各自的性格。在十二位提坦中，克利俄斯和忒亚在希腊神话里都很少单独出场，人们记得他们，主要是因为他们的子女：忒亚是太阳、月亮和黎明的母亲，克利俄斯是阿斯特赖俄斯、帕拉斯和珀耳塞斯的父亲。下面的几章，就是沿着《神谱》的顺序，去看这些子女们怎样出生。','Earth first bore starry Heaven, equal to herself, to cover her on every side and be an ever-sure abiding place for the blessed gods; she brought forth the long hills, graceful haunts of the nymphs who dwell in the glens; and she bore also the fruitless deep with its raging swell, Pontus, without sweet union of love. Then she lay with Heaven and bore deep-swirling Oceanus, Coeus and Crius and Hyperion and Iapetus, Theia and Rhea, Themis and Mnemosyne, gold-crowned Phoebe and lovely Tethys. After them was born the youngest, wily Cronos, most terrible of her children, who hated his lusty sire. These twelve children were later called the Titans. Here the poet simply lists their names without describing their characters. Among the twelve, Crius and Theia seldom appear alone in Greek myth; they are remembered chiefly for their children — Theia as mother of Sun, Moon and Dawn, Crius as father of Astraeus, Pallas and Perses. The chapters that follow trace, in the order of the Theogony, how those children were born.','claim.v0360.theog_titans.03','赫西俄德《神谱》第126–138行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_theogony_titans_children.4','storyver.greek.theogony_titans_children',4,'夜的子女','The children of Night','在讲完克洛诺斯怎样阉割天空、阿佛洛狄忒怎样从海沫中诞生之后，《神谱》回到了黑夜身上。黑夜生下了可憎的厄运摩罗斯、黑色的命运刻耳和死亡塔纳托斯，又生下了睡眠许普诺斯和梦的一族。诗人特意说明，黑夜生这些孩子时，没有和任何一位神同床，她是独自生下他们的。接着她又生下了讥讽之神、痛苦之神，以及在光荣的大洋对岸看守美丽金苹果的赫斯珀里得斯姐妹；她生下了命运女神们和无情的复仇的刻瑞斯，生下了让凡人痛苦的涅墨西斯，还有欺骗、友爱、可恨的衰老和心肠刚硬的争斗。这一长串名字，几乎包括了人生中所有黑暗的、不可避免的东西。在这些孩子中，睡神和死神是一对兄弟，他们同出于黑夜，后来在诗中也住在一起。赫西俄德把睡眠和死亡放在一起，并不是偶然：在古希腊人看来，睡眠就像一次短暂的死亡，而死亡则是一场不再醒来的长眠。','After telling how Cronos maimed Heaven and how Aphrodite was born of the sea-foam, the Theogony turns back to Night. Night bore hateful Doom (Moros), black Fate (Ker) and Death (Thanatos), and she bore Sleep (Hypnos) and the tribe of Dreams. The poet notes that she bore them without lying with any god; she brought them forth alone. Next she bore Blame and painful Woe, and the Hesperides who guard the fair golden apples beyond glorious Ocean; she bore the Destinies and the ruthless avenging Fates, Nemesis to afflict mortal men, and Deceit and Friendship, hateful Old Age and hard-hearted Strife. This long list includes almost everything dark and inescapable in human life. Among these children, Sleep and Death are brothers, born of the same Night, and later in the poem they also live together. Hesiod does not pair sleep and death by chance: to the Greeks sleep was like a brief death, and death a long sleep from which there is no waking.','claim.v0360.theog_titans.04','赫西俄德《神谱》第211–225行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_theogony_titans_children.5','storyver.greek.theogony_titans_children',5,'海的女儿','The daughter of the Sea','接下来，赫西俄德讲大海蓬托斯的家族。蓬托斯首先生下了诚实而不说谎的涅柔斯，他是大海的长者，被称为“老人”，因为他可靠又温和，从不忘记正义，总是怀着公正和善良的心意。然后，蓬托斯又和大地结合，生下了伟大的陶玛斯、骄傲的福耳库斯、面颊美丽的刻托，以及欧律比亚。诗人只用半行诗来描写欧律比亚：她的胸中有一颗坚如金刚石的心。这句话常被理解为她性格刚强、不可动摇。欧律比亚在希腊神话里几乎没有自己的故事，可她的位置很特别：她是海洋与大地的女儿，后来却嫁给了天空与大地所生的提坦克利俄斯，把海的家族和提坦的家族连在一起。她的兄弟姐妹们各有一大群后代：涅柔斯有五十个海中仙女女儿，陶玛斯是彩虹伊里斯和鹰身女妖哈耳庇厄的父亲，福耳库斯和刻托则生下了格赖埃、戈耳工和许多怪物。相比之下，欧律比亚的孩子们是风、星辰与力量的祖先。','Next Hesiod tells of the family of Pontus, the Sea. Sea first begot Nereus, true and unlying, eldest of his children, whom men call the Old Man because he is trusty and gentle, never forgets the laws of righteousness and has just and kindly thoughts. Then, in union with Earth, Sea begot great Thaumas, proud Phorcys, fair-cheeked Ceto and Eurybia. The poet gives Eurybia only half a line: within her breast is a heart of adamant, of flint. The phrase is often taken to mean that she was hard and unyielding of spirit. Eurybia has almost no story of her own in Greek myth, yet her place is special: a daughter of Sea and Earth, she married the Titan Crius, son of Heaven and Earth, joining the sea family to the Titans. Her siblings each have many descendants: Nereus has fifty sea-nymph daughters, Thaumas fathers Iris the rainbow and the Harpies, and Phorcys and Ceto bring forth the Graeae, the Gorgons and many monsters. Eurybia''s children, by contrast, are ancestors of winds, stars and strength.','claim.v0360.theog_titans.05','赫西俄德《神谱》第233–239行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_theogony_titans_children.6','storyver.greek.theogony_titans_children',6,'太阳、月亮与黎明','Sun, Moon and Dawn','在讲完大洋的女儿们和河流之后，《神谱》说：忒亚和许珀里翁相爱结合，生下了伟大的太阳赫利俄斯、明亮的月亮塞勒涅和黎明厄俄斯——厄俄斯为大地上的一切生灵、也为住在宽阔天空中的不死的神照亮道路。这几行诗把天上三种最重要的光都归于一对提坦夫妻：太阳、月亮和黎明是兄妹。许珀里翁这个名字的意思大概是“高处行走的人”，后来的诗人有时干脆用它来称呼太阳本身。月亮女神塞勒涅在这里只出现在一个名字里，可在其他古代作品中，她有着白色的手臂和闪光的长发，每天夜里驾着马车穿过天空。值得注意的是，别的作品里对他们的家世有不同的说法：《荷马颂歌·致赫利俄斯》说太阳的母亲是欧律法厄萨，《荷马颂歌·致赫尔墨斯》称月亮为“帕拉斯的女儿”。这些说法各自保留在自己的文本里，本篇只讲《神谱》。','After the daughters of Ocean and the rivers, the Theogony says: Theia, subject in love to Hyperion, bore great Helios and clear Selene and Eos, who shines upon all that are on earth and upon the deathless gods who live in wide heaven. These lines give the three chief lights of the sky to one Titan couple: Sun, Moon and Dawn are brothers and sisters. Hyperion''s name probably means ''he who goes on high'', and later poets sometimes used it as a name for the sun itself. Selene the Moon appears here only as a name, but in other ancient works she has white arms and gleaming hair and drives her chariot across the sky every night. Other works give different family lines: the Homeric Hymn to Helios names Euryphaessa as the Sun''s mother, and the Hymn to Hermes calls the Moon ''daughter of Pallas''. These accounts stay in their own texts; this retelling follows only the Theogony.','claim.v0360.theog_titans.06','赫西俄德《神谱》第371–374行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_theogony_titans_children.7','storyver.greek.theogony_titans_children',7,'克利俄斯的儿子们','The sons of Crius','紧接着，《神谱》说：欧律比亚，女神中光彩照人的一位，与克利俄斯相爱结合，生下了伟大的阿斯特赖俄斯、帕拉斯，还有在所有人中以智慧出众的珀耳塞斯。这三个儿子在希腊神话里都不算有名，可是在《神谱》中，他们分别开启了三支重要的家族。阿斯特赖俄斯的名字与“星”有关，他将成为风和星辰的父亲；帕拉斯将与大洋的女儿斯梯克斯结合，生下站在宙斯身边的胜利、力量与暴力；珀耳塞斯则将娶福柏的女儿阿斯忒里亚为妻，生下赫卡忒。诗人特意称赞珀耳塞斯的智慧，这在一份几乎只有名字的家谱里显得格外醒目。需要注意的是，这位提坦珀耳塞斯和太阳神的儿子珀耳塞斯、英雄珀尔修斯的儿子珀耳塞斯都不是同一个人，古代作家用同一个名字称呼了好几位不同的人物。克利俄斯本人在诗中再也没有出场，他像许多提坦一样，最后的命运要到提坦之战时才被提及。','Next the Theogony says: Eurybia, bright among goddesses, was joined in love with Crius and bore great Astraeus, and Pallas, and Perses, who was eminent among all men in wisdom. None of the three is famous in Greek myth, yet in the Theogony each begins an important line. Astraeus, whose name is linked to the stars, will father the winds and the stars; Pallas will unite with Styx, daughter of Ocean, and father Victory, Strength and Force, who stand beside Zeus; and Perses will take Phoebe''s daughter Asteria to wife and father Hecate. The poet singles out Perses'' wisdom, which stands out in a genealogy that is almost all names. Note that this Titan Perses is not the same as Perses son of Helios or Perses son of the hero Perseus; ancient writers gave the same name to several different figures. Crius himself does not appear again in the poem; like many Titans, his fate is touched on only in the war of the Titans.','claim.v0360.theog_titans.07','赫西俄德《神谱》第375–377行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_theogony_titans_children.8','storyver.greek.theogony_titans_children',8,'风与星辰','Winds and stars','接着，诗人说：黎明女神厄俄斯和阿斯特赖俄斯——一位女神和一位神——在爱中结合，生下了心意强壮的风：吹得天空晴朗的西风仄费洛斯、迅猛的北风玻瑞阿斯和南风诺托斯。在这之后，早起的厄俄斯又生下了晨星厄俄斯福洛斯，以及点缀天空的明亮星辰。这是一个优美的画面：每天早晨，黎明在天边升起时，风开始吹动，晨星在最后的夜色中闪耀，群星慢慢隐去——它们都是黎明的孩子。诗人在后面还会说明，这三种风是神所生的、对人有益的风；而那些在海上忽然刮起、让船只沉没的狂风，则来自提丰，不属于神的后代。所以在《神谱》里，西风、北风和南风是有秩序的风，而厄俄斯是它们的母亲。阿斯特赖俄斯这个名字本来就和星辰有关，现在他真的成了群星之父。在这个家族里，提坦的后代第二代已经不再是抽象的名字，而是人们每天都能感受到的风与星光。','Then the poet says: Eos, a goddess, bore to Astraeus, a god, the strong-hearted winds — brightening Zephyrus, swift Boreas and Notus — in union of love. After these the Early-born bore the star Eosphorus, the Light-bringer, and the gleaming stars with which heaven is crowned. It is a lovely picture: each morning, as Dawn rises at the edge of the sky, the winds begin to stir, the morning star shines in the last of the night and the stars slowly fade — all of them Dawn''s children. Later the poet explains that these three winds are god-born and a great blessing to men, while the gusts that suddenly fall on the sea and wreck ships come from Typhoeus and are not of divine birth. So in the Theogony West, North and South Winds are ordered winds, and Eos is their mother. Astraeus''s name was already linked with the stars; now he truly becomes father of the stars. In this line the Titans'' grandchildren are no longer abstract names but the wind and starlight people feel every day.','claim.v0360.theog_titans.08','赫西俄德《神谱》第378–382行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_theogony_titans_children.9','storyver.greek.theogony_titans_children',9,'斯梯克斯的孩子','The children of Styx','大洋的女儿斯梯克斯与帕拉斯结合，在自己的宫殿里生下了泽洛斯和脚踝美丽的尼刻——竞争与胜利，又生下了克拉托斯和比亚——力量与暴力，这两个孩子都非常出色。诗人说，他们没有离开宙斯的房子，也没有离开宙斯的住处；凡是神领着他们去的地方，没有一条路他们不跟着走，他们永远和发出巨雷的宙斯住在一起。在希腊人的想象里，胜利、力量和暴力是王权的随从：宙斯之所以成为众神之王，不仅靠计谋，也靠这些站在他身边的力量。在埃斯库罗斯的悲剧《被缚的普罗米修斯》开场，正是力量与暴力奉宙斯之命，押着普罗米修斯去高加索山。帕拉斯本人在《神谱》中几乎没有别的事迹，他是提坦克利俄斯的儿子，后来的神话里关于他的说法很不一致。在这里，他的意义在于成为这四个孩子的父亲；而这一家人后来的命运，与他们的母亲斯梯克斯在众神之战前做出的选择分不开。','Styx, daughter of Ocean, was joined with Pallas and bore Zelos and trim-ankled Nike — Rivalry and Victory — in the house, and also Kratos and Bia — Strength and Force — wonderful children. These, the poet says, have no house apart from Zeus, nor any dwelling or path except where the god leads them; they dwell always with loud-thundering Zeus. To Greek imagination Victory, Strength and Force are the attendants of kingship: Zeus became king of the gods not only by cunning but with these powers at his side. At the opening of Aeschylus''s Prometheus Bound it is Strength and Force who, at Zeus''s command, lead Prometheus to the Caucasus. Pallas himself has hardly any other deeds in the Theogony; he is a son of the Titan Crius, and later accounts of him vary widely. Here his importance lies in fathering these four children, and the family''s later fortune is bound up with the choice their mother Styx made before the war of the gods.','claim.v0360.theog_titans.09','赫西俄德《神谱》第383–388行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_theogony_titans_children.10','storyver.greek.theogony_titans_children',10,'众神的誓言','The oath of the gods','诗人接着讲斯梯克斯为什么享有这样的荣誉。在宙斯与提坦开战之前，奥林波斯的闪电之神召集了所有不死的神来到高高的奥林波斯山，对他们说：凡是跟我一起与提坦作战的神，我都不会剥夺他的权利，每个人都会保有他从前在不死的神中间享有的职分；而凡是在克洛诺斯统治下没有荣誉、没有权利的神，我会按照正义给他荣誉和权利。不朽的斯梯克斯听从父亲大洋的劝告，第一个带着她的孩子们来到奥林波斯。宙斯尊敬她，给了她丰厚的礼物：他让她成为众神的大誓言，又让她的孩子们永远和他住在一起。宙斯对所有的神都履行了诺言，他自己则大权在握，统治着众神。在《神谱》的后半部分，诗人还会详细描写斯梯克斯的水：众神中若有谁以她的水起誓却违背誓言，就要在一整年里没有呼吸地躺着，九年之内不能参加众神的会议和宴会。连宙斯也不能随意违背以斯梯克斯之水立下的誓言。','The poet then tells why Styx has such honour. Before the war with the Titans, the Olympian lightener called all the deathless gods to high Olympus and said that whoever of the gods would fight with him against the Titans, he would not cast him out of his rights, but each should keep the office he had before among the deathless gods; and whoever had been without honour and rights under Cronos, he would give him honour and rights as is just. Then deathless Styx came first to Olympus with her children, through the counsel of her dear father Ocean. Zeus honoured her and gave her very great gifts: he made her the great oath of the gods, and her children to live with him always. As he promised, so he performed for all, and he himself rules mightily and reigns. Later in the Theogony the poet describes the water of Styx: any god who swears falsely by it lies breathless for a full year and for nine years is cut off from the councils and feasts of the gods. Even Zeus cannot lightly break an oath sworn by the water of Styx.','claim.v0360.theog_titans.10','赫西俄德《神谱》第389–403行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_theogony_titans_children.11','storyver.greek.theogony_titans_children',11,'福柏的女儿们','The daughters of Phoebe','提坦福柏来到科俄斯的爱床，女神在男神的爱中怀孕，生下了穿着深色长袍的勒托。诗人用一连串的词语赞美勒托：她永远温和，对凡人和不死的神都很和善，从一开始就温和，是奥林波斯上最温柔的一位。福柏又生下了名字吉祥的阿斯忒里亚。后来，珀耳塞斯把阿斯忒里亚领进他宏大的家，让她被称为自己亲爱的妻子。这样，两位提坦的孩子结成了夫妻：一位是福柏和科俄斯的女儿，一位是欧律比亚和克利俄斯的儿子。勒托后来成了阿波罗和阿耳忒弥斯的母亲，前面的颂歌已经讲过她在得洛斯的漂泊和生产。阿斯忒里亚的名字与“星”有关，在《神谱》里她只出现在这几行诗中；在后来的作家那里，比如阿波罗多洛斯的《书库》，还说她为了躲避宙斯，化作一只鹌鹑跳进了大海，后来变成了一座岛屿，最初叫阿斯忒里亚，后来改名得洛斯。那是另一个文本的说法，这里只作为背景提到。','Phoebe came to the desired bed of Coeus, and the goddess, conceiving in the love of the god, bore dark-gowned Leto. The poet praises Leto in a string of words: always mild, kind to men and to the deathless gods, mild from the beginning, gentlest in all Olympus. Phoebe also bore Asteria of happy name, whom Perses once led to his great house to be called his dear wife. So the children of two Titan couples were joined: a daughter of Phoebe and Coeus and a son of Eurybia and Crius. Leto became mother of Apollo and Artemis, and the hymn retold earlier tells of her wandering and labour on Delos. Asteria''s name is linked to the stars; in the Theogony she appears only in these lines. Later writers such as Apollodorus in the Library say that she fled Zeus''s advances in the form of a quail, leapt into the sea and became an island, first called Asteria and later Delos. That is another text''s account, mentioned here only as background.','claim.v0360.theog_titans.11','赫西俄德《神谱》第404–411行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_theogony_titans_children.12','storyver.greek.theogony_titans_children',12,'赫卡忒的荣耀','The honour of Hecate','阿斯忒里亚怀了孕，生下了赫卡忒。诗人用了几十行诗来赞美她：克洛诺斯之子宙斯尊敬赫卡忒胜过一切，给了她光荣的礼物，让她在大地上、在荒凉的大海里都有一份，在繁星的天空中也享有尊荣，在不死的神中间最受尊敬。直到今天，大地上的人每当按照习俗献上丰盛的祭品、祈求恩惠时，都会呼唤赫卡忒的名字。女神若欣然接受谁的祈祷，谁就会得到极大的荣誉，因为她有这样的力量。在审判时，她坐在可敬的国王身边；在集会上，她让她愿意的人出人头地；在战争中，她站在她愿意的人一边，给他们胜利和荣耀；运动会上，她帮助竞技的人；在海上，她和波塞冬一起，让渔夫捕获大量的鱼，也能随意让鱼消失；在牛棚里，她和赫尔墨斯一起让牛羊增多。虽然她是母亲唯一的孩子，宙斯却在所有的神中尊崇她，让她成为年轻人的养育者。诗人对赫卡忒的这段赞美，比他对许多大神的描写都长，学者们至今还在讨论原因。','Asteria conceived and bore Hecate, and the poet praises her at length: Zeus the son of Cronos honoured her above all and gave her splendid gifts, a share of the earth and the unfruitful sea, and honour in starry heaven, and she is honoured most of all by the deathless gods. To this day, whenever any man on earth offers rich sacrifices and prays for favour according to custom, he calls upon Hecate. Great honour comes easily to him whose prayers the goddess receives favourably, for she has the power. She sits by worshipful kings in judgement; in the assembly whoever she wills is distinguished; in war she stands by whom she will and grants victory and glory; she helps men contending in the games; on the sea, with Poseidon, she gives fishermen a great catch or easily takes it away; in the byres, with Hermes, she increases the flocks. Though she is her mother''s only child, Zeus honoured her among all the deathless ones and made her nurse of the young. This praise of Hecate is longer than the poet''s account of many greater gods, and scholars still debate why.','claim.v0360.theog_titans.12','赫西俄德《神谱》第411–452行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_theogony_titans_children.13','storyver.greek.theogony_titans_children',13,'夜与昼的门槛','The threshold of Night and Day','在描写宙斯战胜提坦、把他们关进塔耳塔罗斯之后，诗人带领听众来到世界的边缘。在那里，伊阿佩托斯的儿子阿特拉斯站着，用头和不知疲倦的双手撑住宽阔的天空；就在那里，黑夜和白昼彼此走近，在巨大的铜门槛前互相问候：一个走进屋里，另一个就走出门外。这座房子从来不同时容纳她们两个，总是一个在屋外，在大地上行走，另一个在屋里等候，直到她出行的时刻到来。白昼为大地上的人们带来明亮的光；黑夜则怀里抱着睡眠——死亡的兄弟——她是笼罩在云雾中的致命的黑夜。这是《神谱》中最美的画面之一：赫墨拉和她的母亲黑夜从不相聚，永远在门槛上擦肩而过。诗人用一座房子和一道门槛，把每天的昼夜交替写成了两位女神之间永不停止的交接。前面讲过，赫墨拉是黑夜和厄瑞玻斯的女儿；在这里，她已经成为每天出门照亮人间的那一位。','After describing how Zeus overcame the Titans and shut them in Tartarus, the poet leads his hearers to the edge of the world. There Atlas, son of Iapetus, stands and holds up the wide heaven on his head and unwearying hands; and there Night and Day draw near and greet one another as they pass the great threshold of bronze: one is about to go down into the house while the other comes out at the door. The house never holds them both within; always one is outside the house passing over the earth, while the other stays at home and waits until the time for her journey comes. Day holds all-seeing light for men on earth; Night holds in her arms Sleep, the brother of Death — deadly Night, wrapped in a cloud of mist. This is one of the loveliest images in the Theogony: Hemera and her mother Night never meet but pass each other for ever on the threshold. With a house and a doorstep the poet turns the daily turn of day and night into an endless handing-over between two goddesses. Hemera, daughter of Night and Erebus, is here the one who goes out each day to light the world.','claim.v0360.theog_titans.13','赫西俄德《神谱》第744–757行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_theogony_titans_children.14','storyver.greek.theogony_titans_children',14,'睡与死的住所','The house of Sleep and Death','就在那里，幽暗黑夜的孩子们也有自己的住所：睡眠和死亡，两位可怕的神。闪耀的太阳无论升上天空还是从天空落下，都从不用他的光线照看他们。两兄弟中，睡眠安静地在大地和宽阔的大海上漫游，对人们温和可亲；死亡却有一颗铁打的心，胸中的灵魂像铜一样无情：凡是被他抓住的人，他就牢牢地抓住不放，他甚至被不死的神们所憎恨。赫西俄德只用了几行诗，就把这一对兄弟的性格写得截然不同：同是黑夜的孩子，一个带来休息和安慰，一个带来无法挽回的终结。在荷马史诗《伊利亚特》里，这对兄弟一起把战死的英雄萨耳珀冬的遗体抬回他的故乡吕基亚；在后来的希腊瓶画上，他们常被画成两个长着翅膀的青年，一左一右地托着阵亡的战士。可在《神谱》里，他们只是住在世界边缘、太阳照不到的地方，一个出门去安抚人间，一个出门去带走生命。','There too the children of dark Night have their dwellings, Sleep and Death, awful gods. The glowing Sun never looks upon them with his beams, neither as he goes up into heaven nor as he comes down from heaven. Of the two, Sleep roams peacefully over the earth and the sea''s broad back and is kindly to men; but Death has a heart of iron, and his spirit within him is pitiless as bronze: whomsoever of men he has once seized he holds fast, and he is hateful even to the deathless gods. In a few lines Hesiod gives the two brothers opposite natures: both children of Night, one brings rest and comfort, the other an end that cannot be undone. In Homer''s Iliad the pair carry the body of the fallen hero Sarpedon home to Lycia, and on later Greek vases they are often painted as two winged young men lifting a dead warrior between them. In the Theogony, though, they simply live at the edge of the world where the sun never shines — one going out to soothe mankind, the other to take life away.','claim.v0360.theog_titans.14','赫西俄德《神谱》第758–766行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_theogony_titans_children.15','storyver.greek.theogony_titans_children',15,'厄俄斯的儿子们','The sons of Eos','《神谱》快结束时，诗人列出了女神与凡间男子所生的孩子。其中说：厄俄斯为提托诺斯生下了头戴青铜盔的门农，埃塞俄比亚人的国王，还有厄玛提翁王。她又为刻法罗斯生下了一个光荣的儿子，强壮的法厄同，一个像神一样的人。当他还是一个温柔的少年，正处在光荣青春的花期，童心未泯的时候，爱笑的阿佛洛狄忒把他抢走，让他在她神圣的神庙里做夜间的守护者，成为一位神灵。这几行诗让黎明女神的故事有了一个结尾。在前面，她是风与星辰的母亲；在这里，她是凡间英雄的母亲。门农后来在特洛伊战争中帮助特洛伊人，被阿喀琉斯杀死，后世的诗人说，黎明的露水就是厄俄斯为儿子流下的眼泪。而提托诺斯的命运——得到了不死却没有得到青春——则在《荷马颂歌·致阿佛洛狄忒》中讲得最为详细。厄俄斯一次又一次地爱上凡人，又一次次地失去他们，这或许正是黎明短暂的写照。','Near the end of the Theogony the poet lists the children goddesses bore to mortal men. Among them: Eos bore to Tithonus brazen-crested Memnon, king of the Ethiopians, and the Lord Emathion. And to Cephalus she bore a splendid son, strong Phaethon, a man like the gods, whom, while he was a tender boy in the flower of glorious youth with childish thoughts, laughter-loving Aphrodite seized and caught away, and made a keeper of her shrine by night, a divine spirit. These lines give the Dawn goddess''s story an ending. Earlier she was mother of the winds and stars; here she is mother of mortal heroes. Memnon later helped the Trojans in the Trojan War and was slain by Achilles, and later poets said the morning dew is the tears Eos weeps for her son. Tithonus''s fate — given deathlessness but not youth — is told most fully in the Homeric Hymn to Aphrodite. Again and again Eos loves mortals and loses them, perhaps a picture of how brief the dawn is.','claim.v0360.theog_titans.15','赫西俄德《神谱》第984–991行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_library_artemis.1','storyver.greek.library_artemis',1,'勒托的孪生子女','Leto''s twins','《书库》在讲提坦的子女时说，科俄斯的女儿阿斯忒里亚为了躲避宙斯，化作一只鹌鹑跳进大海，那座城市先是叫阿斯忒里亚，后来叫得洛斯。她的姐姐勒托因为和宙斯相爱，被赫拉追逐，走遍了整个大地，最后来到得洛斯，先生下了阿耳忒弥斯，然后在阿耳忒弥斯的接生帮助下，才生下了阿波罗。这个说法与《荷马颂歌·致阿波罗》不同：颂歌里阿耳忒弥斯生在俄耳堤癸亚，生育女神厄勒梯亚来到得洛斯后阿波罗才出生；而在《书库》里，刚出生的阿耳忒弥斯自己就做了接生者，这也许正是她后来被看作守护分娩的女神的原因之一。书中接着说：阿耳忒弥斯一心打猎，始终保持处女之身；阿波罗则跟潘学会了预言，来到德尔斐，杀死了守护神谕所的皮同。短短几句，就给这对孪生姐弟分好了各自的领域：姐姐在山林里奔跑，弟弟在神庙里传达宙斯的旨意。','Telling of the Titans'' children, the Library says that Asteria, daughter of Coeus, fled Zeus in the likeness of a quail and flung herself into the sea, and a city was called Asteria after her and later Delos. Her sister Leto, for her love with Zeus, was hunted by Hera over the whole earth, until she came to Delos and brought forth first Artemis, by whose help as midwife she afterwards gave birth to Apollo. This differs from the Homeric Hymn to Apollo, where Artemis is born in Ortygia and Apollo only after Eileithyia comes to Delos; in the Library the newborn Artemis herself acts as midwife, which may be one reason she was later seen as a guardian of childbirth. The book goes on: Artemis devoted herself to the chase and remained a maiden, while Apollo learned prophecy from Pan, came to Delphi and slew the Python that guarded the oracle. In a few sentences the twins receive their domains — the sister running in the woods and mountains, the brother declaring Zeus''s will in the temple.','claim.v0360.lib_artemis.01','阿波罗多洛斯《书库》1.4.1。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_library_artemis.2','storyver.greek.library_artemis',2,'提堤俄斯','Tityus','同一节里，《书库》讲了一件姐弟俩为母亲报仇的事。宙斯曾与俄耳科墨诺斯的女儿厄拉瑞结合，因为害怕赫拉，把她藏到了地下，她在地下生下了一个身躯巨大的儿子提堤俄斯，所以人们说他是大地所生。有一次，勒托来到皮托，提堤俄斯看见了她，被情欲冲昏了头脑，竟想把她拉到自己身边。勒托呼唤她的孩子们来救她，阿耳忒弥斯和阿波罗立刻赶到，用箭把他射倒。《书库》说，提堤俄斯死后还要受罚：在冥府里，秃鹫一直啄食他的心。荷马的《奥德赛》里也说，奥德修斯在冥府看见提堤俄斯巨大的身体躺在地上，两只秃鹫啄食他的肝脏，因为他曾侵犯宙斯光荣的妻子勒托。在这个故事里，阿耳忒弥斯并不是独自行动的女猎手，而是与弟弟并肩、保护母亲的女儿；她的箭第一次射向的，是一个冒犯了勒托的巨人。后来尼俄柏的故事里，姐弟俩也将再一次为母亲的荣誉张弓。','In the same section the Library tells how the twins avenged their mother. Zeus had lain with Elare, daughter of Orchomenus, and for fear of Hera hid her under the earth; there she bore a son of monstrous size, Tityus, so that he is said to be earth-born. Once, when Leto came to Pytho, Tityus saw her and, overcome by lust, tried to drag her to him. Leto called her children to her aid, and Artemis and Apollo came and shot him down with their arrows. The Library adds that Tityus is punished even after death: in Hades vultures eat his heart. Homer''s Odyssey likewise says that Odysseus saw in the underworld the huge body of Tityus stretched on the ground while two vultures tore at his liver, because he had assaulted Leto, the glorious consort of Zeus. In this tale Artemis is not a lone huntress but a daughter fighting beside her brother to protect her mother; the first target of her arrows is a giant who has wronged Leto. In the story of Niobe the twins will draw their bows again for their mother''s honour.','claim.v0360.lib_artemis.02','阿波罗多洛斯《书库》1.4.1。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_library_artemis.3','storyver.greek.library_artemis',3,'俄里翁','Orion','《书库》说：阿耳忒弥斯在得洛斯杀死了俄里翁。据说俄里翁身材巨大，是大地所生；斐瑞库得斯却说他是波塞冬和欧律阿勒的儿子，波塞冬赐给他在海面上行走的本领。他先娶了西得，赫拉因为西得和她比美，把她扔进了冥府。后来他到希俄斯岛向国王俄诺皮翁的女儿墨洛珀求婚，俄诺皮翁把他灌醉，趁他睡着时弄瞎了他的眼睛，把他扔在海滩上。他走到赫菲斯托斯的铁匠铺，抓起一个少年放在肩上，让少年领他走向日出的地方，在那里，阳光治好了他的眼睛。这时黎明女神厄俄斯爱上了俄里翁，把他带到得洛斯——阿佛洛狄忒让她永远陷于爱情之中，因为她曾和阿瑞斯同床。至于俄里翁的死，《书库》并列了两种说法：有人说他向阿耳忒弥斯挑战掷铁饼，被她杀死；也有人说，他侵犯了从北方净土来的少女俄庇斯，被阿耳忒弥斯射死。书中没有说哪一种说法更对。','The Library says: Artemis slew Orion in Delos. He is said to have been of gigantic stature and born of the earth; but Pherecydes says he was a son of Poseidon and Euryale, and Poseidon gave him the power to stride across the sea. He first married Side, whom Hera cast into Hades because she vied with her in beauty. Afterwards he went to Chios and wooed Merope, daughter of King Oenopion; but Oenopion made him drunk, put out his eyes as he slept and cast him on the beach. He went to the smithy of Hephaestus, snatched up a lad, set him on his shoulders and bade him lead him to the sunrise, where the sun''s rays healed him. Then Dawn fell in love with Orion and carried him off to Delos — for Aphrodite had made her perpetually in love, because she had lain with Ares. For Orion''s death the Library gives two accounts side by side: some say Artemis killed him for challenging her at quoits; others that she shot him for assaulting Opis, one of the maidens who had come from the Hyperboreans. The book does not say which is true.','claim.v0360.lib_artemis.03','阿波罗多洛斯《书库》1.4.3–1.4.5。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_library_artemis.4','storyver.greek.library_artemis',4,'阿洛伊代兄弟','The Aloadae','《书库》第一卷还讲了一对巨人兄弟，俄托斯和厄菲阿尔忒斯，人称阿洛伊代兄弟。他们名义上是阿洛欧斯的儿子，实际上是波塞冬的儿子。他们每年宽长一肘、高长一寻，九岁时已经宽九肘、高九寻。他们决心与众神作战，把俄萨山堆在奥林波斯山上，又把珀利翁山堆在俄萨山上，扬言要用这些山把海填平，变成陆地，再把陆地变成海。厄菲阿尔忒斯追求赫拉，俄托斯追求阿耳忒弥斯，他们还把战神阿瑞斯捆了起来，后来是赫尔墨斯把阿瑞斯偷偷救走的。最后，阿耳忒弥斯在纳克索斯岛用计杀死了这对兄弟：她变成一头鹿，从两人中间跳过去，兄弟俩一心想射中猎物，同时向鹿投出标枪，结果刺中了对方。这是一个很能表现阿耳忒弥斯性格的故事：她不和巨人硬拼，而是用猎手最熟悉的东西——一头奔跑的鹿——让傲慢的猎人死在自己的手里。对一位狩猎女神来说，这样的结局恰如其分。','In its first book the Library also tells of two giant brothers, Otus and Ephialtes, called the Aloadae — nominally sons of Aloeus, but in truth sons of Poseidon. Each year they grew a cubit in breadth and a fathom in height, so that at nine years old they were nine cubits broad and nine fathoms tall. They resolved to fight the gods, set Ossa on Olympus and Pelion on Ossa, and threatened to fill up the sea with mountains to make it dry land, and to make the land sea. Ephialtes wooed Hera and Otus wooed Artemis; they even put Ares in bonds, though Hermes stole him away. In the end Artemis killed the Aloadae in Naxos by a trick: she changed herself into a deer and leapt between them, and in their eagerness to hit the quarry they threw their darts at each other. The tale shows Artemis''s nature well: she does not meet the giants with force but uses the thing a hunter knows best — a running deer — so that the arrogant hunters die by their own hands. For a goddess of the hunt, it is a fitting end.','claim.v0360.lib_artemis.04','阿波罗多洛斯《书库》1.7.4。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_library_artemis.5','storyver.greek.library_artemis',5,'卡吕冬的野猪','The Calydonian boar','埃托利亚的卡吕冬国王俄纽斯在一年的收成之后，把最早的果实献给所有的神，唯独忘了阿耳忒弥斯。女神大怒，派来一头身躯庞大、力大无比的野猪。这头野猪让田地无法播种，毁掉了牛羊，伤害了遇见它的人。俄纽斯的儿子墨勒阿革洛斯召集了全希腊最出色的英雄来猎杀它，前来的有卡斯托耳和波吕丢刻斯、伊阿宋、忒修斯、珀琉斯、安菲阿剌俄斯，还有女猎手阿塔兰忒。英雄们在俄纽斯家里宴饮了九天，有些人不愿与一个女子一同打猎，墨勒阿革洛斯却坚持让她参加。猎猪时，有几位英雄被野猪杀死，阿塔兰忒第一个射中野猪的背，最后墨勒阿革洛斯刺中它的腹部，把它杀死。他把猪皮送给阿塔兰忒，引起舅舅们的不满，争执中他杀了舅舅，最终自己也因此丧命。这场流血的猎猪和随后的悲剧，起因都只是一次被遗忘的献祭。在《书库》里，阿耳忒弥斯常常这样出现：她不必亲自到场，被冒犯的愤怒本身就会带来灾难。','Oeneus, king of Calydon in Aetolia, offering the first-fruits of the year''s crops to all the gods, forgot Artemis alone. In her wrath she sent a boar of extraordinary size and strength, which kept the land from being sown and destroyed the cattle and the people it met. Oeneus''s son Meleager called together the best men of Greece to hunt it — among them Castor and Polydeuces, Jason, Theseus, Peleus and Amphiaraus, and the huntress Atalanta. The heroes feasted nine days in Oeneus''s house; some refused to hunt with a woman, but Meleager insisted that she come. In the hunt several heroes were killed by the boar; Atalanta was first to shoot it in the back, and Meleager finally stabbed it in the flank and killed it. He gave the skin to Atalanta, which angered his uncles; in the quarrel he slew them, and in the end lost his own life because of it. The bloody hunt and the tragedy after it all began with a single forgotten sacrifice. In the Library Artemis often appears this way: she need not come in person — her offended wrath itself brings disaster.','claim.v0360.lib_artemis.05','阿波罗多洛斯《书库》1.8.2。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_library_artemis.6','storyver.greek.library_artemis',6,'阿德墨托斯的婚房','Admetus''s bridal chamber','斐赖国王阿德墨托斯向珀利阿斯的女儿阿尔刻提斯求婚。珀利阿斯许诺，谁能把一头狮子和一头野猪套在同一辆车上，就把女儿嫁给谁。阿波罗曾因杀死独眼巨人而被宙斯罚为凡人服役，那时正在阿德墨托斯家里放牛，他帮阿德墨托斯套好了狮子和野猪，阿德墨托斯赢得了阿尔刻提斯。可是在婚礼上献祭时，阿德墨托斯忘了给阿耳忒弥斯献祭。等他打开婚房的门，发现房里满是盘成一团团的蛇。阿波罗让他去平息女神的怒气，又向命运女神们求得一个恩典：当阿德墨托斯死期来临时，如果有人自愿替他去死，他就可以免死。后来死期到了，他的父母都不愿替他死，只有阿尔刻提斯替他死去；冥后科瑞又把她送回了人间，也有人说是赫拉克勒斯与冥王搏斗，把她带了回来。这个故事的中心是阿尔刻提斯的爱，可它的起点却又是阿耳忒弥斯：一位被遗忘的女神，在新婚之夜用满屋的蛇提醒凡人，她是不能被忽略的。','Admetus, king of Pherae, wooed Alcestis, daughter of Pelias. Pelias promised his daughter to whoever yoked a lion and a boar to one car. Apollo, condemned by Zeus to serve a mortal for slaying the Cyclopes, was then herding Admetus''s cattle; he yoked the lion and boar for him, and Admetus won Alcestis. But in offering sacrifice at the marriage he forgot to sacrifice to Artemis, and when he opened the bridal chamber he found it full of coiled snakes. Apollo bade him appease the goddess, and won from the Fates a favour: when Admetus was about to die, he might be released from death if someone would choose to die for him. When his day came, neither his father nor his mother would die for him, but Alcestis died in his stead; Kore sent her back up again, or, as some say, Heracles fought with Hades and brought her back. The heart of the tale is Alcestis''s love, yet its beginning is again Artemis: a forgotten goddess who on the wedding night reminds a mortal with a chamber full of snakes that she is not to be overlooked.','claim.v0360.lib_artemis.06','阿波罗多洛斯《书库》1.9.15。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_library_artemis.7','storyver.greek.library_artemis',7,'刻律涅亚的牝鹿','The Ceryneian hind','《书库》第二卷讲赫拉克勒斯的十二项任务。第三项任务是把刻律涅亚的牝鹿活着带回迈锡尼。这头鹿住在俄诺厄，长着金色的角，是阿耳忒弥斯的圣物。赫拉克勒斯既不想杀死它，也不想伤害它，就追了它整整一年。最后，鹿跑累了，逃到阿耳忒弥西昂山，又从那里跑向拉冬河，就在它要渡河的时候，赫拉克勒斯一箭射中了它，把它抓住扛在肩上，匆匆穿过阿卡狄亚。可是阿耳忒弥斯和阿波罗迎面拦住了他，要把鹿夺回去，阿耳忒弥斯还责备他竟敢伤害自己的圣兽。赫拉克勒斯说这是不得已的事，把责任推到欧律斯透斯身上，这才平息了女神的怒气，把那头鹿活着带到了迈锡尼。这是全书中少有的一幕：最强大的英雄在一位女神面前低头认错。阿耳忒弥斯在这里既是狩猎女神，又是野兽的保护者——她允许人打猎，却不允许人随意伤害属于她的动物。','The Library''s second book tells the labours of Heracles. The third was to bring the Ceryneian hind alive to Mycenae. The hind lived at Oenoe, had golden horns and was sacred to Artemis; so, wishing neither to kill nor wound it, Heracles hunted it a whole year. At last, weary of the chase, the beast fled to the mountain called Artemisius and thence to the river Ladon, and just as it was about to cross the stream Heracles shot it, caught it, put it on his shoulders and hurried through Arcadia. But Artemis and Apollo met him and would have wrested the hind from him, and Artemis rebuked him for trying to kill her sacred animal. By pleading necessity and laying the blame on Eurystheus, he appeased the goddess''s anger and carried the beast alive to Mycenae. It is a rare scene in the book: the strongest of heroes bowing before a goddess and excusing himself. Here Artemis is both goddess of the hunt and protector of wild things — she allows hunting, but not the careless harming of the animals that belong to her.','claim.v0360.lib_artemis.07','阿波罗多洛斯《书库》2.5.3。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_library_artemis.8','storyver.greek.library_artemis',8,'阿克泰翁','Actaeon','《书库》第三卷讲忒拜的故事。卡德摩斯的女儿奥托诺厄与阿里斯泰俄斯生下一个儿子，名叫阿克泰翁。他由马人喀戎抚养长大，学成了一个出色的猎人。后来，他在喀泰戎山上被自己的猎狗吞食。关于他为什么这样死去，《书库》给出了两种说法：据阿库西拉俄斯说，是因为他向塞墨勒求爱，惹怒了宙斯；但更普遍的说法是，他看见了正在沐浴的阿耳忒弥斯。人们说，女神立刻把他变成一头鹿，又让他带着的五十条猎狗发狂，猎狗们没有认出自己的主人，把他吃掉了。阿克泰翁死后，猎狗们四处寻找主人，一路哀嚎，最后来到喀戎的山洞，喀戎为它们做了一尊阿克泰翁的像，这才平息了它们的悲伤。《书库》还记下了这些猎狗的名字。这个故事后来成了西方艺术中最常见的题材之一：一个猎人因为看见了不该看的东西，变成了自己追逐的猎物。','The Library''s third book tells the stories of Thebes. Autonoe, daughter of Cadmus, bore to Aristaeus a son Actaeon, who was raised by the centaur Chiron and became a hunter. Later he was devoured on Cithaeron by his own dogs. Why he perished so, the Library gives two accounts: according to Acusilaus, Zeus was angry because he wooed Semele; but according to the more common view, it was because he saw Artemis bathing. They say the goddess at once turned him into a deer and drove mad the fifty dogs of his pack, which, not knowing their master, devoured him. When Actaeon was dead, the dogs sought their master, howling, and came at last to Chiron''s cave, where Chiron made an image of Actaeon that soothed their grief. The Library even records the dogs'' names. The tale became one of the most common subjects in Western art: a hunter who saw what he should not see and became the quarry he once pursued.','claim.v0360.lib_artemis.08','阿波罗多洛斯《书库》3.4.4。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_library_artemis.9','storyver.greek.library_artemis',9,'尼俄柏','Niobe','忒拜王安菲翁娶了坦塔罗斯的女儿尼俄柏，生了七个儿子和七个女儿——《书库》还注明，赫西俄德说他们有十个儿子十个女儿，荷马说有六个儿子六个女儿。尼俄柏因为儿女众多，竟说自己的子女比勒托的还要好。勒托被这句话刺痛了，就激起阿耳忒弥斯和阿波罗去惩罚他们。阿耳忒弥斯在家中射死了尼俄柏的女儿们，阿波罗则在喀泰戎山上射死了正在打猎的儿子们。儿子中只有安菲翁活了下来，女儿中只有年长的克罗里斯活了下来，后来她嫁给了涅琉斯。尼俄柏离开忒拜，回到西皮洛斯山她父亲坦塔罗斯那里，向宙斯祈祷后变成了一块石头，泪水日日夜夜从石头上流下来。这是姐弟俩第二次为母亲出手。与提堤俄斯的暴行不同，尼俄柏犯的只是一句骄傲的话，可是在希腊神话里，凡人把自己和神相比，本身就是一种僭越。阿耳忒弥斯的箭，在这里成了神与人之间界限的守护者。','Amphion, king of Thebes, married Niobe, daughter of Tantalus, who bore seven sons and seven daughters — the Library notes that Hesiod gives ten of each and Homer six of each. Being blessed with children, Niobe said she was more blessed in children than Leto. Stung by the taunt, Leto stirred up Artemis and Apollo against them: Artemis shot down the daughters in the house, and Apollo killed the sons together as they were hunting on Cithaeron. Of the sons only Amphion was saved, and of the daughters only Chloris, the elder, whom Neleus later married. Niobe left Thebes and went to her father Tantalus at Sipylus, and there, praying to Zeus, she was turned into a stone, and tears flow from the stone night and day. This is the second time the twins act for their mother. Unlike Tityus''s violence, Niobe''s offence was only a proud word; but in Greek myth a mortal who compares herself to the gods already oversteps. Here Artemis''s arrows guard the boundary between gods and men.','claim.v0360.lib_artemis.09','阿波罗多洛斯《书库》3.5.6。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_library_artemis.10','storyver.greek.library_artemis',10,'卡利斯托','Callisto','《书库》讲阿卡狄亚的谱系时，提到了卡利斯托。关于她的出身，说法很多：欧墨洛斯等人说她是吕卡翁的女儿，赫西俄德说她是一位仙女，阿西俄斯说她是倪克透斯的女儿，斐瑞库得斯说她是刻透斯的女儿。她是阿耳忒弥斯打猎时的同伴，穿着和女神一样的衣服，向女神发誓永远保持处女之身。宙斯爱上了她——有人说他变成了阿耳忒弥斯的模样，也有人说变成了阿波罗的模样——违背她的意愿与她同床。为了不让赫拉发现，宙斯把她变成了一头熊。可是赫拉说服阿耳忒弥斯，把她当作野兽射死了；也有人说，阿耳忒弥斯射死她，是因为她没有守住处女之身。卡利斯托死后，宙斯抢出她腹中的婴儿，取名阿尔卡斯，交给迈亚在阿卡狄亚抚养；又把卡利斯托变成星星，称为大熊座。这个故事里，阿耳忒弥斯与她最亲近的同伴之间，隔着一个被打破的誓言，《书库》对此只是平静地记下两种说法。','Recounting the Arcadian line, the Library tells of Callisto. Accounts of her parentage vary: Eumelus and others say she was Lycaon''s daughter, Hesiod that she was a nymph, Asius that she was the daughter of Nycteus, Pherecydes that she was the daughter of Ceteus. She was Artemis''s companion in the chase, wore the same garb and swore to her to remain a maiden. Zeus loved her and — taking the likeness, some say, of Artemis, others of Apollo — lay with her against her will; to escape Hera''s notice he turned her into a bear. But Hera persuaded Artemis to shoot her down as a wild beast; some say, however, that Artemis shot her because she had not kept her maidenhood. When Callisto perished, Zeus snatched the babe, named him Arcas and gave him to Maia to rear in Arcadia; and Callisto he turned into a star and called the Bear. Between Artemis and her closest companion stands a broken vow, and the Library calmly records both explanations.','claim.v0360.lib_artemis.10','阿波罗多洛斯《书库》3.8.2。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_library_artemis.11','storyver.greek.library_artemis',11,'奥里斯的风','The winds at Aulis','《书库》的《摘要》部分讲特洛伊战争。希腊大军第二次在奥里斯集结时，舰队因为没有风而无法出航。预言者卡尔卡斯说，除非把阿伽门农最美丽的女儿献祭给阿耳忒弥斯，否则他们无法出航，因为女神对阿伽门农很生气。《摘要》给出了两个原因：一是阿伽门农射中一头鹿时夸口说，连阿耳忒弥斯自己也射不了这么好；二是他的父亲阿特柔斯从前没有把那只金羊羔献给女神。这又是一个凡人冒犯女神的故事，与卡吕冬的野猪和阿德墨托斯的婚房一样，起因都很小：一句夸口的话，一次没有兑现的献祭。可这一次，阿耳忒弥斯的愤怒落在了整支军队身上，成千上万的人被困在海边，眼看着出征的希望一天天落空。阿伽门农得到神谕后，面临着作为统帅和作为父亲之间最艰难的选择。在希腊悲剧里，这个时刻成了埃斯库罗斯和欧里庇得斯反复书写的题材。','The Epitome of the Library tells of the Trojan War. When the Greek host mustered a second time at Aulis, the fleet could not sail for want of wind. The seer Calchas said they could not sail unless the fairest of Agamemnon''s daughters was sacrificed to Artemis, for the goddess was angry with Agamemnon. The Epitome gives two reasons: first, that when Agamemnon shot a deer he boasted that not even Artemis could have done better; second, that his father Atreus had not sacrificed the golden lamb to her. It is another tale of a mortal offending the goddess, and like the Calydonian boar and Admetus''s bridal chamber its cause is small — a boastful word, an unpaid offering. But this time Artemis''s anger falls on a whole army, thousands held on the shore, watching their hope of sailing fade day by day. When the oracle came, Agamemnon faced the hardest choice between commander and father. In Greek tragedy this moment became a subject Aeschylus and Euripides returned to again and again.','claim.v0360.lib_artemis.11','阿波罗多洛斯《书库·摘要》3.21。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_library_artemis.12','storyver.greek.library_artemis',12,'伊菲革涅亚','Iphigenia','阿伽门农接到神谕后，派奥德修斯和传令官塔尔堤比俄斯去见妻子克吕泰涅斯特拉，向她要伊菲革涅亚，假说他答应把女儿嫁给阿喀琉斯，作为对阿喀琉斯参战的酬谢。克吕泰涅斯特拉把女儿送来了。阿伽门农让她站在祭坛旁边，正要把她献祭的时候，阿耳忒弥斯把她带走，送到了陶里斯人那里，让她做自己的女祭司，又在祭坛上放了一头鹿代替她；也有人说，阿耳忒弥斯让她成了不死的神。这个结局让阿耳忒弥斯的形象变得复杂：她是要求献祭的女神，却又在最后一刻救下了被献祭的少女。在其他作家那里，这个故事有更黑暗的版本，伊菲革涅亚真的死在祭坛上，成了克吕泰涅斯特拉日后杀夫的原因之一。《书库》选择了获救的说法，并且像往常一样，另记下了一种说法。从得洛斯的出生到奥里斯的祭坛，《书库》中的阿耳忒弥斯始终是同一位女神：她守护母亲的荣誉、野兽的安全和少女的誓言，也决不容忍凡人的傲慢。','On receiving the oracle Agamemnon sent Odysseus and the herald Talthybius to Clytemnestra to ask for Iphigenia, pretending he had promised to give her to Achilles to wife as a reward for his service. Clytemnestra sent her. Agamemnon set her beside the altar and was about to slaughter her, when Artemis snatched her away to the Taurians and appointed her to be her priestess, putting a deer in her place at the altar; but some say Artemis made her immortal. This ending makes Artemis''s image complex: the goddess who demands the sacrifice is also the one who at the last moment saves the maiden. In other writers the story has darker versions in which Iphigenia truly dies at the altar and becomes one of the reasons Clytemnestra later kills her husband. The Library chooses the rescue and, as usual, records another account as well. From her birth on Delos to the altar at Aulis, the Library''s Artemis is the same goddess throughout: guardian of her mother''s honour, of wild beasts and of maidens'' vows, who never tolerates mortal pride.','claim.v0360.lib_artemis.12','阿波罗多洛斯《书库·摘要》3.22。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_library_3_4_3_5_dionysus.1','storyver.greek.library_3_4_3_5_dionysus',1,'塞墨勒的请求','Semele''s request','腓尼基人卡德摩斯建立了忒拜，娶了阿瑞斯和阿佛洛狄忒的女儿哈耳摩尼亚，生下了女儿奥托诺厄、伊诺、塞墨勒和阿高厄，还有儿子波吕多罗斯。宙斯爱上了塞墨勒，瞒着赫拉与她同床。赫拉知道后，便设计欺骗她。《书库》只简单地说赫拉骗了她，没有讲细节；后来的作家说，赫拉变成塞墨勒的老保姆，劝她试探情人是不是真的宙斯。宙斯曾答应塞墨勒，她想要什么都可以。于是，在赫拉的欺骗之下，塞墨勒请求宙斯，要他像当初向赫拉求婚时那样来到她身边。宙斯已经许下了诺言，无法拒绝。在希腊神话里，神的誓言是不能收回的，即使宙斯也不能。塞墨勒想证明自己的爱人是天上的众神之王，可她不知道，凡人的身体承受不住神的真身。这一请求，就是整个故事的起点：酒神的诞生，始于一个凡间女子的愿望和一位女神的嫉妒。','Cadmus the Phoenician founded Thebes and married Harmonia, daughter of Ares and Aphrodite, who bore him daughters, Autonoe, Ino, Semele and Agave, and a son, Polydorus. Zeus loved Semele and lay with her without Hera''s knowledge. When Hera found out, she deceived Semele. The Library says only that Hera deceived her, without the details; later writers say Hera took the shape of Semele''s old nurse and urged her to test whether her lover was truly Zeus. Zeus had promised to grant Semele whatever she asked. So, deceived by Hera, Semele asked Zeus to come to her as he came when he wooed Hera. Zeus, having given his word, could not refuse. In Greek myth a god''s oath cannot be taken back, not even by Zeus. Semele wanted proof that her lover was the king of heaven, not knowing that a mortal body cannot bear a god''s true form. This request is the start of the whole story: the birth of the wine god begins with a mortal woman''s wish and a goddess''s jealousy.','claim.v0360.lib_dionysus.01','阿波罗多洛斯《书库》3.4.2–3.4.3。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_library_3_4_3_5_dionysus.2','storyver.greek.library_3_4_3_5_dionysus',2,'雷火','Thunder and fire','宙斯无法拒绝，只好驾着战车，带着闪电和雷鸣来到塞墨勒的屋子，掷出了一道雷电。塞墨勒在恐惧中死去。可是她腹中的孩子还活着：宙斯从火中抢出这个才六个月大、早产的胎儿，把他缝进了自己的大腿里。《书库》在这里的叙述极为简短，却包含了希腊神话中最奇特的一个情节：一位男神用自己的身体怀胎。狄俄倪索斯因此常被称为“两次出生的神”——第一次从母亲的身体里出生，第二次从父亲的大腿里出生。塞墨勒的死，也让她成为希腊神话中很少见的人物：一位因为见到神的真身而死去的凡间母亲，她的儿子却成了奥林波斯的神。古代的忒拜人在卡德摩斯宫殿的废墟中保留着一块被雷电击中的地方，据说那就是塞墨勒的新房，人们在那里纪念她。这一章的结局是悲剧，但在《书库》的叙述中，它也是一个开始：雷火烧毁了一个凡人的家，却成全了一位神的出生。','Unable to refuse, Zeus came to her bridal chamber in a chariot with lightnings and thunderings and launched a thunderbolt. Semele expired of fright. But the child in her womb lived: Zeus snatched the six-month abortive child from the fire and sewed it in his thigh. The Library tells this very briefly, yet it holds one of the strangest episodes in Greek myth: a male god carrying a child in his own body. So Dionysus is often called the twice-born god — born once from his mother and again from his father''s thigh. Semele''s death also makes her a rare figure: a mortal mother who died from seeing a god in his true form, whose son became an Olympian. The ancient Thebans kept a spot in the ruins of Cadmus''s palace said to have been struck by the thunderbolt — Semele''s bridal chamber — where she was remembered. The chapter ends in tragedy, but in the Library it is also a beginning: the thunder-fire destroys a mortal''s home and brings about a god''s birth.','claim.v0360.lib_dionysus.02','阿波罗多洛斯《书库》3.4.3。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_library_3_4_3_5_dionysus.3','storyver.greek.library_3_4_3_5_dionysus',3,'第二次出生','The second birth','塞墨勒死后，卡德摩斯的其他女儿们四处散布流言，说塞墨勒是和一个凡人同床，却谎称是宙斯，所以才被雷电击死。这个流言后来给她们带来了可怕的报应。到了合适的时候，宙斯拆开大腿上的缝线，生下了狄俄倪索斯，把他交给了赫尔墨斯。赫尔墨斯把孩子送到塞墨勒的姐姐伊诺和她的丈夫阿塔玛斯那里，劝他们把孩子当作女孩来抚养，好让赫拉认不出他来。这是狄俄倪索斯一生中第一次以伪装求生，也是后来他在故事中常常变换模样的开端。《书库》的叙述在这里显出一种奇特的对称：塞墨勒的姐妹们因为不信她和宙斯相爱而诽谤她，后来她们的家族却一个接一个地卷入了酒神带来的狂乱之中——伊诺的家将在下一章遭遇不幸，阿高厄的儿子彭透斯和奥托诺厄的儿子阿克泰翁也都将死于非命。在希腊神话里，不承认神的人，往往要付出沉重的代价。','After Semele''s death the other daughters of Cadmus spread the report that she had lain with a mortal man and falsely accused Zeus, and that she was blasted by the thunderbolt for it. That slander would later bring a terrible return on them. At the proper time Zeus undid the stitches and gave birth to Dionysus, and entrusted him to Hermes. Hermes took him to Ino, Semele''s sister, and her husband Athamas, and persuaded them to rear him as a girl, so that Hera would not know him. This is the first time Dionysus survives by disguise, and the beginning of his habit of changing shape in later tales. The Library''s account here has a strange symmetry: Semele''s sisters slandered her because they would not believe she was loved by Zeus, and later their families were drawn one after another into the madness the wine god brought — Ino''s house meets disaster in the next chapter, and Agave''s son Pentheus and Autonoe''s son Actaeon both die terrible deaths. In Greek myth those who deny a god usually pay dearly.','claim.v0360.lib_dionysus.03','阿波罗多洛斯《书库》3.4.3。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_library_3_4_3_5_dionysus.4','storyver.greek.library_3_4_3_5_dionysus',4,'伊诺与阿塔玛斯的疯狂','The madness of Ino and Athamas','可是赫拉还是知道了。她非常愤怒，让伊诺和阿塔玛斯都发了疯。阿塔玛斯把长子勒阿耳科斯当作一头鹿来追猎，杀死了他。伊诺把幼子墨利刻耳忒斯扔进一口滚烫的大锅里，然后抱着那口锅和死去的孩子跳进了大海。《书库》说，伊诺从此被称为琉科忒亚，意思是“白色的女神”，孩子被称为帕莱蒙，水手们在风暴中向他们求救，因为他们会救助遇难的人。科林斯人还为墨利刻耳忒斯举办了地峡运动会，这是由西绪福斯创立的。这是狄俄倪索斯的故事中第一次出现“疯狂”：赫拉的愤怒没有直接落在婴儿身上，而是落在了抚养他的人身上。后来，狄俄倪索斯自己也将用同样的疯狂来惩罚那些不承认他的人。在《荷马史诗·奥德赛》里，正是这位琉科忒亚在海上把一块头巾交给遇难的奥德修斯，救了他的命——一个因疯狂而跳海的母亲，成了海上救人的女神。','But Hera found out, and in her anger drove Ino and Athamas mad. Athamas hunted his elder son Learchus as a deer and killed him, and Ino threw the younger, Melicertes, into a boiling cauldron, then, carrying the cauldron with the dead child, sprang into the sea. The Library says Ino is now called Leucothea, the White Goddess, and the boy Palaemon, names given by sailors, for they help storm-tossed mariners; and the Isthmian games were instituted by Sisyphus in honour of Melicertes. This is the first appearance of madness in Dionysus''s story: Hera''s anger falls not on the infant himself but on those who rear him. Later Dionysus will use the same madness to punish those who refuse him. In Homer''s Odyssey it is this Leucothea who gives the shipwrecked Odysseus a veil at sea and saves his life — a mother who leapt into the sea in madness becomes a goddess who rescues men from the waves.','claim.v0360.lib_dionysus.04','阿波罗多洛斯《书库》3.4.3。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_library_3_4_3_5_dionysus.5','storyver.greek.library_3_4_3_5_dionysus',5,'倪萨的仙女','The nymphs of Nysa','为了不让赫拉再找到他，宙斯把狄俄倪索斯变成了一只小山羊。赫尔墨斯带着这只小山羊，把他送到亚细亚的倪萨，交给那里的仙女们抚养。《书库》说，后来宙斯把这些仙女变成了星星，称为许阿得斯，也就是金牛座中的毕星团。倪萨在哪里，古代作家意见不一：有人说在埃及，有人说在阿拉伯，有人说在印度，《书库》只说它在亚细亚。倪萨这个名字也被认为与狄俄倪索斯的名字有关。这一段很短，却完成了狄俄倪索斯童年的最后一次转移：从母亲的身体到父亲的大腿，从伊诺的家到倪萨的山中，从一个被当作女孩抚养的婴儿，变成一只小山羊。在古希腊的宗教中，山羊和小鹿常常与酒神联系在一起，他的女信徒们披着小鹿的皮，而悲剧这个词，据说也和山羊有关。从这一刻起，狄俄倪索斯不再是一个需要躲藏的孩子，他将在仙女们的山中长大，成为一位四处游历、让人们认识他的神。','To keep him from Hera, Zeus turned Dionysus into a kid, and Hermes took him to the nymphs who dwelt at Nysa in Asia. The Library says Zeus afterwards turned these nymphs into stars and named them the Hyades, the cluster in the constellation of the Bull. Ancient writers disagreed about where Nysa was — some said Egypt, some Arabia, some India — and the Library says only that it was in Asia. The name Nysa was also thought to be linked with Dionysus''s own name. The passage is short, but it completes the last move of his childhood: from his mother''s body to his father''s thigh, from Ino''s house to the mountains of Nysa, from an infant reared as a girl to a young goat. In Greek religion goats and fawns were closely tied to the wine god; his women followers wore fawnskins, and the word tragedy was said to be connected with the goat. From here Dionysus is no longer a child in hiding; he will grow up in the nymphs'' mountains and become a god who travels the world making himself known.','claim.v0360.lib_dionysus.05','阿波罗多洛斯《书库》3.4.3。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_library_3_4_3_5_dionysus.6','storyver.greek.library_3_4_3_5_dionysus',6,'葡萄与疯狂','The vine and the madness','《书库》第三卷接着说：狄俄倪索斯是葡萄的发现者。赫拉让他发了疯，他四处流浪，走遍了埃及和叙利亚。最先接待他的是埃及国王普洛透斯。后来他来到弗里吉亚的库柏拉，在那里，瑞亚为他洗净了疯狂，把秘仪传授给他，给了他举行仪式时穿的服装。从此，他带着这些秘仪穿过色雷斯，前去征讨印度人。这几句话概括了狄俄倪索斯从受难的神变成传教的神的过程：他发现了葡萄这件给人类的礼物，却因为赫拉的嫉妒而失去理智，在异乡流浪；直到众神之母瑞亚——在弗里吉亚，她常常与大母神库柏勒等同——为他净化，他才找回自己，并且得到了秘仪。在古希腊人的观念里，狄俄倪索斯是从外邦来的神，他的崇拜带着弗里吉亚、色雷斯和东方的色彩，这一点在他的游历路线中也能看出来。接下来，他要回到希腊，让那些不承认他的城邦认识他是神。','The third book goes on: Dionysus discovered the vine. Hera drove him mad, and he roamed about Egypt and Syria. At first he was received by Proteus, king of Egypt. Then he came to Cybela in Phrygia, and there he was purified by Rhea and learned the rites of initiation, and received from her the costume. With these he hastened through Thrace against the Indians. These few sentences sum up Dionysus''s change from a suffering god into a god who spreads his worship: he discovered the vine, his gift to mankind, yet through Hera''s jealousy lost his mind and wandered in foreign lands, until Rhea, mother of the gods — often identified in Phrygia with the Great Mother Cybele — purified him, and he recovered himself and gained the rites. To the ancient Greeks Dionysus was a god from abroad, and his worship carried Phrygian, Thracian and eastern colouring, as his route shows. Next he must come back to Greece and make the cities that refuse him know him as a god.','claim.v0360.lib_dionysus.06','阿波罗多洛斯《书库》3.5.1。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_library_3_4_3_5_dionysus.7','storyver.greek.library_3_4_3_5_dionysus',7,'吕枯耳戈斯','Lycurgus','在色雷斯，斯特律蒙河边的厄多涅斯人的国王、德律阿斯的儿子吕枯耳戈斯第一个侮辱了狄俄倪索斯，把他赶了出去。狄俄倪索斯逃进大海，躲在涅柔斯的女儿忒提斯那里。他的女信徒们和跟随他的萨提尔们却被吕枯耳戈斯抓住，关了起来。后来，女信徒们突然被释放了，狄俄倪索斯让吕枯耳戈斯发了疯。吕枯耳戈斯在疯狂中把自己的儿子德律阿斯当作一根葡萄枝，用斧头砍死了他，砍下他的四肢之后，才清醒过来。这时整个国家都荒芜了，土地不长庄稼。神谕说，只有处死吕枯耳戈斯，土地才会重新结出果实。厄多涅斯人听了，就把他带到潘加翁山上捆起来，在狄俄倪索斯的意旨下，让马把他撕碎了。荷马在《伊利亚特》里也提到吕枯耳戈斯追打酒神的保姆们、吓得酒神跳进海里，忒提斯把他藏在怀中，后来宙斯弄瞎了吕枯耳戈斯的眼睛。两个文本对他的结局说法不同，各自保留。','In Thrace, Lycurgus son of Dryas, king of the Edonians who dwell beside the river Strymon, was the first to insult and expel Dionysus. Dionysus took refuge in the sea with Thetis, daughter of Nereus, and the Bacchantes and the multitude of Satyrs who attended him were taken prisoner. But afterwards the Bacchantes were suddenly released, and Dionysus drove Lycurgus mad. In his madness Lycurgus struck his son Dryas with an axe, imagining he was lopping a branch of a vine, and he did not come to his senses until he had cut off his son''s extremities. The land then remained barren, and the god''s oracle declared that it would bear fruit if Lycurgus were put to death. On hearing this the Edonians took him to Mount Pangaeum and bound him, and there, by the will of Dionysus, he died, destroyed by horses. Homer''s Iliad also mentions Lycurgus chasing the wine god''s nurses and frightening the god into the sea, where Thetis took him into her bosom, and says Zeus later blinded Lycurgus. The two texts differ on his end, and each account is kept.','claim.v0360.lib_dionysus.07','阿波罗多洛斯《书库》3.5.1。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_library_3_4_3_5_dionysus.8','storyver.greek.library_3_4_3_5_dionysus',8,'回到忒拜','Back to Thebes','狄俄倪索斯穿过色雷斯和整个印度，在那里竖起了石柱，然后来到了忒拜。这座城市是他母亲的故乡，可是城里的人并不承认他是神，他母亲的姐妹们曾说塞墨勒撒了谎。狄俄倪索斯迫使忒拜的妇女们离开自己的家，在喀泰戎山上举行狂欢的仪式。她们披着小鹿皮，手持缠着常春藤的神杖，在山林中奔跑、唱歌、跳舞。当时统治忒拜的是彭透斯，他是阿高厄和厄喀翁的儿子，卡德摩斯的外孙。彭透斯从卡德摩斯手里接过了王位，他认为这些仪式是对城邦秩序的威胁，想要阻止这一切。《书库》在这里的讲述非常简洁，可它清楚地点出了冲突的双方：一边是来自外乡、却与这座城市有着血缘关系的神，一边是一位年轻的国王，他拒绝承认自己的表兄是神。从塞墨勒的死到狄俄倪索斯回到故乡，故事绕了一个大圈；而这个圈的终点，将是又一场家族内部的悲剧。','Having traversed Thrace and the whole of India and set up pillars there, Dionysus came to Thebes. It was his mother''s city, yet its people did not acknowledge him as a god, and his mother''s sisters had said that Semele lied. He forced the women of Thebes to leave their houses and rave in Bacchic frenzy on Cithaeron. Clad in fawnskins and carrying thyrsi wreathed with ivy, they ran, sang and danced in the mountain woods. Thebes was then ruled by Pentheus, son of Agave and Echion, grandson of Cadmus, who had received the kingdom from Cadmus. He saw the rites as a threat to the order of the city and tried to put a stop to them. The Library''s account here is very brief, yet it marks the two sides of the conflict clearly: a god from abroad who is nonetheless bound to the city by blood, and a young king who refuses to accept that his cousin is a god. From Semele''s death to Dionysus''s return home, the story has come full circle — and at the end of the circle stands another tragedy within the family.','claim.v0360.lib_dionysus.08','阿波罗多洛斯《书库》3.5.2。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_library_3_4_3_5_dionysus.9','storyver.greek.library_3_4_3_5_dionysus',9,'彭透斯','Pentheus','彭透斯想阻止酒神的仪式，就来到喀泰戎山上，暗中窥探那些狂欢的女人。可是他的母亲阿高厄正处在狂乱之中，把自己的儿子当成了一头野兽，和其他女信徒一起把他撕成了碎片。《书库》对这一幕只用了一句话，后来欧里庇得斯在悲剧《酒神的伴侣》中把它写成了希腊戏剧中最令人震撼的场面之一：阿高厄清醒过来，才发现自己手中捧着的是儿子的头。狄俄倪索斯以这种方式向忒拜人证明了自己是神。接着《书库》说，他离开忒拜，来到阿尔戈斯，那里的人也不尊敬他，他又让那里的妇女发了疯，在山上做出了可怕的事。在这几个故事里，狄俄倪索斯总是先被拒绝，然后让拒绝他的人陷入疯狂，最后人们不得不承认他。对古希腊人来说，这正是酒神的两面性：他带来葡萄酒的欢乐，也带来让人失去自我的狂乱；对于接纳他的人，他是一位慷慨的神，对于抗拒他的人，他是一位可怕的神。','To stop the rites Pentheus went to Cithaeron to spy on the raving women. But his mother Agave, in her frenzy, took her own son for a wild beast and, with the other Bacchantes, tore him limb from limb. The Library gives this a single sentence; later Euripides, in the Bacchae, made it one of the most shattering scenes in Greek drama: Agave comes to her senses to find that what she holds is her son''s head. In this way Dionysus showed the Thebans that he was a god. Then, the Library says, he left Thebes and came to Argos, where again the people did not honour him, and he drove the women mad, and on the mountains they did terrible things. In each of these tales Dionysus is first refused, then drives those who refuse him into madness, until at last men must acknowledge him. To the Greeks this was the double nature of the wine god: he brings the joy of wine, and also the frenzy in which one loses oneself; to those who receive him he is a generous god, to those who resist him a terrible one.','claim.v0360.lib_dionysus.09','阿波罗多洛斯《书库》3.5.2。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_library_3_4_3_5_dionysus.10','storyver.greek.library_3_4_3_5_dionysus',10,'第勒尼安海盗','The Tyrrhenian pirates','狄俄倪索斯想从伊卡里亚岛渡海到纳克索斯岛，就雇了一条第勒尼安海盗的船。海盗们把他带上船后，却驶过纳克索斯，直奔亚细亚，打算把他卖掉。狄俄倪索斯把船上的桅杆和船桨变成了蛇，让船上长满了常春藤，四处响起了笛声。海盗们发了疯，纷纷跳进海里，变成了海豚。《书库》说，人们就这样知道了他是神，开始崇拜他。同样的故事在《荷马颂歌·致狄俄倪索斯》里讲得更详细：那里海盗们看见一个穿着紫袍的年轻人坐在海岬上，以为他是王子，把他捆上船，可绳索从他身上自己脱落了；舵手认出他是神，劝大家放了他，却没人听；接着船上流出了芬芳的酒，葡萄藤爬满了船帆，神变成一头狮子，海盗们跳海变成海豚，只有那位好心的舵手得到了神的怜悯。两个文本的细节不同，可核心一样：想把神当作奴隶卖掉的人，最后连人的形状都失去了。','Wishing to be ferried from Icaria to Naxos, Dionysus hired a pirate ship of Tyrrhenians. But when they had him on board they sailed past Naxos and made for Asia, meaning to sell him. Then he turned the mast and oars into snakes, filled the vessel with ivy and the sound of flutes, and the pirates went mad, leapt into the sea and were turned into dolphins. Thus, the Library says, men learned that he was a god and honoured him. The same tale is told at more length in the Homeric Hymn to Dionysus: there the pirates see a young man in a purple robe on a headland, take him for a prince and bind him aboard, but the bonds fall from him of themselves; the helmsman recognises a god and urges them to free him, but no one listens; then fragrant wine flows through the ship, a vine spreads over the sail, the god becomes a lion, and the pirates leap into the sea as dolphins — only the kind helmsman is spared. The details differ, but the core is the same: those who would sell a god as a slave lose even their human shape.','claim.v0360.lib_dionysus.10','阿波罗多洛斯《书库》3.5.3。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_library_3_4_3_5_dionysus.11','storyver.greek.library_3_4_3_5_dionysus',11,'从冥府带回母亲','Bringing his mother up from Hades','《书库》用一句话为狄俄倪索斯在大地上的游历作了结尾：人们知道了他是神，开始崇拜他；他从冥府带回了自己的母亲，给她取名堤俄涅，和她一起升上了天空。这样，故事又回到了它的起点。在第一章里，塞墨勒因为想看到宙斯的真身而死去，她的姐妹们说她撒谎，她的名字蒙上了污点；在最后，她的儿子以神的身份走进冥府，把她带回来，让她和自己一起成为天上的神。后来的作家说，狄俄倪索斯是从阿尔戈利斯的勒耳纳湖下到冥府的，那里被认为是通往冥界的入口之一；《书库》没有讲这些细节。在希腊宗教中，塞墨勒也以堤俄涅之名受到崇拜，品达在诗中说她住在奥林波斯，宙斯和雅典娜都爱她。一个被雷电烧死的凡间女子，最后成了不死的女神——这既是儿子对母亲的报答，也是对当初那些不相信她的人最好的回答。','The Library closes Dionysus''s wanderings on earth in one sentence: men learned that he was a god and honoured him; and having brought up his mother from Hades and named her Thyone, he ascended with her to heaven. So the story returns to its start. In the first chapter Semele died because she wanted to see Zeus in his true form; her sisters said she lied, and her name was stained. At the end her son goes down into Hades as a god, brings her back and makes her a goddess in heaven beside him. Later writers say Dionysus went down to Hades through the Alcyonian lake at Lerna in the Argolid, held to be an entrance to the underworld; the Library gives no such details. In Greek religion Semele was worshipped as Thyone, and Pindar sings that she dwells on Olympus, beloved of Zeus and of Athena. A mortal woman burned by the thunderbolt becomes an immortal goddess — her son''s repayment of his mother, and the best answer to those who once refused to believe her.','claim.v0360.lib_dionysus.11','阿波罗多洛斯《书库》3.5.3。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_library_3_4_3_5_dionysus.12','storyver.greek.library_3_4_3_5_dionysus',12,'伊卡里俄斯','Icarius','《书库》在讲雅典诸王的时候，又提到了狄俄倪索斯。在潘狄翁统治雅典的时候，得墨忒耳和狄俄倪索斯来到了阿提卡：得墨忒耳在厄琉息斯受到刻琉斯的款待，狄俄倪索斯则受到伊卡里俄斯的款待。伊卡里俄斯从神那里得到一根葡萄枝，学会了酿酒的方法。他想把神的恩赐分享给人们，就去找一些牧人，请他们喝酒。牧人们尝了以后，为了痛快，不掺水就大口地喝，结果以为自己中了毒，就把伊卡里俄斯杀死了。第二天他们酒醒了，才把他埋葬。伊卡里俄斯的女儿厄里戈涅四处寻找父亲，一直跟随伊卡里俄斯的狗迈拉找到了他的尸体，厄里戈涅为父亲痛哭之后，上吊自尽了。这个结局并不欢乐，却说明了希腊人对葡萄酒的复杂感情：它是神的礼物，也是一种需要学会如何享用的力量。古希腊人通常把酒掺水饮用，不掺水的烈酒被看作野蛮人的习惯；伊卡里俄斯的死，正发生在人们还不懂得这一点的时候。','Telling of the kings of Athens, the Library mentions Dionysus again. In the reign of Pandion, Demeter and Dionysus came to Attica: Demeter was received by Celeus at Eleusis, and Dionysus by Icarius. Icarius received from the god a branch of a vine and learned the process of making wine. Wishing to share the god''s gifts with men, he went to some shepherds and offered them wine. They tasted it and, for the pleasure of it, drank it without water, then thought they had been bewitched and killed him. By day, when they were sober, they buried him. Erigone, Icarius''s daughter, searched for her father; the dog Maera, which had followed him, found the body, and Erigone, after mourning her father, hanged herself. The ending is not joyful, yet it shows the Greeks'' complex feelings about wine: a god''s gift, and also a power one must learn how to use. The Greeks usually drank wine mixed with water, and drinking it neat was thought a barbarian habit; Icarius died at a time when men had not yet learned this.','claim.v0360.lib_dionysus.12','阿波罗多洛斯《书库》3.14.7。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_iliad_butler_ares.1','storyver.greek.iliad_butler_ares',1,'被领出战场','Led from the battle','《伊利亚特》第五卷是狄俄墨得斯大显身手的一卷，也是阿瑞斯在全诗中出场最多的一卷。战斗刚开始不久，眼睛明亮的雅典娜就拉住狂暴的阿瑞斯的手，对他说：阿瑞斯，阿瑞斯，人类的祸害，沾满鲜血的城墙摧毁者，我们何不让特洛伊人和希腊人自己去打，看看宙斯要把荣耀给哪一方？我们两个退到一边，免得惹宙斯生气。说着，她把阿瑞斯领出了战场，让他坐在斯卡曼德洛斯河长满青草的岸边。可是雅典娜的这番话并不真诚：她本人是希腊人的支持者，把阿瑞斯领开，正是为了让希腊人占上风。阿瑞斯一走，希腊人果然开始击退特洛伊人，每一位希腊首领都杀死了一个敌人。这一开场就定下了整卷的基调：阿瑞斯虽然是战神，力大无穷、勇猛无比，却常常在计谋上输给雅典娜。荷马笔下的这两位战争之神截然不同：一个代表盲目的厮杀，一个代表有谋略的战斗。','Book Five of the Iliad is Diomedes'' great day, and the book in which Ares appears most. Soon after the fighting begins, bright-eyed Athena takes furious Ares by the hand and says: ''Ares, Ares, bane of men, bloodstained stormer of cities, shall we not leave the Trojans and Achaeans to fight it out and see to which Zeus will give glory? Let us two withdraw and avoid the anger of Zeus.'' With that she leads Ares out of the battle and sets him down on the grassy banks of the Scamander. Her words are not sincere: she favours the Achaeans, and leads Ares away precisely so that they may gain the upper hand. As soon as he is gone the Achaeans begin to drive the Trojans back, and each Achaean chief kills his man. This opening sets the tone for the whole book: Ares, god of war, is enormously strong and fierce, yet again and again he is outwitted by Athena. Homer''s two war gods are utterly different — one stands for blind slaughter, the other for war waged with cunning.','claim.v0360.iliad_ares.01','荷马《伊利亚特》第五卷第29–36行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_iliad_butler_ares.2','storyver.greek.iliad_butler_ares',2,'借车','The borrowed chariot','战场上，狄俄墨得斯在雅典娜的鼓舞下所向无敌，连阿佛洛狄忒也被他刺伤了手腕——她是来救自己受伤的儿子埃涅阿斯的。女神流着神的血，痛得放开了儿子，由伊里斯扶着离开战场。她在战场的左边找到了凶猛的阿瑞斯，他正坐在那里，长矛和快马都靠在一团云雾里。阿佛洛狄忒跪倒在哥哥面前，恳求他把那几匹戴着金辔头的马借给她，说：亲爱的哥哥，救救我，把你的马借给我，让我回到不死的神居住的奥林波斯去。一个凡人刺伤了我，就是那个提丢斯的儿子，他现在连父亲宙斯都敢打了。阿瑞斯把马借给了她，她忍着痛登上战车，伊里斯握着缰绳，扬鞭催马，马儿飞快地奔向高高的奥林波斯。这一段很短，却让我们看到阿瑞斯难得的另一面：在战场上他是人类的祸害，在受伤的妹妹面前，他却是一个肯伸出援手的哥哥。荷马常把阿瑞斯和阿佛洛狄忒放在一起，他们都是特洛伊一方的神。','On the field Diomedes, fired by Athena, carries all before him, and he even wounds Aphrodite in the wrist when she comes to rescue her wounded son Aeneas. The goddess, bleeding ichor, lets go of her son in pain, and Iris leads her out of the fighting. On the left of the battle she finds fierce Ares sitting, his spear and his swift horses resting in a cloud. Aphrodite falls on her knees before her brother and begs him for his horses with their golden headbands: ''Dear brother, help me, lend me your horses to take me to Olympus where the gods dwell. A mortal has wounded me — the son of Tydeus, who would now fight even father Zeus.'' Ares gives her the horses; she mounts the chariot in pain, Iris takes the reins and lashes the horses, and they fly to high Olympus. The passage is short, yet it shows a rare side of Ares: on the field he is the bane of men, but before his wounded sister he is a brother who lends a hand. Homer often pairs Ares and Aphrodite; both are gods of the Trojan side.','claim.v0360.iliad_ares.02','荷马《伊利亚特》第五卷第355–363行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_iliad_butler_ares.3','storyver.greek.iliad_butler_ares',3,'铜罐里的十三个月','Thirteen months in a bronze jar','阿佛洛狄忒回到奥林波斯，扑倒在母亲狄俄涅的膝上。狄俄涅把她抱在怀里安慰她，说：忍着点吧，我的孩子，住在奥林波斯的神有许多都吃过凡人的苦头，我们自己也常常互相伤害。接着她举了几个例子，第一个就是阿瑞斯：阿洛欧斯的两个儿子、强壮的俄托斯和厄菲阿尔忒斯，曾经用结实的锁链把阿瑞斯捆起来，关在一个铜罐里整整十三个月。若不是两兄弟的继母、美丽的厄里玻亚把这件事告诉了赫尔墨斯，嗜战不厌的阿瑞斯也许就死在那里了；赫尔墨斯把他偷了出来，那时他已经被那残酷的锁链折磨得筋疲力尽。狄俄涅还讲到赫拉和冥王哈得斯都曾被赫拉克勒斯的箭射伤。这段插话说明，在荷马的世界里，众神虽然不死，却会受伤、受苦，甚至会被囚禁。阿瑞斯作为战神，竟有过被凡间巨人关进罐子的经历，这和他在第五卷中接下来的遭遇遥相呼应。','Aphrodite returns to Olympus and falls on the knees of her mother Dione, who takes her in her arms and comforts her: ''Bear it, my child; many of us who dwell on Olympus have suffered at the hands of men, and we bring hard pains on one another.'' Then she gives examples, and the first is Ares: the two sons of Aloeus, mighty Otus and Ephialtes, bound Ares in strong chains and kept him in a bronze jar for thirteen months. Ares, insatiable of war, might have perished there, had not their stepmother, the lovely Eëriboea, told Hermes, who stole him away, already worn out by the cruel bonds. Dione also tells how Hera and Hades himself were wounded by Heracles'' arrows. The digression shows that in Homer''s world the gods, though deathless, can be wounded, can suffer, can even be imprisoned. That Ares, the war god, was once shut in a jar by giant brothers echoes what will happen to him later in this same book.','claim.v0360.iliad_ares.03','荷马《伊利亚特》第五卷第385–391行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_iliad_butler_ares.4','storyver.greek.iliad_butler_ares',4,'阿波罗的召唤','Apollo''s summons','狄俄墨得斯一连三次冲向被阿波罗保护着的埃涅阿斯，第四次冲上去时，阿波罗厉声喝退了他：提丢斯的儿子，退回去，不要妄想和神平起平坐。阿波罗把埃涅阿斯带到自己在特洛伊城的神庙里养伤，然后对阿瑞斯说：阿瑞斯，阿瑞斯，人类的祸害，沾满鲜血的城墙摧毁者，你去把这个人从战场上赶走好吗？就是那个提丢斯的儿子，他现在敢和父亲宙斯打了。他先刺伤了阿佛洛狄忒的手腕，接着又像一个神一样向我冲来。说完，阿波罗坐到特洛伊的城墙顶上。凶猛的阿瑞斯于是走进特洛伊人的队伍中，化作色雷斯人的首领阿卡马斯的模样，鼓舞特洛伊人，对普里阿摩斯的儿子们大声喊道：你们还要让希腊人杀戮多久？难道要等他们打到城门口吗？埃涅阿斯倒下了，我们去把他从混战中救出来。他的话让每个人都鼓起了勇气。这里，阿瑞斯第一次真正投入了战斗，而他的出场，是用一个凡人的面孔完成的。','Three times Diomedes sprang at Aeneas, whom Apollo was shielding; the fourth time Apollo shouted at him: ''Son of Tydeus, give way, and do not think to match yourself with the gods.'' Apollo carried Aeneas to his temple in Pergamus to be healed, and then said to Ares: ''Ares, Ares, bane of men, bloodstained stormer of cities, will you not go and drive this man from the field — the son of Tydeus, who would now fight even father Zeus? First he wounded Aphrodite in the wrist, then he sprang at me like a god.'' Then Apollo sat down on the walls of Pergamus. Fierce Ares went among the ranks of the Trojans in the likeness of Acamas, leader of the Thracians, and urged on the sons of Priam: ''How long will you let the Achaeans slaughter your people? Till they fight at your gates? Aeneas has fallen; let us rescue him from the press.'' His words put heart into every man. Here Ares truly enters the fighting for the first time — and he does it with the face of a mortal.','claim.v0360.iliad_ares.04','荷马《伊利亚特》第五卷第454–470行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_iliad_butler_ares.5','storyver.greek.iliad_butler_ares',5,'赫克托耳身边的神','The god beside Hector','赫克托耳从战车上跳下来，挥舞着两支长矛，在整个队伍中奔走，激起可怕的战斗。特洛伊人转过身来，面对希腊人。荷马说，走在他们前面的是阿瑞斯和可怕的厄倪俄——厄倪俄是战争的女神，带着无情的战争喧嚣；阿瑞斯手里挥舞着一支巨大的长矛，时而走在赫克托耳前面，时而跟在他后面。狄俄墨得斯看见阿瑞斯，浑身一颤。就像一个人在穿越一片大平原时，忽然来到一条奔腾入海的大河边，看见河水翻滚着白沫，只好往后退；提丢斯的儿子也就这样后退了，对他的部下说：朋友们，难怪我们惊叹赫克托耳是个好枪手、勇敢的战士，原来总有一位神在他身边，保护他免于死亡，现在阿瑞斯就化作凡人的样子站在他旁边。所以，你们一边面朝特洛伊人，一边往后退吧，不要想着和神作战。狄俄墨得斯能看见神，是因为雅典娜拂去了他眼前的迷雾。在他身上，勇敢与谨慎并存：他敢于刺伤阿佛洛狄忒，却知道此刻还不是与战神交手的时候。','Hector sprang from his chariot and went about among the host brandishing his two spears, rousing them to fight, and the Trojans turned and faced the Achaeans. Before them, Homer says, went Ares and dread Enyo — Enyo, goddess of war, bringing the ruthless din of battle — while Ares wielded a monstrous spear and went now in front of Hector, now behind him. When Diomedes saw Ares he shuddered. As a man crossing a wide plain stops in dismay at a swift river rushing to the sea and, seeing it boil with foam, starts back, so the son of Tydeus gave ground, and said to his men: ''My friends, how can we wonder at Hector being such a spearman and so bold? Some god is always by his side to protect him, and now Ares is with him in the likeness of a mortal man. Keep your faces toward the Trojans, but give ground backwards, and do not fight with the gods.'' Diomedes can see the god because Athena has lifted the mist from his eyes. In him courage and caution go together: he dared to wound Aphrodite, but knows this is not yet the time to face the war god.','claim.v0360.iliad_ares.05','荷马《伊利亚特》第五卷第590–606行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_iliad_butler_ares.6','storyver.greek.iliad_butler_ares',6,'退却的希腊人','The Achaeans give ground','希腊人在阿瑞斯和赫克托耳面前既没有逃回船边，也没有挺身迎战，而是一步步地后退，因为他们知道阿瑞斯在特洛伊人中间。荷马在这里向缪斯发问：赫克托耳和青铜的阿瑞斯先杀死的是谁，最后杀死的又是谁？他接着列出了一串名字：神一样的透特剌斯、驾车的俄瑞斯忒斯、埃托利亚的枪手特瑞科斯、俄诺马俄斯、俄诺普斯的儿子赫勒诺斯，还有系着闪亮腰带的俄瑞斯比俄斯——他住在许勒，守着自己的财富，住在刻菲西斯湖边。这些名字在全诗中只出现这一次，他们的生平只用一两句话交代，却是阿瑞斯在战场上留下的真实痕迹。荷马常常这样，在大场面中插进一个个普通战士的名字和家乡，让读者意识到每一次“杀戮”背后都是一个具体的人。对阿瑞斯来说，这是他在整部《伊利亚特》中最得意的时刻：他和凡间最勇敢的特洛伊英雄并肩作战，希腊人在他面前节节败退。可是这一幕被奥林波斯山上的两位女神看在眼里。','Before Ares and Hector the Achaeans neither fled to their ships nor charged, but gave ground step by step, for they knew Ares was among the Trojans. Here Homer asks the Muse: who was the first and who the last whom Hector and bronze Ares slew? He lists them: godlike Teuthras, Orestes the charioteer, Trechus the Aetolian spearman, Oenomaus, Helenus son of Oenops, and Oresbius of the gleaming girdle, who lived in Hyle on the shore of the Cephisian lake, minding his wealth. These names appear only here in the whole poem, each life told in a line or two, yet they are the real marks Ares leaves on the battlefield. Homer often sets the names and homes of ordinary fighters into his great scenes, so that every ''slaying'' is a particular man. For Ares this is his proudest moment in the whole Iliad: fighting beside the bravest Trojan, the Achaeans giving way before him. But two goddesses on Olympus are watching.','claim.v0360.iliad_ares.06','荷马《伊利亚特》第五卷第699–710行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_iliad_butler_ares.7','storyver.greek.iliad_butler_ares',7,'赫拉与雅典娜出征','Hera and Athena drive out','白臂的赫拉看见希腊人在激战中纷纷倒下，就对雅典娜说：真是糟糕，持埃癸斯盾的宙斯的孩子，我们曾向墨涅拉俄斯许诺，他一定会攻下特洛伊城平安回家，如果我们任由阿瑞斯这样发狂，这个诺言就落空了。来吧，让我们也去参战。赫拉亲自套上戴金辔头的马，青春女神赫柏装好车轮；雅典娜脱下自己织的长袍，穿上宙斯的战衣，披上可怕的埃癸斯盾，戴上金盔，拿起又重又大的长矛。天门自动打开，她们驾车来到奥林波斯山顶，赫拉停下车，问坐在那里的宙斯：父亲宙斯，阿瑞斯这样残暴，杀害了这么多勇敢的希腊人，你难道不生气吗？阿佛洛狄忒和银弓的阿波罗倒是很高兴，是他们放出了这个不知法度的疯子。如果我狠狠地把阿瑞斯打出战场，你会生我的气吗？宙斯回答说：去吧，让掠夺战利品的雅典娜去对付他，她最会让他吃苦头。赫拉立刻扬鞭催马，来到特洛伊城下，化作嗓音如铜的斯滕托耳，大声鼓舞希腊人。','White-armed Hera, seeing the Achaeans perish in the fierce fighting, said to Athena: ''Alas, child of aegis-bearing Zeus, we promised Menelaus that he would sack Troy and return home safe; that promise is vain if we let Ares rage like this. Come, let us too join the fight.'' Hera herself harnessed the horses with golden headbands, and Hebe fitted the wheels; Athena put off the robe she had woven, donned the tunic of Zeus, threw the terrible aegis over her shoulders, set on the golden helmet and took up her great heavy spear. The gates of heaven opened of themselves, and they drove to the summit of Olympus, where Hera stopped and asked Zeus: ''Father Zeus, are you not angry with Ares for these violent deeds, slaying so many brave Achaeans? Aphrodite and Apollo of the silver bow are glad — they let loose this madman who knows no law. Will you be angry if I drive Ares from the field with a sore wound?'' Zeus answered: ''Go, set Athena the driver of spoil upon him; she more than any is wont to bring him pain.'' Hera lashed the horses, came down before Troy and, in the likeness of brazen-voiced Stentor, shouted courage into the Achaeans.','claim.v0360.iliad_ares.07','荷马《伊利亚特》第五卷第711–792行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_iliad_butler_ares.8','storyver.greek.iliad_butler_ares',8,'狄俄墨得斯的长矛','The spear of Diomedes','雅典娜找到正在擦拭伤口的狄俄墨得斯，责备他不如父亲提丢斯勇敢。狄俄墨得斯说：女神，是你吩咐我不要和别的神作战，只能刺伤阿佛洛狄忒；现在我看见阿瑞斯在战场上称王，所以才后退。雅典娜说：你不要怕阿瑞斯，也不要怕别的神，我会帮助你。先把马赶向阿瑞斯，近身刺他，别管他是什么疯狂的神。她把驾车的斯忒涅罗斯推下车，自己登上战车，橡木车轴在女神和英雄的重量下嘎吱作响。阿瑞斯正在剥取一个他刚杀死的埃托利亚勇士珀里法斯的铠甲；雅典娜戴上冥王哈得斯的隐身盔，不让阿瑞斯看见她。阿瑞斯看见狄俄墨得斯，丢下尸体，挺矛刺来，雅典娜伸手把矛推开；狄俄墨得斯随即刺出长矛，雅典娜把它推进阿瑞斯的小腹，正中他束腰带的地方。青铜的阿瑞斯发出一声吼叫，响得像九千、一万个人在战场上同时呐喊，希腊人和特洛伊人都吓得发抖。他像夏天暑热中从云里升起的一团黑雾，升上了宽阔的天空。','Athena found Diomedes cooling his wound and taunted him for being less bold than his father Tydeus. Diomedes answered: ''Goddess, you yourself told me not to fight the other gods, except to wound Aphrodite; now I see Ares lording it over the field, and so I gave way.'' Athena said: ''Fear neither Ares nor any other god; I will help you. Drive straight at Ares and strike him at close quarters, raving god though he is.'' She pushed Sthenelus from the chariot and mounted it herself, and the oaken axle groaned under the weight of the goddess and the hero. Ares was stripping the armour of huge Periphas, the bravest of the Aetolians, whom he had just slain; Athena put on the cap of Hades so that Ares should not see her. When Ares saw Diomedes he left the body and thrust at him with his spear, but Athena caught it and turned it aside; then Diomedes thrust, and Athena drove the spear into the pit of Ares'' belly where his belt went round him. Bronze Ares bellowed as loud as nine or ten thousand men shouting in battle, and Achaeans and Trojans trembled. Like a dark cloud rising from the heat of summer, he went up into the broad heaven.','claim.v0360.iliad_ares.08','荷马《伊利亚特》第五卷第825–863行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_iliad_butler_ares.9','storyver.greek.iliad_butler_ares',9,'宙斯的责备','The rebuke of Zeus','阿瑞斯飞快地来到众神居住的奥林波斯，满心痛苦地坐在宙斯身边，让父亲看他伤口里流出的神血，诉苦说：父亲宙斯，看到这种暴行你不生气吗？我们众神为了讨好凡人，总是在互相伤害，彼此吃尽苦头。我们都在怪你，因为你生了一个疯狂的、该死的女儿，她只想着做坏事。别的神都听你的话，唯独她，你从不责备，因为她是你自己生的。现在她怂恿狄俄墨得斯去打不死的神，先刺伤了阿佛洛狄忒，又像神一样向我冲来，要不是我跑得快，我早就躺在死人堆里了。宙斯皱着眉头看着他说：你这个反复无常的家伙，不要坐在我身边哭诉。你是奥林波斯所有神中我最憎恨的一个，你只喜欢争吵、战争和厮杀。你有你母亲赫拉那种不可容忍、不肯让步的脾气，我连说话都管不住她。不过你是我的孩子，你母亲为我生了你，我不能让你一直受苦。他吩咐医神派厄昂为他治伤，派厄昂敷上止痛的草药，伤口像无花果汁让奶凝结一样很快愈合；赫柏为他洗澡，给他穿上华美的衣服，他便坐在宙斯身边，又得意起来。','Ares came swiftly to Olympus, home of the gods, sat down in pain beside Zeus, showed him the immortal blood flowing from his wound and complained: ''Father Zeus, are you not angry at such violence? We gods suffer terribly at one another''s hands while helping mortals. We are all against you, because you begot that mad, accursed daughter who is always bent on mischief. All the other gods obey you, but her you never check, because you bore her yourself. Now she has set the son of Tydeus on the deathless gods: first he wounded Aphrodite, then he sprang at me like a god, and had my feet not carried me away I would be lying among the dead.'' Zeus looked at him darkly: ''Do not sit by me whining, you weathercock. You are the most hateful to me of all the gods on Olympus, for strife is always dear to you, and wars and battles. You have the intolerable, unyielding spirit of your mother Hera, whom I can scarcely rule with words. Yet you are my child, your mother bore you to me, and I cannot leave you in pain.'' He bade Paeon heal him; Paeon spread pain-killing herbs on the wound, and it closed as quickly as fig-juice curdles milk. Hebe bathed him and clothed him in fine raiment, and he sat down by Zeus, exulting again.','claim.v0360.iliad_ares.09','荷马《伊利亚特》第五卷第864–909行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_iliad_butler_ares.10','storyver.greek.iliad_butler_ares',10,'儿子之死','The death of his son','第十三卷里，特洛伊人得伊福玻斯向伊多墨纽斯投出长矛，没有投中，却刺中了阿瑞斯的儿子阿斯卡拉福斯的肩膀，阿斯卡拉福斯倒在尘土里，双手抓地而死。可是阿瑞斯并不知道儿子倒下了，因为他正和其他神一起坐在奥林波斯的金云下面，宙斯不许众神插手战事。到了第十五卷，赫拉回到奥林波斯，有意对众神说：阿瑞斯，你的儿子阿斯卡拉福斯战死了，那是强大的阿瑞斯称为自己儿子的人。阿瑞斯听了，双手拍着大腿，悲痛地说：住在奥林波斯的神们，如果我为了替儿子报仇而下到希腊人的船边，你们可不要责怪我，哪怕我注定要被宙斯的雷电击中，和死人一起躺在血泊和尘土里。他吩咐恐惧和溃逃——得摩斯和福波斯——为他套马，自己穿上闪亮的铠甲。眼看宙斯就要对众神发出更大的怒火，雅典娜怕众神都遭殃，冲出大殿，从阿瑞斯头上摘下头盔，从他肩上取下盾牌，从他手里夺下长矛，责备他说：你这个疯子，你要毁了自己！宙斯会回到奥林波斯，把我们所有的神都痛打一顿。她这样说着，让狂暴的阿瑞斯坐回了座位。','In Book Thirteen the Trojan Deiphobus hurled his spear at Idomeneus and missed, but struck Ascalaphus, son of Ares, through the shoulder, and he fell in the dust clutching the earth. Ares did not know his son had fallen, for he was sitting with the other gods on Olympus under golden clouds, held back by the will of Zeus from the fighting. In Book Fifteen Hera, returning to Olympus, said pointedly to the gods: ''Ares, your son Ascalaphus has fallen — the man whom mighty Ares calls his own.'' At this Ares struck his thighs with his hands and said in grief: ''Gods of Olympus, do not blame me if I go down to the ships of the Achaeans to avenge my son, even if I am fated to be struck by Zeus''s thunderbolt and lie in blood and dust among the dead.'' He bade Fear and Rout — Deimos and Phobos — yoke his horses, and put on his gleaming armour. Then, fearing that Zeus''s anger would fall still more heavily on all the gods, Athena rushed from the hall, tore the helmet from his head, the shield from his shoulders and the spear from his hand, and rebuked him: ''Madman, you are undone! Zeus will come back to Olympus and punish us all.'' So saying she made furious Ares sit down again.','claim.v0360.iliad_ares.10','荷马《伊利亚特》第十三卷第518–525行、第十五卷第110–142行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_iliad_butler_ares.11','storyver.greek.iliad_butler_ares',11,'众神参战','The gods go to war','第二十卷开头，阿喀琉斯重返战场，宙斯召集众神，宣布他们可以随意帮助自己喜欢的一方，免得阿喀琉斯违背命运，提前攻下特洛伊城。于是众神纷纷奔赴战场：赫拉、帕拉斯·雅典娜、环绕大地的波塞冬、带来好运的赫尔墨斯和瘸腿的赫菲斯托斯走向希腊人的船边；头盔闪亮的阿瑞斯则和长发的福玻斯·阿波罗、射手阿耳忒弥斯、勒托、河神克珊托斯和爱笑的阿佛洛狄忒一起走向特洛伊人一边。雅典娜一会儿站在壕沟边，一会儿站在海边，发出响亮的吼声；阿瑞斯则在另一边像一阵黑色的风暴，时而在特洛伊城的城头上，时而在西摩伊斯河边的卡利科洛涅山上，高声激励特洛伊人。宙斯在天上发出可怕的雷声，波塞冬从下面摇动大地，连冥王哈得斯都从宝座上惊跳起来，怕大地裂开，露出他那连众神都憎恶的阴暗住所。众神这样两两相对：阿波罗对波塞冬，雅典娜对战神阿瑞斯，阿耳忒弥斯对赫拉，勒托对赫尔墨斯，河神对赫菲斯托斯。','At the opening of Book Twenty, as Achilles returns to battle, Zeus calls the gods together and tells them they may help whichever side they please, lest Achilles overstep fate and take Troy before its time. So the gods go to war: Hera, Pallas Athena, earth-encircling Poseidon, luck-bringing Hermes and lame Hephaestus go to the ships of the Achaeans; Ares of the flashing helmet goes to the Trojans with long-haired Phoebus Apollo, Artemis the archer, Leto, the river Xanthus and laughter-loving Aphrodite. Athena shouts now from the trench, now from the shore; on the other side Ares, like a dark storm, shouts now from the citadel of Troy, now from Callicolone beside the Simois, urging on the Trojans. Zeus thunders terribly from heaven, Poseidon shakes the earth from below, and even Hades leaps from his throne in fear that the earth will split open and lay bare his dank dwelling, which the gods themselves abhor. So the gods are set against one another: Apollo against Poseidon, Athena against Enyalius the war god, Artemis against Hera, Leto against Hermes, and the river against Hephaestus.','claim.v0360.iliad_ares.11','荷马《伊利亚特》第二十卷第31–74行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_iliad_butler_ares.12','storyver.greek.iliad_butler_ares',12,'雅典娜的石头','Athena''s stone','到了第二十一卷，众神真的动起手来。刺穿盾牌的阿瑞斯第一个出手，手持青铜长矛冲向雅典娜，骂道：你这只狗蝇，为什么又凭着你的狂妄挑起众神的争斗？你还记得你怂恿狄俄墨得斯刺伤我的那一次吗？那时你亲手握着长矛刺向我，现在我要让你为你做过的一切付出代价。说着，他用长矛刺向她那可怕的埃癸斯盾，连宙斯的雷电也打不穿这面盾牌。雅典娜往后一退，用粗壮的手捡起平原上的一块石头，那块石头又黑又粗糙又巨大，是古时候的人放在那里作为田界的。她用石头砸中阿瑞斯的脖子，他四肢一软，倒在地上，身子躺倒后占了七个普勒特戎那么大的地方，头发沾满了尘土，身上的铠甲叮当作响。帕拉斯·雅典娜大笑起来，得意地对他说：傻瓜，你和我比力气，还不知道我比你强多少吗？这就是你母亲的诅咒应验了，她生你的气，要报复你，因为你抛弃了希腊人，转而帮助傲慢的特洛伊人。说完，她转过了明亮的眼睛。','In Book Twenty-One the gods come to blows. Ares, piercer of shields, began it, rushing at Athena with his bronze spear and reviling her: ''Dog-fly, why do you set the gods at strife again in your boundless insolence? Do you remember how you set Diomedes on to wound me, and yourself guided the spear against me? Now I will pay you back for all you did.'' He struck at her tasselled aegis, which not even Zeus''s thunderbolt can pierce. Athena drew back and with her strong hand picked up a stone lying on the plain — black, jagged and huge, which men of old had set there as a boundary mark. She struck Ares on the neck with it and loosed his limbs; he fell, and as he lay he covered seven plethra, his hair was soiled with dust and his armour rang about him. Pallas Athena laughed and boasted over him: ''Fool, have you not yet learned how much stronger I am, that you match your strength with mine? So are your mother''s curses fulfilled; she is angry and plots harm against you, because you have abandoned the Achaeans and are helping the insolent Trojans.'' Then she turned her bright eyes away.','claim.v0360.iliad_ares.12','荷马《伊利亚特》第二十一卷第391–414行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.greek_iliad_butler_ares.13','storyver.greek.iliad_butler_ares',13,'阿佛洛狄忒扶他离开','Aphrodite leads him away','宙斯的女儿阿佛洛狄忒拉住阿瑞斯的手，把不停呻吟、好不容易才缓过气来的他扶离战场。白臂的赫拉看见了，立刻对雅典娜说：真是糟糕，持埃癸斯盾的宙斯的孩子，不知疲倦的女儿，你看，那只狗蝇又把人类的祸害阿瑞斯从混战中领走了，快去追她。雅典娜心中欢喜，追了上去，伸出强壮的手打在阿佛洛狄忒的胸口上，阿佛洛狄忒的膝盖和心一下子都软了，两位神一起倒在了养育众生的大地上。雅典娜得意地说：但愿所有帮助特洛伊人、与希腊人作战的神都像他们一样，像阿佛洛狄忒这样大胆而坚定地来帮助阿瑞斯；那样我们早就攻下特洛伊城、结束战争了。白臂的赫拉听了，笑了起来。在《伊利亚特》里，阿瑞斯的故事就以这样的方式结束：战争之神没有战胜任何一位神，却一次又一次地被雅典娜打倒，最后被爱神扶着离开。荷马笔下的阿瑞斯，是一个强大、狂暴、却让众神和父亲都不喜欢的神，这也许正反映了希腊人对战争本身的复杂感受。','Aphrodite, daughter of Zeus, took Ares by the hand and led him away, groaning continually and scarcely able to get his breath. White-armed Hera saw it and at once said to Athena: ''Look, child of aegis-bearing Zeus, unwearied one: that dog-fly is again leading Ares, bane of men, out of the fray. Go after her!'' Athena sped after them gladly, and struck Aphrodite on the breast with her strong hand, so that her knees and heart failed her, and the two of them lay on the bountiful earth. Athena boasted: ''Would that all who help the Trojans against the Achaeans were as bold and steadfast as Aphrodite was in coming to help Ares; then we should long since have sacked Troy and ended the war.'' White-armed Hera smiled at her words. So Ares'' story in the Iliad ends: the god of war defeats no other god, is felled again and again by Athena, and is at last led away by the goddess of love. Homer''s Ares is mighty and furious, yet disliked by the gods and by his own father — perhaps a reflection of the Greeks'' mixed feelings about war itself.','claim.v0360.iliad_ares.13','荷马《伊利亚特》第二十一卷第415–433行。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.norse_skald_17_hrungnir.1','storyver.norse.skald_17_hrungnir',1,'赛马','The horse race','《诗语法》第17章讲道：索尔到东方去打巨怪了，奥丁骑着他的八足马斯莱普尼尔来到巨人国，去拜访一个名叫赫朗格尼尔的巨人。赫朗格尼尔问：这个戴着金盔、骑着马在天空和海上奔驰的人是谁？他说，这匹马真是好马。奥丁说，他愿意拿自己的头打赌，在巨人国里找不到一匹能和它相比的马。赫朗格尼尔说，这确实是一匹好马，可他有一匹步子大得多的马，名叫古尔法克西，意思是“金鬃”。赫朗格尼尔生了气，跳上自己的马，去追赶奥丁，想为他的狂言报复他。奥丁全速奔驰，始终跑在前面，翻过了一座小山；赫朗格尼尔怒气冲天，一心追赶，等他回过神来，已经冲进了阿斯加尔德的大门。斯诺里讲这个故事，原本是为了解释诗人们给索尔、给巨人、给磨石起的种种别称，可他把它讲成了北欧神话中最生动的故事之一。故事的开头是一场赛马和一次赌头，这与第35章洛基的赌注遥相呼应：在斯诺里的世界里，众神和巨人的冲突，常常从一句夸口开始。','Skáldskaparmál 17 tells: Thor had gone east to fight trolls, and Óðinn rode Sleipnir into Jötunheimr and came to a giant called Hrungnir. Hrungnir asked who this was with the golden helmet, riding through air and sea — a fine horse he had. Óðinn said he would wager his head that there was no horse as good in Jötunheimr. Hrungnir said it was a good horse, but he had one with a much longer stride, called Gullfaxi, Gold-mane. Hrungnir grew angry, leapt on his horse and galloped after Óðinn, meaning to pay him back for his boasting. Óðinn galloped so hard that he kept ahead over the next hill; and Hrungnir was in such a giant rage that before he knew it he had come in through the gates of Ásgarðr. Snorri tells the story to explain the many poetic names for Thor, for giants and for whetstones, yet he makes it one of the liveliest tales in Norse myth. It opens with a horse race and a wager of a head, echoing Loki''s wager in chapter 35: in Snorri''s world, conflicts between gods and giants often begin with a boast.','claim.v0360.skald_hrungnir.01','斯诺里《埃达·诗语法》第17章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.norse_skald_17_hrungnir.2','storyver.norse.skald_17_hrungnir',2,'闯进阿斯加尔德','Into Ásgarðr','赫朗格尼尔骑马冲进了众神的城门，他来到大殿门口时，阿萨神族的众神邀请他进去喝酒。这也许是北欧人待客的规矩：即使来者是巨人，只要他进了门，主人就要以礼相待。赫朗格尼尔走进大殿，要人给他拿酒。众神端来了索尔平常用来喝酒的那两只大碗，赫朗格尼尔把两碗酒都喝得一干二净。在北欧神话里，索尔是出了名的海量，在乌特加尔洛基的城堡里，他曾一口气想要喝干连着大海的角杯；用索尔的酒碗招待巨人，就像是把雷神的座位让给了他。斯诺里没有说众神为什么这样慷慨，也许是因为奥丁觉得这场赛马是自己引起的，也许是因为众神知道赫朗格尼尔是巨人中最强壮的一个，不想在索尔不在的时候和他起冲突。可是好客的主人很快就要后悔了：酒喝得越多，巨人的话就越狂妄，阿斯加尔德的大殿里，一场危机正在酝酿。','Hrungnir rode in through the gods'' gate, and when he came to the hall doors the Æsir invited him in to drink. Perhaps this was the custom of Norse hospitality: even a giant, once he was inside the door, must be treated as a guest. Hrungnir went into the hall and demanded drink. The gods brought the bowls that Thor usually drank from, and Hrungnir drained both. In Norse myth Thor is famous for his thirst — in Útgarða-Loki''s castle he tried to drain a horn whose end lay in the sea — and to serve the giant from Thor''s bowls is almost to give him the thunder god''s seat. Snorri does not say why the gods were so generous; perhaps Óðinn felt the race was his doing, perhaps the gods knew Hrungnir was the strongest of the giants and wanted no fight while Thor was away. But the hospitable hosts were soon to regret it: the more the giant drank, the wilder his words, and a crisis was brewing in the hall of Ásgarðr.','claim.v0360.skald_hrungnir.02','斯诺里《埃达·诗语法》第17章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.norse_skald_17_hrungnir.3','storyver.norse.skald_17_hrungnir',3,'醉后的狂言','The drunken boasts','赫朗格尼尔喝醉了，说起了大话。他说，他要把瓦尔哈拉整个搬起来，带回巨人国去；他要把阿斯加尔德沉入地下，杀死所有的神，只留下芙蕾雅和希芙，把她们带回自己家里。众神中只有芙蕾雅敢给他倒酒。赫朗格尼尔又说，他要把众神所有的麦酒都喝光。众神被他的狂妄激怒了，可是谁也不敢和他动手。在这一幕里，希芙再次以索尔妻子的身份出现在故事中：巨人点名要带走的两位女神，一位是众神中最美的芙蕾雅，一位是索尔的妻子希芙。对巨人来说，夺走雷神的妻子，就是对索尔最大的侮辱。北欧神话里，巨人觊觎女神是一个反复出现的主题：建造城墙的巨人要芙蕾雅、太阳和月亮作报酬，偷走锤子的巨人特里姆要芙蕾雅做新娘。而这一次，巨人的狂言最终让他付出了生命。芙蕾雅为他倒酒的细节也很耐人寻味：在众神都畏缩的时候，只有她敢走到这个醉醺醺的巨人面前。','Hrungnir got drunk and began to boast. He said he would lift up Valhalla and carry it to Jötunheimr, sink Ásgarðr and kill all the gods except Freyja and Sif, whom he would take home with him. Freyja was the only one who dared pour for him. He said he would drink up all the Æsir''s ale. The gods grew weary of his arrogance, yet none dared lay hands on him. In this scene Sif appears again as Thor''s wife: the two goddesses the giant names to carry off are Freyja, fairest of the goddesses, and Sif, wife of Thor. For a giant, to take the thunder god''s wife would be the greatest insult to Thor. The giant''s lust for the goddesses is a recurring theme in Norse myth: the giant builder of the wall demands Freyja, the sun and the moon as his wage, and the giant Þrymr who steals the hammer demands Freyja as his bride. This time the giant''s boasting will cost him his life. The detail of Freyja pouring for him is telling: when all the gods shrink back, she alone dares approach the drunken giant.','claim.v0360.skald_hrungnir.03','斯诺里《埃达·诗语法》第17章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.norse_skald_17_hrungnir.4','storyver.norse.skald_17_hrungnir',4,'索尔回来','Thor returns','众神听够了他的狂言，就喊出了索尔的名字。索尔立刻出现在大殿里，高高举起锤子，怒不可遏。他问：是谁让这个狡猾的巨人坐在这里喝酒？是谁允许赫朗格尼尔待在阿斯加尔德？为什么芙蕾雅像在众神的宴会上一样给他倒酒？赫朗格尼尔用不友善的眼光看着索尔，说是奥丁请他进来喝酒的，他受到奥丁的保护。索尔说，赫朗格尼尔会在离开以前后悔接受这个邀请。赫朗格尼尔说：杀死一个手无寸铁的人，对阿萨·索尔来说没有什么光彩；你若敢在边界的格约特纳加尔德与我决斗，那才算是勇敢。我真是太傻了，把盾牌和磨石留在了家里，要是我带着武器，我们现在就可以较量。你要是杀了一个没有武器的人，就是一个卑鄙的人。他向索尔提出了决斗。斯诺里说，索尔无论如何也不愿意错过这次决斗，因为从来没有人向他挑战过。这是索尔一生中第一次被正式邀请决斗，他的荣誉不允许他拒绝。','When the gods had had enough of his boasting they called on the name of Thor. At once Thor was in the hall, his hammer raised high, in a towering rage. He asked who had let this cunning giant sit drinking there, who had allowed Hrungnir to be in Ásgarðr, and why Freyja was serving him as at a feast of the Æsir. Hrungnir looked at Thor with no friendly eyes and said Óðinn had invited him to drink and he was under Óðinn''s protection. Thor said Hrungnir would regret that invitation before he left. Hrungnir said there was little glory for Ása-Þórr in killing an unarmed man; it would be braver to fight him on the border at Grjótúnagarðar. ''I was a fool to leave my shield and whetstone at home; if I had my weapons we could try it now. You will be a coward if you kill an unarmed man.'' So he challenged Thor to single combat. Snorri says Thor would not miss the duel for anything, for no one had ever challenged him before. It was the first time in Thor''s life he had been formally challenged, and his honour would not let him refuse.','claim.v0360.skald_hrungnir.04','斯诺里《埃达·诗语法》第17章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.norse_skald_17_hrungnir.5','storyver.norse.skald_17_hrungnir',5,'黏土巨人','The clay giant','赫朗格尼尔骑马飞奔回巨人国。他和索尔的决斗在巨人中间传开了，巨人们都觉得这件事关系重大：如果赫朗格尼尔死了，他们就要从索尔那里吃苦头了，因为赫朗格尼尔是他们中间最强壮的一个。于是巨人们在格约特纳加尔德用黏土造了一个巨人，他有九里高，腋下有三里宽。他们找不到一颗大得能配得上他的心，就取来一匹母马的心装了进去，可是这颗心在索尔到来的时候并不坚定。这个泥巨人名叫默库尔卡尔菲。巨人们的这个计划，透露出他们心里的恐惧：他们不敢完全依靠赫朗格尼尔一个人，就想用一个巨大的帮手来吓住索尔。可是一个用黏土造的巨人，配上一颗母马的心，外表再庞大，内心也是怯懦的。斯诺里用这个细节暗示了接下来的结果：决斗还没开始，巨人一方就已经露出了弱点。这也是北欧神话里难得一见的“造人”情节：巨人们也会像众神用树干造人那样，用泥土造出一个有生命的帮手。','Hrungnir galloped back to Jötunheimr. His duel with Thor was much talked of among the giants; they thought a great deal hung on it, for if Hrungnir died they would have trouble from Thor, Hrungnir being the strongest of them. So the giants made a man of clay at Grjótúnagarðar, nine leagues tall and three leagues broad under the arms. They could find no heart big enough for him until they took one from a mare, and it was not steady when Thor came. The clay giant was called Mökkurkálfi. The plan betrays the giants'' fear: not daring to rely on Hrungnir alone, they hoped a vast helper would daunt Thor. But a giant of clay with a mare''s heart, however huge outside, is timid within. Snorri uses the detail to hint at what follows: before the duel has begun, the giants'' side already shows its weakness. It is also a rare ''making of a man'' in Norse myth: the giants, like the gods who made people from logs, shape a living helper from earth.','claim.v0360.skald_hrungnir.05','斯诺里《埃达·诗语法》第17章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.norse_skald_17_hrungnir.6','storyver.norse.skald_17_hrungnir',6,'石头的心','The heart of stone','赫朗格尼尔自己的心则是出了名的：它是用坚硬的石头做的，有棱有角，是三角形的，后来人们刻的一种叫做“赫朗格尼尔之心”的符号，就是照着它的样子画的。他的头也是石头做的。他的盾牌也是石头做的，又宽又厚，他在格约特纳加尔德等索尔的时候，就把这面盾牌举在身前；他的武器是一块磨石，他把它扛在肩上，样子十分可怕。在他身边站着那个泥巨人默库尔卡尔菲，泥巨人非常害怕，据说他一看见索尔，就吓得尿了出来。斯诺里在这里把两个巨人对比得非常鲜明：一个是石心石头的强敌，一个是黏土马心的胆小鬼。赫朗格尼尔的石头之身，正好说明了他的力量：他是巨人中最坚硬、最难对付的一个。北欧的吟唱诗人常用“赫朗格尼尔的头骨”“赫朗格尼尔的脚下”等说法来指盾牌和石头，这些肯宁都是从这个故事来的。决斗的场地已经准备好，双方都在等待：一边是石头的巨人和泥巨人，一边即将到来的是雷神和他的仆人。','Hrungnir himself had a famous heart: it was made of hard stone, spiky and three-cornered, like the carved symbol that has since been called Hrungnir''s heart. His head too was of stone. His shield was of stone, broad and thick, and he held it before him as he stood at Grjótúnagarðar waiting for Thor; his weapon was a whetstone, which he carried on his shoulder, and he looked a fearsome sight. Beside him stood the clay giant Mökkurkálfi, who was terrified; they say he wet himself when he saw Thor. Snorri sets the two giants in sharp contrast: one a formidable foe with a stone heart and stone head, the other a coward of clay with a mare''s heart. Hrungnir''s body of stone shows his might: he is the hardest and most dangerous of the giants. Norse court poets use phrases like ''Hrungnir''s skull'' and ''the soles of Hrungnir''s feet'' for shields and stones, kennings that come from this tale. The field is ready and both sides wait: on one, the stone giant and the clay giant; coming toward them, the thunder god and his servant.','claim.v0360.skald_hrungnir.06','斯诺里《埃达·诗语法》第17章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.norse_skald_17_hrungnir.7','storyver.norse.skald_17_hrungnir',7,'提亚尔菲的诡计','Thjálfi''s trick','索尔带着他的仆人提亚尔菲前去决斗。提亚尔菲先跑到赫朗格尼尔站的地方，对他说：巨人，你站在这里，把盾牌举在前面，太不小心了。索尔已经看见你了，他会从地底下钻出来，从下面攻击你。赫朗格尼尔听了，就把盾牌放到脚下，站在盾牌上，双手握住磨石。提亚尔菲是索尔在旅途中收下的仆人，在乌特加尔洛基的城堡里，他曾和思想赛跑；在这里，他用一句谎话就让巨人放下了防御。这个诡计说明，北欧神话里的众神一方，并不只靠蛮力取胜。索尔从来不会从地下钻出来——他是雷神，他的到来总是伴着天上的闪电与雷鸣；巨人却信以为真，把唯一能保护自己的盾牌踩在了脚下。斯诺里用这一幕为接下来的决斗做了铺垫：当雷神真的从天上降临时，赫朗格尼尔已经无法用盾牌抵挡了。提亚尔菲的谎话也许不太光彩，可在北欧人看来，用智谋打败强敌，正是一个好仆人的本事。','Thor went to the duel with his servant Thjálfi. Thjálfi ran ahead to where Hrungnir stood and said: ''Giant, you are standing carelessly with your shield in front of you. Thor has seen you; he will come at you from below, under the ground.'' Hrungnir pushed the shield under his feet and stood on it, holding the whetstone in both hands. Thjálfi was the servant Thor took on during his travels; in Útgarða-Loki''s castle he raced against Thought. Here, with a single lie, he makes the giant drop his guard. The trick shows that the gods'' side in Norse myth does not win by brute force alone. Thor never comes from under the ground — he is the thunder god, and his coming is always with lightning and thunder in the sky — yet the giant believed it and set his only protection under his feet. Snorri thus prepares the duel: when the thunder god truly comes down from the sky, Hrungnir can no longer shield himself. Thjálfi''s lie may not be noble, but to the Norse, beating a strong foe by wit was just what a good servant should do.','claim.v0360.skald_hrungnir.07','斯诺里《埃达·诗语法》第17章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.norse_skald_17_hrungnir.8','storyver.norse.skald_17_hrungnir',8,'锤与磨石','Hammer and whetstone','接着，赫朗格尼尔看见了闪电，听见了巨大的雷声，然后他看见了索尔带着神的愤怒而来。索尔飞快地冲过来，挥起锤子，从很远的地方就把它掷向赫朗格尼尔。赫朗格尼尔双手举起磨石，也把它掷了出去。锤子和磨石在空中相撞，磨石碎成了两半：一半落在地上，世上所有的磨石山都是从那一半来的；另一半击中了索尔的头，他向前扑倒在地上。而锤子妙尔尼尔正中赫朗格尼尔的头，把他的头颅砸成了碎片。赫朗格尼尔向前倒下，压在索尔身上，一条腿正好横在索尔的脖子上。这一幕是整个故事的高潮，也是斯诺里最精彩的描写之一。锤子和磨石的相撞，不仅决定了胜负，还解释了自然界的一种现象：人们开采磨石的那些岩层，原来是巨人武器的碎片。索尔赢了决斗，却也付出了代价：一块磨石的碎片嵌进了他的头里，而他自己又被死去的巨人压在了地上，动弹不得。胜利的雷神，此刻需要有人来救他。','Then Hrungnir saw lightning and heard great thunderclaps, and he saw Thor in his godly wrath. Thor came on at tremendous speed, swung up the hammer and threw it from a great distance at Hrungnir. Hrungnir raised the whetstone in both hands and threw it back. Hammer and whetstone met in the air, and the whetstone broke in two: one part fell to the ground, and from it come all whetstone rocks; the other struck Thor''s head, so that he fell forward to the ground. But the hammer Mjölnir struck Hrungnir in the middle of the head and smashed his skull into small fragments, and he fell forward over Thor, so that his leg lay across Thor''s neck. This is the climax of the tale and one of Snorri''s finest descriptions. The clash of hammer and whetstone decides the fight and also explains a natural feature: the rocks from which whetstones are quarried are fragments of a giant''s weapon. Thor wins the duel but pays a price: a piece of whetstone is lodged in his head, and he lies pinned under the dead giant. The victorious thunder god now needs someone to rescue him.','claim.v0360.skald_hrungnir.08','斯诺里《埃达·诗语法》第17章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.norse_skald_17_hrungnir.9','storyver.norse.skald_17_hrungnir',9,'泥人倒下','The clay giant falls','就在索尔和赫朗格尼尔交手的同时，提亚尔菲向泥巨人默库尔卡尔菲发起了攻击。斯诺里只用了一句话来描写这场战斗：泥巨人倒下了，倒得很不光彩。这个九里高、三里宽的庞然大物，本该是巨人们用来吓倒索尔的秘密武器，却被索尔的一个仆人轻易地打倒了。一颗母马的心，终究撑不起一个巨人的身体。北欧的吟唱诗人在描写这场战斗时，常把重点放在索尔和赫朗格尼尔身上，泥巨人只是一个可笑的陪衬。可是这一句话也有它的意义：它说明巨人们的计谋全都落空了，他们以为造一个更大的身体就能战胜雷神，却忘了真正决定胜负的是勇气。提亚尔菲在这个故事里两次立功：一次用谎话让赫朗格尼尔放下盾牌，一次亲手打倒了泥巨人。这个凡间出身的少年，在众神的冒险中一点也不逊色。可是接下来，他就会发现，有一件事是他做不到的。','While Thor fought Hrungnir, Thjálfi attacked the clay giant Mökkurkálfi. Snorri gives the fight a single sentence: the clay giant fell with little glory. This monster nine leagues tall and three broad, meant to be the giants'' secret weapon to terrify Thor, was easily felled by Thor''s servant. A mare''s heart could not hold up a giant''s body. Norse court poets describing the battle usually focus on Thor and Hrungnir, the clay giant being only a comic foil. Yet the sentence matters: it shows that all the giants'' schemes came to nothing. They thought a bigger body could beat the thunder god and forgot that courage decides a fight. Thjálfi wins glory twice in this tale — once with the lie that made Hrungnir put down his shield, once by felling the clay giant himself. This boy of mortal birth holds his own in the adventures of the gods. But next he will find there is one thing he cannot do.','claim.v0360.skald_hrungnir.09','斯诺里《埃达·诗语法》第17章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.norse_skald_17_hrungnir.10','storyver.norse.skald_17_hrungnir',10,'三岁的马格尼','Three-year-old Magni','提亚尔菲走到索尔身边，想把赫朗格尼尔的腿从他身上搬开，可是怎么也搬不动。所有的阿萨神听说索尔倒下了，都赶了过来，想把那条腿抬开，可是谁也抬不动。这时，马格尼来了，他是索尔和女巨人雅恩萨克萨的儿子，当时才三岁。他一把就把赫朗格尼尔的腿从索尔身上推开，说：父亲，我来晚了，这真是太可惜了，要是我早一点遇见这个巨人，我想我只用拳头就能把他打死，送他去冥界。索尔站起来，欢迎他的儿子，说他长大以后一定会非常强壮。索尔说：我要把赫朗格尼尔的马古尔法克西送给你。奥丁却说，索尔把这样一匹好马送给一个女巨人的儿子，而不是送给他的父亲，是不对的。这一章是马格尼在北欧神话中最重要的出场：一个三岁的孩子，做到了所有神都做不到的事。在《欺骗古鲁菲》里，斯诺里还说，诸神黄昏之后，马格尼和他的兄弟莫迪将继承索尔的锤子妙尔尼尔。这个刚刚学会走路就能举起巨人之腿的孩子，正是未来世界里雷神力量的继承者。','Thjálfi went to Thor and tried to lift Hrungnir''s leg off him, but could not. All the Æsir came when they heard Thor had fallen and tried to lift the leg, and none of them could move it. Then Magni came, son of Thor and the giantess Járnsaxa; he was three years old. He threw Hrungnir''s leg off Thor and said: ''What a shame, father, that I came so late; I think I would have sent this giant to Hel with my fist if I had met him.'' Thor stood up, welcomed his son and said he would surely grow up strong. ''And I will give you the horse Gullfaxi,'' said Thor. But Óðinn said Thor did wrong to give so fine a horse to the son of a giantess and not to his father. This chapter is Magni''s most important appearance in Norse myth: a three-year-old does what all the gods could not. In Gylfaginning Snorri also says that after Ragnarök Magni and his brother Móði will inherit Thor''s hammer Mjölnir. The child who could lift a giant''s leg when scarcely able to walk is the heir of the thunder god''s strength in the world to come.','claim.v0360.skald_hrungnir.10','斯诺里《埃达·诗语法》第17章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.norse_skald_17_hrungnir.11','storyver.norse.skald_17_hrungnir',11,'格罗亚的咒语','Gróa''s spells','索尔回到了他的家斯鲁德万加尔，可是那块磨石还嵌在他的头里。于是来了一位女巫，她叫格罗亚，是勇敢的奥尔万迪尔的妻子。她对着索尔唱起咒语，一直唱到磨石开始松动。在北欧人的世界里，像格罗亚这样的女巫，通过唱诵咒语来治病、预言和施法，是一种受人敬畏的本领；《女巫的预言》本身就是一位女巫对奥丁唱出的诗。格罗亚的咒语眼看就要成功了，索尔感觉到磨石正在松动，心里很高兴，想要报答她，让她也高兴高兴。斯诺里在这里写出了索尔性格中温和的一面：这位在战场上毫不留情的雷神，对一个为他治伤的女子满怀感激，想用一个好消息来回报她。可正是这份好意，让事情出了差错。在北欧神话里，好心办坏事并不少见，而这一次的后果，将永远留在雷神的头上。斯诺里为这个小插曲安排了一个出人意料的结局，也借此解释了一条古老的禁忌。','Thor went home to Þrúðvangar, and the whetstone remained stuck in his head. Then came a seeress called Gróa, wife of Aurvandill the Bold. She chanted her spells over Thor until the whetstone began to work loose. In the Norse world a seeress like Gróa, who healed, prophesied and worked magic by chanting spells, was held in awe; the Völuspá itself is a seeress''s song to Óðinn. Gróa''s spell was on the point of success; Thor felt the stone loosening and was glad, and wished to reward her and make her glad in turn. Snorri shows a gentle side of Thor here: the thunder god, merciless in battle, is full of gratitude to a woman healing his wound and wants to repay her with good news. Yet it is just that kindness that makes things go wrong. In Norse myth good intentions often lead to bad results, and this time the consequence will stay in the thunder god''s head for ever. Snorri gives this small episode an unexpected ending, and uses it to explain an old taboo.','claim.v0360.skald_hrungnir.11','斯诺里《埃达·诗语法》第17章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.norse_skald_17_hrungnir.12','storyver.norse.skald_17_hrungnir',12,'奥尔万迪尔的脚趾','Aurvandill''s toe','索尔想让格罗亚高兴，就告诉她一个消息。他说，他曾经从北方涉水渡过埃利伐加尔河，把奥尔万迪尔装在一个篮子里，背在背上，从巨人国一路背了回来。为了证明这件事，他说，奥尔万迪尔有一根脚趾从篮子里伸了出来，被冻僵了，索尔就把它折断，扔到天上，把它变成了一颗星星，叫做“奥尔万迪尔的脚趾”。索尔说，奥尔万迪尔用不了多久就会回到家里。格罗亚听了这个消息，高兴得不得了，竟然忘了她的咒语，磨石也就不再松动，一直留在了索尔的头里。斯诺里在最后说：所以，人们不可以把磨石扔过地板，因为那样的话，索尔头里的那块石头就会动起来。这个故事从一次赛马开始，经过一场决斗，最后落在一条日常的禁忌上。马格尼的力量、提亚尔菲的机智、格罗亚的咒语，都围绕着雷神展开；而那块永远嵌在索尔头里的磨石，成了这场胜利留下的永久的伤痕。','Thor wanted to make Gróa glad, so he told her some news. He said he had waded from the north across the river Élivágar carrying Aurvandill in a basket on his back out of Jötunheimr. As proof, he said that one of Aurvandill''s toes had stuck out of the basket and frozen, so Thor broke it off and threw it up into the sky and made it into a star called Aurvandill''s Toe. Thor said it would not be long before Aurvandill came home. Gróa was so delighted at the news that she forgot her spells, and the whetstone stopped coming loose and stayed in Thor''s head. Snorri ends: and so it is forbidden to throw a whetstone across the floor, for then the stone in Thor''s head stirs. The tale begins with a horse race, passes through a duel, and comes to rest on an everyday taboo. Magni''s strength, Thjálfi''s wit and Gróa''s spells all turn around the thunder god, and the whetstone lodged for ever in Thor''s head becomes the lasting scar of his victory.','claim.v0360.skald_hrungnir.12','斯诺里《埃达·诗语法》第17章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_bentresh_stela_louvre_c284.1','storyver.egyptian.bentresh_stela_louvre_c284',1,'那哈林的贡品','Tribute in Naharin','石碑以拉美西斯二世的全套王衔开头：荷鲁斯，强壮的公牛，戴着美丽的王冠；上下埃及之王乌塞尔玛阿特拉-塞泰普恩拉，拉之子拉美西斯-美瑞阿蒙，受底比斯众神之主阿蒙-拉宠爱的人。接着，碑文讲道：陛下按照每年的惯例来到那哈林——那是幼发拉底河一带的地方——各国的王公都怀着和平之心前来，带着贡品向陛下的威名致敬。他们背着金子、银子、青金石、绿松石和神之国度出产的各种木材，一个比一个献得多。巴赫坦的王公也让人送上贡品，他把自己的长女放在贡品的最前面，向陛下致敬，祈求陛下赐给他生命。碑文说，这位公主在陛下的眼中比任何东西都美丽。这样的开头读起来很像一份王家记功的碑文：一位伟大的国王，四方来朝的盛况，各国争相献礼。可是这块石碑真正要讲的，不是国王的武功，而是一位神的远行。国王的荣耀只是一个框架，主角很快就会换成神。','The stela opens with the full titulary of Ramesses II: Horus, Mighty Bull, beautiful of crowns; King of Upper and Lower Egypt Usermaatre-Setepenre, Son of Re Ramesses-Meryamun, beloved of Amun-Re, lord of the gods of Thebes. Then it tells: His Majesty was in Naharin — the land of the Euphrates — as was his custom each year, and the princes of every foreign land came bowing in peace to His Majesty''s might, bringing tribute. They carried gold, silver, lapis lazuli, turquoise and every kind of wood of God''s Land on their backs, each trying to outdo his fellow. The prince of Bakhtan also had his tribute brought, and he placed his eldest daughter at its head, praising His Majesty and begging life from him. The stela says the princess was more beautiful to His Majesty than anything. The opening reads like a royal record of glory: a great king, princes from every quarter, each vying to give more. Yet what the stela truly tells is not the king''s deeds but a god''s journey. The king''s glory is only the frame; the hero will soon be a god.','claim.v0360.bentresh.01','本特蕾什石碑（卢浮宫 C 284）开头：那哈林的贡品。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_bentresh_stela_louvre_c284.2','storyver.egyptian.bentresh_stela_louvre_c284',2,'王后奈芙鲁拉','Queen Neferure','陛下很喜欢这位巴赫坦的公主，娶她为妻，在王衔中写下她的名字，称她为“伟大的王后奈芙鲁拉”，意思是“拉的美丽”。陛下回到埃及以后，她在宫中做了王后应做的一切事。碑文没有详细描写她的生活，只用一句话说明她已经完全成为埃及的王后。在古埃及历史上，法老娶外国公主是常见的外交手段，拉美西斯二世确实娶过赫梯国王的女儿，并给她取了埃及名字；有学者认为，本特蕾什石碑的故事可能就是从这段历史记忆中演变出来的，“巴赫坦”也许是“赫梯”一名在后世流传中的变形，也有人认为它指远在东方的巴克特里亚。无论如何，碑文的作者并不关心地理的准确，而是要借一位远方公主和她的家人，引出一个关于神的故事。王后奈芙鲁拉自己在故事中再也没有直接出现，可是正因为她，埃及和巴赫坦这两个远隔千里的地方，从此有了亲戚般的联系。下一次巴赫坦的使者来到埃及时，带来的将是一个坏消息。','His Majesty was pleased with the princess of Bakhtan and took her as his wife, writing her name in the royal titulary as Great Royal Wife Neferure, ''the beauty of Re''. When His Majesty returned to Egypt she did everything that a queen does. The stela does not describe her life, but in one sentence makes clear that she had become wholly a queen of Egypt. Marrying foreign princesses was a common diplomatic move for pharaohs, and Ramesses II did marry a daughter of the Hittite king and gave her an Egyptian name; some scholars think the Bentresh tale grew from the memory of that history, and that Bakhtan may be a later distortion of the Hittite land, while others think of distant Bactria. Either way, the author was not concerned with exact geography but used a far-off princess and her family to lead into a story about a god. Queen Neferure herself never appears again in the tale, yet because of her, Egypt and Bakhtan, far apart, are now bound like kin. The next time a messenger from Bakhtan comes to Egypt, he will bring bad news.','claim.v0360.bentresh.02','本特蕾什石碑（卢浮宫 C 284）公主成为王后奈芙鲁拉。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_bentresh_stela_louvre_c284.3','storyver.egyptian.bentresh_stela_louvre_c284',3,'底比斯的节日与信使','The feast at Thebes and the messenger','碑文接着说：在陛下在位的第二十三年，夏季第二个月的第二十二天，陛下正在胜利之城底比斯——众城之主——为他的父亲阿蒙-拉，两地王座之主，举行南方伊佩特的美好节日，那是阿蒙从远古以来就最喜爱的地方。这时有人来报告陛下说：巴赫坦王公派来的使者到了，带着许多给王后的礼物。使者被带到陛下面前，向陛下行礼，说：向您致敬，九弓之主的太阳！让我们在您面前得以生存。他再次向陛下叩拜，然后说出了来意：我来见陛下，我的主人，是为了本特蕾什，王后奈芙鲁拉的妹妹。一种病进入了她的身体。愿陛下派一位有知识的人去看看她。南方伊佩特的节日，就是在卢克索神庙举行的奥佩特节，阿蒙的神像在节日期间从卡纳克被抬到卢克索。碑文把这个消息安排在底比斯最盛大的节日里传来，这一细节把故事和底比斯的神庙紧紧地联系在一起：接下来的一切，都将在底比斯众神的注视下发生。','The stela goes on: in regnal year 23, second month of summer, day 22, His Majesty was in Thebes the Victorious, mistress of cities, performing the rites for his father Amun-Re, lord of the Thrones of the Two Lands, at his beautiful Feast of Southern Ipet, his favourite place since the beginning. Then one came to tell His Majesty: a messenger of the prince of Bakhtan has come with many gifts for the queen. He was brought before His Majesty with his gifts and said, saluting him: ''Hail to you, Sun of the Nine Bows! Let us live before you.'' He kissed the ground before His Majesty again and spoke: ''I have come to Your Majesty, my lord, on account of Bentresh, the younger sister of Queen Neferure. An illness has entered her body. May Your Majesty send a man of knowledge to see her.'' The Feast of Southern Ipet is the Opet festival held at Luxor temple, when Amun''s image was carried from Karnak to Luxor. Setting the news at Thebes''s greatest feast ties the tale closely to the Theban temples: all that follows will happen under the eyes of the gods of Thebes.','claim.v0360.bentresh.03','本特蕾什石碑（卢浮宫 C 284）第二十三年：南方伊佩特节时的信使。','碑文中的年份与日期据现代学者的释读，不对应可考的史实。'),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_bentresh_stela_louvre_c284.4','storyver.egyptian.bentresh_stela_louvre_c284',4,'生命之屋的书吏','The scribe of the House of Life','陛下说：把生命之屋的书吏和宫中的官员们带来。他们立刻被带到了陛下面前。陛下对他们说：我召你们来，是要你们从你们中间找出一个心思聪明、手指灵巧的人。王家书吏杰胡提-姆-赫布来到陛下面前，陛下就命令他和那位使者一同前往巴赫坦。“生命之屋”是古埃及神庙里抄写和保存宗教、医学与魔法典籍的地方，那里的书吏通晓咒语和疗法，被认为是最有学问的人。书吏到了巴赫坦，见到了本特蕾什，发现她正处在被一个精灵附身的状态——碑文用的词，可以理解为一种灵或恶灵。他发现这是一个难以对付的敌人，他的学问和咒语都无法把它赶走。书吏在这里代表了人间最好的知识：他是从众人中挑选出来的智者，可是面对附在公主身上的精灵，他也束手无策。碑文用这个失败为下文作了铺垫：凡人的学问做不到的事，需要神亲自出面。书吏回到了埃及，巴赫坦的公主依然病着。','His Majesty said: ''Bring me the scribes of the House of Life and the officials of the residence.'' They were brought to him at once. His Majesty said: ''I have summoned you so that you find among you a man wise of heart and skilled with his fingers.'' The royal scribe Djehutyemheb came before His Majesty, and His Majesty ordered him to go to Bakhtan with the messenger. The House of Life was the place in Egyptian temples where religious, medical and magical books were copied and kept; its scribes knew spells and remedies and were thought the most learned of men. The scribe reached Bakhtan and found Bentresh in the state of one possessed by a spirit — the word may be understood as a spirit or a hostile ghost. He found it an enemy too strong for him; his learning and his spells could not drive it out. The scribe stands for the best of human knowledge, chosen from many, yet before the spirit in the princess he is helpless. His failure prepares what follows: what human learning cannot do needs a god in person. The scribe returned to Egypt, and the princess of Bakhtan remained ill.','claim.v0360.bentresh.04','本特蕾什石碑（卢浮宫 C 284）书吏杰胡提-姆-赫布。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_bentresh_stela_louvre_c284.5','storyver.egyptian.bentresh_stela_louvre_c284',5,'第二次请求','The second request','到了陛下在位的第二十六年，夏季的第一个月，陛下又在底比斯为阿蒙举行节日的时候，巴赫坦的王公再一次派使者来到陛下面前，说：我的主人陛下，请陛下派一位神来，和这个精灵作战。这一次，巴赫坦人要的不再是一位有学问的人，而是一位神。从第一次求救到第二次求救，中间隔了三年。碑文没有讲这三年里公主经历了什么，只用这个时间间隔暗示：病魔一直没有离开她，巴赫坦的王公已经无计可施。对古埃及人来说，神并不只住在遥远的天上，他们的力量也寄寓在神庙中的神像里。神像可以被抬出神庙，参加节日的游行，可以给人神谕，甚至可以出远门，到很远的地方去为人治病。这个故事最特别的地方，正是它讲述了一尊神像的远行。在下一章里，国王将去请求底比斯的孔苏，让他决定派谁前往巴赫坦。故事的主角，从这里开始真正变成了神。','In regnal year 26, first month of summer, while His Majesty was again at Thebes celebrating the feast of Amun, the prince of Bakhtan sent another messenger before His Majesty, saying: ''O sovereign, my lord, may Your Majesty send a god to fight with this spirit.'' This time the people of Bakhtan asked not for a learned man but for a god. Three years lay between the first plea and the second. The stela says nothing of what the princess suffered in those years; the gap alone tells us that the affliction never left her and that the prince of Bakhtan had no other recourse. To the Egyptians, gods did not live only in a far-off heaven: their power also dwelt in the images in their temples. An image could be carried out for festival processions, could give oracles, and could even travel far to heal. What is most unusual in this tale is that it tells of a divine image''s long journey. In the next chapter the king will go to Khonsu of Thebes to decide whom to send to Bakhtan. From here on, the hero of the tale is truly a god.','claim.v0360.bentresh.05','本特蕾什石碑（卢浮宫 C 284）第二十六年：第二次请求。','碑文中的年份与日期据现代学者的释读，不对应可考的史实。'),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_bentresh_stela_louvre_c284.6','storyver.egyptian.bentresh_stela_louvre_c284',6,'两位孔苏','The two Khonsus','陛下来到底比斯的孔苏·奈弗尔霍特普面前，说：我的好主人，我又一次来到您面前，是为了巴赫坦王公的女儿。于是，人们把底比斯的孔苏·奈弗尔霍特普抬到了驱邪者孔苏面前——驱邪者孔苏是驱赶病魔的伟大的神。陛下对底比斯的孔苏·奈弗尔霍特普说：我的好主人，请您把脸转向驱邪者孔苏，这位伟大的神，驱赶病魔的神，让他前往巴赫坦。神用力点了两次头。陛下又说：请把您的护佑交给他，我要让他的神像前往巴赫坦，去救巴赫坦王公的女儿。底比斯的孔苏·奈弗尔霍特普用力地点头，把他的护佑四重地交给了驱邪者孔苏。这一章展示了古埃及宗教中一个独特的观念：同一位神可以有多个神像和多种形式，它们之间可以互相传递力量。孔苏是底比斯三神中阿蒙和穆特的儿子，是一位月神，也被认为能驱除疾病。神像通过“点头”来回答国王的问题，这正是新王国以后神谕的常见方式：抬神像的祭司们感受到神像的动静，就把它当作神的回答。','His Majesty went before Khonsu-in-Thebes-Neferhotep and said: ''My good lord, I am once again before you on account of the daughter of the prince of Bakhtan.'' Then Khonsu-in-Thebes-Neferhotep was carried to Khonsu-the-Provider, the great god who expels disease demons. His Majesty said to Khonsu-in-Thebes-Neferhotep: ''My good lord, turn your face to Khonsu-the-Provider, the great god who expels disease demons, and let him go to Bakhtan.'' The god nodded strongly twice. His Majesty said: ''Give him your protection; I will have His Majesty''s image go to Bakhtan to save the daughter of the prince of Bakhtan.'' Khonsu-in-Thebes-Neferhotep nodded strongly and gave his protection fourfold to Khonsu-the-Provider. The chapter shows a distinctive Egyptian idea: one god may have several images and forms, which can pass power to one another. Khonsu, son of Amun and Mut in the Theban triad, is a moon god also believed to drive away illness. The image answers the king by ''nodding'' — the usual form of oracle from the New Kingdom on, when the priests carrying an image felt its movement and took it as the god''s reply.','claim.v0360.bentresh.06','本特蕾什石碑（卢浮宫 C 284）底比斯的孔苏·奈弗尔霍特普与驱邪者孔苏。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_bentresh_stela_louvre_c284.7','storyver.egyptian.bentresh_stela_louvre_c284',7,'十七个月的旅程','Seventeen months on the road','陛下下令，让驱邪者孔苏登上一条大船，由五条小船护送，还配备了战车和许多马匹，从东到西护送着神前往巴赫坦。这位神走了整整一年零五个月，才到达巴赫坦。碑文特意记下这个漫长的时间，好让读者感受到巴赫坦有多远、埃及的神有多么不辞辛劳。神像一到，巴赫坦的王公就带着他的士兵和官员们来到驱邪者孔苏面前，扑倒在地，说：请您到我们这里来，按照上下埃及之王乌塞尔玛阿特拉-塞泰普恩拉的命令，对我们施恩吧。这里的场面和故事开头形成了对照：开头是各国王公向埃及国王俯首献礼，这里则是巴赫坦的王公向埃及的神俯伏在地。神的到来，是埃及国王对盟友的恩赐，也是埃及众神的力量远达异国的证明。古埃及确实有派遣神像出国的记载：据阿马尔纳书信等文献，米坦尼国王曾把伊什塔尔的神像送到埃及，为法老治病。本特蕾什石碑把这种做法倒了过来，让埃及的神前往异国。','His Majesty had Khonsu-the-Provider placed in a great bark, with five boats as escort, and chariots and many horses on the east and the west, to take the god to Bakhtan. The god travelled a year and five months before he reached Bakhtan. The stela records this long time to make the reader feel how far Bakhtan was and how tireless the Egyptian god. When the image arrived, the prince of Bakhtan came with his soldiers and officials before Khonsu-the-Provider and threw himself on his belly, saying: ''Come to us, be gracious to us, at the command of the King of Upper and Lower Egypt Usermaatre-Setepenre.'' The scene mirrors the opening: there the princes of every land bowed before the king of Egypt with tribute; here the prince of Bakhtan lies prostrate before Egypt''s god. The god''s coming is the Egyptian king''s favour to an ally and proof that the power of Egypt''s gods reaches foreign lands. Egyptian records do mention divine images sent abroad: the Amarna letters tell of a Mitannian king sending an image of Ishtar to Egypt to heal the pharaoh. The Bentresh Stela turns the practice around, sending Egypt''s god abroad.','claim.v0360.bentresh.07','本特蕾什石碑（卢浮宫 C 284）前往巴赫坦的旅程。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_bentresh_stela_louvre_c284.8','storyver.egyptian.bentresh_stela_louvre_c284',8,'公主痊愈','The princess is healed','神来到本特蕾什所在的地方，为巴赫坦王公的女儿施行了护佑。她立刻就好了。碑文对治愈的过程只写了这么简单的一句，与前面书吏的无能为力形成了鲜明的对比：书吏用尽了学问，什么也做不了；神一到，病就消失了。这里的“护佑”，就是底比斯的孔苏·奈弗尔霍特普交给驱邪者孔苏的那份四重的护佑，它通过神像传到了公主身上。古埃及人相信，许多疾病是由恶灵、死者的鬼魂或敌对的神灵造成的，治病的方法除了药物，还有咒语、护身符和神的力量。“驱邪者孔苏”这个名字本身就说明了他的职能：他是专门驱赶致病恶灵的神。在埃及人留下的许多护身符和咒语中，都能看到向孔苏祈求健康的话语。本特蕾什的痊愈，是这个故事的中心事件，可是故事并没有在这里结束。接下来发生的事情，在古埃及文献中非常少见：那个被赶走的精灵，竟然开口和神说话了。','The god went to the place where Bentresh was and made the protection over the daughter of the prince of Bakhtan, and she was well at once. The stela gives the healing a single plain sentence, in sharp contrast with the scribe''s helplessness: the scribe used all his learning and could do nothing; the god came and the illness was gone. The ''protection'' is the fourfold protection that Khonsu-in-Thebes-Neferhotep gave to Khonsu-the-Provider, passed through the image to the princess. The Egyptians believed many illnesses were caused by hostile spirits, ghosts of the dead or enemy powers, and healing used not only remedies but spells, amulets and the power of the gods. The very name Khonsu-the-Provider — ''he who carries out plans'', the expeller of disease demons — tells his office. Many Egyptian amulets and spells ask Khonsu for health. Bentresh''s healing is the central event of the tale, but the tale does not end here. What follows is very rare in Egyptian writing: the spirit that has been driven out speaks to the god.','claim.v0360.bentresh.08','本特蕾什石碑（卢浮宫 C 284）本特蕾什的痊愈。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_bentresh_stela_louvre_c284.9','storyver.egyptian.bentresh_stela_louvre_c284',9,'精灵的话','The spirit speaks','那个附在公主身上的精灵，当着驱邪者孔苏的面说：欢迎你平安到来，驱赶病魔的伟大的神！巴赫坦是你的城市，它的人民是你的仆人，我也是你的仆人。我会回到我来的地方去，让你的心为了你此行的目的而得到满足。只是请陛下下令，让我和巴赫坦的王公一起过一个节日。神对他的祭司点了点头，说：让巴赫坦的王公在这个精灵面前献上一份丰盛的祭品。这是古埃及文献中很少见的一幕：被驱赶的精灵没有和神打斗，也没有被消灭，而是恭敬地向神致敬，承认自己是神的仆人，然后提出了一个请求——它希望在离开之前得到一场宴会。神答应了它的请求。这样的结局说明，在古埃及人的观念里，许多致病的精灵并不是绝对的邪恶，它们可以被安抚、被送走，而不一定要被摧毁。用祭品和节日来送走一个精灵，是一种和平的解决办法；孔苏在这里不像一个战士，更像一位威严而宽厚的主人。','Then the spirit that was with her said before Khonsu-the-Provider: ''Welcome in peace, great god who expels disease demons! Bakhtan is your town, its people are your servants, and I am your servant. I shall go to the place from which I came, to set your heart at rest concerning the purpose of your coming. Only let Your Majesty command that a feast day be made with me and the prince of Bakhtan.'' The god nodded to his priest, saying: ''Let the prince of Bakhtan make a great offering before this spirit.'' It is a scene rarely found in Egyptian writing: the spirit that is being driven out does not fight the god nor is it destroyed; it greets him respectfully, calls itself his servant, and asks a favour — a feast before it leaves. The god grants it. The ending shows that to the Egyptians many disease-bringing spirits were not wholly evil: they could be appeased and sent away rather than destroyed. Sending a spirit off with offerings and a feast is a peaceful settlement; Khonsu here is less a warrior than a stern and generous lord.','claim.v0360.bentresh.09','本特蕾什石碑（卢浮宫 C 284）精灵的话。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_bentresh_stela_louvre_c284.10','storyver.egyptian.bentresh_stela_louvre_c284',10,'和精灵一起过节','A feast with the spirit','就在驱邪者孔苏和那个精灵这样交谈的时候，巴赫坦的王公和他的士兵们站在一旁，心里非常害怕。巴赫坦的王公随后为驱邪者孔苏和那个精灵献上了丰盛的祭品，为他们举行了一个节日。然后，那个精灵平平安安地离开，到它想去的地方去了，这都是按照驱邪者孔苏的命令。巴赫坦的王公和所有在巴赫坦的人都欢喜极了。碑文在这里写出了凡人的恐惧：他们亲眼看见一位神和一个精灵面对面地说话，这样的场面让他们既敬畏又害怕。可是一切都在神的掌握之中，节日过后，精灵离开了，公主恢复了健康，整个国家都充满了欢乐。这一章是故事的转折点：问题已经解决了，按理说神应该启程回国。可是巴赫坦的王公看到了神的力量，心里起了一个念头。人们往往在危难过去之后，舍不得放走帮助过他们的神；这个念头，将让故事又延续了将近四年。','While Khonsu-the-Provider and the spirit were speaking thus, the prince of Bakhtan stood by with his army in great fear. Then the prince of Bakhtan made a great offering before Khonsu-the-Provider and the spirit and held a feast day for them. And the spirit went in peace to wherever it wished, as Khonsu-the-Provider had commanded. The prince of Bakhtan rejoiced very greatly, together with every man who was in Bakhtan. The stela captures mortal fear here: they saw with their own eyes a god and a spirit speaking face to face, a sight that filled them with awe and dread. Yet everything was in the god''s hands; after the feast the spirit left, the princess was well, and the whole land was full of joy. This chapter is the turning point: the problem is solved, and by rights the god should set out for home. But the prince of Bakhtan has seen the god''s power, and a thought has entered his heart. When danger is past, people often cannot bear to let go of the god who helped them — and that thought will keep the story going for nearly four more years.','claim.v0360.bentresh.10','本特蕾什石碑（卢浮宫 C 284）节日与精灵离去。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_bentresh_stela_louvre_c284.11','storyver.egyptian.bentresh_stela_louvre_c284',11,'金色的鹰','The golden falcon','巴赫坦的王公在心里盘算着，说：我要让这位神留在巴赫坦，我不让他回埃及去。于是，这位神在巴赫坦一住就是三年零九个月。有一天，巴赫坦的王公躺在床上，看见这位神从他的神龛里走出来，化作一只金色的鹰，向着埃及飞去。王公醒过来，觉得浑身无力，就对驱邪者孔苏的祭司说：这位神原来还在我们这里，可他要回埃及去了！让他的车驶回埃及吧。这个梦是神表达意愿的方式：神不会被人永远留住，他的家在底比斯。鹰在古埃及是许多神的形象，尤其是与太阳和王权相关的神；孔苏本人也常被描绘成鹰头的神，头顶有新月和满月。金色象征神的身体——古埃及人相信神的肌肤是黄金做的。王公梦醒后的“无力”，也许是一种警告：强留一位神是危险的。在古代世界里，许多故事都讲到神像被留在异乡后显灵要求回家，本特蕾什石碑的这一段，是这类故事在古埃及最生动的例子之一。','The prince of Bakhtan considered in his heart, saying: ''I will make this god stay here in Bakhtan; I will not let him go to Egypt.'' So the god remained in Bakhtan three years and nine months. Then, as the prince of Bakhtan lay on his bed, he saw this god come out of his shrine as a falcon of gold and fly toward Egypt. He awoke feeling faint and said to the priest of Khonsu-the-Provider: ''This god was still here with us, but he will go to Egypt! Let his chariot go to Egypt.'' The dream is the god''s way of showing his will: a god cannot be held for ever; his home is Thebes. The falcon was the form of many Egyptian gods, especially those linked with the sun and kingship; Khonsu himself is often shown falcon-headed, wearing the crescent and full moon. Gold signifies the god''s body — the Egyptians believed the gods'' flesh was of gold. The prince''s faintness on waking may be a warning: to keep a god by force is dangerous. Many ancient tales tell of divine images held abroad that showed signs demanding to go home; this passage of the Bentresh Stela is one of the most vivid Egyptian examples.','claim.v0360.bentresh.11','本特蕾什石碑（卢浮宫 C 284）金鹰之梦。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_bentresh_stela_louvre_c284.12','storyver.egyptian.bentresh_stela_louvre_c284',12,'回到底比斯','Return to Thebes','巴赫坦的王公让这位神启程回埃及，给了他许多各种各样的好东西作为礼物，还派了很多士兵和马匹护送。他们平安地到达了底比斯。驱邪者孔苏来到了底比斯的孔苏·奈弗尔霍特普的神庙，把巴赫坦王公送给他的所有礼物都放在底比斯的孔苏·奈弗尔霍特普面前，一点也没有留给自己的神庙。驱邪者孔苏平安地回到了他在底比斯的家，那是在陛下在位的第三十三年，冬季第二个月的第十九天。这个结尾把故事带回了起点：那份四重的护佑是从底比斯的孔苏·奈弗尔霍特普那里来的，所有的荣耀和礼物也都归还给他。许多学者认为，这块石碑本身就是孔苏神庙的祭司们立下的，用来宣扬他们的神的威力和神庙的古老荣耀，所以它特意强调礼物全部归于底比斯的主神庙。从那哈林的贡品到底比斯的礼物，故事以财富开始，也以财富结束；而在中间，是一位神跨越千山万水、为一位异国公主驱除病魔的远行。','The prince of Bakhtan let the god go to Egypt and gave him very many gifts of every good thing, with many soldiers and horses. They arrived in peace at Thebes. Khonsu-the-Provider went to the house of Khonsu-in-Thebes-Neferhotep and placed all the gifts the prince of Bakhtan had given him before Khonsu-in-Thebes-Neferhotep, keeping nothing at all for his own house. Khonsu-the-Provider returned in peace to his house in regnal year 33, second month of winter, day 19. The ending brings the tale back to its beginning: the fourfold protection came from Khonsu-in-Thebes-Neferhotep, and all the glory and the gifts go back to him. Many scholars think the stela was set up by the priests of the Khonsu temple to proclaim their god''s power and their temple''s ancient glory, which is why it stresses that every gift went to the chief temple at Thebes. From tribute in Naharin to gifts at Thebes, the tale begins and ends with riches; and between them lies a god''s long journey across mountains and rivers to drive out an illness from a foreign princess.','claim.v0360.bentresh.12','本特蕾什石碑（卢浮宫 C 284）第三十三年：回到底比斯。','碑文中的年份与日期据现代学者的释读，不对应可考的史实。'),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_dorbiney_two_brothers.1','storyver.egyptian.dorbiney_two_brothers',1,'两兄弟','The two brothers','纸草一开头说：从前有两个兄弟，同一个母亲、同一个父亲所生。哥哥叫阿努比斯，弟弟叫巴塔。阿努比斯有房子，有妻子，弟弟和他住在一起，就像他的儿子一样。是弟弟为他做衣服，是弟弟跟着他的牛群到田野里去，是弟弟犁地，是弟弟为他收割，田里的所有活计都是弟弟替他做的。他的弟弟是一个出色的人，在整个国家里没有人能和他相比，因为神的力量就在他身上。这个开头描绘了一幅古埃及农村的家庭图景：哥哥是一家之主，弟弟没有成家，在哥哥家里干活，像儿子侍奉父亲一样侍奉哥哥。纸草特意说明两兄弟是同父同母所生，又说巴塔身上有“神的力量”，为后面的奇迹埋下了伏笔。这份纸草是第十九王朝的书吏恩纳纳抄写的，字迹工整，保存得几乎完整，是古埃及流传下来最著名的故事之一。故事里的兄弟俩都用了神的名字，可在纸草中，他们首先是一对普通的农家兄弟。','The papyrus begins: once there were two brothers of one mother and one father. The elder was called Anubis and the younger Bata. Anubis had a house and a wife, and his younger brother lived with him like a son. It was the younger who made clothes for him, who went behind his cattle to the fields, who ploughed and who harvested for him; he did all the work of the fields. His younger brother was an excellent man; there was none like him in the whole land, for the strength of a god was in him. The opening paints a family of the Egyptian countryside: the elder brother is head of the house, the younger unmarried, working in his brother''s house and serving him as a son serves a father. The papyrus stresses that they had the same father and mother, and says Bata had ''the strength of a god'', preparing the wonders to come. The papyrus was copied by the Nineteenth-Dynasty scribe Ennana in a careful hand and is almost complete — one of the most famous tales to survive from ancient Egypt. Both brothers bear the names of gods, but in the papyrus they are first of all two farming brothers.','claim.v0360.two_brothers.01','多比尼纸草（大英博物馆 EA 10183）开头：两兄弟。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_dorbiney_two_brothers.2','storyver.egyptian.dorbiney_two_brothers',2,'会说话的牛','The cattle that spoke','每天早晨，巴塔赶着牛群出去，晚上再把它们赶回来，背上背着田里的各种蔬菜、牛奶、木柴和一切好东西，放在哥哥面前，哥哥和嫂子吃喝完毕，他才回到牛棚里，和牛群睡在一起。天亮以后，他做好饭，放在哥哥面前，哥哥给他带上去田里吃的面包，他就赶着牛群到田野里去。在路上，牛群会对他说：某某地方的草很好。他听了牛的话，就把牛赶到它们想去的那片好草地上。于是，他照看的牛长得非常好，生下的小牛也非常多。这一段带着童话般的色彩：牛会说话，巴塔能听懂牛的语言，人和动物之间有一种亲密的默契。古埃及人非常珍视他们的牛，在墓葬壁画里，常常能看到牧人赶着牛群过河、给母牛挤奶的场面。纸草让牛群开口说话，不仅表现了巴塔的善良与勤劳，也为后面的情节做了准备：正是这些被他细心照料的牛，在危急关头救了他的命。','Every morning Bata drove the cattle out and every evening brought them back, laden with all the vegetables of the field, with milk, with wood and with every good thing of the field, and laid them before his elder brother; when Anubis and his wife had eaten and drunk, Bata went to the stable and slept among his cattle. When the land grew light he prepared food and set it before his brother, who gave him bread for the field, and he drove the cattle out to the pastures. As he walked behind them they would say to him: ''The grass is good in such and such a place.'' He listened to them and took them to the good grass they wanted. So the cattle in his care grew very fine and their calves were very many. The passage has a fairy-tale colour: cattle speak, Bata understands them, and man and beast share a close understanding. The Egyptians prized their cattle; tomb paintings often show herdsmen driving cattle through water and milking cows. Making the cattle speak shows Bata''s goodness and diligence and also prepares the plot: these very cattle, tended with such care, will save his life.','claim.v0360.two_brothers.02','多比尼纸草（大英博物馆 EA 10183）会说话的牛。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_dorbiney_two_brothers.3','storyver.egyptian.dorbiney_two_brothers',3,'嫂子的诱惑','The temptation','到了耕种的季节，兄弟俩一起到田里去犁地。哥哥让弟弟回村里去取种子。巴塔回到家里，看见嫂子正坐着梳头，就对她说：起来，给我种子，我好赶回田里去，哥哥在等我。她说：自己去仓库拿吧，别打扰我梳头。巴塔走进仓库，拿了一个大罐子，装了许多种子——三袋大麦和两袋小麦——扛在肩上走了出来。嫂子看见他扛着这么重的东西，惊叹他的力气，心中起了邪念，便站起来拉住他，提出要他与她同床，还答应给他做漂亮的衣服。巴塔一听，就像上埃及的豹子一样大怒，对她说：你对我来说就像母亲，你的丈夫对我来说就像父亲，是他把我养大的。你对我说的这是多么大的罪过！不要再对我说这样的话，我不会告诉任何人，也不会让它从我嘴里传出去。说完，他扛起种子，回到了田里。纸草对这一段只用了几句话，重点在巴塔的拒绝：他把嫂子看作母亲，把哥哥看作父亲，坚守着家庭的伦常。','At ploughing time the brothers went together to plough. The elder sent the younger back to the village to fetch seed. Bata came home and found his brother''s wife sitting and braiding her hair. He said: ''Get up and give me seed, so I can hurry back to the field; my brother is waiting.'' She said: ''Go, open the storeroom and take what you want; don''t make me leave my hair unfinished.'' He went into the storeroom, took a large jar and loaded himself with a great deal of seed — three sacks of barley and two of emmer — and came out carrying it. Seeing him with so great a load, she marvelled at his strength, desire came into her heart, and she rose, took hold of him and asked him to lie with her, promising to make him fine clothes. At this Bata became like an Upper Egyptian leopard in fury and said: ''You are like a mother to me, and your husband like a father; he raised me. What is this great wrong you have said to me? Do not say it to me again, and I will tell no one; I will not let it come out of my mouth.'' He took up his load and went to the field. The papyrus gives this only a few lines, its weight on Bata''s refusal: he holds his brother''s wife as a mother and his brother as a father, keeping the order of the family.','claim.v0360.two_brothers.03','多比尼纸草（大英博物馆 EA 10183）嫂子的诱惑。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_dorbiney_two_brothers.4','storyver.egyptian.dorbiney_two_brothers',4,'谎言','The lie','巴塔走后，嫂子害怕自己说的话被传出去。她拿来油脂和布条，把自己弄成一副被人殴打过的样子，躺在床上，装作病了。傍晚，哥哥照常回到家里，弟弟赶着牛群跟在后面。哥哥走进屋里，发现妻子躺着，像是被人打了，她没有像往常那样往他手上倒水，也没有点灯，屋子里一片漆黑，她躺在那里呕吐。丈夫问她：谁和你说过话了？她说：除了你的弟弟，没有人和我说过话。他回来拿种子的时候，看见我一个人坐着，就对我说：来，我们一起躺一会儿，你把头发松开。我没有听他的，我说：我不是你的母亲吗？你的哥哥不就像你的父亲一样吗？他害怕了，就打了我，不让我告诉你。如果你让他活着，我就死给你看。等他晚上回来的时候，你就杀了他，因为他做了这样的坏事。这段谎言把巴塔说过的话原样反了过来：巴塔对她说的是“你就像我的母亲”，她却把这句话安在自己嘴上，把罪名推给了巴塔。','When Bata had gone, his brother''s wife was afraid of what she had said. She took fat and grease and made herself look like one who had been beaten, and lay down as if ill. In the evening her husband came home as usual, his younger brother following with the cattle. Anubis entered the house and found his wife lying as though beaten; she did not pour water on his hands as usual, nor had she lit the lamp; the house was in darkness, and she lay vomiting. Her husband asked: ''Who has spoken with you?'' She said: ''No one but your younger brother. When he came to fetch seed he found me sitting alone and said: Come, let us lie together for an hour; loosen your hair. I would not listen to him. I said: Am I not your mother, and is not your elder brother like a father to you? He was afraid and beat me so that I would not tell you. If you let him live, I shall die. When he comes home this evening, kill him, for he has done this evil thing.'' Her lie turns Bata''s words inside out: he had told her ''you are like a mother to me''; she puts those words in her own mouth and lays the guilt on him.','claim.v0360.two_brothers.04','多比尼纸草（大英博物馆 EA 10183）妻子的谎言。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_dorbiney_two_brothers.5','storyver.egyptian.dorbiney_two_brothers',5,'母牛的警告','The cows'' warning','哥哥一听，就像上埃及的豹子一样暴怒。他磨快了长矛，拿在手里，躲到牛棚的门后，要在弟弟晚上赶牛回来时杀死他。太阳落山了，巴塔照常背着田里的各种蔬菜回来。走在最前面的母牛一进牛棚就对它的牧人说：你的哥哥拿着长矛站在门后，要杀你，快从他面前逃走吧。巴塔听懂了领头母牛的话。第二头母牛进来，也说了同样的话。他从牛棚门下往里看，看见了哥哥的脚，哥哥正拿着长矛站在门后。巴塔放下背上的东西，拔腿就跑，哥哥拿着长矛在后面紧追。在这里，第二章里那些会说话的牛终于派上了用场：巴塔平日对它们的照顾，换来了它们在关键时刻的警告。纸草让两头母牛先后说出同样的话，这种重复是古埃及故事常用的手法，使情节显得庄重，也让读者感受到危险的迫近。从这一刻起，原本像父子一样亲密的兄弟，变成了追杀者和逃亡者。','Hearing this, the elder brother became like an Upper Egyptian leopard. He sharpened his spear, took it in his hand and stood behind the door of the stable to kill his younger brother when he came home in the evening with the cattle. When the sun set, Bata came home as usual, laden with every vegetable of the field. The lead cow entered the stable and said to her herdsman: ''Your elder brother is standing before you with his spear to kill you; run away from him.'' He understood what his lead cow said. The next cow entered and said the same. He looked under the door of the stable and saw his brother''s feet as he stood behind the door, spear in hand. He set down his load and fled, and his elder brother went after him with his spear. Here the speaking cattle of the second chapter come into their own: Bata''s daily care for them is repaid by their warning at the crucial moment. The papyrus has two cows say the same words in turn — a repetition common in Egyptian tales, giving the scene weight and making the danger feel close. From this moment brothers once close as father and son become pursuer and fugitive.','claim.v0360.two_brothers.05','多比尼纸草（大英博物馆 EA 10183）母牛的警告。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_dorbiney_two_brothers.6','storyver.egyptian.dorbiney_two_brothers',6,'鳄鱼之河','The water full of crocodiles','巴塔一边逃跑，一边向太阳神拉-哈拉赫提祈祷，说：我的好主人，您是分辨恶人和义人的神。拉听见了他所有的祈求。拉在他和他哥哥之间造出了一大片水，水里满是鳄鱼，一个在这边，一个在那边。哥哥两次拍打自己的手，因为他没能杀死弟弟。弟弟在对岸叫他说：你在那边等到天亮吧，太阳升起的时候，我会当着太阳的面和你辩明是非，因为它会把恶人交给义人。从今以后，我再也不和你住在一起了，我要到松树谷去。纸草称太阳神为“拉-哈拉赫提”，意思是“拉，地平线上的荷鲁斯”，是升起和落下的太阳的合称。在古埃及人看来，太阳神每天巡视世界，看见一切，所以他是最公正的法官。巴塔向他祈求的，不是力量，而是公正的裁判；太阳神的回答，不是替他惩罚哥哥，而是在两人之间隔开一道河，让真相有机会在天亮时被说出来。在这个故事里，神总是在关键时刻出现，却从不代替人去行动。','As he fled, Bata prayed to Pre-Harakhti, saying: ''My good lord, it is you who judge the wrongdoer from the just.'' And Re heard all his plea. Re made a great water appear between him and his elder brother, full of crocodiles, one of them on one side and the other on the other. The elder brother struck his hand twice because he had not killed him. The younger called to him from the other side: ''Wait there until dawn. When the sun disk rises I shall be judged with you before him, and he will deliver the wrongdoer to the just. I shall not be with you any more; I shall go to the Valley of the Pine.'' The papyrus names the sun god Pre-Harakhti, ''Re, Horus of the Horizon'', the rising and setting sun in one. To the Egyptians the sun god sees all as he crosses the world each day, and so is the most just of judges. Bata asks not for strength but for a fair judgement; and the god does not punish the brother for him but sets a river between them, so that truth may be spoken at dawn. In this tale the gods appear at the decisive moment yet never act in place of men.','claim.v0360.two_brothers.06','多比尼纸草（大英博物馆 EA 10183）向拉-哈拉赫提的祈祷。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_dorbiney_two_brothers.7','storyver.egyptian.dorbiney_two_brothers',7,'黎明时的真相','The truth at dawn','天亮了，太阳升起来，两兄弟隔着河相望。巴塔对哥哥说：你为什么在背后追我，要不分青红皂白地杀我？你连我的话都不听。我是你的弟弟，你像我的父亲，你的妻子像我的母亲，不是吗？你派我回去拿种子的时候，是你的妻子对我说：来，我们一起躺一会儿。可是你看，她却把事情颠倒过来告诉了你。他把真相一五一十地告诉了哥哥，然后以太阳神的名义起誓。为了证明自己的清白，他拿起一把芦苇刀，割下了自己身上的一部分，扔进水里，被一条鲶鱼吞了下去。他变得虚弱无力。哥哥听明白了，悲痛欲绝，放声大哭，可是因为水里满是鳄鱼，他无法渡河到弟弟身边。纸草在这里只是平静地叙述，没有渲染。巴塔用一种极端的方式表明了自己的清白：他宁愿伤害自己，也要证明他对嫂子没有任何邪念。哥哥终于明白了真相，可是兄弟之间已经隔着一条无法跨越的河。','When the land grew light and the sun rose, the brothers looked at each other across the water. Bata said to his elder brother: ''Why have you come after me to kill me wrongfully, without hearing what I had to say? I am your younger brother; you are like a father to me, and your wife like a mother, are you not? When you sent me to fetch seed, it was your wife who said to me: Come, let us lie together for an hour. But see, she has turned it around for you.'' He told his brother everything that had happened and swore by Pre-Harakhti. To prove his innocence he took a reed knife, cut away part of his own body and threw it into the water, where a catfish swallowed it. He grew faint and weak. His elder brother understood and grieved greatly, weeping aloud, but he could not cross to his brother because of the crocodiles. The papyrus tells this calmly, without dwelling on it. Bata proves his innocence by an extreme act: he would rather harm himself than leave any doubt that he had no desire for his brother''s wife. The elder brother at last knows the truth, but now an uncrossable river lies between them.','claim.v0360.two_brothers.07','多比尼纸草（大英博物馆 EA 10183）黎明时的真相。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_dorbiney_two_brothers.8','storyver.egyptian.dorbiney_two_brothers',8,'松树谷的心','The heart in the Valley of the Pine','巴塔对哥哥说：你只记得坏事，就不能记得一件好事，或者我为你做过的一件事吗？现在你回家去照看你的牛吧，我不会再待在你待的地方了，我要到松树谷去。你要为我做的事是这样的：我要把我的心取出来，放在松树的花顶上。如果松树被砍倒，我的心掉在地上，你就要来找它，哪怕找上七年，你也不要灰心。找到以后，你把它放进一碗清凉的水里，我就会活过来，为那个伤害我的人报仇。你会知道我出了事的：当有人把一罐啤酒递到你手里，啤酒起了泡沫，那就是我出事的征兆，你不要耽搁。说完，他就前往松树谷，哥哥则回到家里，手按在头上，身上涂满尘土。他回到家，杀死了妻子，把她扔给狗吃，然后坐下来为弟弟哀悼。这一章是整个故事的关键：巴塔把自己的心放在了身体之外，他的生命从此系在一棵树上，也系在哥哥的忠诚上。古埃及人认为心是生命和智慧的所在，把心放在别处，是一种神奇的保命之法，也是一个巨大的弱点。','Bata said to his elder brother: ''You remember one bad thing; can you not remember one good thing, or a thing I did for you? Now go home and care for your cattle, for I will not stay where you are; I am going to the Valley of the Pine. This is what you shall do for me: I shall take out my heart and place it on top of the flower of the pine. If the pine is cut down and my heart falls to the ground, you shall come to seek it, even if you spend seven years seeking it; do not let your heart be weary. When you find it, put it in a bowl of cool water, and I shall live to take revenge on him who wronged me. You will know that something has happened to me when a jug of beer is given into your hand and it ferments; do not delay.'' He went away to the Valley of the Pine, and his elder brother went home, his hand on his head, smeared with dust. He reached his house, killed his wife, cast her to the dogs and sat mourning his younger brother. This chapter is the hinge of the tale: Bata places his heart outside his body, binding his life to a tree and to his brother''s loyalty. The Egyptians held the heart to be the seat of life and mind; to place it elsewhere is a marvellous safeguard and also a great weakness.','claim.v0360.two_brothers.08','多比尼纸草（大英博物馆 EA 10183）松树谷的心。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_dorbiney_two_brothers.9','storyver.egyptian.dorbiney_two_brothers',9,'九神与克努姆','The Ennead and Khnum','巴塔在松树谷里独自生活，白天打猎，晚上回到松树下睡觉，他的心就放在松树的花顶上。后来，他为自己建了一座住宅，里面摆满了各种好东西。有一天，他走出去，遇见了九柱神，他们正在巡视整个国家。九柱神彼此商量，然后对他说：巴塔，九柱神的公牛，你为什么一个人在这里？你因为哥哥阿努比斯的妻子离开了家乡，现在哥哥已经杀了她，你已经为所受的一切冤屈报了仇。他们非常同情他。拉-哈拉赫提对克努姆说：为巴塔造一个妻子吧，不要让他一个人生活。克努姆就为他造了一个伴侣，她的身体比全国任何女子都美丽，所有神的精华都在她的身上。七位哈托尔来看她，异口同声地说：她将死于刀下。克努姆是古埃及人心目中用陶轮造人的神，他把人的身体像陶器一样塑造出来。在这里，正是拉-哈拉赫提——那位在河边回应了巴塔祈祷的太阳神——再一次关照他，命令克努姆为他造一个妻子。七位哈托尔则是古埃及人相信会在孩子出生时预言命运的女神，她们的预言为故事后来的结局埋下了伏笔。','Bata lived alone in the Valley of the Pine, hunting desert game by day and coming back at evening to sleep under the pine on whose flower his heart lay. After some days he built himself a mansion and filled it with every good thing. One day he went out and met the Ennead, walking about administering the whole land. The Ennead conferred and said to him: ''Bata, bull of the Ennead, are you here alone? You left your town because of the wife of your elder brother Anubis; now he has killed his wife, and you are avenged for all that was done to you.'' They felt very sorry for him. Pre-Harakhti said to Khnum: ''Fashion a wife for Bata, that he not live alone.'' Khnum made him a companion more beautiful in body than any woman in the whole land, for the fluid of every god was in her. The Seven Hathors came to see her and said with one voice: ''She will die by the knife.'' Khnum, to the Egyptians, was the god who shaped people on the potter''s wheel as a potter shapes vessels. Here it is Pre-Harakhti — the sun god who answered Bata''s prayer by the river — who cares for him again and commands Khnum to make him a wife. The Seven Hathors were the goddesses believed to foretell a child''s fate at birth, and their words foreshadow the ending.','claim.v0360.two_brothers.09','多比尼纸草（大英博物馆 EA 10183）九柱神与克努姆。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_dorbiney_two_brothers.10','storyver.egyptian.dorbiney_two_brothers',10,'大海与一缕头发','The sea and a lock of hair','巴塔深深地爱着她。她住在他的家里，他整天在野外打猎，把猎物带回来放在她面前。他对她说：不要到外面去，免得大海把你抢走，我没法从它那里把你救回来，因为我和你一样是个女人——这句话指他已失去了男子的身体。他把心的秘密告诉了她：我的心放在松树的花顶上，如果有人找到它，我就得和他搏斗。有一天，巴塔出去打猎，她出门到松树下散步，大海看见了她，涌起波浪追赶她。她赶紧跑回家里，大海对松树说：替我抓住她！松树就抓下了她的一缕头发。大海把这缕头发带到了埃及，放在法老的洗衣工们洗衣服的地方。这缕头发的香气染在了法老的衣服上，法老责怪洗衣工，说他的衣服上有油膏的气味。洗衣工头每天都挨骂，不知道该怎么办，他走到河边，看见水里有一缕头发，就让人把它捞上来，发现它香气扑鼻，便把它带给了法老。在古埃及人的想象里，大海常常是陌生而危险的力量，在这里，它就像一个贪婪的追求者，从远方伸手夺走了女神造出的美人。','Bata loved her very much. She stayed in his house while he spent the day hunting desert game and brought it to lay before her. He said to her: ''Do not go outside, lest the Sea carry you off; I could not rescue you from it, for I am a woman like you'' — meaning he had lost his manhood. And he told her his secret: ''My heart is placed on top of the flower of the pine; if someone finds it, I shall have to fight him.'' One day, when Bata had gone hunting, she went out to stroll under the pine. The Sea saw her and surged after her. She ran back into the house, and the Sea called to the pine: ''Seize her for me!'' and the pine took a lock of her hair. The Sea carried it to Egypt and laid it down at the place of Pharaoh''s launderers. The scent of the lock got into Pharaoh''s clothes, and he quarrelled with the launderers, saying his clothes smelled of ointment. The chief launderer, scolded every day, did not know what to do; going down to the shore, he saw the lock of hair in the water, had it brought up, found its scent very sweet, and took it to Pharaoh. In the Egyptian imagination the sea was often a strange and dangerous power; here it is like a greedy suitor reaching from afar to snatch the beauty the gods had made.','claim.v0360.two_brothers.10','多比尼纸草（大英博物馆 EA 10183）大海与一缕头发。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_dorbiney_two_brothers.11','storyver.egyptian.dorbiney_two_brothers',11,'法老的使者','Pharaoh''s envoys','法老召来了精通学问的书吏，他们对法老说：这缕头发属于拉-哈拉赫提的一个女儿，所有神的精华都在她身上，这是从另一个国度给您的礼物。让人到所有的外国去寻找她吧，至于那缕头发被送来的松树谷，就派许多人去，把她带回来。法老说：你们说得很好。于是派出了使者。许多天以后，派到各国去的人都回来向法老报告，只有派到松树谷去的人没有回来，因为巴塔把他们都杀了，只留下一个人回来报信。法老于是派了许多士兵和战车去把她带回来，还派了一个女人，手里拿着女子喜欢的各种美丽首饰。那个女子跟着她回到了埃及，全国上下都为她欢呼。法老非常爱她，封她为“伟大的贵妇”。在这里，克努姆为巴塔造的妻子被书吏们称为“拉-哈拉赫提的女儿”——她本来就是在太阳神的命令下诞生的。可是她没有忠于巴塔，而是被首饰和王宫的荣华所吸引，自愿来到了法老身边。这一转折让人想起故事开头嫂子的背叛：巴塔又一次被身边的女人出卖了。','Pharaoh summoned the learned scribes, who said to him: ''This lock of hair belongs to a daughter of Pre-Harakhti, in whom is the fluid of every god; it is a greeting to you from another land. Let envoys go to every foreign land to seek her; and to the Valley of the Pine, from which the lock came, send many men to fetch her.'' Pharaoh said: ''What you have said is very good,'' and envoys were sent. After many days the men sent to the foreign lands came back to report, but those sent to the Valley of the Pine did not, for Bata had killed them, letting only one return to tell Pharaoh. Then Pharaoh sent many soldiers and charioteers to bring her back, and with them a woman bearing every kind of fine lady''s jewellery. The woman came back to Egypt with her, and the whole land rejoiced over her. Pharaoh loved her very much and named her Great Noble Lady. Here the wife Khnum fashioned for Bata is called by the scribes a daughter of Pre-Harakhti — she was born at the sun god''s command. Yet she is not faithful to Bata; drawn by jewels and the splendour of the palace, she goes willingly to Pharaoh. The turn recalls the sister-in-law''s betrayal at the start: once again Bata is betrayed by the woman beside him.','claim.v0360.two_brothers.11','多比尼纸草（大英博物馆 EA 10183）法老的使者。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_dorbiney_two_brothers.12','storyver.egyptian.dorbiney_two_brothers',12,'砍倒松树','The pine is cut down','法老问她关于她丈夫的事，她对法老说：让人去砍倒那棵松树，把它砍成碎片。法老就派了士兵带着工具去砍松树。他们来到松树前，砍下了放着巴塔之心的那朵花。就在那一刻，巴塔倒下死去了。纸草对巴塔之死只写了这样一句话，却显得格外沉重：他曾在河边以太阳神的名义证明自己的清白，曾在松树谷里独自打猎、建起住宅，曾把自己最大的秘密告诉了最亲近的人，而正是这个秘密，让他失去了生命。这一章也应验了七位哈托尔的预言的一部分：这个女子的命运将与刀联系在一起，而在应验之前，她先让巴塔死于斧下。古埃及的故事中常常出现这样的情节：英雄的生命寄托在某一件外物上——一棵树、一件东西——只要这件东西完好，英雄就不会死。这类主题在世界许多民族的民间故事中都能看到，人们称之为“体外之心”或“外在的灵魂”。现在，巴塔的心落在了地上，他唯一的希望，就是哥哥会遵守诺言，来寻找他的心。','Pharaoh asked her about her husband, and she said to Pharaoh: ''Let the pine be felled and cut to pieces.'' So Pharaoh sent soldiers with their tools to fell the pine. They came to the pine and cut the flower on which Bata''s heart lay, and he fell dead at that moment. The papyrus gives Bata''s death a single sentence, and it weighs heavily: he had proved his innocence by the river in the sun god''s name, hunted alone and built a house in the Valley of the Pine, and told his greatest secret to the one closest to him — and it was that secret that cost him his life. The chapter fulfils part of the Seven Hathors'' word: this woman''s fate is bound up with the blade, but before it comes upon her she brings Bata down by the axe. Egyptian tales often place a hero''s life in some outside thing — a tree, an object — so that while it is whole he cannot die. The motif appears in folktales of many peoples and is known as the ''external heart'' or ''external soul''. Now Bata''s heart lies on the ground, and his one hope is that his brother will keep his word and come to seek it.','claim.v0360.two_brothers.12','多比尼纸草（大英博物馆 EA 10183）砍倒松树。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_dorbiney_two_brothers.13','storyver.egyptian.dorbiney_two_brothers',13,'起沫的啤酒','The frothing beer','就在松树被砍倒的第二天，巴塔的哥哥阿努比斯走进家里，坐下洗手，有人递给他一罐啤酒，啤酒起了泡沫；又递给他一罐葡萄酒，葡萄酒也变浑浊了。他立刻拿起手杖和凉鞋，带上衣服和武器，出发前往松树谷。他走进弟弟的住宅，发现弟弟躺在床上，已经死了。他看着弟弟的尸体痛哭，然后去寻找弟弟的心。他在松树底下寻找，从傍晚一直找到天亮。他就这样找了三年，没有找到。到了第四年，他的心想要回到埃及去，他说：明天我就走。天亮以后，他又在松树下走了一整天，傍晚往回走的时候，他又回头看了一眼，找到了一颗果实——那正是弟弟的心。他拿来一碗清凉的水，把心放进去，然后像往常一样坐下来。夜里，心吸饱了水，巴塔的四肢颤动起来，他睁开眼睛看着哥哥。哥哥把那碗水端到他嘴边，他喝了下去，心回到了原来的位置，巴塔又变回了从前的样子。兄弟俩紧紧拥抱，互相诉说。哥哥没有忘记诺言，在第一章里像父亲一样的哥哥，在这里终于以父亲般的坚持把弟弟救了回来。','On the day after the pine was felled, Anubis, Bata''s elder brother, entered his house and sat down to wash his hands; he was given a jug of beer, and it fermented; he was given another of wine, and it turned bad. He took his staff and sandals, his clothes and his weapons, and set out for the Valley of the Pine. He entered his younger brother''s mansion and found him lying dead on his bed. He wept over him, and then went to seek his heart under the pine, searching from evening to dawn. He spent three years seeking it without finding it. In the fourth year his heart longed to return to Egypt, and he said: ''I will go tomorrow.'' At dawn he walked under the pine once more all day, and in the evening, turning back for a last look, he found a fruit — it was his brother''s heart. He brought a bowl of cool water, placed the heart in it and sat down as usual. When night came the heart absorbed the water; Bata''s limbs trembled, and he looked at his elder brother. Anubis held the bowl of water to his lips, he drank, his heart stood in its place, and he became as he had been. The brothers embraced and talked together. The elder brother kept his word; the one who was like a father in the first chapter at last brings his brother back with a father''s persistence.','claim.v0360.two_brothers.13','多比尼纸草（大英博物馆 EA 10183）起沫的啤酒与寻找心。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_dorbiney_two_brothers.14','storyver.egyptian.dorbiney_two_brothers',14,'公牛','The bull','巴塔对哥哥说：看，我要变成一头公牛，身上有各种美丽的颜色，没有人知道它的来历。你坐在我的背上，等太阳升起时，我们就到我妻子所在的地方去，我要为自己报仇。你把我带到法老那里去，法老会为我给你银子和金子作为报酬，因为你给他带来了一个大奇迹，人们会在全国为我欢呼，然后你就回你的村子去。天亮以后，巴塔变成了他所说的那头公牛，哥哥坐在他的背上，天亮时到了法老所在的地方。法老看见这头公牛非常高兴，为它举行了盛大的祭祀，说：这是一个大奇迹。全国都为它欢呼。法老给了哥哥银子和金子，哥哥回到了自己的村子。有一天，公牛走进厨房，站在那位贵妇的身边，对她说：看，我还活着。她问：你是谁？公牛说：我是巴塔。你让法老砍倒了那棵松树，你知道那是为了不让我活下去。可是看，我还活着，我是一头公牛。贵妇非常害怕。她陪法老饮酒作乐时，让法老发誓答应她的一个要求，然后说：让我吃这头公牛的肝吧。法老很为难，可是只好照办。','Bata said to his elder brother: ''See, I shall become a great bull of every beautiful colour, whose nature no one will know. Sit on my back, and when the sun rises we shall be where my wife is, and I shall avenge myself. Take me to the place where Pharaoh is; he will reward you with silver and gold for bringing him a great wonder, and the whole land will rejoice over me; then you will go back to your village.'' When it was dawn Bata became the bull he had spoken of, and his elder brother sat on his back; at dawn he reached the place where Pharaoh was. Pharaoh was very glad of the bull and made a great offering for it, saying: ''It is a great wonder.'' The whole land rejoiced over it. Pharaoh gave the elder brother silver and gold, and he went home to his village. One day the bull entered the kitchen and stood beside the Lady and said to her: ''See, I am still alive.'' She said: ''Who are you?'' He said: ''I am Bata. You knew when you had Pharaoh fell the pine that it was to keep me from living. But see, I am alive; I am a bull.'' The Lady was very frightened. When she was drinking with Pharaoh she made him swear to grant her request, and said: ''Let me eat the liver of this bull.'' Pharaoh was troubled, but had to do it.','claim.v0360.two_brothers.14','多比尼纸草（大英博物馆 EA 10183）公牛。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_dorbiney_two_brothers.15','storyver.egyptian.dorbiney_two_brothers',15,'两棵鳄梨树与新国王','The persea trees and the new king','公牛被宰杀时，它的两滴血落在法老宫殿门口的两根门柱旁，长成了两棵高大的鳄梨树，每一棵都美极了。人们告诉法老，法老为它们举行了祭祀，全国都为它们欢呼。后来，法老和贵妇一起坐在树下，一棵树对贵妇说：你这个骗子，我是巴塔，我还活着。你让法老砍倒了松树，又让他杀了我变成的公牛。贵妇又让法老发誓，然后说：让人把这两棵鳄梨树砍倒，做成漂亮的家具吧。法老派了工匠去砍树，贵妇站在一旁看，一片木屑飞进了她的嘴里，她咽了下去，就怀了孕。日子满了，她生下一个儿子。法老非常喜爱这个孩子，先让他做库什的王子，后来又立他为全国的王储。过了许多年，法老升天了，王储继承了王位。新国王说：把陛下的大臣们召来，我要让他们知道我经历的一切。人们把他的妻子带来，他当着大臣们的面审判了她，大臣们都同意他的判决。他又把哥哥召来，立他为全国的王储。巴塔做了三十年的埃及国王，他去世后，他的哥哥继承了王位。七位哈托尔的预言应验了，而两兄弟最终以国王和继承人的身份重聚，故事在和解与公正中结束。','When the bull was slaughtered, two drops of its blood fell beside the two doorposts of Pharaoh''s palace and grew into two great persea trees, each of them very beautiful. Pharaoh was told, made offerings to them, and the whole land rejoiced. Later, as Pharaoh sat beneath them with the Lady, one tree said to her: ''You liar! I am Bata, and I am still alive. You had Pharaoh fell the pine and kill the bull I became.'' Again the Lady made Pharaoh swear, and said: ''Let these two persea trees be felled and made into fine furniture.'' Pharaoh sent craftsmen to fell the trees, and the Lady stood watching; a splinter flew into her mouth, she swallowed it, and she conceived. In due time she bore a son. Pharaoh loved the child greatly, made him King''s Son of Kush and later Crown Prince of the whole land. After many years Pharaoh flew up to heaven, and the Crown Prince became king. The new king said: ''Let His Majesty''s great officials be brought, that I may tell them all that has happened to me.'' His wife was brought, he judged her before them, and they agreed with his judgement. He summoned his elder brother and made him Crown Prince of the whole land. Bata reigned as king of Egypt for thirty years, and when he died his elder brother succeeded him. The Seven Hathors'' word is fulfilled, and the brothers meet again as king and heir; the tale ends in reconciliation and justice.','claim.v0360.two_brothers.15','多比尼纸草（大英博物馆 EA 10183）鳄梨树与巴塔为王。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_deir_el_bahari_birth_cycle.1','storyver.egyptian.deir_el_bahari_birth_cycle',1,'众神的会议','The council of the gods','浮雕的第一幕画的是众神之王阿蒙-拉坐在宝座上，面前是底比斯和赫利奥波利斯的众神。阿蒙-拉向众神宣布他的打算：他要生下一位未来的统治者，一位将统治两地、受众神护佑的国王。在古埃及的王权观念里，每一位法老都被看作太阳神的儿子，“拉之子”是国王王衔中的固定称号。可是哈特谢普苏特的情况很特别：她是一位女性，原本是图特摩斯二世的王后，后来以年幼的图特摩斯三世摄政的身份开始执政，最后自己加冕为王。为了说明她有资格坐上王位，她在自己的神庙里刻下了这组浮雕，宣称她不仅是图特摩斯一世的女儿，更是阿蒙-拉亲生的女儿。这组浮雕位于神庙中层柱廊的北侧，与南侧描绘远征蓬特的浮雕相对。神的会议这一幕，就像一出戏剧的开场：在凡人的故事开始以前，天上的众神已经为这个孩子的诞生做好了安排。','The first scene shows Amun-Re, king of the gods, enthroned before the gods of Thebes and Heliopolis. He announces his purpose to them: he will beget a future ruler, a king who will rule the Two Lands under the gods'' protection. In Egyptian kingship every pharaoh was a son of the sun god, and ''Son of Re'' was a fixed title in the royal names. Hatshepsut''s case was unusual: a woman, once queen of Thutmose II, she began to govern as regent for the young Thutmose III and at last was crowned king herself. To show that she was fit for the throne, she carved this cycle in her temple, claiming to be not only the daughter of Thutmose I but the daughter of Amun-Re himself. The reliefs stand on the north side of the middle colonnade, facing the reliefs of the expedition to Punt on the south. The council of the gods opens the cycle like the first scene of a drama: before the mortal story begins, the gods in heaven have already arranged the child''s birth.','claim.v0360.hatshepsut_birth.01','代尔拜赫里神庙中层柱廊北侧诞生浮雕：众神的会议。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_deir_el_bahari_birth_cycle.2','storyver.egyptian.deir_el_bahari_birth_cycle',2,'阿蒙与托特','Amun and Thoth','下一幕里，阿蒙与智慧之神托特交谈。托特是众神的书记和使者，以朱鹭头的形象出现。他向阿蒙谈起了一位凡间的女子：她就是图特摩斯一世的王后雅赫摩斯，比全国任何女子都美丽。在浮雕的叙述中，托特像一位宫廷中的谋臣，为神物色一位配得上神子的母亲。选择王后雅赫摩斯，本身就有深意：她是在位国王的正妻，她所生的孩子有着无可争议的王室血统。这样，哈特谢普苏特既是凡间国王的女儿，又是天上之神的女儿，两重身份互不矛盾，反而互相加强。托特在这组浮雕中扮演着重要的角色：他提出人选，引路，报喜，最后把孩子呈给阿蒙。古埃及人相信托特掌管文字、计算和知识，他也是神与神之间、神与人之间的信使。由他来安排这桩神圣的婚姻，说明一切都合乎众神的秩序。','In the next scene Amun speaks with Thoth, god of wisdom, scribe and messenger of the gods, shown with the head of an ibis. Thoth tells Amun of a mortal woman: Ahmose, queen of Thutmose I, more beautiful than any woman in the land. In the reliefs Thoth acts like a counsellor at court, finding a mother worthy of the god''s child. The choice of Queen Ahmose is itself meaningful: she is the reigning king''s chief wife, and her child''s royal blood cannot be questioned. So Hatshepsut is both daughter of an earthly king and daughter of a god in heaven, and the two claims do not conflict but strengthen each other. Thoth plays a large part throughout the cycle: he proposes the mother, leads the way, announces the birth and finally presents the child to Amun. The Egyptians held that Thoth ruled writing, reckoning and knowledge and carried messages between gods and between gods and men; that he arranges this sacred union shows that all is in keeping with the gods'' order.','claim.v0360.hatshepsut_birth.02','代尔拜赫里神庙中层柱廊北侧诞生浮雕：阿蒙与托特。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_deir_el_bahari_birth_cycle.3','storyver.egyptian.deir_el_bahari_birth_cycle',3,'托特引路','Thoth leads the way','第三幕画的是托特在前面领路，阿蒙跟在后面，一起前往王后雅赫摩斯所在的王宫。这一幕没有多少文字，画面本身却传达出一种庄严的气氛：两位神一前一后，像是要去完成一件隆重的仪式。在这组浮雕里，神与人之间的界限不断被跨越：众神之王离开天上的宝座，亲自来到凡间的王宫。古埃及的神庙浮雕往往像连环画一样，一幕接一幕地展开，观者沿着柱廊走过，就能依次读完整个故事。在哈特谢普苏特的时代，这些浮雕原本色彩鲜艳，有些地方至今还保留着蓝色和红色的颜料痕迹。浮雕中阿蒙的形象后来在阿赫那吞推行单一崇拜阿吞的时候遭到破坏，许多阿蒙的名字和形象被凿去，后来又被修复过；而哈特谢普苏特本人的形象，则在她去世以后被图特摩斯三世下令凿毁。今天的观者看到的，是一组经历过两次破坏的画面，这也让这个神圣诞生的故事带上了一层历史的伤痕。','The third scene shows Thoth leading the way and Amun following, going together to the palace where Queen Ahmose is. There is little text, but the image conveys solemnity: two gods, one before the other, as if going to perform a great rite. In this cycle the boundary between gods and mortals is crossed again and again: the king of the gods leaves his throne in heaven and comes in person to an earthly palace. Egyptian temple reliefs often unfold like a picture-strip, scene after scene, so that one walking along the colonnade reads the story in order. In Hatshepsut''s day the reliefs were brightly painted, and traces of blue and red survive in places. Amun''s figures were later damaged when Akhenaten promoted the sole worship of the Aten, many of Amun''s names and images being hacked out and afterwards restored; and Hatshepsut''s own figures were defaced after her death on the orders of Thutmose III. Today''s viewer sees scenes that have suffered twice, and the tale of the sacred birth carries the scars of history.','claim.v0360.hatshepsut_birth.03','代尔拜赫里神庙中层柱廊北侧诞生浮雕：托特引路。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_deir_el_bahari_birth_cycle.4','storyver.egyptian.deir_el_bahari_birth_cycle',4,'王后的寝宫','In the queen''s chamber','阿蒙化作王后的丈夫图特摩斯一世的模样，来到雅赫摩斯的寝宫。王后正在宫中最美的房间里安睡，神的香气使她醒了过来，她对着神的威严微笑。神立刻走到她身边，让她看见了他作为神的真身。她看见神的美貌，心中欢喜，神的爱流进了她的四肢，整个宫殿都弥漫着神的芬芳，那香气全是来自蓬特的香料。浮雕中，阿蒙和王后面对面坐在一张床上，床由两位女神塞尔凯特和奈特托着，阿蒙把象征生命的符号递到王后面前。王后对神说：您的威名是多么伟大，看见您的面容是多么美好。阿蒙对她说：“与阿蒙结合的哈特谢普苏特”将是我放在你身体里的这个女儿的名字，她将在这整片土地上行使美好的王权。这一幕是整个浮雕的核心，可是它的表现方式极为含蓄：画面上没有任何露骨的描绘，只有香气、光芒和生命之符，神的结合被表现为一种神圣的给予。','Amun came to Ahmose''s chamber in the form of her husband, Thutmose I. The queen was sleeping in the loveliest room of the palace; the fragrance of the god woke her, and she smiled at his majesty. At once he went to her and let her see him in his form as a god. She rejoiced at the sight of his beauty, his love passed into her limbs, and the palace was flooded with the god''s fragrance — all its scents were those of Punt. In the relief Amun and the queen sit facing each other on a couch borne by the goddesses Selket and Neith, and Amun holds the sign of life to her. The queen says to the god: how great is your fame, how fine it is to see your face. Amun says to her: Khenemet-Amun Hatshepsut shall be the name of this daughter whom I have placed in your body; she shall exercise the excellent kingship in this whole land. This scene is the heart of the cycle, yet it is shown with great restraint: nothing explicit, only fragrance, radiance and the sign of life — the god''s union is shown as a sacred gift.','claim.v0360.hatshepsut_birth.04','代尔拜赫里神庙中层柱廊北侧诞生浮雕：阿蒙与雅赫摩斯王后。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_deir_el_bahari_birth_cycle.5','storyver.egyptian.deir_el_bahari_birth_cycle',5,'阿蒙的吩咐','Amun''s command','阿蒙离开王后以后，来到陶工之神克努姆面前，吩咐他塑造将要出生的孩子。在浮雕中，阿蒙对克努姆说：去为我塑造她，我的女儿，还有她的卡，用这些高贵的四肢；我要让她成为一位比所有神都伟大的人，她将成为两地之王。克努姆回答说，他会照阿蒙的吩咐去做：他要把她塑造得比所有的神都美好，她的形象将在众神之上，为了上下埃及之王的尊严。在古埃及人的观念里，“卡”是人的生命力和精神上的分身，它与人同时诞生，伴随人的一生，人死后，卡依然存在，需要供品的供养。克努姆不仅要塑造孩子的身体，还要塑造她的卡，这意味着这个孩子从一开始就被赋予了完整的、神圣的生命。在前面的故事里，克努姆曾在拉-哈拉赫提的命令下为巴塔造了一个妻子；在这里，他又在阿蒙的命令下为一位未来的国王塑造身体。两次都是由一位更高的神发出命令，克努姆则用他的陶轮来完成。','Leaving the queen, Amun came to Khnum, the potter god, and commanded him to fashion the child to be born. In the relief Amun says to Khnum: go and fashion her for me, my daughter, and her ka, with these noble limbs; I will make her greater than all the gods; she shall be king of the Two Lands. Khnum answers that he will do as Amun commands: he will make her better than all the gods, her form exalted above theirs, for the dignity of the King of Upper and Lower Egypt. To the Egyptians the ka was a person''s life-force and spiritual double, born with him, accompanying him through life, and surviving after death, when it needed offerings. Khnum is to shape not only the child''s body but her ka, which means she is given a whole and sacred life from the start. In another tale Khnum made a wife for Bata at Pre-Harakhti''s command; here he shapes the body of a future king at Amun''s command. Both times a higher god gives the order, and Khnum carries it out on his wheel.','claim.v0360.hatshepsut_birth.05','代尔拜赫里神庙中层柱廊北侧诞生浮雕：阿蒙吩咐克努姆。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_deir_el_bahari_birth_cycle.6','storyver.egyptian.deir_el_bahari_birth_cycle',6,'陶轮上的孩子','The child on the potter''s wheel','这一幕是整组浮雕中最著名的画面之一：羊头的克努姆坐在陶轮前，陶轮上站着两个一模一样的小孩，一个是哈特谢普苏特的身体，一个是她的卡。在克努姆对面，蛙头的生育女神赫凯特跪着，把象征生命的符号伸向两个孩子，赋予她们生命。题记中，克努姆对孩子说：我用阿蒙的这些四肢塑造了你，我来塑造你，要让你比所有的神都美好；我赐给你一切生命和满足、一切稳固、一切心中的喜悦；我赐给你一切健康、一切土地、一切国度和一切人民；我赐给你在荷鲁斯的宝座上作为两地之王出现，像拉一样，直到永远。在古埃及艺术中，陶轮上塑造婴儿的画面后来成了“神圣诞生”主题的标准图像，在卢克索神庙阿蒙霍特普三世的浮雕和后来托勒密时代神庙的“诞生之屋”里都能看到。克努姆用泥土造人的观念非常古老，这一形象说明：每一个生命都由神亲手塑造，而国王的生命则格外完美。','This is one of the most famous scenes of the cycle: ram-headed Khnum sits at his potter''s wheel, on which stand two identical small children — Hatshepsut''s body and her ka. Facing Khnum kneels the frog-headed birth goddess Heqet, holding the sign of life toward the two children to give them life. In the caption Khnum says to the child: I have formed you of these limbs of Amun; I have come to fashion you better than all the gods; I give you all life and satisfaction, all stability, all joy of heart; I give you all health, all lands, all countries and all people; I give you to appear upon the throne of Horus as king of the Two Lands, like Re, for ever. In Egyptian art the infant shaped on the wheel became the standard image of the divine birth, seen again in Amenhotep III''s reliefs at Luxor temple and in the ''birth houses'' of Ptolemaic temples. The idea of Khnum making people from clay is very old; the image says that every life is shaped by a god''s hand, and the king''s life is shaped to perfection.','claim.v0360.hatshepsut_birth.06','代尔拜赫里神庙中层柱廊北侧诞生浮雕：克努姆的陶轮。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_deir_el_bahari_birth_cycle.7','storyver.egyptian.deir_el_bahari_birth_cycle',7,'托特的报喜','Thoth''s announcement','孩子在陶轮上塑造完成以后，托特又一次出场。这一次，他来到王后雅赫摩斯面前，向她报喜。在浮雕中，托特举起手，对王后说出了一长串称号：她是上下埃及之王的伟大的妻子，是众神喜爱的人，是两地的女主人，阿蒙对她的高贵地位感到满意。托特的这番话，等于正式宣布王后将成为神子的母亲。这一幕的构图很像后来许多文化中的“报喜”图像：一位神的使者来到一位将要怀上神子的女子面前，宣告她的荣耀。当然，古埃及的这组浮雕比那些后来的作品早了一千多年，它们之间是否有联系，学者们看法不一，本篇不作推测。对古埃及人来说，这一幕的意义在于确认：王后雅赫摩斯怀的孩子，是在众神的见证下、按照众神的意旨而来的。托特作为书记之神出现在这里，也像是在为这件事做一份神圣的记录。','When the child had been shaped on the wheel, Thoth appeared again — this time before Queen Ahmose, to announce the birth to her. In the relief Thoth raises his hand and addresses the queen with a long string of titles: great wife of the King of Upper and Lower Egypt, beloved of the gods, mistress of the Two Lands; Amun is satisfied with her great dignity. Thoth''s words amount to a formal proclamation that the queen will be mother of the god''s child. The composition resembles the ''annunciation'' images of many later cultures: a divine messenger comes to a woman who will bear a god''s child and declares her glory. These Egyptian reliefs are more than a thousand years older than those later works, and scholars disagree whether there is any link; this retelling makes no conjecture. For the Egyptians the meaning of the scene was to confirm that the child Queen Ahmose carried came by the gods'' will and before their witness. Thoth, god of scribes, appears here as if making a sacred record of the event.','claim.v0360.hatshepsut_birth.07','代尔拜赫里神庙中层柱廊北侧诞生浮雕：托特向王后报喜。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_deir_el_bahari_birth_cycle.8','storyver.egyptian.deir_el_bahari_birth_cycle',8,'走向产房','To the birth chamber','接下来，克努姆和赫凯特一左一右，牵着王后雅赫摩斯的手，领她走向产房。浮雕中的王后腹部微微隆起，神态安详，身后跟着一列神祇。这一幕把天上的创造和人间的生产连接在一起：在陶轮上塑造孩子的克努姆和赋予她生命的赫凯特，现在亲自陪伴母亲走向分娩的地方。古埃及的妇女分娩时，往往在一个专门的地方进行，有时是家中的一个小房间，有时是屋外搭起的凉棚，人们会在那里供奉保护产妇的神灵，如赫凯特、塔维瑞特和贝斯。在王室的诞生浮雕中，这些民间的习俗被升华成了神圣的仪式：护送王后的不是凡间的接生婆，而是创造生命的神本身。这一幕的题记中，赫凯特和克努姆向王后说着祝福的话，许诺她平安生产。从众神的会议到这里，整个故事一步一步地从天上走到了人间，下一幕，孩子就要诞生了。','Next Khnum and Heqet, one on each side, take Queen Ahmose by the hands and lead her to the birth chamber. In the relief the queen is gently swollen with child and serene, followed by a line of gods. The scene joins heavenly creation to earthly birth: Khnum, who shaped the child on the wheel, and Heqet, who gave it life, now accompany the mother in person to the place of birth. Egyptian women gave birth in a special place — sometimes a small room of the house, sometimes a bower set up outside — where they honoured the gods who protected women in labour, such as Heqet, Taweret and Bes. In the royal birth reliefs these everyday customs are raised into sacred ritual: the queen is escorted not by mortal midwives but by the very gods who make life. In the captions Heqet and Khnum speak blessings over the queen and promise her a safe delivery. From the council of the gods to here, the story has come step by step from heaven to earth; in the next scene the child is born.','claim.v0360.hatshepsut_birth.08','代尔拜赫里神庙中层柱廊北侧诞生浮雕：王后被领往产房。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_deir_el_bahari_birth_cycle.9','storyver.egyptian.deir_el_bahari_birth_cycle',9,'诞生','The birth','诞生的一幕画得庄严而拥挤：王后雅赫摩斯坐在宝座上，抱着刚出生的婴儿，四周围绕着许多神灵和精灵，它们分成几排，有的跪着，有的举着手表示欢呼。其中有掌管出生和命运的女神梅斯赫奈特，她坐在产砖之上——古埃及妇女分娩时蹲坐在砖上，这些砖本身也被神化了。梅斯赫奈特对孩子说出了祝福的话，许诺她将像拉一样永远出现在荷鲁斯的宝座上。浮雕中还有象征上下埃及的女神，有人认为她们在为新生的统治者守护两地。这一幕的许多细节已经残缺，学者们根据不同的线描和照片，对其中一些神灵的身份有不同的看法，本篇只讲能够辨认的部分。可以确定的是，浮雕的设计者想要表现的，是整个神界都在为这个孩子的诞生而欢庆。对一位要以女性身份称王的统治者来说，这样隆重的诞生场面，正是她最有力的宣言：她的王位不是偶然得来的，而是从出生那一刻起就被众神认定了的。','The birth scene is solemn and crowded: Queen Ahmose sits enthroned holding the newborn child, surrounded by many gods and spirits in rows, some kneeling, some raising their hands in acclaim. Among them is Meskhenet, goddess of birth and destiny, seated on the birth-brick — Egyptian women gave birth crouching on bricks, and the bricks themselves were deified. Meskhenet speaks blessings over the child, promising that she will appear on the throne of Horus like Re for ever. There are also goddesses of Upper and Lower Egypt, thought by some to guard the Two Lands for the new ruler. Many details of the scene are damaged, and scholars differ on the identity of some figures depending on the drawings and photographs they use; this retelling tells only what can be made out. What is certain is that the designers meant to show the whole divine world rejoicing at the child''s birth. For a ruler who was to reign as king though a woman, so solemn a birth was her strongest declaration: her throne did not come by chance but was confirmed by the gods from the moment she was born.','claim.v0360.hatshepsut_birth.09','代尔拜赫里神庙中层柱廊北侧诞生浮雕：诞生。','诞生一幕残缺较多，部分神灵的身份学者意见不一。'),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_deir_el_bahari_birth_cycle.10','storyver.egyptian.deir_el_bahari_birth_cycle',10,'献给阿蒙','Presented to Amun','孩子出生以后，女神哈托尔把她抱到阿蒙面前。阿蒙伸出双手接过孩子，对她说话。题记中，阿蒙称她为自己的女儿：来吧，平安地来吧，我亲生的女儿，我心爱的玛阿特卡拉，你是占有两地、坐在荷鲁斯宝座上的国王，直到永远。玛阿特卡拉是哈特谢普苏特称王以后的王名，意思是“玛阿特是拉的卡”。在这里，阿蒙用她作为国王的名字来称呼一个刚出生的婴儿，说明这组浮雕是在她称王以后、按照王权的需要来设计的：她从出生起就被叫作国王。这一幕和故事的第一幕前后呼应：在开头，阿蒙在众神面前宣布他要生下一位统治者；在这里，他亲手接过这位统治者，承认她是自己的女儿，并且当着众神的面宣布她的王权。哈托尔在这组浮雕中扮演着母亲与保护者的角色：她是天空和爱的女神，也常被描绘成一头神牛，下一幕里，她还要哺育这个孩子。','After the birth the goddess Hathor carried the child to Amun. Amun stretched out his hands, took the child and spoke to her. In the caption Amun calls her his daughter: come, come in peace, daughter of my body, my beloved Maatkare; you are the king who takes possession of the Two Lands upon the throne of Horus for ever. Maatkare was the throne name Hatshepsut took as king, meaning ''Maat is the ka of Re''. Here Amun calls a newborn infant by her royal name, which shows that the cycle was designed after she became king and to serve her kingship: she is called king from her birth. The scene answers the first: at the start Amun announced to the gods that he would beget a ruler; here he takes that ruler in his own hands, acknowledges her as his daughter, and proclaims her kingship before the gods. Hathor plays mother and protector in the cycle: goddess of the sky and of love, often shown as a divine cow, she will also nurse the child in the next scene.','claim.v0360.hatshepsut_birth.10','代尔拜赫里神庙中层柱廊北侧诞生浮雕：哈托尔把孩子献给阿蒙。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_deir_el_bahari_birth_cycle.11','storyver.egyptian.deir_el_bahari_birth_cycle',11,'神的哺育','The divine nursing','接下来，阿蒙吩咐众女神哺育这个孩子。浮雕中，哈托尔和其他乳母女神坐着，给婴儿和她的卡喂奶；在下面，还画着两头神牛，婴儿和她的卡跪在牛的身下吮吸乳汁。在古埃及的宗教中，哈托尔常以母牛的形象出现，她的乳汁被认为能赐予神圣的力量。法老被描绘成吮吸女神之乳的形象，在许多神庙和雕像中都能看到，这象征着国王从神那里获得了生命和权力。在这组浮雕里，哺育的场面也同时出现了孩子的卡，说明她的身体和她的精神分身都得到了神的滋养。尼罗河的神和其他赐予丰饶的神灵也出现在这一部分，为孩子带来生命所需的一切。这一幕温柔而庄严：一个刚出生的孩子，被天上的母亲们围绕着，喝着神的乳汁长大。对哈特谢普苏特来说，这一幕的意义在于强调她与众神的亲密关系——她不仅由神所生、由神塑造，还由神亲自养育。','Next Amun commands the goddesses to nurse the child. In the relief Hathor and other nurse goddesses sit suckling the infant and her ka; below, two divine cows are shown, with the infant and her ka kneeling to drink beneath them. In Egyptian religion Hathor often takes the form of a cow, and her milk was believed to give divine power. Pharaohs shown drinking from a goddess''s breast appear in many temples and statues, symbolising that the king receives life and power from the gods. Here the nursing scene includes the child''s ka as well, showing that both her body and her spiritual double are nourished by the gods. Nile gods and other bringers of plenty also appear in this part, bringing the child all that life needs. The scene is tender and solemn: a newborn surrounded by heavenly mothers, growing on the milk of the gods. For Hatshepsut its meaning is her closeness to the gods — not only born of a god and shaped by a god, but nursed by the gods themselves.','claim.v0360.hatshepsut_birth.11','代尔拜赫里神庙中层柱廊北侧诞生浮雕：哺育孩子。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_deir_el_bahari_birth_cycle.12','storyver.egyptian.deir_el_bahari_birth_cycle',12,'托特呈子与神定寿命','Thoth presents the child, and the gods fix her lifetime','在诞生浮雕的最后几幕，托特把孩子和她的卡一起呈到阿蒙面前（学者布鲁纳编为第十四幕）。接着，众神为这个孩子定下了她的寿命（第十五幕）：画面中，托特和书写女神塞莎特等神灵记录下她将统治的年数——千百万年，象征着永恒的王权。在古埃及的观念里，神灵在王的诞生或加冕时为他定下在位的年数，并把它记录在圣树的叶子或年历上，这是确认王权的重要仪式。至此，整个故事画上了句号：从众神的会议开始，经过神与王后的结合、陶轮上的塑造、报喜、诞生、献子和哺育，最后以众神确定她的寿命结束。这组浮雕是古埃及“神圣诞生”主题最早的完整实例之一，后来阿蒙霍特普三世在卢克索神庙也刻下了类似的诞生浮雕。哈特谢普苏特用这个故事向世人宣告：她是阿蒙-拉亲生的女儿，她的王位来自天上。讽刺的是，在她死后，她的名字和形象被从许多纪念物上抹去，正是这些残存的神庙浮雕，让她的故事流传至今。','In the last scenes of the cycle Thoth presents the child and her ka together to Amun (Brunner''s scene 14). Then the gods fix the child''s lifetime (scene 15): Thoth, the writing goddess Seshat and other gods record the years she will reign — millions of years, a sign of eternal kingship. In Egyptian thought the gods set a king''s years of rule at his birth or coronation and recorded them on the leaves of the sacred tree or on annals, an important rite confirming kingship. So the whole story closes: it began with the council of the gods and ran through the union of the god and the queen, the shaping on the wheel, the announcement, the birth, the presentation and the nursing, and ends with the gods fixing her lifetime. The cycle is one of the earliest complete examples of the Egyptian ''divine birth'' theme; later Amenhotep III carved a similar birth cycle at Luxor temple. With this story Hatshepsut declared that she was the true daughter of Amun-Re and that her throne came from heaven. Ironically, after her death her name and images were erased from many monuments, and it is these surviving temple reliefs that have carried her story to us.','claim.v0360.hatshepsut_birth.12','代尔拜赫里神庙中层柱廊北侧诞生浮雕：托特呈子（第十四幕）与众神定寿（第十五幕）。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_shabaka_stone_memphite.1','storyver.egyptian.shabaka_stone_memphite',1,'被虫蛀的古书','The worm-eaten book','石板最上面的两行横写的文字，是沙巴卡国王的王衔和一段说明：上下埃及之王沙巴卡，受“城墙之南的普塔”喜爱的人，愿他像拉一样永远活着。陛下在他父亲“城墙之南的普塔”的神庙里，把这篇文字重新抄写了一遍。陛下发现它是祖先留下的一部作品，已经被虫蛀坏了，从头到尾都读不懂。于是陛下重新抄写了它，让它比原来更好，好让他的名字和他的纪念物在他父亲普塔的神庙里永远长存，这是上下埃及之王为他的父亲普塔所做的事，愿他永远拥有生命。这段话很有意思：一位法老说自己在神庙里找到了一卷被虫蛀坏的古书，并下令把它刻在石头上，好让它永不毁坏。沙巴卡是来自南方库施的国王，他统治埃及时，特别注重恢复古老的宗教传统，把这篇文字刻在石头上，也是在宣示自己是古老秩序的继承者。讽刺的是，这块为了“永存”而刻的石头，后来却被人当作磨盘，中间的许多文字都被磨掉了。','The two horizontal lines at the top of the slab give King Shabaka''s titles and a statement: the King of Upper and Lower Egypt Shabaka, beloved of Ptah-South-of-his-Wall, living like Re for ever. His Majesty copied this text anew in the house of his father Ptah-South-of-his-Wall. His Majesty had found it as a work of the ancestors that was worm-eaten, so that it could not be understood from beginning to end. His Majesty copied it anew so that it became better than it had been before, in order that his name might endure and his monument last in the house of his father Ptah — what the King of Upper and Lower Egypt did for his father Ptah, may he be given life for ever. The statement is striking: a pharaoh says he found a worm-eaten book in the temple and had it carved in stone so that it would never perish. Shabaka was a king from Kush in the south who, ruling Egypt, set great store on restoring ancient religious tradition; carving this text in stone also declared him heir to the old order. Ironically the stone carved to last for ever was later used as a millstone, and much of its text was ground away.','claim.v0360.memphite.01','沙巴卡石（大英博物馆 EA 498）沙巴卡的序言。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_shabaka_stone_memphite.2','storyver.egyptian.shabaka_stone_memphite',2,'塔特恩','Ta-tenen','序言之后，碑文称颂普塔：上下埃及之王就是这个伟大的名字——塔特恩，城墙之南，永恒之主。“塔特恩”的意思是“升起的土地”，指的是在创世之初从原始之水中升起的第一块土丘。在孟菲斯的神学里，普塔与塔特恩合而为一，他既是创造万物的神，也是那第一块从混沌之水中露出的大地。“城墙之南”指普塔在孟菲斯的神庙位于“白城墙”——孟菲斯古老的城墙——的南边。碑文接着说，他就是统一这片土地的人。在古埃及人看来，孟菲斯是上下埃及交界的地方，传说中第一位统一埃及的国王就在这里建都，所以孟菲斯被称为“两地的天平”。普塔作为孟菲斯的主神，被看作这种统一的神圣保证者。这一段为后面的两个故事做了铺垫：盖布把埃及给了荷鲁斯，荷鲁斯在孟菲斯统一了两地；而普塔，则是这一切背后的创造者。在其他城市的神学里，创世之神是阿图姆或别的神，孟菲斯的祭司们则通过这篇文字，宣称普塔才是最古老、最伟大的神。','After the preface the text praises Ptah: the King of Upper and Lower Egypt is this Great Name, Ta-tenen, South-of-his-Wall, Lord of Eternity. Ta-tenen means ''the risen land'', the first mound that rose from the primeval waters at the beginning of creation. In the theology of Memphis Ptah and Ta-tenen are one: he is both the god who made all things and the first earth that emerged from the waters of chaos. ''South-of-his-Wall'' refers to Ptah''s temple standing south of the White Wall, the ancient wall of Memphis. The text goes on: he is the one who united this land. To the Egyptians Memphis lay where Upper and Lower Egypt met; the first king to unite Egypt was said to have founded his capital there, so Memphis was called ''the Balance of the Two Lands''. Ptah, god of Memphis, was held to be the divine guarantor of that unity. This prepares the two stories that follow: Geb gives Egypt to Horus, and Horus unites the Two Lands at Memphis; and Ptah stands behind it all as creator. In the theologies of other cities the creator was Atum or another god; through this text the priests of Memphis claimed that Ptah was the oldest and greatest of the gods.','claim.v0360.memphite.02','沙巴卡石（大英博物馆 EA 498）普塔-塔特恩的称号。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_shabaka_stone_memphite.3','storyver.egyptian.shabaka_stone_memphite',3,'盖布的第一次裁决','Geb''s first judgement','碑文接着讲了一个关于王权的故事。众神之主盖布下令，让九柱神聚集到他的身边。他在荷鲁斯和塞特之间进行裁判，结束了他们的争吵。他让塞特在上埃及的土地上做上埃及之王，一直到塞特出生的地方苏；又让荷鲁斯在下埃及的土地上做下埃及之王，一直到他父亲淹死的地方，那个地方叫做“两地的分界”。这样，荷鲁斯管辖一个地区，塞特管辖一个地区，他们在阿延为两地订立了和约。这就是两地的划分。盖布是大地之神，是奥西里斯和塞特的父亲，荷鲁斯的祖父，在众神的谱系中，他是王权的传承者。荷鲁斯和塞特之争是古埃及最著名的神话之一：塞特杀死了他的哥哥奥西里斯，奥西里斯的儿子荷鲁斯要为父报仇、夺回王位。在其他版本里，这场争斗持续了八十年，在众神的法庭上反复辩论。在孟菲斯神学里，裁决却显得简洁而有秩序：盖布先把国家一分为二，让两位神各治一方，以和平的方式结束争端。','Next the text tells a story of kingship. Geb, lord of the gods, commanded that the Nine Gods gather to him. He judged between Horus and Seth and ended their quarrel. He made Seth king of Upper Egypt in the land of Upper Egypt, up to the place where he was born, which is Su; and Geb made Horus king of Lower Egypt in the land of Lower Egypt, up to the place where his father was drowned, which is ''Division-of-the-Two-Lands''. So Horus stood over one region and Seth over another, and they made peace over the Two Lands at Ayan. That was the division of the Two Lands. Geb, god of the earth, father of Osiris and Seth and grandfather of Horus, is the one through whom kingship passes in the genealogy of the gods. The contest of Horus and Seth is one of the most famous Egyptian myths: Seth killed his brother Osiris, and Osiris''s son Horus sought to avenge him and win back the throne. In other versions the dispute lasts eighty years and is argued over and over before the gods'' tribunal. In the Memphite Theology the judgement is brief and orderly: Geb first divides the land, letting each god rule one part and ending the quarrel in peace.','claim.v0360.memphite.03','沙巴卡石（大英博物馆 EA 498）盖布的第一次裁决。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_shabaka_stone_memphite.4','storyver.egyptian.shabaka_stone_memphite',4,'全部遗产归于荷鲁斯','The whole inheritance to Horus','可是后来，盖布觉得荷鲁斯的份额和塞特的份额一样，这样不对。于是盖布把他全部的遗产都给了荷鲁斯，因为荷鲁斯是他儿子的儿子，是他的长子之子。盖布对九柱神说：我已经选定了你，荷鲁斯，你是长子。盖布说：我把遗产给这位继承人，给我儿子的儿子，给上埃及的豺狼，给开路者，给我所生的那一位的儿子。于是，荷鲁斯站在这片土地之上，成为这片土地的统一者，被宣称为伟大的名字：塔特恩，城墙之南，永恒之主。两位伟大的魔法女神——也就是上埃及和下埃及的王冠——长在了他的头上。他就是荷鲁斯，作为上下埃及之王出现，在“城墙之州”统一了两地，那正是两地统一的地方。这一段把荷鲁斯的王权与孟菲斯紧紧地联系在一起：统一埃及的地方就是孟菲斯，而荷鲁斯在这里得到的称号，正是普塔的称号“塔特恩”。这样，人间的王权、荷鲁斯的神话和孟菲斯的主神普塔，在这篇文字里融为一体。','But then it seemed wrong to Geb that the portion of Horus was like the portion of Seth. So Geb gave his entire inheritance to Horus, for he was the son of his son, his firstborn. Geb said to the Nine Gods: ''I have chosen you, Horus, the firstborn.'' Geb said: ''I give the inheritance to this heir, to the son of my son, the Upper Egyptian jackal, the opener of the ways, the son born of my son.'' Then Horus stood over the land, the uniter of this land, proclaimed in the great name Ta-tenen, South-of-his-Wall, Lord of Eternity. The Two Great Magicians — the crowns of Upper and Lower Egypt — grew upon his head. He is Horus who arose as King of Upper and Lower Egypt, who united the Two Lands in the Wall Nome, the place where the Two Lands were united. This passage binds Horus''s kingship tightly to Memphis: the place where Egypt was united is Memphis, and the title Horus receives there is Ptah''s own title, Ta-tenen. So earthly kingship, the myth of Horus and Memphis''s god Ptah merge into one in this text.','claim.v0360.memphite.04','沙巴卡石（大英博物馆 EA 498）全部遗产归于荷鲁斯。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_shabaka_stone_memphite.5','storyver.egyptian.shabaka_stone_memphite',5,'芦苇与纸草','Reed and papyrus','碑文接着说：芦苇和纸草被放在普塔神庙的双扇大门上。芦苇是上埃及的象征植物，纸草是下埃及的象征植物，把它们一起放在普塔神庙的门口，象征着两地在普塔的庇护下统一。这意味着荷鲁斯和塞特得到了平息、联合了起来，他们像兄弟一样相处，不再争吵，在他们所在的任何地方，都在普塔的神庙——两地的天平——里结为一体，在那里，上埃及和下埃及被衡量。在这篇文字里，荷鲁斯和塞特的故事没有以一方的毁灭告终，而是以和解告终：他们在孟菲斯和好了。这与其他一些版本不同，在那些版本里，塞特被打败、受到惩罚，甚至被描绘成邪恶的化身。孟菲斯的祭司们更看重统一与和平，他们把普塔的神庙描绘成两地和解的地方。这一段之后，石板上有一大段文字因为被当作磨盘使用而几乎完全磨损了，学者们只能辨认出零星的词句，有人认为那里原本是一段戏剧性的对话。本篇不补写这段残缺。','The text goes on: reed and papyrus were placed on the double door of the house of Ptah. The reed was the emblem of Upper Egypt and the papyrus of Lower Egypt; set together at the door of Ptah''s temple, they signify the union of the Two Lands under Ptah''s protection. That means Horus and Seth were pacified and united; they fraternised and ceased to quarrel wherever they might be, being united in the house of Ptah, the Balance of the Two Lands, in which Upper and Lower Egypt are weighed. Here the tale of Horus and Seth ends not with one side destroyed but in reconciliation: they make peace at Memphis. This differs from other versions in which Seth is defeated and punished, even portrayed as the embodiment of evil. The priests of Memphis valued unity and peace and made Ptah''s temple the place where the Two Lands were reconciled. After this passage a long stretch of the slab was almost entirely worn away by its use as a millstone; scholars can read only scattered words, and some think it held a dramatic dialogue. This retelling does not fill in the gap.','claim.v0360.memphite.05','沙巴卡石（大英博物馆 EA 498）普塔神庙门上的芦苇与纸草。','此段之后石板有大段文字因被用作磨盘而磨损，残缺处不补写。'),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_shabaka_stone_memphite.6','storyver.egyptian.shabaka_stone_memphite',6,'普塔的诸形','The forms of Ptah','在磨损的部分之后，碑文进入了最著名的“神学”部分。它首先列出以普塔的形式出现的众神：在伟大宝座上的普塔；普塔-努恩，生出阿图姆的父亲；普塔-瑙内特，生下阿图姆的母亲；伟大的普塔，九柱神的心与舌；还有其他几种形式，其中一些名字已经残缺。努恩和瑙内特是原始之水的男性和女性化身，在赫利奥波利斯的创世神话里，阿图姆从努恩之中出现，自己创造了世界。孟菲斯的祭司们在这里做了一个巧妙的安排：他们并不否认阿图姆的创世之功，而是说，阿图姆本身是由普塔生出来的——普塔以原始之水的形式，成为阿图姆的父亲和母亲。这样，普塔就站在了一切神的源头，比阿图姆更古老、更根本。这种把其他城市的主神解释为本城主神的某种形式或产物的做法，在古埃及宗教中很常见，学者们称之为“神学的整合”。普塔在这里不再只是孟菲斯的工匠之神，而是成了万物之始。','After the worn section the text enters its most famous part, the ''theology''. It first lists the gods who came into being as Ptah: Ptah upon the Great Throne; Ptah-Nun, the father who made Atum; Ptah-Naunet, the mother who bore Atum; Ptah the Great, heart and tongue of the Nine Gods; and several other forms, some of whose names are damaged. Nun and Naunet are the male and female forms of the primeval waters; in the creation myth of Heliopolis Atum emerged from Nun and made the world by himself. The priests of Memphis make a clever move here: they do not deny Atum''s work of creation but say that Atum himself was born of Ptah — that Ptah, as the primeval waters, was Atum''s father and mother. So Ptah stands at the source of all the gods, older and more fundamental than Atum. Interpreting the chief god of another city as a form or product of one''s own was common in Egyptian religion; scholars call it theological integration. Ptah is no longer only the craftsman god of Memphis but the beginning of all things.','claim.v0360.memphite.06','沙巴卡石（大英博物馆 EA 498）以普塔形式出现的众神。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_shabaka_stone_memphite.7','storyver.egyptian.shabaka_stone_memphite',7,'心与舌','Heart and tongue','碑文接着说：在心中成形的，在舌上成形的，是阿图姆的形象。因为那位极其伟大的神是普塔，他通过这颗心和这条舌头，把生命赐给了所有的神和他们的卡；荷鲁斯以普塔的形式在这颗心中成形，托特以普塔的形式在这条舌头上成形。于是，心和舌支配着所有的肢体，因为心存在于每一个身体之中，舌存在于每一张嘴里——所有的神、所有的人、所有的牲畜、所有爬行的东西、一切有生命的东西，心想它想要的，舌命令它想要的。在古埃及人的观念里，心是思想、意志和情感的所在，舌则把心中所想的说出来、变成命令。孟菲斯神学把这两者放在创世的中心：普塔先在心中构想，再用舌头说出，世界就这样诞生了。荷鲁斯与心相连，托特与舌相连——托特是智慧和文字之神，常被称为“普塔的舌头”。这种通过思想和话语创造世界的观念，在古代近东的宗教文献中极为罕见，许多学者把它与后来的“道”或“逻各斯”的观念相比较，但本篇不推测它们之间的联系。','The text goes on: there took shape in the heart, there took shape on the tongue the form of Atum. For the very great one is Ptah, who gave life to all the gods and their kas through this heart and through this tongue, in which Horus had taken shape as Ptah, in which Thoth had taken shape as Ptah. Thus heart and tongue rule over all the limbs, for the heart is in every body and the tongue in every mouth — of all gods, all men, all cattle, all creeping things, all that lives — the heart thinking whatever it wishes and the tongue commanding whatever it wishes. To the Egyptians the heart was the seat of thought, will and feeling, and the tongue spoke what the heart conceived and turned it into command. The Memphite Theology places the two at the centre of creation: Ptah first conceives in his heart, then speaks with his tongue, and so the world is born. Horus is joined to the heart and Thoth to the tongue — Thoth, god of wisdom and writing, often called the tongue of Ptah. Creation through thought and word is very rare in ancient Near Eastern religious texts, and many scholars compare it with later ideas of the Word or Logos; this retelling makes no claim about any link.','claim.v0360.memphite.07','沙巴卡石（大英博物馆 EA 498）心与舌的神学。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_shabaka_stone_memphite.8','storyver.egyptian.shabaka_stone_memphite',8,'牙齿与嘴唇','Teeth and lips','碑文接着把普塔的九柱神和阿图姆的九柱神作了对比：普塔的九柱神在他面前，是心与舌、牙齿与嘴唇。阿图姆的九柱神是通过他的精液和手指诞生的；而普塔的九柱神，则是这张嘴里的牙齿和嘴唇，这张嘴说出了一切事物的名字，舒和泰芙努特就是从这张嘴里出来的，这张嘴生出了九柱神。在赫利奥波利斯的创世神话里，阿图姆独自一人，用身体的方式——他的精液和手——生出了第一对神舒和泰芙努特；有的版本则说他打喷嚏生出舒，吐口水生出泰芙努特。孟菲斯的祭司们在这里并没有否定那个古老的神话，而是重新解释了它：所谓“精液和手指”，在普塔这里就是“牙齿和嘴唇”；舒和泰芙努特不是从身体里生出来的，而是被说出来的。这是对创世方式的一次深刻改写：从身体的生殖，变成了语言的命名。说出一样东西的名字，就是创造了它。在古埃及人看来，名字有着神奇的力量，知道一个神的真名，就能掌握他的力量；在这里，命名本身就成了创造。','The text then compares Ptah''s Ennead with Atum''s: his Ennead is before him as heart and tongue, teeth and lips. Atum''s Ennead came into being through his semen and his fingers; but Ptah''s Ennead is the teeth and lips in this mouth which pronounced the name of every thing, from which Shu and Tefnut came forth, and which gave birth to the Ennead. In the creation myth of Heliopolis Atum, alone, brought forth the first pair, Shu and Tefnut, from his body — from his semen and hand; in some versions he sneezed out Shu and spat out Tefnut. The priests of Memphis do not deny the old myth but reinterpret it: what was ''semen and fingers'' for Atum is ''teeth and lips'' for Ptah; Shu and Tefnut are not born of a body but spoken. It is a profound rewriting of creation, from bodily begetting to the naming of words. To speak the name of a thing is to create it. To the Egyptians names had magical power — to know a god''s true name was to hold his power — and here naming itself becomes creation.','claim.v0360.memphite.08','沙巴卡石（大英博物馆 EA 498）牙齿与嘴唇。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_shabaka_stone_memphite.9','storyver.egyptian.shabaka_stone_memphite',9,'心想与舌说','What the heart devises and the tongue commands','碑文接着解释这种创造是怎样发生的：眼睛的看见，耳朵的听见，鼻子的呼吸，都报告给心。是心使一切认识产生，是舌说出心所想的。就这样，所有的神都诞生了，他的九柱神也完成了。因为神的每一句话，都是通过心所构想、舌所命令而产生的。就这样，卡被造了出来，赫姆苏特也被指定了——它们供给一切食物和一切养料——都是通过这句话。正义被给予做被爱之事的人，惩罚被给予做被恨之事的人。就这样，生命被给予和平的人，死亡被给予犯罪的人。就这样，一切劳动、一切技艺都被造了出来，手臂的动作，腿的移动，所有肢体的运动，都依照这个由心构想、由舌说出、使万物得以成就的命令。在这一段里，普塔的创造不只是造出了众神和世界，还造出了道德与社会的秩序：正义与惩罚、生与死、劳动与技艺，都来自神的话语。普塔原本就是工匠和技艺之神，孟菲斯的工匠们崇拜他；在这里，一切手艺被说成是普塔的心与舌的延伸。','The text then explains how this creation works: the sight of the eyes, the hearing of the ears, the breathing of the nose report to the heart. It is the heart that makes every understanding come forth, and the tongue that announces what the heart thinks. Thus all the gods were born and his Ennead was completed. For every word of the god came about through what the heart devised and the tongue commanded. Thus the kas were made and the hemsut appointed — they that make all food and all nourishment — through this word. Justice is given to him who does what is loved, and punishment to him who does what is hated. Thus life is given to the peaceful and death to the criminal. Thus all labour and all crafts are made, the action of the arms, the motion of the legs, the movement of all the limbs, according to this command devised by the heart and spoken by the tongue, which makes all things happen. In this passage Ptah''s creation brings forth not only gods and world but moral and social order: justice and punishment, life and death, labour and craft all come from the god''s word. Ptah was god of craftsmen and the arts, worshipped by the craftsmen of Memphis; here every craft is described as an extension of his heart and tongue.','claim.v0360.memphite.09','沙巴卡石（大英博物馆 EA 498）心想舌说而生万物。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_shabaka_stone_memphite.10','storyver.egyptian.shabaka_stone_memphite',10,'普塔歇息','Ptah rests','碑文说：所以人们说普塔是“创造一切、生出众神的那一位”。他就是塔特恩，生出众神的神，一切都从他那里出来——食物、供给、神的祭品和一切美好的事物。于是人们认识到、明白了，他是众神中最强大的一位。于是，在他造完万物和一切神圣的话语之后，普塔满足了，安息了。“安息”在埃及语里用的词是“霍特普”，同时有“满足”“平静”和“安息”的意思，普塔的一个常见称号“奈弗尔-霍尔”与之相关，在孟菲斯，人们常说普塔是“满足的神”。学者们常常把这一句与其他创世神话中“造物之后休息”的说法相比较，但两者的语境不同，本篇不作牵连。在这一段里，孟菲斯神学达到了它的高潮：它明确宣称普塔是“众神中最强大的一位”，一切美好的事物——包括神庙中献给众神的祭品——都来源于他。这正是这篇文字的写作目的之一：为孟菲斯的神庙和祭司们确立崇高的地位，说明他们侍奉的神，是一切神的创造者。','The text says: so it is said of Ptah, ''He who made all and created the gods.'' He is Ta-tenen, who gave birth to the gods, and from whom every thing came forth — foods, provisions, divine offerings, all good things. Thus it is recognised and understood that he is the mightiest of the gods. And so Ptah was satisfied, or rested, after he had made all things and all divine words. The Egyptian word is hotep, meaning at once ''satisfied'', ''at peace'' and ''at rest''; at Memphis Ptah was often called a god at peace. Scholars often compare this line with other creation myths in which the creator rests after making the world, but the contexts differ, and this retelling draws no connection. Here the Memphite Theology reaches its climax: it states plainly that Ptah is the mightiest of the gods, and that all good things — even the offerings given to the gods in temples — come from him. That was one purpose of the text: to raise the temple and priests of Memphis to the highest rank, showing that the god they served was the maker of all gods.','claim.v0360.memphite.10','沙巴卡石（大英博物馆 EA 498）普塔造物后安息。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_shabaka_stone_memphite.11','storyver.egyptian.shabaka_stone_memphite',11,'众神进入他们的身体','The gods enter their bodies','碑文接着说：他生出了众神，他建造了城市，他设立了各州，他把众神安置在他们的神龛里，他确定了他们的祭品，他建立了他们的神殿，他按照他们的心愿做出了他们的身体。于是众神进入了他们的身体，这些身体用各种木头、各种石头、各种泥土，以及在他身上生长的一切东西做成，众神就在其中显现。于是，所有的神和他们的卡都聚集到他的身边，心满意足，与两地之主结合在一起。这一段非常特别：它把神像的制作也归于普塔的创造。在古埃及，神像是用木头、石头、金属等材料做成的，人们相信神灵会进入这些神像，住在神庙之中，接受供奉。普塔作为工匠之神，正是一切神像制作者的保护神。在这里，众神的“身体”——也就是神庙里的神像——被说成是普塔按照众神的心愿亲手造出来的，而那些木头和石头，又都“生长在他身上”，因为他就是大地塔特恩。这样，从天上的众神到神庙中的神像，从城市和州的设立到祭品的安排，一切宗教生活的秩序都来自普塔。','The text goes on: he gave birth to the gods, he made the towns, he established the nomes, he placed the gods in their shrines, he settled their offerings, he established their shrines, he made their bodies according to their wishes. Thus the gods entered into their bodies, of every kind of wood, every kind of stone, every kind of clay, every thing that grows upon him, in which they came to be. Thus were gathered to him all the gods and their kas, content, united with the Lord of the Two Lands. The passage is remarkable: it credits the making of divine images to Ptah''s creation too. In Egypt cult statues were made of wood, stone and metal, and the gods were believed to enter them, dwell in the temples and receive offerings. Ptah, as god of craftsmen, was patron of all who made such images. Here the gods'' ''bodies'' — the statues in the temples — are said to be made by Ptah himself according to the gods'' wishes, and the wood and stone ''grow upon him'', for he is the earth, Ta-tenen. So from the gods in heaven to the images in the temples, from the founding of towns and nomes to the ordering of offerings, the whole order of religious life comes from Ptah.','claim.v0360.memphite.11','沙巴卡石（大英博物馆 EA 498）众神进入他们的身体。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.egyptian_shabaka_stone_memphite.12','storyver.egyptian.shabaka_stone_memphite',12,'孟菲斯的奥西里斯','Osiris at Memphis','碑文的最后一部分回到了奥西里斯的故事。它先说，在普塔神庙里那使众神心中欢喜的伟大宝座，就是塔特恩的粮仓，一切生命的女主人，两地的给养由此而来。接着说：奥西里斯淹没在他的水中。伊西斯和奈芙蒂斯向外张望，看见了他，就去照料他。荷鲁斯急忙命令伊西斯和奈芙蒂斯抓住奥西里斯，不让他沉没。她们及时听从了命令，把他带到了岸上。他走进了神秘的门户，带着永恒之主们的荣耀。就这样，奥西里斯在王家要塞进入了大地，那是在他来到的那片土地的北边。他的儿子荷鲁斯在他父亲奥西里斯的怀抱中，作为上下埃及之王出现，众神在他的前面，也在他的后面。在这个版本里，奥西里斯之死的地点被放在了孟菲斯附近：他在水中淹溺，被救上孟菲斯的岸边，在那里进入大地。孟菲斯的祭司们由此把奥西里斯的墓、荷鲁斯的王权与普塔的城市联系在一起。整篇文字从王权开始，经过创世，又以王权结束：荷鲁斯在父亲的怀中成为国王，这正是每一位法老继承王位的神话原型。','The last part of the text returns to Osiris. It says first that the Great Throne in the house of Ptah, which gives joy to the hearts of the gods, is the granary of Ta-tenen, mistress of all life, through which the sustenance of the Two Lands is provided. Then: Osiris drowned in his water. Isis and Nephthys looked out, beheld him and attended to him. Horus quickly commanded Isis and Nephthys to grasp Osiris and prevent his drowning. They heeded in time and brought him to land. He entered the mysterious portals in the glory of the lords of eternity. Thus Osiris came into the earth at the Royal Fortress, to the north of the land to which he had come. And his son Horus arose as King of Upper and Lower Egypt in the arms of his father Osiris, in the presence of the gods who were before him and behind him. In this version Osiris''s death is placed near Memphis: he drowns, is brought ashore at Memphis, and there enters the earth. Through it the priests of Memphis linked Osiris''s tomb and Horus''s kingship to Ptah''s city. The whole text begins with kingship, passes through creation and ends with kingship again: Horus becoming king in his father''s arms — the mythic model for every pharaoh''s succession.','claim.v0360.memphite.12','沙巴卡石（大英博物馆 EA 498）奥西里斯在孟菲斯被救上岸。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.ugaritic_ktu_1_3_1_4_palace.1','storyver.ugaritic.ktu_1_3_1_4_palace',1,'巴力的宴会','Baal''s feast','KTU 1.3 第一栏开头，风暴之神强大的巴力坐在宴席上。侍者拉德马努侍奉他，在他面前切肉，用刀割下肥美的牛胸，然后站起来，给他斟酒，把一只杯子放进他的手里，一只大杯子，大得要用双手捧着——那是一只连女人都没见过、连阿西拉特都没见过的杯子。一千坛酒倒进这只杯子里，一万坛酒混在一起调和。一位歌手站起来唱歌，声音美妙，手里敲着钹，唱的是英俊的少年巴力在萨方山的高处。这一段写的是巴力最得意的时刻：在前一块泥板里，他刚刚用工匠之神为他造的两根棍棒打败了海神雅姆，成为众神之中的胜利者。宴会上的大杯、千坛美酒和歌手的歌声，都是在庆祝他的胜利和他的王权。可是在这片欢乐之中，有一件事情让巴力不满：他虽然得到了王权，却还没有一座属于自己的宫殿。这个问题将贯穿整整两块泥板。乌加里特的诗歌常常用排比的诗句来描写宴会，一句说一千，下一句说一万，这是古代迦南诗歌的典型风格。','At the opening of KTU 1.3, column I, Mightiest Baal the storm god sits at a feast. His servant Radmanu waits on him, carving before him, cutting the breast of a fatling with a knife; then he rises, pours drink, and puts a cup into his hands — a great cup, held in both hands, a cup no woman has seen, not even Athirat. A thousand jars of wine go into it, ten thousand are mixed in the mixing. A singer rises and sings in a sweet voice, clashing cymbals, singing of the gracious youth Baal on the heights of Saphon. It is Baal''s proudest moment: on the preceding tablet he has defeated Yamm the Sea with the two clubs the craftsman god made for him and become victor among the gods. The great cup, the thousand jars and the singer celebrate his victory and kingship. Yet amid the joy one thing troubles Baal: though he has won kingship, he has no palace of his own. That question runs through two whole tablets. Ugaritic poetry often describes feasts in parallel lines — one line says a thousand, the next ten thousand — the typical style of ancient Canaanite verse.','claim.v0360.baal_palace.01','乌加里特巴力史诗泥板 KTU 1.3 第一栏。','泥板此处有残缺，栏次划分与断句据现代学者的整理，残缺处不补写。'),
('storysec.v0360.ugaritic_ktu_1_3_1_4_palace.2','storyver.ugaritic.ktu_1_3_1_4_palace',2,'阿娜特的战斗','Anat''s battle','第二栏讲巴力的妹妹、少女阿娜特。她关上宫殿的大门，在两座城之间的山谷里大战，杀死了海边的人，消灭了日出之处的人。在她脚下，人头像球一样滚动，人手像蝗虫一样飞散。她把人头绑在背上，把人手系在腰带上，在战士的血中蹚到膝盖，在士兵的血污中蹚到脖子。她用棍棒驱赶俘虏，用弓弦拉开敌阵。然后她回到自己的宫中，可是战斗还没有让她满足，她把椅子当作战士，把桌子当作士兵，把脚凳当作英雄，又一次厮杀起来。她的心中充满了欢乐，肝脏里满是喜悦。最后，她用天上的露水和地上的油脂洗净双手——那是驾云者巴力降下的雨水。这一段是古代近东文学中最著名、也最令人震撼的战斗描写之一，学者们对阿娜特战斗的对象和意义有许多不同的看法：有人认为这是一种仪式的象征，有人认为它表现的是战神的本性。本篇只按泥板的文字讲述，不加评判。值得注意的是，这位凶猛的女神最后用的是巴力的雨水来洗手，暗示了她与巴力之间亲密的联系。','Column II tells of Baal''s sister, the maiden Anat. She shuts the gates of her house and fights in the valley between two towns; she smites the people of the seashore and destroys the men of the sunrise. Under her, heads roll like balls, hands fly like locusts. She binds heads to her back and ties hands to her belt; she wades knee-deep in warriors'' blood, neck-deep in the gore of soldiers. With her club she drives captives, with her bowstring the foe. Then she returns to her house, but the fighting has not sated her: she makes chairs into warriors, tables into soldiers, footstools into heroes, and fights again. Her heart swells with joy, her liver with gladness. At last she washes her hands in the dew of heaven and the oil of earth — the rain the Rider of the Clouds pours down. It is one of the most famous and startling battle descriptions in ancient Near Eastern literature, and scholars differ widely on whom Anat fights and what it means — some see a ritual symbol, others the nature of a war goddess. This retelling follows the tablet without judging. Notably the fierce goddess washes her hands at last in Baal''s rain, a hint of her close bond with him.','claim.v0360.baal_palace.02','乌加里特巴力史诗泥板 KTU 1.3 第二栏。','泥板此处有残缺，栏次划分与断句据现代学者的整理，残缺处不补写。'),
('storysec.v0360.ugaritic_ktu_1_3_1_4_palace.3','storyver.ugaritic.ktu_1_3_1_4_palace',3,'巴力的口信','Baal''s message','第三栏里，巴力派他的两个使者古帕努和乌加尔去找阿娜特。他吩咐他们说：你们去对少女阿娜特、对民族的嫂子说：强大的巴力的话，最强者的话是这样的——把战争从大地上除去，在土地上放下爱情，在大地的中心浇上和平，在田野里洒满爱意。快来吧，赶到我这里来，因为我有一句话要告诉你，一件事要对你说：那是树木的话语，石头的低语，天空对大地、深渊对星辰的叹息。我懂得闪电，那是天空不知道的；那是人们不知道的话语，大地上的众人不懂得的。来吧，我会把它揭示出来，在我的山上，在萨方山的圣地，在我继承的山上，在美好的地方，在力量之山上。这段话是乌加里特诗歌中最优美的段落之一。巴力要阿娜特放下武器，把战场变成充满爱与和平的土地；他所说的“树木的话语、石头的低语”，被许多学者理解为雷声和闪电——风暴之神特有的语言。这也预示了后面的结局：巴力最终要在自己的宫殿里，通过窗户发出他的雷声。','In column III Baal sends his two messengers, Gupn and Ugar, to Anat. He tells them: say to the maiden Anat, to the sister-in-law of the peoples: the word of Mightiest Baal, the speech of the Mightiest of Warriors — remove war from the earth, set love in the land, pour peace into the heart of the earth, rain love on the fields. Hurry, hasten to me, for I have a word to tell you, a message to recount to you: the word of the tree and the whisper of the stone, the sighing of the heavens to the earth, of the deeps to the stars. I understand the lightning which the heavens do not know, a word that men do not know, nor the multitudes of the earth understand. Come, and I will reveal it, in the midst of my mountain, divine Saphon, in the holy place, on the mountain of my inheritance, in the pleasant place, on the hill of might. This is among the loveliest passages of Ugaritic poetry. Baal asks Anat to lay down her weapons and turn the battlefield into a land of love and peace; his ''word of the tree and whisper of the stone'' is taken by many scholars to mean thunder and lightning — the storm god''s own language. It also foreshadows the end: Baal will at last send his thunder through the window of his own palace.','claim.v0360.baal_palace.03','乌加里特巴力史诗泥板 KTU 1.3 第三栏。','泥板此处有残缺，栏次划分与断句据现代学者的整理，残缺处不补写。'),
('storysec.v0360.ugaritic_ktu_1_3_1_4_palace.4','storyver.ugaritic.ktu_1_3_1_4_palace',4,'阿娜特的惊恐','Anat''s alarm','阿娜特看见两位使者走来，双脚发抖，腰部打颤，脸上流汗。她问：古帕努和乌加尔为什么来了？是什么敌人起来反对巴力了？是什么对手要攻击驾云者了？难道我没有打败伊勒宠爱的雅姆吗？难道我没有消灭河神纳哈尔吗？难道我没有捆住巨龙图南、给它戴上嘴套吗？我打败了那扭曲的蛇，那七个头的沙利亚特；我打败了伊勒宠爱的阿尔什，消灭了伊勒的小牛阿提克，打败了伊勒的母狗伊沙特，消灭了伊勒的女儿兹巴布。我为了金子而战斗，赶走了那个要把巴力从萨方山的高处赶下去的人。是什么敌人起来反对巴力了？两位使者回答说：没有敌人起来反对巴力，没有对手攻击驾云者。他们转达了巴力的话。阿娜特于是放下一切，奔向萨方山。巴力远远地看见妹妹走来，就打发女人们离开，在她面前摆上一头牛，为她举行宴会。阿娜特列出的这一长串被她打败的敌人，是乌加里特神话中许多已经失传的故事的线索；其中的雅姆与图南，也出现在巴力的故事里，不同文本中由谁打败它们，说法并不一致。','When Anat saw the two messengers coming, her feet shook, her loins trembled, her face sweated. She asked: why have Gupn and Ugar come? What enemy has risen against Baal, what foe against the Rider of the Clouds? Did I not destroy Yamm, beloved of El? Did I not finish off River, the great god? Did I not muzzle the dragon Tunnan? I destroyed the twisting serpent, Shalyat of the seven heads; I destroyed Arsh, beloved of El, finished off Atik, the calf of El, destroyed Ishat, the bitch of El, finished off Dhabub, daughter of El. I fought for gold and drove out him who would drive Baal from the heights of Saphon. What enemy has risen against Baal? The messengers answered: no enemy has risen against Baal, no foe against the Rider of the Clouds — and they gave Baal''s message. Anat set everything aside and sped to Saphon. Baal saw his sister coming from afar, sent the women away and set an ox before her for a feast. Anat''s long list of defeated foes is a thread to many lost Ugaritic myths; Yamm and Tunnan also appear in Baal''s own story, and the texts do not agree on who defeated them.','claim.v0360.baal_palace.04','乌加里特巴力史诗泥板 KTU 1.3 第三至四栏。','泥板此处有残缺，栏次划分与断句据现代学者的整理，残缺处不补写。'),
('storysec.v0360.ugaritic_ktu_1_3_1_4_palace.5','storyver.ugaritic.ktu_1_3_1_4_palace',5,'巴力没有房子','Baal has no house','在萨方山上，巴力向阿娜特说出了他的苦恼：巴力没有像众神那样的房子，没有像阿西拉特的儿子们那样的庭院。伊勒的住所是他儿子的庇护所，女王阿西拉特的住所是她女儿们的庇护所，皮德拉伊、塔莱和阿尔萨伊——巴力的三个女儿——也都寄住在别人那里。阿娜特听了，说：我要去见公牛伊勒，我的父亲。如果他不给巴力一座房子，我就要像拖羊羔一样把他拖到地上，让他的白发流满鲜血，让他的白胡子沾满血污。她来到伊勒的住处，在两条河的源头，在两个深渊的泉水中间。她闯进伊勒的帐幕，伊勒在七个房间深处、八道门后面回答她：我知道你，我的女儿，女神们中间没有像你这样不受约束的。你想要什么，少女阿娜特？阿娜特说：伊勒啊，你的命令是英明的，你的智慧是永恒的。我们的王是强大的巴力，我们的审判者，没有人在他之上。可是巴力没有像众神那样的房子。泥板在这里有残缺，伊勒的回答没有保存下来；从后文看，阿娜特的威胁没有成功，巴力还需要另一位女神来为他说情。','On Saphon Baal tells Anat his grief: Baal has no house like the gods, no court like the sons of Athirat. The dwelling of El is the shelter of his son, the dwelling of Lady Athirat the shelter of her daughters; Pidray, Tallay and Arsay — Baal''s three daughters — live under others'' roofs. Hearing this, Anat says: I will go to Bull El, my father. If he does not give Baal a house, I will drag him like a lamb to the ground, make his grey hair run with blood, his grey beard with gore. She comes to El at the source of the two rivers, amid the springs of the two deeps, and bursts into his tent. From seven chambers within, through eight doors, El answers her: I know you, my daughter; there is none so unrestrained among the goddesses. What do you desire, maiden Anat? Anat says: your decree, El, is wise, your wisdom everlasting. Our king is Mightiest Baal, our judge, and there is none above him. Yet Baal has no house like the gods. The tablet is broken here, and El''s answer is lost; from what follows, Anat''s threat did not succeed, and Baal will need another goddess to plead for him.','claim.v0360.baal_palace.05','乌加里特巴力史诗泥板 KTU 1.3 第四至五栏。','泥板此处有残缺，栏次划分与断句据现代学者的整理，残缺处不补写。'),
('storysec.v0360.ugaritic_ktu_1_3_1_4_palace.6','storyver.ugaritic.ktu_1_3_1_4_palace',6,'寻找工匠','Sending for the craftsman','第六栏里，一位使者被派去寻找工匠之神科塔尔-瓦-哈西斯，意思是“灵巧而聪慧的神”。使者要穿过一千里，一万里，越过海洋和许多地方，去到卡夫托尔——科塔尔的宝座所在之地，去到希克普特——他继承的土地。科塔尔在乌加里特神话中是一位伟大的工匠，前一块泥板里，正是他为巴力打造了打败雅姆的两根棍棒。他住在遥远的海外，学者们一般认为“卡夫托尔”指克里特岛，“希克普特”指埃及的孟菲斯，那里正是工匠之神普塔的城市。这样的地理安排说明，在乌加里特人眼中，最好的工艺来自克里特和埃及。泥板的这一部分残缺较多，派遣使者的具体经过不完全清楚；从下一块泥板的开头来看，巴力和阿娜特最终决定先用工匠打造的礼物去打动众神之母阿西拉特，请她到伊勒那里去说情。这是一个聪明的计划：既然威胁不起作用，就用礼物和说情来赢得父亲的同意。','In column VI a messenger is sent to find the craftsman god Kothar-wa-Khasis, ''Skilful-and-Wise''. The messenger must cross a thousand fields, ten thousand acres, over the sea and many lands, to Kaphtor, the throne where Kothar sits, to Hikpat, the land of his inheritance. Kothar is the great craftsman of Ugaritic myth; on the earlier tablet he made the two clubs with which Baal defeated Yamm. He lives far across the sea; scholars generally take Kaphtor to be Crete and Hikpat to be Memphis in Egypt — the very city of the craftsman god Ptah. The geography shows that, to the people of Ugarit, the finest craft came from Crete and Egypt. This part of the tablet is badly broken, and the details of the mission are not wholly clear; from the opening of the next tablet, Baal and Anat decide first to win over Athirat, mother of the gods, with gifts made by the craftsman, and to ask her to plead with El. It is a shrewd plan: since threats have failed, gifts and intercession may win the father''s consent.','claim.v0360.baal_palace.06','乌加里特巴力史诗泥板 KTU 1.3 第六栏。','泥板此处有残缺，栏次划分与断句据现代学者的整理，残缺处不补写。'),
('storysec.v0360.ugaritic_ktu_1_3_1_4_palace.7','storyver.ugaritic.ktu_1_3_1_4_palace',7,'给阿西拉特的礼物','Gifts for Athirat','KTU 1.4 第一栏又一次重复了巴力的哀叹：巴力没有像众神那样的房子，没有像阿西拉特的儿子们那样的庭院。接着，工匠之神科塔尔-瓦-哈西斯走到风箱前，拿起钳子，熔化了银子，倒出了金子。他为众神之母阿西拉特打造礼物：他熔了几千舍客勒的银子，几万舍客勒的金子，打造出一个神圣的宝座，一个用金子做的脚凳，一张用金子装饰的床，一张满是各种动物装饰的桌子，还有来自阿穆鲁地区的碗盏，碗上装饰着像亚姆鲁地区的野兽一样的图案，数以万计。这些礼物被描写得极为华丽，诗句一再强调金银的数量之多，工艺之精。阿西拉特是伊勒的妻子，是众神之母，她的儿子们——七十位神——都住在自己的房子里；而巴力要请她帮忙，就必须先送上配得上她身份的厚礼。在乌加里特社会中，向有权势的人送礼求情是常见的做法，神话中的众神也遵循着同样的规矩。这一段也让我们看到了科塔尔的手艺：他能把金银变成任何东西，后来巴力的宫殿也将由他建造。','Column I of KTU 1.4 repeats Baal''s lament: Baal has no house like the gods, no court like the sons of Athirat. Then the craftsman Kothar-wa-Khasis goes to the bellows, takes the tongs, melts silver and pours out gold. He makes gifts for Athirat, mother of the gods: he casts silver by thousands of shekels, gold by tens of thousands; he makes a divine dais, a footstool of gold, a couch adorned with gold, a table full of every kind of creature, and bowls from the land of Amurru, shaped like the beasts of Yamhad, by the tens of thousands. The gifts are described with great splendour, the verse insisting again and again on the amount of gold and silver and the fineness of the work. Athirat is El''s wife and mother of the gods; her sons, the seventy gods, all have their houses; if Baal is to ask her help, he must first bring gifts worthy of her rank. In Ugaritic society bringing gifts to the powerful to win their favour was common practice, and the gods of the myth keep the same custom. The passage also shows Kothar''s skill: he can turn gold and silver into anything, and he will build Baal''s palace too.','claim.v0360.baal_palace.07','乌加里特巴力史诗泥板 KTU 1.4 第一栏。','泥板此处有残缺，栏次划分与断句据现代学者的整理，残缺处不补写。'),
('storysec.v0360.ugaritic_ktu_1_3_1_4_palace.8','storyver.ugaritic.ktu_1_3_1_4_palace',8,'海边的女神','The goddess by the sea','第二栏里，众神之母阿西拉特正在海边。她手里拿着纺锤，纺锤在她手中转动，她把衣服放进海里，把长袍浸在河中。她把锅放在火上，把碗放在炭上，像是在做家务。就在这时，她抬起眼睛，看见强大的巴力和少女阿娜特走来。她的双脚发抖，腰部打颤，脸上流汗。她大声说：强大的巴力为什么来了？少女阿娜特为什么来了？他们是来打我的儿子的吗？还是来消灭我的族人的？可是当她看见那些银子和金子的光芒，看见那些闪闪发亮的礼物，她就高兴了起来，吩咐她的仆人说：去看看那些工匠的手艺吧。她还让她的渔夫仆人去海里撒网捕鱼。阿西拉特在乌加里特神话中被称为“海上的阿西拉特”，与海有着密切的联系。她的恐惧不是没有理由的：巴力刚刚打败了海神雅姆，而雅姆被称为“伊勒的宠儿”，可能也是阿西拉特的孩子之一。在后面的故事里，巴力与阿西拉特的儿子们之间仍然存在着紧张关系。这一幕把一位众神之母的形象写得非常生动：她既威严，又会担心自己的孩子。','In column II Athirat, mother of the gods, is by the sea. She holds a spindle that turns in her hand; she puts her garments into the sea, her robes into the river; she sets a pot on the fire, a bowl on the coals, as if at household work. Then she lifts her eyes and sees Mightiest Baal and the maiden Anat coming. Her feet shake, her loins tremble, her face sweats. She cries: why has Mightiest Baal come? Why has the maiden Anat come? Have they come to smite my sons, or to make an end of my kin? But when she sees the gleam of the silver and gold, the shining gifts, she rejoices and tells her servant: look at the craftsmen''s work. She bids her fisherman servant cast a net into the sea. Athirat is called ''Athirat of the Sea'' in Ugaritic myth and is closely linked with it. Her fear has reason: Baal has just defeated Yamm, called ''beloved of El'', who may have been one of her own children, and later in the cycle there is still tension between Baal and the sons of Athirat. The scene draws the mother of the gods vividly: majestic, and yet anxious for her children.','claim.v0360.baal_palace.08','乌加里特巴力史诗泥板 KTU 1.4 第二栏。','泥板此处有残缺，栏次划分与断句据现代学者的整理，残缺处不补写。'),
('storysec.v0360.ugaritic_ktu_1_3_1_4_palace.9','storyver.ugaritic.ktu_1_3_1_4_palace',9,'阿西拉特的疑问','Athirat''s question','第三栏开头残缺，接着是巴力向众神抱怨自己在众神的集会上受到了侮辱：他站起来，说有人在众神的集会上对他吐唾沫。巴力憎恶两种宴会，驾云者憎恶三种：可耻的宴会，卑贱的宴会，女仆们放荡的宴会。在这些宴会上，可耻的事情被看见了，卑贱的事情发生了。巴力和阿娜特来到阿西拉特面前，献上礼物，向她恳求。阿西拉特问他们：你们为什么来恳求海上的阿西拉特？为什么来请求众神的创造者？你们恳求过公牛伊勒、仁慈的神吗？你们请求过万物的创造者吗？少女阿娜特回答说：我们先恳求海上的阿西拉特，请求众神的创造者，然后我们再去恳求他。于是他们一起吃喝，众神一起宴饮，用刀切下肥美的牛肉，用金杯喝着葡萄酒。阿西拉特的疑问很有意思：她提醒巴力和阿娜特，真正有权决定的是伊勒，而不是她。可是阿娜特的回答表明，她们的计划正是要让阿西拉特去说服伊勒。在乌加里特神话里，伊勒是众神的父亲和最高的决策者，阿西拉特则是唯一能直接影响他的人。','Column III opens broken; then Baal complains that he was insulted in the assembly of the gods: he stood and said someone spat upon him in the gathering of the gods. Two kinds of feast Baal hates, three the Rider of the Clouds: a feast of shame, a feast of baseness, and a feast where handmaids act lewdly — for there shame is seen and baseness done. Baal and Anat come before Athirat, bring their gifts and entreat her. Athirat asks them: why do you entreat Athirat of the Sea, beseech the creatress of the gods? Have you entreated Bull El the Kindly, or beseeched the creator of creatures? The maiden Anat answers: we entreat Athirat of the Sea, beseech the creatress of the gods first; afterwards we shall entreat him. So they eat and drink; the gods feast together, carving fat cattle with knives and drinking wine from golden cups. Athirat''s question is telling: she reminds Baal and Anat that the real decision lies with El, not with her. But Anat''s reply shows that their plan is exactly to have Athirat persuade El. In Ugaritic myth El is father of the gods and final authority, and Athirat is the one who can sway him directly.','claim.v0360.baal_palace.09','乌加里特巴力史诗泥板 KTU 1.4 第三栏。','泥板此处有残缺，栏次划分与断句据现代学者的整理，残缺处不补写。'),
('storysec.v0360.ugaritic_ktu_1_3_1_4_palace.10','storyver.ugaritic.ktu_1_3_1_4_palace',10,'骑驴见伊勒','Riding to El','第四栏里，阿西拉特吩咐她的仆人、渔夫库德什-瓦-阿姆鲁尔：给驴套上鞍子，给驴装上银子的缰绳，金子的鞍具，为我的母驴备好鞍子。库德什-瓦-阿姆鲁尔照着做了。他把阿西拉特扶上驴背，自己像火炬一样走在前面，少女阿娜特跟在后面，巴力则回到了萨方山的高处。阿西拉特来到伊勒那里，在两条河的源头，在两个深渊的泉水之间。她揭开伊勒的帐幕，走进君王、万物之父的居所。她跪在伊勒脚下，俯伏下拜，向他致敬。伊勒看见她，脸上露出了笑容，他把脚放在脚凳上，打了个响指，提高声音说：海上的女王阿西拉特为什么来了？众神的创造者为什么到这里来了？你饿了吗？你走了这么远的路。你渴了吗？你走了一整夜。吃吧，喝吧！桌上有食物，杯中有酒，金杯里有葡萄的血。难道是君王伊勒的爱让你动了心？公牛的情意让你思念了吗？这一段把伊勒写得非常亲切：这位年迈的众神之父看见妻子远道而来，又惊又喜，殷勤地请她吃喝，还开玩笑地问她是不是想念他了。','In column IV Athirat bids her servant, the fisherman Qodesh-wa-Amrur: saddle the donkey, harness the ass, put on the trappings of silver, the gear of gold, make ready my she-ass. Qodesh-wa-Amrur obeys. He sets Athirat on the donkey''s back and goes before her like a torch, the maiden Anat following behind, while Baal returns to the heights of Saphon. Athirat comes to El at the source of the rivers, amid the springs of the two deeps. She opens El''s tent and enters the dwelling of the King, Father of Years. At El''s feet she bows down and falls prostrate, honouring him. El sees her and smiles; he sets his feet on the footstool, snaps his fingers and lifts his voice: why has Lady Athirat of the Sea come? Why has the creatress of the gods come here? Are you hungry from your journey? Are you thirsty from travelling all night? Eat, drink! There is food on the table, wine in the cups, the blood of the vine in golden goblets. Or has the love of King El stirred you, the affection of the Bull aroused you? The passage paints El with warmth: the aged father of the gods, surprised and pleased to see his wife come so far, presses food and drink on her and teases her whether she has missed him.','claim.v0360.baal_palace.10','乌加里特巴力史诗泥板 KTU 1.4 第四栏。','泥板此处有残缺，栏次划分与断句据现代学者的整理，残缺处不补写。'),
('storysec.v0360.ugaritic_ktu_1_3_1_4_palace.11','storyver.ugaritic.ktu_1_3_1_4_palace',11,'伊勒的允许','El''s consent','阿西拉特回答伊勒说：伊勒啊，你的命令是英明的，你的智慧与永恒同在，你的命令带来幸运的生活。我们的王是强大的巴力，我们的审判者，没有人在他之上。我们都要为他捧杯，都要为他端碗。可是他在哀叹：巴力没有像众神那样的房子，没有像阿西拉特的儿子们那样的庭院。仁慈的伊勒，慈悲的神回答说：难道我是一个奴隶，阿西拉特是一个女仆，要我拿着泥铲做砖吗？让人为巴力建一座像众神那样的房子，像阿西拉特的儿子们那样的庭院吧。阿西拉特高兴地说：伊勒啊，你是伟大的，你的白发给了你智慧。现在巴力将按时降下他的雨水，按季节降下浓雾和雪；他将在云中发出他的声音，向大地闪耀他的闪电。让人为他建一座雪松的房子，一座砖砌的房子吧。在这一段里，伊勒终于答应了巴力的请求，而说服他的，正是阿西拉特对巴力王权的承认。伊勒那句“难道我是奴隶”的反问也很有趣：他的意思是，建房子是工匠们的事，他只需要下令就够了。巴力的宫殿与雨水的关系在这里第一次被说明：有了宫殿，巴力才能按时降雨，大地才会丰收。','Athirat answers El: your decree, El, is wise, your wisdom is with eternity, your command brings a fortunate life. Our king is Mightiest Baal, our judge, and there is none above him. All of us will bear his cup, all of us bear his bowl. Yet he laments: Baal has no house like the gods, no court like the sons of Athirat. Kindly El, the Compassionate, answers: am I a slave, is Athirat a handmaid, that I should hold the trowel and make bricks? Let a house be built for Baal like the gods, a court like the sons of Athirat. Athirat rejoices: you are great, El, your grey hair has made you wise. Now Baal will appoint the time of his rain, the season of mist and snow; he will give his voice in the clouds and flash his lightning to the earth. Let a house of cedar be built for him, a house of bricks. Here El at last grants Baal''s request, and what persuades him is Athirat''s acknowledgement of Baal''s kingship. El''s retort ''am I a slave?'' is amusing: building is the craftsmen''s business; he need only give the order. The link between Baal''s palace and the rain is stated here for the first time: with a palace, Baal can send the rain in its season and the earth will bear.','claim.v0360.baal_palace.11','乌加里特巴力史诗泥板 KTU 1.4 第四至五栏。','泥板此处有残缺，栏次划分与断句据现代学者的整理，残缺处不补写。'),
('storysec.v0360.ugaritic_ktu_1_3_1_4_palace.12','storyver.ugaritic.ktu_1_3_1_4_palace',12,'窗户之争','The dispute over the window','阿娜特高兴得跳了起来，奔向萨方山，对巴力说：好消息，巴力！你将有一座像你的兄弟们那样的房子，一座像你的亲族们那样的庭院。去召集山上的商队吧，让他们带来金子和银子，带来最好的宝石。巴力欢喜极了，派人把工匠科塔尔-瓦-哈西斯请来，摆上宴席款待他。宴后，巴力吩咐他尽快建造房子。科塔尔说：听我说，强大的巴力，让我在这座房子里开一扇窗户，在宫殿当中开一个窗口。巴力说：不要在房子里开窗户，不要在宫殿当中开窗口。泥板在这里有残缺，巴力拒绝的理由没有完全保存下来，学者们根据残存的字句推测，他担心自己的女儿皮德拉伊和塔莱，也可能是担心海神雅姆借此进入宫殿。科塔尔说：你会回心转意的，巴力，你会再想起我的话。这场关于窗户的争论，成了整个故事最后的悬念：窗户是巴力发出雷声和降下雨水的通道，没有窗户，巴力的力量就无法传到大地上。工匠早已看出这一点，巴力却还要经过一番征战，才会明白过来。','Anat leaps for joy and speeds to Saphon, telling Baal: good news, Baal! You shall have a house like your brothers, a court like your kin. Summon the caravans of the mountains; let them bring gold and silver and the choicest gems. Baal rejoices and sends for the craftsman Kothar-wa-Khasis, setting a feast before him; after the feast he bids him build the house quickly. Kothar says: listen, Mightiest Baal — let me put a window in the house, a casement in the midst of the palace. Baal says: do not put a window in the house, nor a casement in the midst of the palace. The tablet is broken here, and Baal''s reason is not fully preserved; from the surviving words scholars infer that he feared for his daughters Pidray and Tallay, or perhaps that Yamm the Sea might enter by it. Kothar says: you will come back to my word, Baal; you will think again of what I said. The dispute over the window becomes the last suspense of the tale: the window is the channel through which Baal sends thunder and rain; without it his power cannot reach the earth. The craftsman sees this at once; Baal will understand only after a campaign of conquest.','claim.v0360.baal_palace.12','乌加里特巴力史诗泥板 KTU 1.4 第五至六栏。','泥板此处有残缺，栏次划分与断句据现代学者的整理，残缺处不补写。'),
('storysec.v0360.ugaritic_ktu_1_3_1_4_palace.13','storyver.ugaritic.ktu_1_3_1_4_palace',13,'火中成屋','The house made in fire','第六栏接着讲房子的建造。人们去黎巴嫩和它的树林，去西里翁山和它最好的雪松，砍下木材。然后，火被放进房子里，火焰被放进宫殿中。第一天，第二天，火在房子里燃烧，火焰在宫殿中吞噬；第三天，第四天，火在房子里燃烧，火焰在宫殿中吞噬；第五天，第六天，火在房子里燃烧，火焰在宫殿中吞噬。到了第七天，火离开了房子，火焰离开了宫殿：银子变成了板，金子变成了砖。强大的巴力欢喜地说：我用银子建造了我的房子，用金子建造了我的宫殿。巴力为宫殿举行了盛大的宴会：他宰杀了牛和羊，宰杀了公牛和肥牛犊、一岁的羊羔和小山羊。他请来了他的兄弟们和亲族，请来了阿西拉特的七十个儿子，给他们斟上葡萄酒：给公羊神斟满杯，给母羊女神斟满碗，给公牛神斟满杯，给母牛女神斟满碗。这一段用“第一天、第二天……第七天”的排比，营造出一种庄严的节奏，学者们常把它与古代近东其他“七日”主题的文献相比较。火中炼成金银之屋的奇迹，说明这座宫殿不是凡人的建筑，而是神圣的作品。','Column VI goes on with the building. They go to Lebanon and its trees, to Sirion and its choicest cedars, and cut timber. Then fire is set in the house, flame in the palace. On the first day and the second, fire burns in the house, flame consumes in the palace; on the third day and the fourth, fire burns in the house, flame consumes in the palace; on the fifth day and the sixth, fire burns in the house, flame consumes in the palace. On the seventh day the fire leaves the house, the flame leaves the palace: the silver has turned into plates, the gold into bricks. Mightiest Baal rejoices: my house I have built of silver, my palace of gold. Baal holds a great feast for the palace: he slaughters oxen and sheep, bulls and fatted calves, yearling lambs and kids. He invites his brothers and his kin, the seventy sons of Athirat, and pours them wine: to the ram-gods cups of wine, to the ewe-goddesses bowls; to the bull-gods cups, to the cow-goddesses bowls. The repeated ''first day, second day … seventh day'' gives the passage a solemn rhythm, and scholars often compare it with other ancient Near Eastern texts on the seven-day pattern. A house of silver and gold refined in fire shows that the palace is no mortal building but a divine work.','claim.v0360.baal_palace.13','乌加里特巴力史诗泥板 KTU 1.4 第六栏。','泥板此处有残缺，栏次划分与断句据现代学者的整理，残缺处不补写。'),
('storysec.v0360.ugaritic_ktu_1_3_1_4_palace.14','storyver.ugaritic.ktu_1_3_1_4_palace',14,'开窗与雷声','The window and the thunder','第七栏里，巴力离开宫殿，从一座城走到另一座城，从一个镇走到另一个镇，夺取了六十六座城、七十七个镇。然后他回到了自己的房子，对工匠科塔尔-瓦-哈西斯说：我要开窗了，科塔尔，就在今天；让人在房子里开一扇窗，在宫殿当中开一个窗口。科塔尔笑了，提高声音说：我不是早就告诉过你了吗，强大的巴力，你会回心转意的！他在房子里开了一扇窗，在宫殿中开了一个窗口。巴力从云中打开一道裂缝，发出他神圣的声音，巴力的嘴唇一张一合，他的声音让大地震动，群山颤抖，东方和西方的高处都在摇晃。巴力的敌人们逃进了森林，他的对手们躲进了山坡。巴力说：无论是王还是非王，谁能在这片土地上建立统治？我要派一位使者去见伊勒之子穆特，去见伊勒的宠儿、英雄。第八栏里，他吩咐使者古帕努和乌加尔前往穆特的城市，那里的宝座叫“深坑”，那里的土地叫“污秽”，警告他们不要走近穆特，免得被他像羊羔一样放进嘴里。窗户终于打开，巴力的雷声传遍大地；而他向死神发出的宣告，则引出了下一块泥板里巴力与穆特之间更加凶险的斗争。','In column VII Baal leaves the palace and goes from city to city, town to town, seizing sixty-six cities, seventy-seven towns. Then he returns to his house and says to Kothar-wa-Khasis: I will put it in, Kothar, this very day; let a window be opened in the house, a casement in the midst of the palace. Kothar laughs and lifts his voice: did I not tell you, Mightiest Baal, that you would come back to my word? He opens a window in the house, a casement in the palace. Baal opens a rift in the clouds and gives forth his holy voice; Baal''s lips open and shut, and at his voice the earth quakes, the mountains tremble, the high places east and west shake. Baal''s enemies flee to the forests, his foes to the mountainsides. Baal says: king or no king, who shall establish dominion in the land? I will send a messenger to Mot, son of El, to the Beloved, the Hero. In column VIII he sends Gupn and Ugar to Mot''s city, whose throne is called ''Pit'' and whose land is ''Filth'', warning them not to come near Mot lest he put them in his mouth like a lamb. The window opens at last and Baal''s thunder spreads over the earth; and his message to Death leads to the far more perilous struggle between Baal and Mot on the next tablets.','claim.v0360.baal_palace.14','乌加里特巴力史诗泥板 KTU 1.4 第七至八栏。','泥板此处有残缺，栏次划分与断句据现代学者的整理，残缺处不补写。'),
('storysec.v0360.sumerian_inana_ebih_etcsl.1','storyver.sumerian.inana_ebih_etcsl',1,'可怖的女神','The goddess of terror','诗歌以对伊南娜的赞美开始：她是掌握可怕神力的女神，身披恐怖，骑在伟大的神力之上。伊南娜手持神圣的阿努纳武器，浑身沾满鲜血，在伟大的战斗中奔驰，把盾牌放在地上，被风暴和洪水所笼罩。伟大的女主人伊南娜，深知如何谋划冲突，她用箭和力量摧毁强大的国度，征服各个地方。在苏美尔人的宗教中，伊南娜是爱与战争的女神，是金星的化身，也是乌鲁克城的守护神。这首颂歌只突出她作为战争女神的一面：她不是温柔的爱人，而是令人畏惧的战士，她的出现就意味着毁灭。“神力”在苏美尔语中叫做“美”，指的是构成文明和宇宙秩序的种种力量与规则，伊南娜被描写成“骑在伟大的神力之上”，说明她掌握着强大的力量。这首诗后来被归于恩赫杜安娜——阿卡德国王萨尔贡的女儿、乌尔的月神女祭司——她是世界上已知最早有名字的作者之一，不过这一归属在学界仍有争议。','The poem opens in praise of Inana: goddess of the fearsome divine powers, clad in terror, riding on the great divine powers. Inana, holding the holy weapon of the Anuna, drenched in blood, rushes through great battles, her shield resting on the ground, covered in storm and flood. Great lady Inana, knowing well how to plan conflicts, destroys mighty lands with arrow and strength and overpowers lands. In Sumerian religion Inana was goddess of love and war, the planet Venus and patron of Uruk. This hymn stresses only her warrior side: not a tender lover but a fearsome fighter whose coming means destruction. The ''divine powers'' — me in Sumerian — are the forces and rules that make up civilisation and the order of the cosmos; Inana ''riding on the great divine powers'' shows her command of mighty forces. The poem was later attributed to Enḫeduana, daughter of King Sargon of Akkad and priestess of the moon god at Ur, one of the earliest named authors known in the world, though the attribution is still debated.','claim.v0360.inana_ebih.01','苏美尔文《伊南娜与埃比赫》（ETCSL 1.3.2）开篇颂歌。','本篇按段落定位，不标具体行号；个别诗行残缺或意义不明。'),
('storysec.v0360.sumerian_inana_ebih_etcsl.2','storyver.sumerian.inana_ebih_etcsl',2,'天地间的狮吼','A lion''s roar in heaven and on earth','颂歌继续赞美伊南娜：在天上和地上，你像狮子一样吼叫，把人们打倒；你像一头巨大的野牛，在敌对的国度中奔驰；你像一头狮子，用毒液让敌人和反叛者倒下。我的女主人啊，你的手指伸得很长，你的力量令人敬畏。在你的面前，众神都像飞走的鸟一样四散，阿努纳众神像蝙蝠一样飞进裂缝之中，不敢直视你可怕的目光。诗中一连串的比喻——狮子、野牛、风暴、洪水——都在强调伊南娜的力量是无法抵挡的，连其他的神也要在她面前退避。在苏美尔诗歌里，这种连续的比喻和重复是赞美诗的典型写法，诗人用一个个形象叠加起来，营造出一种不断增强的气势。这一部分为后面的故事做了准备：既然伊南娜是这样一位连众神都畏惧的女神，那么一座胆敢不向她低头的山，就犯了不可饶恕的罪。读者也会在后面看到，伊南娜毁灭埃比赫时，用的正是这里所赞美的风暴、洪水和狮子般的力量。','The hymn goes on: in heaven and on earth you roar like a lion and strike people down; like a huge wild bull you rush through hostile lands; like a lion you make enemies and rebels fall with your venom. My lady, your fingers reach far, your strength inspires awe. Before you the gods scatter like flying birds; the Anuna gods flit like bats into the clefts, not daring to meet your terrible gaze. The string of images — lion, wild bull, storm, flood — insists that Inana''s power cannot be withstood, so that even other gods give way before her. In Sumerian poetry such chains of images and repetitions are typical of hymns, the poet heaping figure upon figure to build a rising force. This part prepares the story: since Inana is a goddess even the gods fear, a mountain that dares not bow to her has committed an unforgivable offence. Readers will see later that when Inana destroys Ebiḫ she uses just the storm, flood and lion-like strength praised here.','claim.v0360.inana_ebih.02','苏美尔文《伊南娜与埃比赫》（ETCSL 1.3.2）伊南娜之威的颂歌。','本篇按段落定位，不标具体行号；个别诗行残缺或意义不明。'),
('storysec.v0360.sumerian_inana_ebih_etcsl.3','storyver.sumerian.inana_ebih_etcsl',3,'不肯低头的山','The mountain that would not bow','接着，伊南娜自己开口说话：我，女神，在天上巡行，在地上巡行；我，伊南娜，在天上巡行，在地上巡行，在埃拉姆和苏巴尔图巡行，在卢卢布山区巡行。当我来到群山的中心，当我，女神，走近那座山时，它没有尊敬我；当我，伊南娜，走近那座山时，它没有尊敬我。当我走近埃比赫山时，它没有尊敬我。它没有把鼻子贴到地上，没有用嘴唇去亲吻尘土。在苏美尔人的世界里，“把鼻子贴到地上”“用嘴唇亲吻尘土”是向神或国王表示臣服的姿势。埃比赫山——通常被认为是美索不达米亚东北部的哈姆林山——高耸而美丽，却没有向伊南娜行礼，这对一位以威严著称的女神来说是极大的冒犯。诗人在这里用了重复的手法，把同一句话说了好几遍，既表现了伊南娜的愤怒在一次次加深，也让听众记住了埃比赫的罪名。从神话的角度看，一座山的“不敬”，也许反映了古代美索不达米亚人与东北山地民族之间的冲突。','Then Inana herself speaks: I, the goddess, was walking around in heaven, walking around on earth; I, Inana, was walking around in heaven and on earth, in Elam and Subir, in the Lulubi mountains. When I came to the heart of the mountains, when I, the goddess, approached the mountain, it showed me no respect; when I, Inana, approached the mountain, it showed me no respect. When I approached the mountain range of Ebiḫ, it showed me no respect. It did not put its nose to the ground for me; it did not rub its lips in the dust for me. In the Sumerian world, putting one''s nose to the ground and rubbing one''s lips in the dust were gestures of submission to a god or king. Ebiḫ — usually identified with the Jebel Hamrin in north-eastern Mesopotamia — lofty and beautiful, did not bow to Inana, a grave affront to a goddess famed for her majesty. The poet repeats the same words several times, showing Inana''s anger growing and fixing Ebiḫ''s offence in the listeners'' minds. Mythically, a mountain''s ''disrespect'' may reflect conflicts between the people of Mesopotamia and the peoples of the north-eastern highlands.','claim.v0360.inana_ebih.03','苏美尔文《伊南娜与埃比赫》（ETCSL 1.3.2）伊南娜对埃比赫的控诉。','本篇按段落定位，不标具体行号；个别诗行残缺或意义不明。'),
('storysec.v0360.sumerian_inana_ebih_etcsl.4','storyver.sumerian.inana_ebih_etcsl',4,'伊南娜的誓言','Inana''s vow','伊南娜接着发誓：我要让恐怖充满这座高耸的山脉。我要在它的面前立起攻城锤，在它的身边放上战斧；我要在它的山坡上挑起战斗，准备好箭和箭袋；我要让它的森林燃起大火，用斧子惩罚它的罪恶；我要让净化之神吉比尔在它的水道中施行他的工作。我要在那难以接近的山脉中散布恐怖。就像一座被安诅咒的城市，它将不会被重建；就像一座被恩利尔怒视的城市，它将永远不能再抬起头来。这段誓言里出现了苏美尔的两位最高神：天神安和风暴之神恩利尔。伊南娜把她要毁灭的埃比赫，比作被这两位大神诅咒的城市，说明她认为自己有权像最高的神一样施行惩罚。吉比尔是火神，诗中让他“在水道中施行工作”，可能是指用火烧毁山中的一切。这段誓言充满了战争的意象，让人想起美索不达米亚国王们征伐山地时的铭文。可是伊南娜并没有立刻行动，在出发之前，她还要先去见她的父亲安。','Inana goes on to vow: I shall fill the soaring mountain range with my terror. Against its flanks I shall set battering rams, against its sides I shall set axes; I shall raise battle on its slopes and make ready arrows and quivers; I shall set fire to its thick forests and take an axe to its evil deeds; I shall make Gibil, the purifier, do his work at its watercourses. I shall spread terror through the inaccessible mountain range. Like a city cursed by An, it will not be restored; like a city on which Enlil has frowned, may it never again lift up its neck. The vow names two of the highest Sumerian gods, An of the sky and Enlil of the storm; Inana likens Ebiḫ to a city cursed by them, claiming the right to punish as the highest gods do. Gibil is the fire god; having him ''work at its watercourses'' may mean burning everything in the mountains. The vow is full of the imagery of war, recalling the inscriptions of Mesopotamian kings campaigning in the highlands. Yet Inana does not act at once; before she sets out, she will go to her father An.','claim.v0360.inana_ebih.04','苏美尔文《伊南娜与埃比赫》（ETCSL 1.3.2）伊南娜的誓言。','本篇按段落定位，不标具体行号；个别诗行残缺或意义不明。'),
('storysec.v0360.sumerian_inana_ebih_etcsl.5','storyver.sumerian.inana_ebih_etcsl',5,'盛装出发','Arrayed for the journey','月神苏恩的女儿伊南娜穿上了她的衣服，披上了王后的服饰，让喜悦笼罩全身。她在额头上戴上了恐怖与光芒，在神圣的脖子上戴上了红玉髓的花饰，手里握着七个头的权杖，那是她的战斗武器。她在黎明时出发，踏上了青金石的台阶。伊南娜的盛装在苏美尔文学中常常是一个重要的场景：在《伊南娜下冥界》里，她穿戴七件神力出发；在这里，她的打扮同样充满了力量的象征。“恐怖与光芒”是苏美尔诗歌中神的特有属性，被称为“尼”与“美拉姆”，表示神那令人畏惧的光辉。红玉髓和青金石是美索不达米亚最珍贵的宝石，它们常常来自遥远的东方山地——讽刺的是，伊南娜要征伐的，正是这样出产宝石的山区。七个头的权杖则强调她作为战神的身份。诗中说她是“苏恩的女儿”，这是苏美尔传统中常见的说法；在其他传统中，伊南娜也被说成是安的女儿。这首诗里，她称安为“父亲”，两种说法都保留着。','Inana, daughter of Suen, put on her garments, the garments of queenship, and wrapped herself in joy. She placed terror and radiance on her brow, set rosettes of carnelian around her holy neck, and grasped the seven-headed mace, her weapon of battle. At dawn she set out and placed her foot upon the lapis lazuli steps. Inana''s adornment is often a key scene in Sumerian literature: in Inana''s Descent she sets out wearing seven divine powers; here too her dress is full of the symbols of power. ''Terror and radiance'' — ni and melam in Sumerian — are the particular attributes of the gods, their awe-inspiring splendour. Carnelian and lapis lazuli were the most precious stones in Mesopotamia, often brought from the distant eastern highlands — and ironically it is just such stone-yielding mountains that Inana sets out to conquer. The seven-headed mace marks her as a war goddess. The poem calls her ''daughter of Suen'', the moon god, a common Sumerian tradition; elsewhere Inana is said to be the daughter of An. In this poem she calls An her father, and both traditions stand side by side.','claim.v0360.inana_ebih.05','苏美尔文《伊南娜与埃比赫》（ETCSL 1.3.2）伊南娜盛装出发。','本篇按段落定位，不标具体行号；个别诗行残缺或意义不明。'),
('storysec.v0360.sumerian_inana_ebih_etcsl.6','storyver.sumerian.inana_ebih_etcsl',6,'向父亲安致敬','Before her father An','伊南娜来到天神安的面前，在他面前俯伏下拜，然后对他说：我的父亲安，我向你致敬！请听我的话。安让我在天上令人畏惧，让我在大地上令人敬畏。在众神的居所中，我是最出众的；在阿努纳众神中，我是最强大的。可是那埃比赫山，当我走近它时，它没有尊敬我；它没有把鼻子贴到地上，没有用嘴唇亲吻尘土。让我去对付它吧，让我用我的力量让它屈服，让它知道我的威严。在苏美尔的众神中，安是天空之神，是众神之父和最古老的神之一，他的神庙在乌鲁克，与伊南娜的神庙伊安那相邻。“伊安那”这个名字本身的意思就是“安的房子”。在神话中，安常常是一位遥远而威严的长者，众神遇到大事时要向他请示。伊南娜在出征之前专程来见安，表明她虽然自信，却仍然尊重天神的权威，希望得到他的认可。这一幕也为下一章安的警告做了铺垫：这位最古老的神，对伊南娜的计划有着不同的看法。','Inana came before An, the sky god, prostrated herself before him and said: My father An, I greet you! Hear my words. An has made me terrifying in heaven, awesome on earth. Among the dwellings of the gods I am foremost; among the Anuna gods I am mightiest. But Ebiḫ — when I approached it, it showed me no respect; it did not put its nose to the ground nor rub its lips in the dust for me. Let me deal with it; let me bring it low with my power and make it know my majesty. Among the Sumerian gods An is god of the sky, father of the gods and one of the oldest of them; his temple stood at Uruk beside Inana''s temple, the Eanna — a name meaning ''House of An''. In myth An is often a distant, majestic elder whom the gods consult on great matters. That Inana comes to him before her campaign shows that, confident as she is, she still respects the sky god''s authority and seeks his approval. The scene prepares An''s warning in the next chapter: the oldest of the gods sees her plan differently.','claim.v0360.inana_ebih.06','苏美尔文《伊南娜与埃比赫》（ETCSL 1.3.2）伊南娜向安请求。','本篇按段落定位，不标具体行号；个别诗行残缺或意义不明。'),
('storysec.v0360.sumerian_inana_ebih_etcsl.7','storyver.sumerian.inana_ebih_etcsl',7,'安的警告','An''s warning','众神之王安回答她说：我的小女儿要求毁灭那座山——她要去做什么？伊南娜要求毁灭那座山——她要去做什么？她要求毁灭埃比赫——她要去做什么？那座山让可怕的恐怖降落在众神的居所，让恐惧散布在阿努纳众神神圣的住处。它的威严和光芒令人畏惧，它的荣耀遍及四方。它的果实沉甸甸地垂下，繁茂的植物生长着；它的森林里有狮子，有野公羊和雄鹿，有野牛在草地上吃草，有鹿在林中交配。你无法穿过它可怕的地方，那里的恐怖令人生畏。年轻的女子啊，你无法对付它。安的这番话很有意思：他并没有说埃比赫没有冒犯伊南娜，也没有说伊南娜无权惩罚它，而是提醒她，这座山本身就拥有强大的力量，连众神都惧怕它。他描写山中丰美的果实和成群的野兽，既是在警告伊南娜，也像是在赞美这座山。在苏美尔诗歌里，长者的劝告常常被年轻的英雄或女神拒绝——在《吉尔伽美什与胡瓦瓦》中，吉尔伽美什也不顾劝阻前往雪松林。安的警告在这里成了伊南娜愤怒的导火索。','An, king of the gods, answered her: my little one demands the mountain''s destruction — what is she taking on? Inana demands the mountain''s destruction — what is she taking on? The young woman demands the destruction of Ebiḫ — what is she taking on? That mountain has made fearsome terror fall on the dwellings of the gods; it has spread fear through the holy places of the Anuna. Its terror and radiance are awesome, its glory spreads abroad. Its fruits hang heavy, its luxuriant growth flourishes; in its forests are lions, wild rams and stags, wild bulls grazing in the meadows, deer mating among the trees. You cannot pass through its fearsome places; its terror is dreadful. Young woman, you cannot go against it. An''s words are striking: he does not say Ebiḫ has not offended Inana, nor that she has no right to punish it; he warns her that the mountain itself is mighty, feared even by the gods. His picture of its rich fruit and teeming beasts is both a warning and almost a praise of the mountain. In Sumerian poetry an elder''s counsel is often refused by a young hero or goddess — in Gilgameš and Ḫuwawa Gilgameš too sets out for the Cedar Forest against advice. Here An''s warning only kindles Inana''s anger.','claim.v0360.inana_ebih.07','苏美尔文《伊南娜与埃比赫》（ETCSL 1.3.2）安的回答。','本篇按段落定位，不标具体行号；个别诗行残缺或意义不明。'),
('storysec.v0360.sumerian_inana_ebih_etcsl.8','storyver.sumerian.inana_ebih_etcsl',8,'打开武库','The arsenal thrown open','安这样说完，女主人伊南娜在愤怒和狂怒之中，打开了战斗之屋的大门，推开了青金石的城门。她带出了伟大的战斗，召来了巨大的洪水，掀起了邪恶的风暴，让大雨与狂风一同扑向埃比赫。她用可怕的光芒覆盖了它，用恐怖笼罩了它，像风暴一样向它席卷而去。在这一段里，伊南娜不再请求，而是行动。前面颂歌中赞美的风暴、洪水和狮子般的力量，现在全都化成了对埃比赫的攻击。苏美尔诗歌中，神的愤怒常常被描写成自然的灾难：风暴、洪水、火焰；伊南娜作为战争女神，同时也掌握着这些自然力量。青金石城门和战斗之屋的意象，让人想起神庙和城市的防御工事，伊南娜像一位国王一样，从自己的城中出发，率领着无形的大军前去征讨。安的警告没有让她退缩，反而让她更加愤怒：她要证明，就算是连众神都畏惧的山，也必须向她低头。泥板在这一部分有一些残缺，个别诗句的意思不完全清楚，本篇只讲能读通的部分，不补写残缺处。','When An had spoken, the lady, in her rage and anger, opened the gate of the house of battle and pushed open the lapis lazuli door. She brought out the great battle, summoned the great flood, and raised an evil storm, sending rain and gale together against Ebiḫ. She covered it with fearsome radiance, wrapped it in terror and swept down on it like a storm. Here Inana no longer asks; she acts. The storm, flood and lion-like strength praised in the opening hymn now all turn into an assault on Ebiḫ. In Sumerian poetry divine wrath is often pictured as natural disaster — storm, flood, fire — and Inana, as war goddess, commands these forces too. The lapis lazuli door and the house of battle recall the defences of temples and cities: like a king, Inana sets out from her own city at the head of an invisible army. An''s warning has not held her back but only fed her anger: she will prove that even a mountain the gods fear must bow to her. The tablets are somewhat broken in this part, and some lines are not wholly clear; this retelling follows only what can be read and does not fill the gaps.','claim.v0360.inana_ebih.08','苏美尔文《伊南娜与埃比赫》（ETCSL 1.3.2）对埃比赫的进攻。','本篇按段落定位，不标具体行号；个别诗行残缺或意义不明。'),
('storysec.v0360.sumerian_inana_ebih_etcsl.9','storyver.sumerian.inana_ebih_etcsl',9,'山的毁灭','The destruction of the mountain','伊南娜抓住了埃比赫的脖子，像割下一把草一样；她把匕首的利齿刺进它的内部，像雷声一样吼叫。构成埃比赫身体的石头从山坡上哗啦啦地滚落下来，从山的侧面和裂缝里，巨大的蛇吐出毒液。她诅咒了它的森林，咒骂了它的树木，用干旱让它的橡树枯死，在它的山坡上倾倒火焰，让浓烟笼罩着它。女神在这座山上确立了她的权威。在这一段里，诗人把一座山写成了一个有生命的敌人：它有脖子、有内脏，它的石头像骨头一样崩落。这种把自然地貌人格化的写法在苏美尔文学中很常见，山在这里既是一个地方，也是一个与女神对抗的对手。曾经被安描写得果实丰美、草木繁茂的埃比赫，现在只剩下干旱、火焰和浓烟。从现实的角度看，这首诗可能反映了美索不达米亚王国对东北山区的军事征伐，伊南娜作为战争女神，被看作这些征伐的神圣保护者。可是在诗中，这一切都被写成了女神个人的愤怒与胜利：一座山的命运，取决于它是否向女神低头。','Inana seized Ebiḫ by the neck as one cuts grass; she pressed the teeth of the dagger into its interior and roared like thunder. The stones that formed Ebiḫ''s body came clattering down its flanks, and from its sides and crevices great serpents spat venom. She cursed its forests and damned its trees; she killed its oaks with drought, poured fire on its slopes and made its smoke dense. The goddess established her authority over the mountain. Here the poet makes a mountain into a living enemy: it has a neck and an inner body, and its stones fall away like bones. Personifying the landscape is common in Sumerian literature; the mountain is both a place and an adversary who stands against the goddess. Ebiḫ, which An had described as rich in fruit and luxuriant growth, is left with only drought, fire and smoke. Historically the poem may reflect the campaigns of Mesopotamian kingdoms against the north-eastern highlands, Inana as war goddess being seen as their divine patron. But in the poem all of it is the goddess''s own anger and victory: a mountain''s fate depends on whether it bows to her.','claim.v0360.inana_ebih.09','苏美尔文《伊南娜与埃比赫》（ETCSL 1.3.2）埃比赫的毁灭。','本篇按段落定位，不标具体行号；个别诗行残缺或意义不明。'),
('storysec.v0360.sumerian_inana_ebih_etcsl.10','storyver.sumerian.inana_ebih_etcsl',10,'胜利的宣言','The victory speech','伊南娜站在被毁灭的山上，宣告她的胜利：山脉啊，因为你高耸，因为你巍峨，因为你迷人，因为你美丽，因为你穿着神圣的衣服，因为你伸向天空，因为你没有把鼻子贴到地上，因为你没有用嘴唇亲吻尘土，所以我杀死了你，让你倒下。我像抓住大象一样抓住了你的獠牙，像对付一头巨大的野牛一样，抓住你粗壮的角，把你摔倒在地。我让你感受到了我的力量。接着她说，她在那里建起了神庙、设立了宝座，让她的威名在那里流传。诗的最后一行赞美道：为了毁灭埃比赫，伟大的苏恩之子、少女伊南娜，愿你受到赞美。这篇宣言用一连串“因为”把埃比赫的“罪名”列了出来，其中有许多其实是赞美：高耸、巍峨、美丽，这些都是山的本性。诗人似乎在暗示，埃比赫真正的罪过不是它的美，而是它的骄傲：它以为自己伸向天空，就可以不向女神低头。整首诗从对伊南娜的赞美开始，又以对伊南娜的赞美结束，讲述了一位不容冒犯的女神如何惩罚一座骄傲的山。','Standing on the ruined mountain, Inana proclaims her victory: Mountain range, because of your elevation, because of your height, because of your attractiveness, because of your beauty, because you wore a holy garment, because you reached up to heaven, because you did not put your nose to the ground, because you did not rub your lips in the dust, I have killed you and brought you low. As one seizes an elephant, I have seized you by your tusks; as with a great wild bull, I have brought you to the ground by your thick horns. I have made you feel my strength. Then she says that she built a temple there and set up a throne, so that her fame would be known there. The last line gives praise: for destroying Ebiḫ, great child of Suen, maiden Inana, be praised. The speech lists Ebiḫ''s ''offences'' in a string of ''becauses'', many of which are really praise — height, loftiness and beauty are simply the mountain''s nature. The poet seems to hint that Ebiḫ''s real fault was not its beauty but its pride: it thought that reaching to heaven excused it from bowing to the goddess. The poem begins and ends in praise of Inana, telling how a goddess who will not be slighted punishes a proud mountain.','claim.v0360.inana_ebih.10','苏美尔文《伊南娜与埃比赫》（ETCSL 1.3.2）伊南娜的胜利宣言与结尾颂词。','本篇按段落定位，不标具体行号；个别诗行残缺或意义不明。'),
('storysec.v0360.chinese_lunheng_leixu.1','storyver.chinese.lunheng_leixu',1,'盛夏的雷电','Summer thunder','王充在《雷虚》的开头写道：盛夏的时候，雷电来得又快又猛，劈断树木，毁坏房屋，有时还会打死人。世俗的人认为，劈断树木、毁坏房屋，是上天来取龙；而打死人，是因为那人有暗中的过错，比如给别人吃了不干净的食物，上天发怒，就用雷把他打死。那隆隆的雷声，就是上天发怒的声音，就像人生气时大声呵斥一样。王充说，世上不论是愚笨的人还是聪明的人，没有一个人不这样认为。可是，如果按照人间的道理来推论，这些都是虚妄的说法。这一段话保存了汉代人对雷的普遍信仰：雷电不是自然现象，而是上天意志的表现；它既能取走天上的龙，也能惩罚人间的罪过。在这种信仰里，雷带有强烈的道德色彩，人们畏惧雷，也就是畏惧上天的审判。直到今天，汉语里还有“天打雷劈”这样的说法，用来诅咒做了坏事的人，可见这种观念流传之久。王充写这篇文章，正是要挑战这种人人深信不疑的看法。','Wang Chong opens ''Lei Xu'' thus: in high summer thunder and lightning come swift and fierce, splitting trees, wrecking houses and sometimes killing people. Common people believe that when trees are split and houses wrecked, Heaven is taking a dragon; and when a man is killed, it is for a hidden fault — having fed others unclean food, for instance — so that Heaven in anger strikes him dead. The rumbling is the sound of Heaven''s anger, like a man shouting in rage. Wang says that in all the world, foolish or wise, no one thinks otherwise; yet reasoned out by human sense, these are false words. The passage preserves a common Han belief: thunder and lightning are not natural events but signs of Heaven''s will, able both to take dragons up to the sky and to punish wrongdoing on earth. In this belief thunder carries a strong moral colour; to fear thunder is to fear Heaven''s judgement. Even today Chinese has the curse ''may Heaven strike you with thunder'' for those who do evil, showing how long the idea has lasted. Wang wrote this chapter to challenge a view everyone held without doubt.','claim.v0360.leigong_leixu.01','王充《论衡·雷虚》开篇：世俗之说。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.chinese_lunheng_leixu.2','storyver.chinese.lunheng_leixu',2,'天怒之说','Heaven''s anger','王充首先反驳“雷是天怒”的说法。他说：雷发作的时候，只是一股气、一种声音。它劈断树木、毁坏房屋的时候，也会打死人；打死人的时候，也会劈断树木、毁坏房屋。可是世人却偏偏说，劈断树木、毁坏房屋是上天取龙，打死人是惩罚暗中的过错。取龙是吉事，罚过是凶事，两者吉凶不同，却在同一时间、发出同一种声音，这是讲不通的。王充又说：主张这种说法的人认为，隆隆的雷声是上天发怒时的呵斥声。这个说法用来解释惩罚罪过还勉强说得通，用来解释取龙就不合适了。惩罚罪过，上天发怒是可以理解的；可是取龙的时候，龙又有什么过错，让上天对它发怒呢？王充的论证方法很有特点：他并不直接否认上天的存在，而是从人们自己的说法里找出矛盾，用对方的逻辑来驳倒对方。这种方法贯穿了整部《论衡》，“论衡”的意思，就是用天平来衡量各种言论的轻重真假。','Wang first refutes the idea that thunder is Heaven''s anger. When thunder breaks out, he says, it is one breath, one sound. When it splits trees and wrecks houses it may also kill people, and when it kills people it may also split trees and wreck houses. Yet people say that splitting trees and wrecking houses is Heaven taking a dragon, and killing people is punishing a hidden fault. Taking a dragon is auspicious, punishing a fault is ominous; that two things so different should happen at the same moment with the same sound makes no sense. He goes on: those who hold this view say the rumbling is Heaven''s angry shouting. That might suit the punishment of faults, but not the taking of dragons. For punishing faults Heaven''s anger is understandable; but in taking a dragon, what fault has the dragon committed that Heaven should be angry with it? Wang''s method is distinctive: he does not deny Heaven outright but finds contradictions in people''s own claims and refutes them by their own logic. The method runs through the whole Lunheng — whose title means ''weighing discourses'' on a balance to test their weight and truth.','claim.v0360.leigong_leixu.02','王充《论衡·雷虚》驳天怒之说。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.chinese_lunheng_leixu.3','storyver.chinese.lunheng_leixu',3,'取龙与罚过','Taking dragons and punishing faults','王充在《雷虚》中反复回到同一个问题：雷击中的人和物，究竟是被上天特意选中的，还是偶然碰上的？他在篇末的结论里说得很清楚：人在树下、在屋子里，偶然被雷打中，就死了。在他看来，这就像一个人走在路上，偶然被落下的石头砸中一样，并不是因为这个人有什么不为人知的罪过。至于“取龙”的说法，汉代人相信龙能升天，雷雨之时龙随着云气上升，所以把雷电劈开大树看作龙从树中飞出、被上天取走。王充在《论衡》的另一篇《龙虚》里专门讨论过这个问题，认为龙是一种生活在水中的动物，和鱼、蛇一类相近，它在雷雨中出现，是因为喜欢水和云气，并不是上天特意来取它。在《雷虚》里，他就这样把雷的“道德含义”一层层地剥去：雷打中的是偶然在场的人和物，它没有选择，也没有判断。这在当时是非常大胆的看法，因为汉代的许多学者都相信天能感应人事，用雷电、日食、地震等灾异来警告人间的君主和百姓。','In ''Lei Xu'' Wang returns again and again to one question: are the people and things struck by thunder chosen by Heaven, or simply in the way? In the chapter''s conclusion he says plainly: a man under a tree or in a house who happens to be struck by thunder dies. To him this is like a man on the road who happens to be hit by a falling stone; it is not because of some hidden fault. As for ''taking a dragon'', Han people believed dragons could rise to heaven, ascending with the clouds in thunderstorms, so a tree split by lightning was seen as a dragon flying out of it and being taken up by Heaven. Wang discusses this in another chapter of the Lunheng, ''Long Xu'' (''Dragons: the Falsehood''), holding that the dragon is a creature living in water, akin to fish and snakes, which appears in storms because it loves water and cloud, not because Heaven comes to take it. So in ''Lei Xu'' he strips away thunder''s moral meaning layer by layer: it strikes whatever people and things happen to be there, with no choice and no judgement. It was a bold view in its time, when many Han scholars believed Heaven responded to human affairs and warned rulers and people through portents such as thunder, eclipses and earthquakes.','claim.v0360.leigong_leixu.03','王充《论衡·雷虚》人在木下屋间，偶中而死。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.chinese_lunheng_leixu.4','storyver.chinese.lunheng_leixu',4,'画中的雷公','Leigong in the paintings','王充接着描述了汉代画工所画的雷的样子：画工们画雷的形状，是一面接一面串在一起的鼓，像一串连鼓；又画一个人，有着力士一样的容貌，称他为雷公，让他左手拉着连鼓，右手举着槌子，像是正在敲打的样子。他们的意思是：雷声隆隆，就是连鼓互相撞击的声音；那突然像什么东西被撕裂一样的巨响，就是槌子敲打发出的声音；雷打死人，就是雷公拉着连鼓、举槌一齐敲打的结果。世人都相信这些画，没有人认为不对。王充说，如果追究其本原，这是虚妄的形象。这段文字非常珍贵，它是现存最早明确描写“雷公”形象的文献之一：一个力士般的人物，身边环绕着连鼓，手持槌子。这一形象在汉代画像石、画像砖上确实能够看到，后来又演变成民间和道教中长着鸟嘴、带着翅膀、手持锤钻的雷公。王充记录这一形象本是为了反驳它，却因此为后人保存下了汉代雷神信仰的面貌。','Wang then describes how Han painters drew thunder: painters picture thunder as drums strung together one after another, like a chain of linked drums; and they draw a man with the look of a strongman, whom they call Leigong, the Thunder Lord, making him pull the linked drums with his left hand and raise a mallet in his right, as if striking. Their meaning is that the rumbling of thunder is the sound of the linked drums knocking together; the sudden crack like something tearing is the sound of the mallet striking; and when thunder kills a man, Leigong is pulling the drums and striking with the mallet together. People believe these pictures, and none says otherwise. Traced back to its origin, Wang says, it is a false image. The passage is precious: it is one of the earliest surviving texts to describe the figure of Leigong explicitly — a strongman ringed by linked drums, mallet in hand. The figure can indeed be seen on Han carved stones and tiles, and later grew into the bird-beaked, winged Leigong with hammer and chisel of folk religion and Daoism. Wang recorded the image to refute it, and so preserved for us the face of Han thunder belief.','claim.v0360.leigong_leixu.04','王充《论衡·雷虚》图画之工图雷之状。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.chinese_lunheng_leixu.5','storyver.chinese.lunheng_leixu',5,'雷公怎样飞','How could Leigong fly?','王充对画中的雷公提出了好几条质疑。他说：雷不是声音，就是气。声音和气，怎么能被拉住、推动，变成连鼓的样子呢？如果雷公真的存在，他敲鼓的时候，鼓要挂在什么地方才能响？他的脚又踩在什么地方？没有可以踩的地方，又怎么能打雷呢？王充又拿画中的仙人来比较：会飞的东西都有翅膀，没有翅膀却能飞的，人们称之为仙人，所以画工画仙人的时候，就给他画上翅膀，让他身上长毛，手臂变成翅膀。如果雷公和仙人一样能飞，就应该也给他画上翅膀；如果雷公不会飞，画工却说他在天上打雷，这就不对了；如果雷公真的会飞，却不给他画翅膀，这也不对。王充的这条论证很有意思：他用画工自己的绘画规则，指出了雷公形象的矛盾。有趣的是，后来的雷公画像果然长出了翅膀，在宋元以后的民间版画和寺观壁画中，雷公常被画成长着双翅的样子——也许正是为了弥补王充指出的这个“漏洞”。','Wang raises several objections to the painted Leigong. Thunder, he says, is either a sound or a breath (qi). How can a sound or a breath be pulled and pushed and made into linked drums? If Leigong really exists, where are the drums hung so that they sound when he strikes them? And where does he stand? With nothing to stand on, how could he make thunder? Wang compares the painted immortals: whatever flies has wings, and those who fly without wings people call immortals, so when painters draw immortals they give them wings — hair grows on their bodies and their arms become wings. If Leigong flies like the immortals, he too should be drawn with wings; if he does not fly, the painters are wrong to say he thunders in the sky; and if he truly flies but is drawn without wings, that is wrong too. The argument is clever: Wang uses the painters'' own conventions to expose a contradiction in Leigong''s image. Interestingly, later Leigong did grow wings: in folk prints and temple murals from the Song and Yuan onward he is often shown with two wings — perhaps to mend the very gap Wang pointed out.','claim.v0360.leigong_leixu.05','王充《论衡·雷虚》雷公无翼何以飞。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.chinese_lunheng_leixu.6','storyver.chinese.lunheng_leixu',6,'雷是火','Thunder is fire','王充提出了自己的看法：雷，是火。他举出五条证据来证明这一点。第一条：被雷打死的人，如果检查他的身体，打中头部的，胡须和头发都被烧焦；打中身体的，皮肤被灼伤；走近他的尸体，能闻到火的气味。这是第一条证据。第二条：懂得道术的人认为，雷把石头烧成红色，把烧红的石头投进井里，石头烫而井水冷，激发出巨大的响声，就像雷的声音一样。这是第二条证据。第三条：人受了寒气，寒气进入腹中，腹中原本温暖，温与寒相争，就激发出像雷一样的肠鸣。这是第三条证据。第四条：打雷的时候，闪电时时出现，大得像火光一样明亮。这是第四条证据。第五条：雷打下来的时候，有时会烧毁人的房屋和地上的草木。这是第五条证据。王充说，有这五条证据，就可以知道雷是火了。这五条证据，有的来自对雷击死者的观察，有的来自术士的实验，有的来自人体的感受，有的来自对闪电的观察，体现了一种难得的、重视经验与验证的思考方式。','Wang states his own view: thunder is fire. He gives five proofs. First: if you examine the body of a man killed by thunder, where the head was struck the beard and hair are singed, where the body was struck the skin is burned, and near the corpse you can smell fire. That is the first proof. Second: masters of the arts hold that thunder burns stones red; throw a red-hot stone into a well, and the hot stone and cold water clash with a great noise like thunder. That is the second proof. Third: when a man is chilled, the cold enters his belly; the belly being warm, warmth and cold contend and make a rumbling like thunder. That is the third proof. Fourth: when it thunders, lightning flashes again and again, bright as the glare of fire. That is the fourth proof. Fifth: when thunder strikes, it sometimes burns people''s houses and the plants and trees on the ground. That is the fifth proof. With these five proofs, Wang says, we know that thunder is fire. Drawn from examining the struck dead, from the experiments of adepts, from bodily sensation and from watching lightning, the proofs show a rare way of thinking that values experience and verification.','claim.v0360.leigong_leixu.06','王充《论衡·雷虚》雷者火也之五验。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.chinese_lunheng_leixu.7','storyver.chinese.lunheng_leixu',7,'炉中的水','Water on the furnace','王充又用一个日常的经验来说明雷的威力：拿一斗水浇进冶炼铸造的炉火里，水气激荡，迸裂开来，发出的声音就像雷一样；如果有人靠得太近，一定会被灼伤身体。天地就是一个大火炉，阳气就是猛烈的炉火，云和雨就是大量的水；它们相争、激荡、迸射，怎么会不迅猛呢？打中了人的身体，人怎么会不死呢？这个比喻把雷电和汉代人熟悉的冶铁、铸铜工艺联系在了一起。汉代的冶铁业非常发达，许多人都见过炉火遇水时发出的爆裂声，王充用这个大家都能理解的现象来解释雷，让抽象的天象变得可以想象、可以检验。在他看来，雷之所以能杀人，不是因为雷公的槌子，也不是因为上天的愤怒，而是因为天地这个大炉子中，火与水相遇时那股巨大的力量。这种把宇宙看作一个巨大熔炉的想法，在中国古代思想中有着悠久的传统，贾谊的《鵩鸟赋》里就有“天地为炉兮，造化为工”的名句。','Wang also uses an everyday experience to show thunder''s force: pour a peck of water onto the fire of a smelting furnace, and the steam bursts and cracks with a sound like thunder; anyone too close will surely be scorched. Heaven and earth are a great furnace, the yang energy is a fierce fire, the clouds and rain are a great deal of water; when they contend, clash and burst forth, how could it not be violent? And when it strikes a man''s body, how could he not die? The comparison links thunder to the iron-smelting and bronze-casting the Han knew well. Han iron-working was highly developed, and many had heard the crack when furnace fire meets water; Wang uses a familiar sight to explain thunder, making an abstract heavenly sign something one can imagine and test. For him thunder kills not because of Leigong''s mallet nor Heaven''s anger but because of the immense force of fire meeting water in the great furnace of heaven and earth. The image of the cosmos as a vast furnace has a long tradition in Chinese thought; Jia Yi''s ''Rhapsody on the Owl'' has the famous line ''Heaven and earth are the furnace, creation the smith.''','claim.v0360.leigong_leixu.07','王充《论衡·雷虚》一斗水灌冶铸之火。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.chinese_lunheng_leixu.8','storyver.chinese.lunheng_leixu',8,'太阳的激气','The surging energy of great yang','在《雷虚》的结论部分，王充说：雷是太阳之气激发出来的。怎么证明呢？正月阳气开始萌动，所以正月开始打雷；五月阳气最盛，所以五月雷声最猛烈；秋冬阳气衰弱，所以秋冬雷就潜藏起来了。盛夏的时候，太阳之气当令，阴气却来侵犯它，阴阳相争，就互相摩擦撞击，摩擦撞击就会激射，激射就成为毒，打中人，人就死；打中树，树就折断；打中房屋，房屋就毁坏。人在树下或屋里，偶然被打中就死了。王充用阴阳二气的消长来解释雷的季节变化，这是汉代自然哲学的基本框架；可他与当时许多学者不同，他不认为天会有意地用雷来赏善罚恶。在这篇文章的最后，雷公这位汉代人画中的神，被王充一步步地还原成了一种自然的气。不过，雷公信仰并没有因此消失：后来的民间和道教把雷部众神发展成一个庞大的体系。王充的《雷虚》，既是对雷公信仰最早的详细记录，也是对它最早的系统批评，两者恰好保存在同一篇文字里。','In the conclusion of ''Lei Xu'' Wang says: thunder is the surging energy of great yang. How can this be shown? In the first month yang begins to stir, so thunder begins in the first month; in the fifth month yang is at its height, so thunder is fiercest in the fifth month; in autumn and winter yang weakens, so thunder lies hidden. In high summer great yang holds sway, and yin encroaches on it; yin and yang contend and grind against each other; grinding, they burst forth; bursting, they become a poison — striking a man, it kills him; striking a tree, it breaks it; striking a house, it wrecks it. A man under a tree or in a house who happens to be struck dies. Wang explains the seasons of thunder by the waxing and waning of yin and yang, the basic frame of Han natural philosophy; but unlike many scholars of his day he denies that Heaven uses thunder on purpose to reward good and punish evil. By the chapter''s end Leigong, the god of the Han paintings, has been step by step reduced to a natural energy. Yet belief in Leigong did not vanish: later folk religion and Daoism built a vast ministry of thunder gods. Wang Chong''s ''Lei Xu'' is both the earliest detailed record of the Leigong belief and its earliest systematic critique, preserved in one and the same text.','claim.v0360.leigong_leixu.08','王充《论衡·雷虚》雷者太阳之激气也。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.norse_gylf_4_12_creation_long.1','storyver.norse.gylf_4_12_creation_long',1,'尼夫尔海姆','Niflheim','瑞典国王古鲁菲来到阿斯加尔德，向坐在三个高座上的“至高者”“同样高者”和“第三者”提问。他问：万物最初是怎样开始的？至高者先引了《女巫的预言》中的诗句：太古之时，尤弥尔还活着，那时没有沙，没有海，也没有冰冷的波浪，没有大地，也没有上面的天空，只有一道张开的鸿沟，哪里都没有青草。同样高者接着说：在大地被造出以前许多个时代，就已经有了尼夫尔海姆，那是一个寒冷、雾气弥漫的世界。在它的中央有一眼泉水，叫做赫瓦格尔米尔，从泉中流出许多条河，它们的名字是斯沃尔、贡斯拉、菲约尔姆、芬布尔图尔、斯利德、赫里德、叙尔格、于尔格、维德和雷普特；还有一条叫约尔，就流在冥界的大门旁边。斯诺里在这里把古诗和散文解说交织在一起，这是整部《欺骗古鲁菲》的写法：三位神秘的人物一问一答，把北欧神话的宇宙从头到尾讲给一个凡间的国王听。读者要记住，这是十三世纪冰岛人斯诺里的编排，而不是一部古老的经典。','King Gylfi of Sweden came to Ásgarðr and put his questions to the three seated on high thrones — High, Just-as-high and Third. He asked: what was the beginning, and how did things start? High first quoted the Völuspá: in the morning of time, when Ymir lived, there was no sand nor sea nor cool waves, no earth nor heaven above, only a yawning gap, and grass nowhere. Just-as-high went on: many ages before the earth was made, Niflheimr was made, a cold and misty world. In its middle lies a spring called Hvergelmir, and from it flow the rivers named Svöl, Gunnþrá, Fjörm, Fimbulþul, Slíðr, Hríð, Sylgr, Ylgr, Víð and Leiptr; and Gjöll, which runs next to the gates of Hel. Snorri weaves old verse and prose explanation together here, as throughout Gylfaginning: three mysterious figures answer question by question, laying out the whole Norse cosmos for a mortal king. Readers should remember that this is the arrangement of Snorri, a thirteenth-century Icelander, not an ancient scripture.','claim.v0360.gylf_creation.01','斯诺里《埃达·欺骗古鲁菲》第4章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.norse_gylf_4_12_creation_long.2','storyver.norse.gylf_4_12_creation_long',2,'穆斯佩尔与苏尔特','Muspell and Surtr','第三者接着说：最先存在的，是南方的一个世界，叫做穆斯佩尔。它明亮而炽热，熊熊燃烧，凡是不属于那里、不在那里出生的人，都无法在那里停留。在它的边界上坐着一个名叫苏尔特的巨人，守卫着这片土地。他手里握着一把燃烧的剑。到了世界末日，他将出发去劫掠，打败所有的神，用火焚烧整个世界。斯诺里在这里又引了《女巫的预言》：苏尔特从南方带着枝条的毁灭者而来，他的剑上闪耀着众神之日的光芒。这样，世界的开端就已经预示了它的结局：在宇宙形成之前，那个要在诸神黄昏中焚烧一切的巨人，就已经坐在火焰之国的边界上等待了。北方是寒冷的尼夫尔海姆，南方是炽热的穆斯佩尔，两者之间，便是下一章要讲的那道鸿沟。苏尔特在这一部分只出现了短短几句，可是读过诸神黄昏的读者会知道，他手中那把燃烧的剑，最后会把整个世界投入火海。','Third went on: first of all there was a world in the southern region called Muspell. It is bright and hot, flaming and burning, and impassable for those who are foreign to it and not native there. At its border sits one called Surtr, guarding the land. He has a flaming sword, and at the end of the world he will go and wage war, defeat all the gods and burn the whole world with fire. Snorri quotes the Völuspá again: Surtr comes from the south with the bane of branches; the sun of the slaughter-gods shines from his sword. So the beginning of the world already foretells its end: before the cosmos was shaped, the giant who will burn everything at Ragnarök was already sitting at the border of the land of fire, waiting. In the north lies cold Niflheimr, in the south burning Muspell, and between them the gap that the next chapter describes. Surtr appears here in only a few sentences, but readers who know the twilight of the gods know that his flaming sword will at last cast the whole world into fire.','claim.v0360.gylf_creation.02','斯诺里《埃达·欺骗古鲁菲》第4章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.norse_gylf_4_12_creation_long.3','storyver.norse.gylf_4_12_creation_long',3,'金伦加鸿沟','Ginnungagap','古鲁菲问：在人类出现以前，世界是什么样子？至高者说：那些叫做埃利伐加尔的河流，离源头越来越远，河里带着的毒液就像熔炉里流出的熔渣一样变硬，凝成了冰。冰停住不再流动时，冰上的毒液蒸腾成雾气，又冻成白霜，一层又一层地堆满了金伦加鸿沟。同样高者说：金伦加鸿沟朝北的那一边，充满了沉重的冰和白霜，里面是阴冷的雾气和狂风；而朝南的那一边，有从穆斯佩尔飞来的火星和火花，明亮温暖。第三者说：正如从尼夫尔海姆来的一切都是寒冷和严酷的，靠近穆斯佩尔的一切都是炽热和明亮的；而金伦加鸿沟的中间，就像无风的空气一样温和。当热风吹到白霜上，白霜开始融化、滴落。这一段被学者们称为北欧神话的“冰与火的宇宙起源论”：世界不是被某位神从无中造出来的，而是在寒冷与炎热相遇的地方，从融化的霜中生长出来的。','Gylfi asked how things were before generations of men came to be. High said: the rivers called Élivágar, when they had come so far from their source that the poison in them hardened like the slag that runs from a furnace, turned to ice. When the ice stopped flowing, the poison drizzling from it rose as vapour and froze into rime, layer upon layer, until it filled Ginnungagap. Just-as-high said: the part of Ginnungagap facing north filled with heavy ice and rime, with mist and gusts within; but the southern part was lit by the sparks and glowing embers flying out of Muspell. Third said: just as cold and all things harsh came from Niflheimr, so everything near Muspell was hot and bright; but Ginnungagap was as mild as windless air. When the hot breath met the rime, it melted and dripped. Scholars call this the Norse cosmogony of fire and ice: the world is not made from nothing by a god, but grows out of melting rime where cold and heat meet.','claim.v0360.gylf_creation.03','斯诺里《埃达·欺骗古鲁菲》第5章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.norse_gylf_4_12_creation_long.4','storyver.norse.gylf_4_12_creation_long',4,'尤弥尔','Ymir','至高者接着说：融化的霜滴在热力的作用下有了生命，变成了一个人的形状，他名叫尤弥尔，霜巨人们叫他奥尔格尔米尔，所有的霜巨人都是他的后代。斯诺里引了《短女巫的预言》和《瓦夫斯鲁德尼尔之歌》的诗句来证明这一点：所有的女巫都来自维德尔夫，所有的巫师来自维尔梅迪，所有的巨人来自尤弥尔；从埃利伐加尔溅出的毒滴长成了巨人。古鲁菲问：那些后代是怎样生出来的？尤弥尔是神吗？至高者说：我们绝不承认他是神。他是邪恶的，他的后代也都是邪恶的，我们称他们为霜巨人。据说，尤弥尔睡着的时候出了汗，从他的左臂下长出了一男一女，他的一只脚又和另一只脚生下了一个儿子。巨人的种族就是这样开始的。在这个世界上最早出现的生命是一个巨人，而不是一位神。斯诺里让至高者特别强调，尤弥尔不是神，这样的区分贯穿了整个北欧神话：众神与巨人既是亲戚，又是永远的敌人。','High went on: when the drops of melting rime were quickened by the power of the heat, they took the shape of a man, who is called Ymir; the frost-giants call him Aurgelmir, and from him are descended all the families of frost-giants. Snorri quotes the Shorter Völuspá and Vafþrúðnismál to bear this out: all seeresses are from Viðólfr, all wizards from Vilmeiðr, all giants from Ymir; drops of poison spurted from Élivágar and grew into a giant. Gylfi asked how the generations came from him, and whether he was a god. High said: we by no means acknowledge him as a god. He was evil, and all his descendants; we call them frost-giants. It is said that as he slept he sweated, and under his left arm grew a male and a female, and one of his legs begot a son with the other. So the race of giants began. The first living being in this world is a giant, not a god. Snorri has High insist that Ymir is no god, a distinction that runs through all of Norse myth: gods and giants are kin, and yet eternal enemies.','claim.v0360.gylf_creation.04','斯诺里《埃达·欺骗古鲁菲》第5章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.norse_gylf_4_12_creation_long.5','storyver.norse.gylf_4_12_creation_long',5,'母牛与布里','The cow and Búri','古鲁菲问：尤弥尔住在哪里，靠什么活着？至高者说：接下来，融化的霜滴又变成了一头母牛，名叫欧德姆布拉，从她的乳房里流出四条奶河，尤弥尔就靠这些奶活着。古鲁菲又问：那母牛靠什么活着？至高者说：她去舔那些带着盐味的冰块。第一天，她舔到傍晚，冰块里露出了一个人的头发；第二天，露出了一个人的头；第三天，整个人都露出来了。这个人名叫布里，他长得英俊，身材高大，力量强大。布里是众神的始祖，可是《欺骗古鲁菲》除了这几句之外，再也没有讲他做过什么。他不是从霜巨人的身体里生出来的，而是被一头母牛从冰里舔出来的；这一点很重要，因为它让众神的祖先有了与巨人不同的起源。一边是从融霜中自然生长出来、带着恶意的巨人尤弥尔，一边是被母牛的舌头一天天唤醒的、英俊而强大的布里——冰与火之间的世界里，第一次出现了两种不同的生命。','Gylfi asked where Ymir lived and what he lived on. High said: next, as the rime dripped, there came into being a cow called Auðumbla, and four rivers of milk flowed from her teats, and she fed Ymir. Gylfi asked what the cow lived on. High said: she licked the ice-blocks, which were salty. On the first day she licked them, by evening there came out of the blocks a man''s hair; on the second day a man''s head; on the third day the whole man was there. He was called Búri; he was beautiful in appearance, big and powerful. Búri is the forefather of the gods, yet apart from these lines Gylfaginning tells nothing of his deeds. He is not born from the body of the frost-giant but licked out of the ice by a cow — and this matters, for it gives the ancestors of the gods a different origin from the giants. On one side is Ymir, grown of himself from the melting rime and full of evil; on the other, Búri, beautiful and strong, awakened day by day by a cow''s tongue. In the world between ice and fire, two kinds of life appear for the first time.','claim.v0360.gylf_creation.05','斯诺里《埃达·欺骗古鲁菲》第6章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.norse_gylf_4_12_creation_long.6','storyver.norse.gylf_4_12_creation_long',6,'博尔与贝斯特拉','Borr and Bestla','至高者接着说：布里有了一个儿子，名叫博尔。博尔娶了一个名叫贝斯特拉的女子，她是巨人博尔索恩的女儿。他们生了三个儿子：第一个叫奥丁，第二个叫维利，第三个叫维。至高者说：我们相信，奥丁和他的兄弟们一定是天地的统治者；我们认为这就是他的名字，这是我们所知道的最伟大、最光荣的人的名字，你们也可以这样称呼他。这几句话透露了一个重要的事实：众神之父奥丁的母亲是一位巨人的女儿。也就是说，从一开始，众神的血统里就混着巨人的血。博尔本人和他的父亲布里一样，除了作为奥丁的父亲之外，几乎没有自己的故事；北欧的诗人常常用“博尔之子”来称呼奥丁和他的兄弟们。在下一章里，这三兄弟将做出一件决定整个宇宙面貌的事：他们要杀死尤弥尔——他们母亲那一族的始祖——用他的身体来建造世界。','High went on: Búri begot a son called Borr. Borr married a woman called Bestla, daughter of the giant Bölþorn, and they had three sons: the first Óðinn, the second Vili, the third Vé. High said: it is our belief that Óðinn and his brothers must be the rulers of heaven and earth; we think that this is his name — it is the name of the greatest and most glorious man we know, and you may well call him so. These sentences reveal something important: the mother of Óðinn, father of the gods, was a giant''s daughter. From the very beginning, giants'' blood is mixed into the line of the gods. Borr himself, like his father Búri, has almost no story apart from being Óðinn''s father; Norse poets often call Óðinn and his brothers ''the sons of Borr''. In the next chapter the three brothers will do the deed that shapes the whole cosmos: they will kill Ymir — forefather of their mother''s own people — and build the world from his body.','claim.v0360.gylf_creation.06','斯诺里《埃达·欺骗古鲁菲》第6章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.norse_gylf_4_12_creation_long.7','storyver.norse.gylf_4_12_creation_long',7,'杀死尤弥尔','The slaying of Ymir','古鲁菲问：他们之间是和睦相处，还是互相敌对？至高者说：博尔的儿子们杀死了巨人尤弥尔。当他倒下的时候，从他的伤口里流出了大量的血，所有的霜巨人都淹死在血里，只有一个逃了出来，霜巨人们叫他贝尔格尔米尔。他和他的妻子爬上了他的“卢德”——这个词的意思不太确定，可能是一种箱子、磨盘的木架或者小船——在那里活了下来，所有霜巨人的后代都是从他们传下来的。斯诺里又引了《瓦夫斯鲁德尼尔之歌》：在大地被造出以前无数个冬天，贝尔格尔米尔就出生了；我最早记得的，是那位智慧的巨人被放在卢德上的情景。这一章短得出奇，斯诺里没有讲博尔的儿子们为什么要杀尤弥尔，也没有描写这场杀戮的过程。可是它的后果是巨大的：第一位巨人的死，既造成了一场淹没巨人的血洪水，又为众神提供了建造世界的材料。巨人一族并没有灭绝，贝尔格尔米尔一家幸存下来，众神与巨人之间的世仇就这样开始了。','Gylfi asked whether there was peace between them or not. High said: the sons of Borr killed the giant Ymir. When he fell, so much blood flowed from his wounds that with it they drowned all the race of frost-giants, except that one escaped with his household; the giants call him Bergelmir. He climbed up on to his lúðr with his wife and was saved there — the word is uncertain, perhaps a box, the frame of a mill or a boat — and from them are descended the families of frost-giants. Snorri quotes Vafþrúðnismál: countless winters before the earth was shaped, Bergelmir was born; the first thing I remember is when that wise giant was laid on a lúðr. The chapter is strikingly short: Snorri gives no reason why the sons of Borr killed Ymir and does not describe the killing. Yet its results are vast: the first giant''s death causes a flood of blood that drowns the giants and gives the gods the material to build the world. The giants are not wiped out — Bergelmir''s household survives — and so the feud between gods and giants begins.','claim.v0360.gylf_creation.07','斯诺里《埃达·欺骗古鲁菲》第7章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.norse_gylf_4_12_creation_long.8','storyver.norse.gylf_4_12_creation_long',8,'大地与海','Earth and sea','古鲁菲问：如果你们认为博尔的儿子们是神，那他们后来做了什么？至高者说：这可不是一件小事。他们把尤弥尔抬到金伦加鸿沟的正中央，用他造出了大地：用他的肉造成大地，用他的血造成海洋和湖泊；大地用他的骨头造成，岩石用他的牙齿和碎骨造成。同样高者说：从伤口里流出来的血，他们用来造成了海洋，把大地放在海中间，让海环绕着大地，大多数人都会觉得渡过这片海是不可能的。斯诺里在这里描写的世界，是一个圆形的大地被浩瀚的海洋环绕的样子，这与中世纪北欧人对世界的想象相符：人们居住在中间的陆地上，四周是无边的大海，海的边缘住着巨人。这个造世界的方法令人印象深刻：世界不是从虚无中创造出来的，而是从一个被杀死的巨人身上一块一块地改造出来的。这种以原始巨人的身体造世界的神话，在其他文化中也能看到，比如中国的盘古传说，可是北欧的版本带着一种独特的严酷：世界诞生于一场杀戮。','Gylfi asked what the sons of Borr did then, if they are to be believed gods. High said: there is no little to tell. They took Ymir and carried him to the middle of Ginnungagap, and from him made the earth: from his flesh the earth, from his blood the sea and the lakes; the rocks were made of his bones, and stones and scree from his teeth and broken bones. Just-as-high said: from the blood that flowed from his wounds they made the sea, setting the earth in its midst and placing the sea round it in a ring, so that most men would think it impossible to cross. The world Snorri describes is a round earth ringed by a vast ocean, matching the medieval Norse picture: people live on the land in the middle, the endless sea lies all around, and on its edges dwell the giants. The way the world is made is striking: not from nothing, but piece by piece from the body of a slain giant. Myths of a world made from a primordial being''s body appear in other cultures too, such as the Chinese tale of Pangu, but the Norse version has its own harshness: the world is born of a killing.','claim.v0360.gylf_creation.08','斯诺里《埃达·欺骗古鲁菲》第8章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.norse_gylf_4_12_creation_long.9','storyver.norse.gylf_4_12_creation_long',9,'天空与群星','Sky and stars','第三者说：他们又拿起尤弥尔的头骨，用它造成天空，把它架在大地的上方，四个角各让一个矮人撑着，这四个矮人的名字是奥斯特里、韦斯特里、诺尔德里和苏德里——东、西、北、南。接着，他们从穆斯佩尔飞来、四处飘散的火星和火花中取来一些，把它们放在金伦加鸿沟的中间，高高地挂在天空上，从上面和下面照亮天空和大地。他们给所有的火光都定了位置：有些固定在天空中，有些在天空下游走，他们为它们安排了位置，规定了运行的路线。古诗里说，从那以后，人们才能计算日子和年份。斯诺里引《女巫的预言》说：那时太阳不知道它的居所在哪里，月亮不知道它的力量有多大，星辰不知道它们的位置在何处。这一章把天上的秩序归功于博尔的儿子们：天空是巨人的头骨，四方由矮人支撑，群星是南方火焰之国的火星。世界的每一部分，都来自冰与火之间那场最初的相遇。','Third said: they also took his skull and made the sky from it, set it up over the earth with four points, and under each corner put a dwarf, named Austri, Vestri, Norðri and Suðri — East, West, North and South. Then they took molten particles and sparks that were flying about uncontrolled, shot out of Muspell, and set them in the middle of Ginnungagap, high in the sky, to give light above and below. They fixed all the lights, some in the sky, while others moved in a wandering course beneath the sky, and they appointed their positions and ordained their paths. So, old poems say, from then on days and years were reckoned. Snorri quotes the Völuspá: the sun did not know where her dwelling was, the moon did not know what power he had, the stars did not know where their places were. This chapter credits the order of the heavens to the sons of Borr: the sky is a giant''s skull, its corners held by dwarves, and the stars are sparks from the southern land of fire. Every part of the world comes from that first meeting of ice and fire.','claim.v0360.gylf_creation.09','斯诺里《埃达·欺骗古鲁菲》第8章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.norse_gylf_4_12_creation_long.10','storyver.norse.gylf_4_12_creation_long',10,'米德加尔德','Miðgarðr','至高者说：大地是圆的，四周环绕着深深的海洋。他们把沿海的土地给巨人的族类居住；在大地的内部，他们用尤弥尔的眉毛（原文 brár，也可理解为睫毛）筑起一道围墙，抵御巨人，保护整个世界。他们把这座要塞叫做米德加尔德，意思是“中间的围场”。他们还把尤弥尔的脑子抛向空中，造成了云。斯诺里又引了《格里姆尼尔之歌》的诗句：用尤弥尔的肉造成了大地，用他的血造成了海洋，用骨头造成了山岭，用头发造成了树木，用头骨造成了天空；用他的眉毛，仁慈的神灵为人类的儿子们造了米德加尔德；用他的脑子造成了所有那些阴沉的云。这段诗与散文的叙述稍有不同，比如散文里没有提到用头发造树木，斯诺里把它们并列在一起，两者都保留了下来。米德加尔德后来成了人类世界的名字：一个被围墙保护着、夹在巨人的海岸和众神的天空之间的地方。在北欧人的想象里，人类的家园从一开始就是一座需要防守的城堡。','High said: the earth is circular, and around it lies the deep sea. Along the shore they gave lands to the giants to settle; but inland on the earth they built a fortification round the world against the hostility of giants, using Ymir''s eyelashes, and they called this fortress Miðgarðr, the middle enclosure. They also took his brains and threw them into the sky and made the clouds from them. Snorri quotes Grímnismál: from Ymir''s flesh the earth was shaped, from his blood the sea, from his bones the hills, from his hair the trees, from his skull the sky; from his eyelashes the kindly powers made Miðgarðr for the sons of men; and from his brains were made all the gloomy clouds. The verse differs slightly from the prose — the prose does not mention trees from his hair — and Snorri sets them side by side, keeping both. Miðgarðr became the name of the human world: a place protected by a wall, lying between the giants'' shores and the gods'' sky. In the Norse imagination the home of mankind was from the start a fortress to be defended.','claim.v0360.gylf_creation.10','斯诺里《埃达·欺骗古鲁菲》第8章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.norse_gylf_4_12_creation_long.11','storyver.norse.gylf_4_12_creation_long',11,'最初的人与奥丁的家','The first people and Odin''s household','同样高者说：博尔的儿子们沿着海岸走，发现了两根树干，就拿起它们，用它们造出了人。第一个给了他们气息和生命，第二个给了他们智慧和行动，第三个给了他们面容、语言、听觉和视觉。他们给这两个人衣服和名字：男人叫阿斯克，女人叫恩布拉，从他们传下了人类，人们被给予了米德加尔德居住。接着，他们在世界的中央为自己建了一座城，叫做阿斯加尔德——斯诺里说，我们称之为特洛伊——众神和他们的家族住在那里。那里有一个叫希利德斯克亚尔夫的地方，奥丁坐在那里的高座上，能看到整个世界。他的妻子叫弗丽嘉，他们的家族被称为阿萨神族。至高者还说：大地约尔德是奥丁的女儿，也是他的妻子；他从她那里得到了第一个儿子，那就是阿萨·索尔，他力大无穷，能战胜一切活物。在这里，约尔德——大地——第一次以人格化的女神出现，她是雷神索尔的母亲。斯诺里把阿斯加尔德说成特洛伊，反映了中世纪学者把北欧众神解释为古代人间君王的做法。','Just-as-high said: as the sons of Borr walked along the shore they found two logs and picked them up and made people of them. The first gave breath and life, the second consciousness and movement, the third a face, speech, hearing and sight. They gave them clothes and names: the man was called Askr and the woman Embla, and from them were descended mankind, who were given Miðgarðr to dwell in. Next they built themselves a city in the middle of the world, called Ásgarðr — which, Snorri says, we call Troy — where the gods and their descendants lived. There is a place called Hliðskjálf, and when Óðinn sat in the high seat there he could see over the whole world. His wife was called Frigg, and their family is called the Æsir. High also said: Jörð, the Earth, was his daughter and his wife; from her he had his first son, Ása-Þórr, who has strength and might that overcome all living things. Here Jörð, the Earth, appears for the first time as a goddess in person, mother of the thunder god Thor. Snorri''s identifying Ásgarðr with Troy reflects the medieval habit of explaining the Norse gods as ancient human kings.','claim.v0360.gylf_creation.11','斯诺里《埃达·欺骗古鲁菲》第9章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.norse_gylf_4_12_creation_long.12','storyver.norse.gylf_4_12_creation_long',12,'夜与昼','Night and Day','同样高者接着说：住在巨人国的一个巨人名叫纳尔菲，他有一个女儿叫夜，诺特。她像她的家族一样，皮肤黝黑。她先嫁给一个叫纳格尔法里的人，生了一个儿子叫奥德；然后她嫁给一个叫安纳尔的人，他们的女儿叫约尔德，大地；最后她嫁给属于阿萨神族的德林，他们的儿子叫昼，达格，他像父亲一家一样明亮而美丽。众神之父把夜和她的儿子昼带到天上，给了他们两匹马和两辆车，让他们每二十四小时环绕大地一周。夜先骑着名叫赫里姆法克西的马，每天早晨，马嘴里的泡沫滴在大地上，就是露水；昼的马叫斯金法克西，它的鬃毛照亮了整个天空和大地。这里出现了一个值得注意的细节：前一章说约尔德是奥丁的女儿，这一章又说她是夜和安纳尔的女儿。斯诺里的书中把这两种说法都写了进去，并没有加以调和；本篇照样保留，不替他统一。无论如何，大地和昼、夜一样，都被安排进了天上的秩序之中。','Just-as-high went on: there was a giant living in Jötunheimr called Nörfi or Narfi. He had a daughter called Nótt, Night, who was black and dark like her family. She was married to a man called Naglfari, and their son was Auðr; next she married one called Annarr, and their daughter was called Jörð, Earth; last she married Dellingr, who was of the race of the Æsir, and their son was Dagr, Day, bright and beautiful like his father''s people. Allfather took Night and her son Day and gave them two horses and two chariots and set them up in the sky to ride around the earth every twenty-four hours. Night rides first on the horse called Hrímfaxi, and every morning he bedews the earth with the foam from his bit; Day''s horse is Skinfaxi, and the whole sky and earth are lit by his mane. A detail stands out: the previous chapter called Jörð Óðinn''s daughter, while this one makes her the daughter of Night and Annarr. Snorri''s book contains both statements without reconciling them, and this retelling keeps both. Either way, Earth, like Day and Night, is set into the order of the heavens.','claim.v0360.gylf_creation.12','斯诺里《埃达·欺骗古鲁菲》第10章。','《欺骗古鲁菲》第9章称约尔德为奥丁之女，第10章称她为夜与安纳尔之女，两说并存。'),
('storysec.v0360.norse_gylf_4_12_creation_long.13','storyver.norse.gylf_4_12_creation_long',13,'太阳与月亮','Sun and Moon','古鲁菲问：太阳和月亮是怎样运行的？至高者说：有一个人名叫蒙迪尔法利，他有两个孩子，长得非常美丽，他就给儿子取名叫月亮，玛尼，给女儿取名叫太阳，索尔，并把女儿嫁给了一个叫格伦的人。众神对他的傲慢很生气，就把这两个孩子带到天上，让索尔驾着拉太阳的马车——那太阳是众神用穆斯佩尔飞出的火星造成、用来照亮世界的。拉车的马叫阿尔瓦克和阿尔斯维德，众神在马的肩胛下放了两个风箱来给它们降温，有些古诗称之为“铁的凉气”。玛尼驾驭月亮的行程，掌管月亮的盈亏。他从大地上带走了两个孩子，比尔和赫尤基，当时他们正从比尔吉尔泉打水回来，肩上用一根叫西穆尔的扁担挑着一只叫赛格尔的水桶；他们的父亲叫维兹芬。这两个孩子跟随着玛尼，人们从大地上就能看见他们。学者们常把这两个孩子与月亮上的阴影联系起来。在这一章里，天上的日月也有了人的面孔：它们原本是一个骄傲父亲的美丽孩子，被众神带去天上，永远驾车奔跑。','Gylfi asked how the sun and moon are steered. High said: there was a man called Mundilfari who had two children so fair and beautiful that he called his son Máni, Moon, and his daughter Sól, Sun, and married her to a man called Glenr. The gods were angry at this arrogance and took the brother and sister and set them up in the sky; they made Sól drive the horses that drew the chariot of the sun, which the gods had made to light the worlds from the sparks that flew from Muspell. The horses are called Árvakr and Alsviðr, and under their shoulders the gods put two bellows to cool them, which some sources call iron-coolness. Máni steers the course of the moon and governs its waxing and waning. He took from the earth two children, Bil and Hjúki, as they were coming from the spring called Byrgir carrying on their shoulders the pail called Sægr on the pole Simul; their father was Viðfinnr. These children go with Máni, as can be seen from the earth. Scholars often link the two children with the shadows on the moon. In this chapter sun and moon take on human faces: once the lovely children of a proud father, taken up by the gods to drive across the sky for ever.','claim.v0360.gylf_creation.13','斯诺里《埃达·欺骗古鲁菲》第11章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.norse_gylf_4_12_creation_long.14','storyver.norse.gylf_4_12_creation_long',14,'追逐日月的狼','The wolves that chase sun and moon','古鲁菲说：太阳跑得飞快，好像害怕什么，就算她怕死，也不会跑得更快了。至高者说：难怪她跑得这样快，因为追她的那个东西已经很近了，她除了逃跑没有别的办法。古鲁菲问：是谁这样折磨她？至高者说：是两只狼。追太阳的那只叫斯科尔，它一直追赶太阳，总有一天会追上她；另一只叫哈提，是赫罗德维特尼尔的儿子，它跑在太阳前面，想要抓住月亮，这件事也终将发生。古鲁菲问：这些狼是从哪里来的？至高者说：在米德加尔德的东边，有一个老巨妇住在一片叫做铁树林的森林里，那里住着一些被称为铁树林女巫的女巨人。这个老巨妇生了许多巨人儿子，个个都是狼的模样，这两只狼就是从她那里来的。据说，这一族里最强大的一个叫做玛纳加尔姆，“月亮之犬”，他会吞食所有死者的血肉，吞掉月亮，把血溅满天空和天上，于是太阳失去光芒，狂风四起。创世的故事讲到这里，又一次指向了终结：诸神黄昏时，太阳和月亮终将被狼吞噬。','Gylfi said: the sun moves fast, almost as if she were afraid; she could not go faster if she feared for her life. High said: it is not surprising she goes so fast — her pursuer is close, and she has no way of escape but to run. Gylfi asked who torments her so. High said: two wolves. The one pursuing her is called Sköll; he frightens her, and he will catch her. The other, called Hati Hróðvitnisson, runs ahead of her and wants to catch the moon, and so it will happen. Gylfi asked where the wolves come from. High said: a giantess lives east of Miðgarðr in a forest called Járnviðr, Ironwood, where live the troll-wives called Járnviðjur. The old giantess bore many giants as sons, all in wolf shape, and these wolves come from her. It is said that the mightiest of this race will be the one called Mánagarmr, Moon-dog, who will be filled with the life-blood of all who die; he will swallow the moon and spatter heaven and sky with blood, so that the sun loses its light and the winds roar. The story of creation turns once more toward the end: at Ragnarök sun and moon will be devoured by wolves.','claim.v0360.gylf_creation.14','斯诺里《埃达·欺骗古鲁菲》第12章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.norse_skald_35_sif_long.1','storyver.norse.skald_35_sif_long',1,'希芙与乌勒尔','Sif and Ullr','在斯诺里的《欺骗古鲁菲》里，至高者向古鲁菲一一介绍阿萨神族的众神。说到乌勒尔时，他说：乌勒尔是希芙的儿子，索尔的继子。他是一个出色的弓箭手，又是一个滑雪好手，没有人能和他相比；他相貌英俊，具备战士的种种品质，在一对一的决斗中，向他祈求是很好的。这短短几句话告诉我们：雷神索尔的妻子希芙，在嫁给索尔之前已经有一个儿子，乌勒尔的父亲是谁，斯诺里没有说。在《诗语法》里，乌勒尔被称为“希芙之子”“索尔的继子”“滑雪之神”“弓箭之神”“狩猎之神”和“盾牌之神”，诗人们常用“乌勒尔的船”来指盾牌。至于希芙自己，她在北欧神话里最著名的，就是她那一头金色的头发。在斯诺里的书中，希芙几乎没有开口说话的场面，她出现的地方，总是和她的家人联系在一起：索尔的妻子，乌勒尔的母亲。下面的故事，就从她失去头发的那个早晨开始。','In Snorri''s Gylfaginning, High tells Gylfi about each of the Æsir. Of Ullr he says: Ullr is Sif''s son and Thor''s stepson. He is such a good archer and skier that no one can rival him; he is beautiful in appearance and has a warrior''s qualities, and it is good to pray to him in single combat. These few words tell us that Sif, wife of the thunder god Thor, already had a son before she married Thor; who Ullr''s father was, Snorri does not say. In Skáldskaparmál Ullr is called son of Sif, stepson of Thor, ski-god, bow-god, hunting-god and shield-god, and poets call a shield ''Ullr''s ship''. As for Sif herself, she is most famous in Norse myth for her golden hair. In Snorri''s book she hardly ever speaks; she appears always in relation to her family — Thor''s wife, Ullr''s mother. The story that follows begins on the morning she lost her hair.','claim.v0360.skald_sif.01','斯诺里《埃达·欺骗古鲁菲》第31章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.norse_skald_35_sif_long.2','storyver.norse.skald_35_sif_long',2,'被剪掉的金发','The shorn hair','《诗语法》第35章从一个问题开始：为什么金子被称为“希芙的头发”？斯诺里回答说：狡猾的洛基出于恶作剧，把希芙的头发全部剪掉了。斯诺里没有说洛基为什么要这样做，只说是出于“恶意的玩笑”。在北欧古代的社会里，头发对一个女子来说是美丽和尊严的象征，把一位女神的头发剪光，是极大的侮辱。希芙的头发据说是金色的，这也是后来诗人们用“希芙的头发”来比喻黄金的原因之一。这种用神话典故代替普通名词的说法，叫做“肯宁”，是北欧古诗最重要的修辞手法，《诗语法》这本书就是斯诺里为了教年轻诗人理解这些肯宁而写的。所以，这个故事的讲述方式很特别：它不是为了讲故事而讲故事，而是为了解释一个诗歌用语的来历。可是正是这一次恶作剧，引出了一连串的事件，最后让众神得到了他们最珍贵的几件宝物，包括索尔的锤子妙尔尼尔。','Skáldskaparmál 35 begins with a question: why is gold called Sif''s hair? Snorri answers: Loki Laufeyjarson, for the sake of mischief, cut off all Sif''s hair. He gives no reason beyond malicious fun. In old Norse society a woman''s hair was a sign of beauty and honour, and to shear a goddess''s hair was a grave insult. Sif''s hair is said to have been golden, one reason poets later used ''Sif''s hair'' for gold. This way of replacing an ordinary word with a mythological allusion is called a kenning, the chief ornament of Old Norse verse, and Snorri wrote Skáldskaparmál to teach young poets to understand such kennings. So the tale is told in an unusual way: not for its own sake but to explain where a poetic phrase comes from. Yet this one piece of mischief set off a chain of events that ended with the gods receiving their most precious treasures, among them Thor''s hammer Mjölnir.','claim.v0360.skald_sif.02','斯诺里《埃达·诗语法》第35章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.norse_skald_35_sif_long.3','storyver.norse.skald_35_sif_long',3,'索尔的愤怒','Thor''s anger','索尔发现妻子的头发被剪光后，立刻抓住了洛基。他气得要把洛基身上的每一根骨头都打断。洛基吓坏了，连忙发誓说，他会去请黑精灵——也就是矮人——用金子为希芙做一头新头发，而且这头发要像真的头发一样，能在她头上生长。索尔这才放过了他。在北欧神话里，洛基常常这样：先闯下大祸，然后再想办法弥补，而他想出的办法，往往比原来的局面更好，也更危险。索尔和洛基的关系也很有意思：他们常常一起旅行，比如去乌特加尔洛基的城堡、去寻找被偷走的锤子，可是只要洛基闯了祸，索尔总是第一个抓住他的人。斯诺里在这里对希芙本人的反应只字未提，我们不知道她是哭了还是生气了；故事的焦点很快就从受害的女神转到了两位男神之间的争执，又从争执转到了矮人的铁匠铺。这种叙述方式本身也说明了希芙在神话中的位置：她是故事的起因，却很少是故事的主角。','When Thor found his wife''s hair shorn, he seized Loki and would have broken every bone in his body. Terrified, Loki swore that he would get the black elves — the dwarves — to make Sif a head of hair from gold that would grow like any other hair. Only then did Thor let him go. Loki is often like this in Norse myth: first he causes great trouble, then he finds a way to make it good, and his remedy is often better — and more dangerous — than what came before. Thor and Loki''s relationship is interesting too: they often travel together, to Útgarða-Loki''s castle or in search of the stolen hammer, yet whenever Loki causes mischief, Thor is the first to lay hands on him. Snorri says nothing here about Sif''s own reaction; we do not know whether she wept or raged. The story quickly turns from the wronged goddess to the quarrel between two gods, and from the quarrel to the dwarves'' smithy. That itself shows Sif''s place in the myths: she is the cause of the story, seldom its hero.','claim.v0360.skald_sif.03','斯诺里《埃达·诗语法》第35章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.norse_skald_35_sif_long.4','storyver.norse.skald_35_sif_long',4,'伊瓦尔迪的儿子们','The sons of Ívaldi','洛基于是去找那些被称为伊瓦尔迪之子的矮人。他们为希芙打造了一头金发，同时还造了一艘名叫斯基德布拉德尼尔的船，以及一支名叫贡尼尔的长矛——后来它成了奥丁的矛。这三件宝物都非常出色。斯诺里没有描写矮人们是怎样工作的，也没有说洛基许给了他们什么报酬；也许洛基只是用花言巧语说服了他们。在北欧神话里，矮人是最好的工匠，他们住在山里和地下，掌握着神也不具备的锻造技艺。众神手中的许多宝物都出自矮人之手：除了这里的三件，还有束缚芬里尔狼的绳索格莱普尼尔、芙蕾雅的项链布里欣加曼等等。伊瓦尔迪的儿子们只在这个故事里出现，他们的名字也没有被记下来。洛基带着这三件宝物，本可以直接回到阿斯加尔德，平息索尔的怒气；可他偏偏又在矮人中间惹出了另一件事。这一次，他押上的赌注不是别的，而是他自己的头。','So Loki went to the dwarves called the sons of Ívaldi. They made the hair for Sif, and also the ship Skíðblaðnir and the spear called Gungnir, which became Óðinn''s. All three treasures were excellent. Snorri does not describe how the dwarves worked, nor what Loki promised them; perhaps he simply talked them round. In Norse myth dwarves are the finest craftsmen, living in mountains and underground, masters of a smithcraft even the gods lack. Many of the gods'' treasures come from dwarves: besides these three there are Gleipnir, the fetter that binds the wolf Fenrir, and Freyja''s necklace Brísingamen. The sons of Ívaldi appear only in this tale, and their own names are not recorded. With these three treasures Loki could have gone straight back to Ásgarðr and calmed Thor''s anger; but he went and started something else among the dwarves. This time the stake was nothing less than his own head.','claim.v0360.skald_sif.04','斯诺里《埃达·诗语法》第35章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.norse_skald_35_sif_long.5','storyver.norse.skald_35_sif_long',5,'洛基的赌注','Loki''s wager','洛基拿着宝物，找到一个名叫勃洛克的矮人，跟他打赌，把自己的头押上：他说勃洛克的兄弟埃特里——有些抄本和后人叫他辛德里——不可能打造出三件和这三件一样好的宝物。勃洛克接受了赌注。斯诺里没有说洛基为什么要冒这个险，也许是为了炫耀，也许只是因为他天生喜欢挑起争端。可这个赌注却成了众神的幸运：正因为洛基想证明伊瓦尔迪之子的宝物无人能比，另一对矮人兄弟才拼尽全力，打造出了更加了不起的东西。北欧神话里常常有这样的赌局：奥丁和巨人瓦夫斯鲁德尼尔以头为注比智慧，索尔和乌特加尔洛基的手下比力气。在这些故事里，“以头为注”是一种极端的赌法，输了的一方就要付出生命。洛基押上自己的头，看起来很勇敢，其实他早已为自己留好了退路，这一点要到故事的最后才会揭晓。勃洛克和埃特里兄弟俩则一起走进了铁匠铺，一个负责打铁，一个负责拉风箱。','Loki took the treasures and wagered his head with a dwarf called Brokkr that his brother Eitri — called Sindri in some manuscripts and later writers — could not make three treasures as good as these. Brokkr took the wager. Snorri does not say why Loki took the risk — to show off, perhaps, or simply because he loved to stir up trouble. Yet the wager turned out to be the gods'' good fortune: because Loki wanted to prove the sons of Ívaldi unmatched, another pair of dwarf brothers strained every nerve to make still more wonderful things. Norse myth is full of such contests: Óðinn wagers his head against the giant Vafþrúðnir in a contest of wisdom, and Thor matches strength with Útgarða-Loki''s people. In these tales a wager of one''s head is the most extreme stake: the loser pays with his life. Loki''s bet looks brave, but he has already prepared his escape, as the end of the story will show. Brokkr and Eitri went into the smithy together, one to work the metal and one to blow the bellows.','claim.v0360.skald_sif.05','斯诺里《埃达·诗语法》第35章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.norse_skald_35_sif_long.6','storyver.norse.skald_35_sif_long',6,'金鬃野猪','The golden-bristled boar','埃特里把一张猪皮放进炉子里，吩咐勃洛克拉风箱，在他把东西从炉子里取出来以前，一刻也不许停下。埃特里一离开铁匠铺，就有一只苍蝇落在勃洛克的手上，狠狠地叮他。可是勃洛克照样拉着风箱，一直等到铁匠回来，从炉子里取出东西——那是一头长着金色鬃毛的野猪。这头野猪后来被称为古林博斯帝，意思是“金鬃”。斯诺里在后面会讲到它的本领。这只苍蝇是谁？斯诺里在这里没有明说，可是读者很容易猜到：会变化形体、又最希望勃洛克失败的，只有洛基。在其他故事里，洛基曾变成母马、鲑鱼、海豹和老妇人，变成一只苍蝇对他来说毫不困难。勃洛克忍着疼痛，没有让风箱停下，这是兄弟俩的第一次胜利。在古代北欧的铁匠铺里，拉风箱是一件非常要紧的活，火候稍有差池，整件作品就会毁掉，所以埃特里才一再叮嘱，无论发生什么都不能停。','Eitri laid a pigskin in the forge and told Brokkr to blow the bellows and not stop until he took out what he had put in. As soon as Eitri had gone out of the smithy, a fly settled on Brokkr''s hand and stung him hard. But Brokkr kept blowing until the smith came back and took the thing out of the forge — a boar with bristles of gold. It is called Gullinbursti, Golden-bristle, and Snorri will tell its powers later. Who was the fly? Snorri does not say here, but the reader can easily guess: the shape-changer who most wanted Brokkr to fail could only be Loki. In other tales Loki becomes a mare, a salmon, a seal and an old woman; a fly is no trouble to him. Brokkr bore the pain and kept the bellows going — the brothers'' first victory. In an old Norse smithy working the bellows was vital work; a moment''s lapse in the heat could ruin the whole piece, which is why Eitri warned him again and again not to stop, whatever happened.','claim.v0360.skald_sif.06','斯诺里《埃达·诗语法》第35章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.norse_skald_35_sif_long.7','storyver.norse.skald_35_sif_long',7,'指环德劳普尼尔','The ring Draupnir','接着，埃特里把金子放进炉子里，又吩咐勃洛克拉风箱，在他回来以前不许停下。他走了出去，那只苍蝇又来了，这一次落在勃洛克的脖子上，叮得比上一次厉害一倍。可勃洛克还是不停地拉着风箱，直到铁匠回来，从炉子里取出了一枚金指环，名叫德劳普尼尔，意思是“滴落者”。这枚指环的本领，斯诺里也要到后面才说。在北欧神话里，德劳普尼尔还有另一个故事：在巴德尔的葬礼上，奥丁把这枚指环放在儿子的火葬堆上；后来赫尔莫德到冥界去求赫尔放回巴德尔，巴德尔又把指环交给他带回给奥丁。这说明德劳普尼尔是奥丁最珍爱的宝物之一。在这一次锻造中，苍蝇的攻击比上一次更凶，可勃洛克依然没有动摇。兄弟俩已经做成了两件宝物，离赢得赌局只差最后一件。而洛基——如果那只苍蝇真的是他——也只剩下最后一次机会了。','Next Eitri laid gold in the forge and bade Brokkr blow and not stop until he came back. He went out, and the fly came again, settling on Brokkr''s neck and stinging twice as hard as before. Still Brokkr kept blowing until the smith came back and took out of the forge a gold ring called Draupnir, the Dripper. Its powers, too, Snorri tells later. Draupnir has another story in Norse myth: at Baldr''s funeral Óðinn laid the ring on his son''s pyre, and when Hermóðr rode to Hel to beg for Baldr''s return, Baldr sent the ring back to Óðinn with him. Clearly Draupnir was among Óðinn''s most treasured possessions. In this second forging the fly''s attack was fiercer than before, yet Brokkr did not waver. The brothers had made two treasures and needed only one more to win the wager. And Loki — if the fly was indeed Loki — had only one chance left.','claim.v0360.skald_sif.07','斯诺里《埃达·诗语法》第35章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.norse_skald_35_sif_long.8','storyver.norse.skald_35_sif_long',8,'锤子与苍蝇','The hammer and the fly','最后，埃特里把铁放进炉子里，对勃洛克说，必须一直拉风箱，否则一切都会毁掉。这一次，苍蝇落在勃洛克两眼之间，叮他的眼皮，血流进了他的眼睛，他什么也看不见了。勃洛克只好用手去赶苍蝇，就在这一瞬间，风箱停了下来，炉火也弱了下去。这时铁匠回来了，说炉子里的东西差一点就全毁了。他从炉子里取出的是一把锤子。这就是后来索尔的锤子妙尔尼尔。因为那一瞬间的停顿，锤子的柄做得短了一些——这是它唯一的缺陷。斯诺里把这个细节讲得很清楚：众神最强大的武器，是在一只苍蝇的干扰下诞生的，它的小小缺陷，正是那场恶作剧留下的痕迹。勃洛克把三件宝物收好，带着它们和洛基一起前往阿斯加尔德，去请众神裁决这场赌局。在路上，洛基大概已经在盘算，怎样才能保住自己的脑袋了。','Last, Eitri laid iron in the forge and told Brokkr he must keep blowing or everything would be spoiled. This time the fly settled between Brokkr''s eyes and stung his eyelids, so that blood ran into his eyes and he could not see. He let go of the bellows just long enough to brush the fly away with his hand — and in that moment the bellows stopped and the fire sank. Then the smith came back and said that what was in the forge had come close to being spoiled entirely. He took out a hammer — the hammer that became Thor''s, Mjölnir. Because of that moment''s pause its handle was rather short; that was its only flaw. Snorri makes the detail plain: the mightiest weapon of the gods was born under the attack of a fly, and its small defect is the mark left by that mischief. Brokkr gathered the three treasures and set off with Loki to Ásgarðr to have the gods judge the wager. On the way, no doubt, Loki was already planning how to keep his head.','claim.v0360.skald_sif.08','斯诺里《埃达·诗语法》第35章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.norse_skald_35_sif_long.9','storyver.norse.skald_35_sif_long',9,'三位裁判','The three judges','勃洛克带着他的宝物来到阿斯加尔德，洛基也带来了伊瓦尔迪之子的宝物。众神坐上审判的座位，决定由奥丁、索尔和弗雷三位神来裁决，他们说的话就是最后的判决。双方各自把宝物献给这三位神：洛基献给他们伊瓦尔迪之子的作品，勃洛克献给他们埃特里的作品。这是北欧神话里少有的一场“工艺比赛”，评判的标准不是谁的出身更高贵，也不是谁的力气更大，而是哪一件宝物对众神更有用。选出奥丁、索尔和弗雷三位来做裁判，也许是因为每一方的宝物都正好有三件，而每一件都分别送给这三位神中的一位：奥丁得到一件，索尔得到一件，弗雷得到一件。在古代北欧的法律中，重大的争端常常交给公认的裁判或在“庭”上公开裁决，斯诺里的叙述也带着这种色彩：众神像冰岛人在法庭上一样，坐下来听取双方的陈述，然后作出判决。接下来，两边轮流展示宝物。','Brokkr came to Ásgarðr with his treasures, and Loki brought the treasures of the sons of Ívaldi. The gods took their seats in judgement, and it was agreed that the verdict of Óðinn, Thor and Freyr should stand. Each side presented its treasures to these three: Loki the work of the sons of Ívaldi, Brokkr the work of Eitri. It is a rare ''craft contest'' in Norse myth, judged not by birth or strength but by which treasures are more useful to the gods. Óðinn, Thor and Freyr may have been chosen because each side had exactly three treasures, and each treasure went to one of the three — one to Óðinn, one to Thor, one to Freyr. In old Norse law great disputes were often given to acknowledged judges or settled openly at an assembly, and Snorri''s account carries that colour: the gods sit down like Icelanders at the Thing, hear both sides and give judgement. Then the two sides present their treasures in turn.','claim.v0360.skald_sif.09','斯诺里《埃达·诗语法》第35章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.norse_skald_35_sif_long.10','storyver.norse.skald_35_sif_long',10,'洛基的礼物','Loki''s gifts','洛基先献上他的宝物。他把长矛贡尼尔给了奥丁，把头发给了索尔，让他送给希芙，又把船斯基德布拉德尼尔给了弗雷。他一一说明了这些宝物的好处：这支长矛刺出去，无论刺向什么，都不会停下；这头头发，只要一放到希芙的头上，就会像天生的头发一样生长；这艘船，只要升起船帆，就总有顺风，无论要去哪里都能到达，不用的时候，还能像一块布一样折叠起来，放进口袋里。希芙就这样得回了她的头发，而且是一头真正的金发，从此“希芙的头发”成了黄金的代称。这里，被剪掉的头发终于有了一个完美的结局：失去的东西不但被找了回来，而且变得比原来更加珍贵。至于斯基德布拉德尼尔，斯诺里在《欺骗古鲁菲》里还说，它是所有船中最好的，是由伊瓦尔迪的儿子们造的，而纳吉尔法号是最大的船。贡尼尔则成了奥丁的标志，在诸神黄昏的最后一战中，奥丁也将手持贡尼尔与芬里尔狼作战。','Loki presented his treasures first. He gave Óðinn the spear Gungnir, Thor the hair for Sif, and Freyr the ship Skíðblaðnir, and he explained the virtues of each: the spear never stops in its thrust; the hair, as soon as it is placed on Sif''s head, will grow to her like natural hair; and the ship has a fair wind as soon as the sail is hoisted, wherever it is going, and when not in use it can be folded up like a cloth and put in a pocket. So Sif got back her hair, and real golden hair at that, and ever after ''Sif''s hair'' meant gold. The shorn hair has its perfect ending: what was lost is not only restored but made more precious than before. Of Skíðblaðnir Snorri says in Gylfaginning that it is the best of all ships, made by the sons of Ívaldi, while Naglfar is the biggest. Gungnir became Óðinn''s emblem, and in the last battle at Ragnarök Óðinn will carry Gungnir against the wolf Fenrir.','claim.v0360.skald_sif.10','斯诺里《埃达·诗语法》第35章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.norse_skald_35_sif_long.11','storyver.norse.skald_35_sif_long',11,'勃洛克的礼物与判决','Brokkr''s gifts and the verdict','接着，勃洛克献上他的宝物。他把指环德劳普尼尔给了奥丁，说每到第九个夜晚，就会从这枚指环上滴落下八枚同样重的金指环。他把金鬃野猪给了弗雷，说它能在空中和海上奔跑，日夜不停，比任何马都快，无论夜晚多么黑暗，无论在什么阴暗的地方，只要它在，它的金鬃就会发出足够的光亮。他把锤子给了索尔，说索尔可以用它随意猛击任何东西，锤子永远不会失手；如果他把锤子扔出去，它永远不会扔偏，也不会飞得太远而回不到他手里；而且只要他愿意，锤子还能变得很小，可以藏在衬衣里。只是它有一个缺陷：锤柄有点短。众神的判决是：这把锤子是所有宝物中最好的，因为有了它，众神就能抵御霜巨人的侵犯。于是他们判定，矮人赢得了这场赌局。斯诺里在这里点出了妙尔尼尔真正的意义：它不只是一件武器，更是整个阿斯加尔德的守护。正因为如此，后来锤子被巨人偷走时，众神才会那样惊慌。','Then Brokkr presented his treasures. He gave Óðinn the ring, saying that every ninth night eight rings of equal weight would drip from it. He gave Freyr the boar, saying it could run through air and over sea, night and day, faster than any horse, and that it would never be so dark at night or in the worlds of darkness that there would not be light enough wherever it went, so bright did its bristles shine. He gave Thor the hammer, saying that Thor could strike as hard as he liked at whatever he wished and the hammer would never fail; if he threw it, it would never miss and never fly so far that it would not return to his hand; and when he wished, it would be so small that he could carry it inside his tunic. Its one flaw was that the handle was rather short. The gods'' verdict was that the hammer was the best of all the treasures, for it was a defence against the frost-giants; and they judged that the dwarf had won the wager. Snorri makes Mjölnir''s real meaning clear: not only a weapon but the guard of all Ásgarðr. That is why the gods panic when, in another tale, a giant steals it.','claim.v0360.skald_sif.11','斯诺里《埃达·诗语法》第35章。',NULL),
('storysec.v0360.norse_skald_35_sif_long.12','storyver.norse.skald_35_sif_long',12,'缝上的嘴','The sewn lips','洛基提出要用钱赎回自己的头，可矮人说没有这回事。洛基说：那你就来抓我吧！勃洛克去抓他时，洛基已经跑远了——他有一双鞋，穿上它能在空中和海上奔跑。矮人请索尔去抓他，索尔把洛基抓了回来。矮人要砍下洛基的头，洛基却说，头是你的，可脖子不是。于是矮人拿来一根皮绳和一把刀，想在洛基的嘴唇上钻孔，把他的嘴缝起来，可是刀扎不进去。矮人说：要是我兄弟的锥子在这里就好了。他话音刚落，锥子就出现在那里，扎穿了洛基的嘴唇。他用一根叫做瓦塔里的皮绳把洛基的嘴唇缝在一起，洛基后来把皮绳从嘴唇上扯了下来。斯诺里在这一章的最后说：这就是为什么金子被称为希芙的头发。从一次剪发开始，到一张被缝上的嘴结束，这个故事让众神得到了贡尼尔、斯基德布拉德尼尔、德劳普尼尔、古林博斯帝、妙尔尼尔，还有希芙那一头会生长的金发；而那个惹出一切麻烦的洛基，最后也为他的恶作剧付出了代价。','Loki offered to redeem his head, but the dwarf said there was no hope of that. ''Catch me, then,'' said Loki, and when Brokkr tried to catch him he was far away, for Loki had shoes in which he could run through air and over sea. The dwarf asked Thor to catch him, and Thor did. The dwarf was going to cut off his head, but Loki said the head was his, but not the neck. So the dwarf took a thong and a knife and tried to bore holes in Loki''s lips to stitch his mouth up, but the knife would not cut. Then he said it would be better if his brother''s awl were there — and as soon as he spoke the awl was there, and it pierced the lips. He stitched the lips together with a thong called Vartari, and Loki tore the thong out of his lips. At the end of the chapter Snorri says: that is why gold is called Sif''s hair. Beginning with a shearing and ending with a sewn mouth, the tale gives the gods Gungnir, Skíðblaðnir, Draupnir, Gullinbursti, Mjölnir and Sif''s growing golden hair — and Loki, who caused all the trouble, pays at last for his mischief.','claim.v0360.skald_sif.12','斯诺里《埃达·诗语法》第35章。',NULL);

